const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"London. An ante-chamber in the King's palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CHORUS.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","lines":["O for a Muse of fire, that would ascend","The brightest heaven of invention,","A kingdom for a stage, princes to act,","And monarchs to behold the swelling scene!","Then should the warlike Harry, like himself,","Assume the port of Mars; and at his heels,","Leash'd in like hounds, should famine, sword, and fire","Crouch for employment. But pardon, gentles all,","The flat unraised spirits that hath dar'd","On this unworthy scaffold to bring forth","So great an object. Can this cockpit hold","The vasty fields of France? Or may we cram","Within this wooden O the very casques","That did affright the air at Agincourt?","O, pardon! since a crooked figure may","Attest in little place a million;","And let us, ciphers to this great accompt,","On your imaginary forces work.","Suppose within the girdle of these walls","Are now confin'd two mighty monarchies,","Whose high upreared and abutting fronts","The perilous narrow ocean parts asunder;","Piece out our imperfections with your thoughts:","Into a thousand parts divide one man,","And make imaginary puissance;","Think, when we talk of horses, that you see them","Printing their proud hoofs i' the receiving earth.","For 'tis your thoughts that now must deck our kings,","Carry them here and there, jumping o'er times,","Turning the accomplishment of many years","Into an hour-glass: for the which supply,","Admit me Chorus to this history;","Who, prologue-like, your humble patience pray,","Gently to hear, kindly to judge, our play."],"id":2,"m":"If only we had a Muse of fire, able to climb to the brightest heaven of imagination: a whole kingdom for a stage, princes as actors, and kings to watch the swelling scene! Then warlike Harry, true to himself, would take on the bearing of Mars, god of war, and at his heels famine, sword and fire would crouch like leashed hounds, waiting to be let loose. But forgive us, gentle audience, for the dull, uninspired minds that have dared to bring so great a subject onto this unworthy platform. Can this cockpit of a theatre hold the vast fields of France? Can we cram into this wooden O even the helmets that frightened the very air at Agincourt? Oh, forgive us! Since a little zero added to a figure can stand for a million in a small space, let us, mere zeros beside this great sum, work on your imaginations. Imagine that within the circle of these walls two mighty kingdoms are now shut up, whose high, facing fronts are separated only by the dangerous narrow sea. Make up for our shortcomings with your thoughts. Divide one man into a thousand and create an imaginary army. When we talk of horses, picture them pressing their proud hooves into the soft earth. For it is your thoughts that must dress our kings now, carry them from place to place, leap over time, and squeeze the events of many years into an hour-glass. To help with that, let me be the Chorus to this history, and, like a prologue, humbly ask you to listen patiently and judge our play kindly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":3},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Bishop of Ely.","id":4},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["My lord, I'll tell you: that self bill is urg'd,","Which in the eleventh year of the last king's reign","Was like, and had indeed against us pass'd,","But that the scambling and unquiet time","Did push it out of farther question."],"id":5,"m":"My lord, I'll tell you: that very bill is being pushed again, the one that in the eleventh year of the last king's reign was likely to pass, and would have passed against us, if the disorder and unrest of the times had not pushed it out of further debate."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["But how, my lord, shall we resist it now?"],"id":6,"m":"But how, my lord, are we going to resist it now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["It must be thought on. If it pass against us,","We lose the better half of our possession;","For all the temporal lands, which men devout","By testament have given to the Church,","Would they strip from us; being valu'd thus:","As much as would maintain, to the King's honour,","Full fifteen earls and fifteen hundred knights,","Six thousand and two hundred good esquires;","And, to relief of lazars and weak age,","Of indigent faint souls, past corporal toil,","A hundred almshouses right well suppli'd;","And to the coffers of the King beside,","A thousand pounds by the year. Thus runs the bill."],"id":7,"m":"It needs thinking about. If it passes against us, we lose the better half of our property, because they would strip from us all the lands that devout men have left to the Church in their wills, valued like this: enough to keep, to the King's credit, fifteen earls and fifteen hundred knights, six thousand two hundred squires, and, to care for lepers and the old and needy who are past physical work, a hundred well-stocked almshouses; and on top of that a thousand pounds a year for the King's treasury. That's what the bill says."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["This would drink deep."],"id":8,"m":"That would take a big drink out of our wealth."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["'Twould drink the cup and all."],"id":9,"m":"It would drink the cup and everything in it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["But what prevention?"],"id":10,"m":"But how can we prevent it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["The King is full of grace and fair regard."],"id":11,"m":"The King is gracious and well-intentioned."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["And a true lover of the holy Church."],"id":12,"m":"And a true lover of the holy Church."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["The courses of his youth promis'd it not.","The breath no sooner left his father's body,","But that his wildness, mortifi'd in him,","Seem'd to die too; yea, at that very moment","Consideration like an angel came","And whipp'd the offending Adam out of him,","Leaving his body as a paradise","To envelope and contain celestial spirits.","Never was such a sudden scholar made;","Never came reformation in a flood","With such a heady currance, scouring faults;","Nor never Hydra-headed wilfulness","So soon did lose his seat, and all at once,","As in this king."],"id":13,"m":"His behaviour as a youth did not promise it. But no sooner had the breath left his father's body than his wildness seemed to die too, killed off in him. Yes, at that very moment, reflection came like an angel and whipped the sinful Adam out of him, leaving his body like a paradise fit to hold heavenly spirits. There was never such a sudden scholar made. Reform never came in such a headlong flood, scouring away faults, and never did many-headed stubbornness, like the Hydra, lose its throne so quickly and all at once as in this king."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["We are blessed in the change."],"id":14,"m":"We are blessed by the change."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["Hear him but reason in divinity,","And, all-admiring, with an inward wish","You would desire the King were made a prelate;","Hear him debate of commonwealth affairs,","You would say it hath been all in all his study;","List his discourse of war, and you shall hear","A fearful battle rend'red you in music;","Turn him to any cause of policy,","The Gordian knot of it he will unloose,","Familiar as his garter; that, when he speaks,","The air, a charter'd libertine, is still,","And the mute wonder lurketh in men's ears,","To steal his sweet and honey'd sentences;","So that the art and practic' part of life","Must be the mistress to this theoric:","Which is a wonder how his Grace should glean it,","Since his addiction was to courses vain,","His companies unletter'd, rude, and shallow,","His hours fill'd up with riots, banquets, sports,","And never noted in him any study,","Any retirement, any sequestration","From open haunts and popularity."],"id":15,"m":"Just hear him reason about theology, and, full of admiration, you would secretly wish the King were a bishop. Hear him debate the affairs of state, and you would say he had studied nothing else. Listen to him talk about war, and you will hear a terrible battle turned into music. Give him any political problem, and he will untie its Gordian knot as easily as his garter. When he speaks, the air, free as a licensed libertine, falls still, and silent wonder waits in men's ears to catch his sweet, honeyed sentences. It must be that experience and practice of life taught him this theory. Which is a wonder, since how could his Grace have picked it up, when he was given to idle pursuits, his companions ignorant, rough and shallow, his hours filled with riots, feasts and games, and no one ever saw him study, or withdraw, or keep apart from public places and common people?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["The strawberry grows underneath the nettle,","And wholesome berries thrive and ripen best","Neighbour'd by fruit of baser quality;","And so the Prince obscur'd his contemplation","Under the veil of wildness; which, no doubt,","Grew like the summer grass, fastest by night,","Unseen, yet crescive in his faculty."],"id":16,"m":"The strawberry grows under the nettle, and good berries thrive and ripen best next to fruit of a lower kind. So the Prince hid his serious thoughts under a veil of wildness, and no doubt they grew like summer grass, fastest at night, unseen but steadily growing."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["It must be so; for miracles are ceas'd,","And therefore we must needs admit the means","How things are perfected."],"id":17,"m":"It must be so, because the age of miracles is over, so we have to accept that there are natural means by which things are made perfect."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["But, my good lord,","How now for mitigation of this bill","Urg'd by the commons? Doth his Majesty","Incline to it, or no?"],"id":18,"m":"But, my good lord, what about softening this bill the Commons are pushing? Does his Majesty lean towards it or not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["He seems indifferent,","Or rather swaying more upon our part","Than cherishing the exhibiters against us;","For I have made an offer to his Majesty,","Upon our spiritual convocation","And in regard of causes now in hand,","Which I have open'd to his Grace at large,","As touching France, to give a greater sum","Than ever at one time the clergy yet","Did to his predecessors part withal."],"id":19,"m":"He seems neutral, or rather leans more towards our side than towards those who brought it in against us. For I have made his Majesty an offer, on behalf of our church assembly and in view of the business now in hand, which I explained to him in full, concerning France: to give him a larger sum than the clergy have ever given at one time to any of his predecessors."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["How did this offer seem receiv'd, my lord?"],"id":20,"m":"How did he seem to receive the offer, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["With good acceptance of his Majesty;","Save that there was not time enough to hear,","As I perceiv'd his Grace would fain have done,","The severals and unhidden passages","Of his true titles to some certain dukedoms,","And generally to the crown and seat of France","Deriv'd from Edward, his great-grandfather."],"id":21,"m":"His Majesty accepted it well, except that there wasn't time enough to hear, as I could see he wanted to, the details and clear line of descent of his true claim to certain dukedoms, and in general to the crown and throne of France, inherited from Edward the Third, his great-grandfather."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["What was the impediment that broke this off?"],"id":22,"m":"What interrupted it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["The French ambassador upon that instant","Crav'd audience; and the hour, I think, is come","To give him hearing. Is it four o'clock?"],"id":23,"m":"The French ambassador asked for an audience at that moment, and I think the time has come to hear him. Is it four o'clock?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["It is."],"id":24,"m":"It is."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["Then go we in, to know his embassy;","Which I could with a ready guess declare,","Before the Frenchman speak a word of it."],"id":25,"m":"Then let's go in and hear his message, though I could guess it before the Frenchman says a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["I'll wait upon you, and I long to hear it."],"id":26,"m":"I'll come with you, and I'm eager to hear it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":27},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"London. The presence chamber in the palace.","id":28},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King Henry, Gloucester, Bedford, Exeter, Warwick, Westmoreland and Attendants.","id":29},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Where is my gracious Lord of Canterbury?"],"id":30,"m":"Where is my gracious Lord of Canterbury?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Not here in presence."],"id":31,"m":"He isn't here."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Send for him, good uncle."],"id":32,"m":"Send for him, good uncle."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Shall we call in the ambassador, my liege?"],"id":33,"m":"Shall we call in the ambassador, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Not yet, my cousin. We would be resolv'd,","Before we hear him, of some things of weight","That task our thoughts, concerning us and France."],"id":34,"m":"Not yet, cousin. Before we hear him, we want to settle some weighty questions that occupy our thoughts about ourselves and France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Bishop of Ely.","id":35},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["God and his angels guard your sacred throne","And make you long become it!"],"id":36,"m":"May God and his angels guard your sacred throne, and may you grace it for a long time!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Sure, we thank you.","My learned lord, we pray you to proceed","And justly and religiously unfold","Why the law Salique that they have in France","Or should, or should not, bar us in our claim;","And God forbid, my dear and faithful lord,","That you should fashion, wrest, or bow your reading,","Or nicely charge your understanding soul","With opening titles miscreate, whose right","Suits not in native colours with the truth;","For God doth know how many now in health","Shall drop their blood in approbation","Of what your reverence shall incite us to.","Therefore take heed how you impawn our person,","How you awake our sleeping sword of war.","We charge you, in the name of God, take heed;","For never two such kingdoms did contend","Without much fall of blood, whose guiltless drops","Are every one a woe, a sore complaint","'Gainst him whose wrongs gives edge unto the swords","That makes such waste in brief mortality.","Under this conjuration speak, my lord;","For we will hear, note, and believe in heart","That what you speak is in your conscience wash'd","As pure as sin with baptism."],"id":37,"m":"Thank you, truly. My learned lord, please go on and explain fairly and devoutly whether the Salic law they have in France ought or ought not to bar us in our claim. And God forbid, my dear and faithful lord, that you should twist, distort or bend your reading, or burden your conscience with subtle arguments for a false title that does not, in its true colours, match the truth. For God knows how many men now healthy will shed their blood to prove whatever your reverence urges us to do. So take care how you commit our person, how you wake our sleeping sword of war. We order you, in God's name, take care. For never did two such kingdoms fight without great bloodshed, and every innocent drop is a grief, a bitter accusation against the man whose wrongs sharpen the swords that waste so many short lives. Speak under this solemn charge, my lord, and we will listen, take note, and believe in our heart that what you say is washed as clean in your conscience as sin is in baptism."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["Then hear me, gracious sovereign, and you peers,","That owe yourselves, your lives, and services","To this imperial throne. There is no bar","To make against your Highness' claim to France","But this, which they produce from Pharamond:","\"In terram Salicam mulieres ne succedant,\"","\"No woman shall succeed in Salique land;\"","Which Salique land the French unjustly gloze","To be the realm of France, and Pharamond","The founder of this law and female bar.","Yet their own authors faithfully affirm","That the land Salique is in Germany,","Between the floods of Sala and of Elbe;","Where Charles the Great, having subdu'd the Saxons,","There left behind and settled certain French;","Who, holding in disdain the German women","For some dishonest manners of their life,","Establish'd then this law, to wit, no female","Should be inheritrix in Salique land;","Which Salique, as I said, 'twixt Elbe and Sala,","Is at this day in Germany call'd Meisen.","Then doth it well appear the Salique law","Was not devised for the realm of France;","Nor did the French possess the Salique land","Until four hundred one and twenty years","After defunction of King Pharamond,","Idly suppos'd the founder of this law,","Who died within the year of our redemption","Four hundred twenty-six; and Charles the Great","Subdu'd the Saxons, and did seat the French","Beyond the river Sala, in the year","Eight hundred five. Besides, their writers say,","King Pepin, which deposed Childeric,","Did, as heir general, being descended","Of Blithild, which was daughter to King Clothair,","Make claim and title to the crown of France.","Hugh Capet also, who usurp'd the crown","Of Charles the Duke of Lorraine, sole heir male","Of the true line and stock of Charles the Great,","To find his title with some shows of truth,","Though, in pure truth, it was corrupt and naught,","Convey'd himself as the heir to the Lady Lingare,","Daughter to Charlemain, who was the son","To Lewis the Emperor, and Lewis the son","Of Charles the Great. Also, King Lewis the Tenth,","Who was sole heir to the usurper Capet,","Could not keep quiet in his conscience,","Wearing the crown of France, till satisfied","That fair Queen Isabel, his grandmother,","Was lineal of the Lady Ermengare,","Daughter to Charles, the foresaid Duke of Lorraine;","By the which marriage the line of Charles the Great","Was re-united to the crown of France.","So that, as clear as is the summer's sun,","King Pepin's title and Hugh Capet's claim,","King Lewis his satisfaction, all appear","To hold in right and title of the female.","So do the kings of France unto this day,","Howbeit they would hold up this Salique law","To bar your Highness claiming from the female,","And rather choose to hide them in a net","Than amply to imbar their crooked titles","Usurp'd from you and your progenitors."],"id":38,"m":"Then hear me, gracious king, and you lords who owe your selves, lives and service to this royal throne. There is no obstacle to your Highness's claim to France except this, which they quote from King Pharamond: \"In terram Salicam mulieres ne succedant\", \"No woman shall inherit in Salic land\". The French wrongly interpret this Salic land as the kingdom of France, and Pharamond as the founder of this law that bars women. Yet their own historians honestly state that the Salic land is in Germany, between the rivers Saale and Elbe, where Charlemagne, after conquering the Saxons, left some Frenchmen settled. They despised the German women for their loose way of life, and so made this law that no woman should inherit in Salic land. That Salic land, as I said, between the Elbe and the Saale, is now called Meissen in Germany. So it is clear the Salic law was not made for the kingdom of France. And the French did not hold the Salic land until four hundred and twenty-one years after the death of King Pharamond, wrongly supposed the founder of this law, who died in the year of our Lord 426; and Charlemagne conquered the Saxons and settled the French beyond the river Saale in the year 805. Besides, their writers say that King Pepin, who deposed Childeric, claimed the crown of France as general heir, because he was descended from Blithild, daughter of King Clothair. Hugh Capet, too, who seized the crown from Charles, Duke of Lorraine, the only male heir of Charlemagne's true line, tried to give his title some show of truth, though in fact it was corrupt and worthless, by presenting himself as heir to Lady Lingare, daughter of Charlemain, who was son of the Emperor Louis, who was son of Charlemagne. Also King Louis the Tenth, who was sole heir of the usurper Capet, could not rest easy in his conscience wearing the crown of France until he was satisfied that his grandmother, fair Queen Isabel, descended from Lady Ermengare, daughter of that same Charles, Duke of Lorraine. By that marriage Charlemagne's line was joined to the crown of France again. So, as clear as the summer sun, King Pepin's title, Hugh Capet's claim and King Louis's peace of mind all rest on descent through the female line. The kings of France do the same to this day, though they would hold up this Salic law to bar your Highness from claiming through the female, preferring to hide in a tangle of excuses rather than openly expose their crooked titles, stolen from you and your ancestors."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["May I with right and conscience make this claim?"],"id":39,"m":"May I make this claim rightfully and with a clear conscience?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["The sin upon my head, dread sovereign!","For in the book of Numbers is it writ,","When the man dies, let the inheritance","Descend unto the daughter. Gracious lord,","Stand for your own! Unwind your bloody flag!","Look back into your mighty ancestors!","Go, my dread lord, to your great-grandsire's tomb,","From whom you claim; invoke his warlike spirit,","And your great-uncle's, Edward the Black Prince,","Who on the French ground play'd a tragedy,","Making defeat on the full power of France,","Whiles his most mighty father on a hill","Stood smiling to behold his lion's whelp","Forage in blood of French nobility.","O noble English, that could entertain","With half their forces the full pride of France","And let another half stand laughing by,","All out of work and cold for action!"],"id":40,"m":"Let the sin be on my head, dread king! For in the Book of Numbers it is written: when a man dies, let his inheritance pass to his daughter. Gracious lord, stand up for what is yours! Unfurl your bloody battle flag! Look back to your mighty ancestors! Go, my dread lord, to the tomb of your great-grandfather, from whom you claim; call up his warlike spirit, and that of your great-uncle Edward the Black Prince, who staged a tragedy on French soil, defeating the whole army of France, while his mighty father stood smiling on a hill to watch his lion's cub feed on the blood of French nobles. Oh, those noble English, who could take on the full pride of France with half their forces, while the other half stood by laughing, idle and cold for want of action!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELY","lines":["Awake remembrance of these valiant dead,","And with your puissant arm renew their feats.","You are their heir; you sit upon their throne;","The blood and courage that renowned them","Runs in your veins; and my thrice-puissant liege","Is in the very May-morn of his youth,","Ripe for exploits and mighty enterprises."],"id":41,"m":"Bring back the memory of these brave dead men, and with your powerful arm repeat their deeds. You are their heir; you sit on their throne; the blood and courage that made them famous runs in your veins; and my mighty king is in the May morning of his youth, ripe for great exploits and enterprises."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Your brother kings and monarchs of the earth","Do all expect that you should rouse yourself,","As did the former lions of your blood."],"id":42,"m":"Your fellow kings and rulers of the earth all expect you to rouse yourself as the earlier lions of your family did."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["They know your Grace hath cause and means and might;","So hath your Highness. Never King of England","Had nobles richer, and more loyal subjects,","Whose hearts have left their bodies here in England","And lie pavilion'd in the fields of France."],"id":43,"m":"They know your Grace has the cause, the means and the strength, and so you have. No King of England ever had richer nobles or more loyal subjects, whose hearts have already left their bodies here in England and are camped in tents in the fields of France."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["O, let their bodies follow, my dear liege,","With blood and sword and fire to win your right;","In aid whereof we of the spiritualty","Will raise your Highness such a mighty sum","As never did the clergy at one time","Bring in to any of your ancestors."],"id":44,"m":"Oh, let their bodies follow, my dear lord, with blood, sword and fire to win your right. To help with it, we of the clergy will raise your Highness a greater sum than the clergy ever brought to any of your ancestors at one time."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We must not only arm to invade the French,","But lay down our proportions to defend","Against the Scot, who will make road upon us","With all advantages."],"id":45,"m":"We must not only arm to invade the French, but also set aside forces to defend ourselves against the Scots, who will raid us whenever they have the chance."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["They of those marches, gracious sovereign,","Shall be a wall sufficient to defend","Our inland from the pilfering borderers."],"id":46,"m":"The people of the border country, gracious king, will be wall enough to defend our heartland against thieving raiders."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We do not mean the coursing snatchers only,","But fear the main intendment of the Scot,","Who hath been still a giddy neighbour to us;","For you shall read that my great-grandfather","Never went with his forces into France","But that the Scot on his unfurnish'd kingdom","Came pouring, like the tide into a breach,","With ample and brim fullness of his force,","Galling the gleaned land with hot assays,","Girdling with grievous siege castles and towns;","That England, being empty of defence,","Hath shook and trembled at the ill neighbourhood."],"id":47,"m":"We don't mean only the hit-and-run raiders; we fear a full invasion by the Scots, who have always been an unreliable neighbour to us. For you will read that my great-grandfather never took his forces into France without the Scots pouring into his undefended kingdom like the tide through a gap in a sea wall, in full force, harrying the stripped land with fierce attacks and surrounding castles and towns with cruel sieges, so that England, empty of defenders, shook and trembled at such a bad neighbour."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["She hath been then more fear'd than harm'd, my liege;","For hear her but exampl'd by herself:","When all her chivalry hath been in France,","And she a mourning widow of her nobles,","She hath herself not only well defended","But taken and impounded as a stray","The King of Scots; whom she did send to France","To fill King Edward's fame with prisoner kings,","And make her chronicle as rich with praise","As is the ooze and bottom of the sea","With sunken wreck and sumless treasuries."],"id":48,"m":"Then she was more frightened than hurt, my lord. Just take her own past as an example: when all her knights were in France and she was a widow mourning her absent nobles, she not only defended herself well but captured the King of Scots like a stray animal and sent him to France, to add captive kings to King Edward's fame, and to make her history as rich with praise as the ooze at the bottom of the sea is with sunken wrecks and countless treasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["But there's a saying very old and true,","\"If that you will France win,","Then with Scotland first begin.\"","For once the eagle England being in prey,","To her unguarded nest the weasel Scot","Comes sneaking and so sucks her princely eggs,","Playing the mouse in absence of the cat,","To tear and havoc more than she can eat."],"id":49,"m":"But there's an old and true saying: \"If you want to win France, start with Scotland.\" For once England, the eagle, is out hunting, the weasel Scot comes creeping to her unguarded nest and sucks her royal eggs, playing the mouse while the cat is away, and ruining more than she can eat."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["It follows then the cat must stay at home;","Yet that is but a crush'd necessity,","Since we have locks to safeguard necessaries,","And pretty traps to catch the petty thieves.","While that the armed hand doth fight abroad,","The advised head defends itself at home;","For government, though high and low and lower,","Put into parts, doth keep in one consent,","Congreeing in a full and natural close,","Like music."],"id":50,"m":"So it follows that the cat must stay at home. But that is a forced conclusion, since we have locks to protect our goods and clever traps to catch petty thieves. While the armed hand fights abroad, the wise head defends itself at home. For government, though made of high, low and lower ranks, each with its part, keeps in harmony, coming together in one full and natural ending, like music."},{"t":"sp","who":"CANTERBURY","lines":["Therefore doth heaven divide","The state of man in divers functions,","Setting endeavour in continual motion,","To which is fixed, as an aim or butt,","Obedience; for so work the honey-bees,","Creatures that by a rule in nature teach","The act of order to a peopled kingdom.","They have a king and officers of sorts,","Where some, like magistrates, correct at home,","Others like merchants, venture trade abroad,","Others, like soldiers, armed in their stings,","Make boot upon the summer's velvet buds,","Which pillage they with merry march bring home","To the tent-royal of their emperor;","Who, busied in his majesty, surveys","The singing masons building roofs of gold,","The civil citizens kneading up the honey,","The poor mechanic porters crowding in","Their heavy burdens at his narrow gate,","The sad-eyed justice, with his surly hum,","Delivering o'er to executors pale","The lazy yawning drone. I this infer,","That many things, having full reference","To one consent, may work contrariously.","As many arrows, loosed several ways,","Come to one mark; as many ways meet in one town;","As many fresh streams meet in one salt sea;","As many lines close in the dial's centre;","So many a thousand actions, once afoot,","End in one purpose, and be all well borne","Without defeat. Therefore to France, my liege!","Divide your happy England into four,","Whereof take you one quarter into France,","And you withal shall make all Gallia shake.","If we, with thrice such powers left at home,","Cannot defend our own doors from the dog,","Let us be worried and our nation lose","The name of hardiness and policy."],"id":51,"m":"That is why heaven divides human society into different roles, keeping effort always in motion, with obedience fixed as its aim or target. That is how honey-bees work, creatures that by natural law teach a populated kingdom how to be orderly. They have a king and officers of different ranks: some, like magistrates, keep order at home; others, like merchants, trade abroad; others, like soldiers armed with their stings, raid the soft buds of summer and bring the plunder home in merry procession to the royal tent of their emperor. He, busy with his royal duties, watches the singing masons building roofs of gold, the orderly citizens kneading the honey, the poor labourers crowding their heavy loads through his narrow gate, and the grave-eyed judge, with his stern hum, handing the lazy, yawning drone over to the pale executioners. From this I conclude: that many things, all directed to one agreed end, may work in different ways: as many arrows shot from different places hit one target, as many roads meet in one town, as many fresh streams flow into one salt sea, as many lines meet at the centre of a sundial, so a thousand actions, once begun, can end in one purpose and all succeed without failure. So, to France, my lord! Divide your fortunate England into four, take one quarter into France, and with it you will make all France shake. If we, with three times that many left at home, can't defend our own doors from the dog, let us be savaged, and let our nation lose its name for courage and good government."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Call in the messengers sent from the Dauphin."],"id":52,"m":"Call in the messengers sent from the Dauphin."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt some Attendants.","id":53},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Now are we well resolv'd; and, by God's help,","And yours, the noble sinews of our power,","France being ours, we'll bend it to our awe,","Or break it all to pieces. Or there we'll sit,","Ruling in large and ample empery","O'er France and all her almost kingly dukedoms,","Or lay these bones in an unworthy urn,","Tombless, with no remembrance over them.","Either our history shall with full mouth","Speak freely of our acts, or else our grave,","Like Turkish mute, shall have a tongueless mouth,","Not worshipp'd with a waxen epitaph."],"id":54,"m":"Now we have made up our mind, and, with God's help and yours, the noble muscles of our power, since France is ours, we'll bend it to our authority or break it all to pieces. Either we'll sit there ruling in wide, full command over France and its almost royal dukedoms, or lay these bones in a humble urn, with no tomb and no memorial over them. Either our history will speak loudly and freely of our deeds, or else our grave, like a Turkish mute with his tongue cut out, will have no voice and not even the honour of a wax epitaph."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ambassadors of France.","id":55},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Now are we well prepar'd to know the pleasure","Of our fair cousin Dauphin; for we hear","Your greeting is from him, not from the King."],"id":56,"m":"Now we are ready to hear what our fair cousin the Dauphin wants, since we hear your greeting is from him and not from the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"AMBASSADOR","lines":["May't please your Majesty to give us leave","Freely to render what we have in charge,","Or shall we sparingly show you far off","The Dauphin's meaning and our embassy?"],"id":57,"m":"Will your Majesty give us permission to speak our message freely, or should we just hint at the Dauphin's meaning and our mission from a distance?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We are no tyrant, but a Christian king,","Unto whose grace our passion is as subject","As is our wretches fett'red in our prisons;","Therefore with frank and with uncurbed plainness","Tell us the Dauphin's mind."],"id":58,"m":"We are no tyrant but a Christian king, whose passion is as much ruled by our self-control as the wretches chained in our prisons. So tell us the Dauphin's mind openly and plainly."},{"t":"sp","who":"AMBASSADOR","lines":["Thus, then, in few.","Your Highness, lately sending into France,","Did claim some certain dukedoms, in the right","Of your great predecessor, King Edward the Third.","In answer of which claim, the prince our master","Says that you savour too much of your youth,","And bids you be advis'd there's nought in France","That can be with a nimble galliard won.","You cannot revel into dukedoms there.","He therefore sends you, meeter for your spirit,","This tun of treasure; and, in lieu of this,","Desires you let the dukedoms that you claim","Hear no more of you. This the Dauphin speaks."],"id":59,"m":"Then, in brief: your Highness recently sent to France claiming certain dukedoms by right of your great ancestor King Edward the Third. In answer, the prince our master says that you smack too much of your youth, and advises you that nothing in France can be won by dancing a lively galliard; you cannot party your way into dukedoms there. So he sends you this barrel of treasure, more suitable to your character, and in return asks that the dukedoms you claim hear no more from you. That is the Dauphin's message."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What treasure, uncle?"],"id":60,"m":"What treasure, uncle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Tennis-balls, my liege."],"id":61,"m":"Tennis balls, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We are glad the Dauphin is so pleasant with us.","His present and your pains we thank you for.","When we have match'd our rackets to these balls,","We will, in France, by God's grace, play a set","Shall strike his father's crown into the hazard.","Tell him he hath made a match with such a wrangler","That all the courts of France will be disturb'd","With chaces. And we understand him well,","How he comes o'er us with our wilder days,","Not measuring what use we made of them.","We never valu'd this poor seat of England;","And therefore, living hence, did give ourself","To barbarous licence; as 'tis ever common","That men are merriest when they are from home.","But tell the Dauphin I will keep my state,","Be like a king, and show my sail of greatness","When I do rouse me in my throne of France.","For that I have laid by my majesty","And plodded like a man for working days,","But I will rise there with so full a glory","That I will dazzle all the eyes of France,","Yea, strike the Dauphin blind to look on us.","And tell the pleasant prince this mock of his","Hath turn'd his balls to gun-stones, and his soul","Shall stand sore charged for the wasteful vengeance","That shall fly with them; for many a thousand widows","Shall this his mock mock out of their dear husbands,","Mock mothers from their sons, mock castles down;","And some are yet ungotten and unborn","That shall have cause to curse the Dauphin's scorn.","But this lies all within the will of God,","To whom I do appeal; and in whose name","Tell you the Dauphin I am coming on","To venge me as I may, and to put forth","My rightful hand in a well-hallow'd cause.","So get you hence in peace; and tell the Dauphin","His jest will savour but of shallow wit,","When thousands weep more than did laugh at it.--","Convey them with safe conduct.--Fare you well."],"id":62,"m":"We are glad the Dauphin is so playful with us. We thank you for his present and your trouble. When we have matched our rackets to these balls, we will, by God's grace, play a set in France that will knock his father's crown into the hazard. Tell him he has made a match with such a tough opponent that all the courts of France will be disturbed with chases. And we understand him well, how he taunts us with our wilder days, without measuring what use we made of them. We never valued this humble throne of England, and so, living away from it, we gave ourselves up to wild freedom, as men are always merriest when they are away from home. But tell the Dauphin I will keep my royal dignity, behave like a king, and show the full sail of my greatness when I rise up on my throne of France. For that I have set aside my majesty and plodded like a common working man; but I will rise there in such full glory that I will dazzle every eye in France, yes, strike the Dauphin blind to look at us. And tell the playful prince that his joke has turned his balls into cannonballs, and his soul will carry the heavy guilt for the destructive vengeance that flies with them. This mockery of his will mock many thousand widows out of their dear husbands, mock mothers out of their sons, mock castles down; and some not yet conceived or born will have reason to curse the Dauphin's scorn. But all this lies in the will of God, to whom I appeal, and in whose name tell the Dauphin I am coming to avenge myself as I can, and to stretch out my rightful hand in a holy cause. So go in peace, and tell the Dauphin his joke will seem shallow when thousands weep for it, more than ever laughed at it. (To attendants) Escort them safely. (To the Ambassadors) Farewell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Ambassadors.","id":63},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["This was a merry message."],"id":64,"m":"That was a merry message."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We hope to make the sender blush at it.","Therefore, my lords, omit no happy hour","That may give furtherance to our expedition;","For we have now no thought in us but France,","Save those to God, that run before our business.","Therefore, let our proportions for these wars","Be soon collected, and all things thought upon","That may with reasonable swiftness add","More feathers to our wings; for, God before,","We'll chide this Dauphin at his father's door.","Therefore let every man now task his thought,","That this fair action may on foot be brought."],"id":65,"m":"We hope to make the sender blush for it. So, my lords, waste no good moment that could speed our expedition, for we now have no thought except France, apart from our thoughts of God, which come before our business. So let our supplies for these wars be gathered quickly, and everything considered that might sensibly add feathers to our wings; for, with God leading, we'll scold this Dauphin at his father's door. So let every man now put his mind to it, so that this good enterprise may be set in motion."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":66},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"London. A street.","id":67},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter Chorus.","id":68},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","lines":["Now all the youth of England are on fire,","And silken dalliance in the wardrobe lies.","Now thrive the armourers, and honour's thought","Reigns solely in the breast of every man.","They sell the pasture now to buy the horse,","Following the mirror of all Christian kings,","With winged heels, as English Mercuries.","For now sits Expectation in the air,","And hides a sword from hilts unto the point","With crowns imperial, crowns, and coronets,","Promis'd to Harry and his followers.","The French, advis'd by good intelligence","Of this most dreadful preparation,","Shake in their fear, and with pale policy","Seek to divert the English purposes.","O England! model to thy inward greatness,","Like little body with a mighty heart,","What mightst thou do, that honour would thee do,","Were all thy children kind and natural!","But see thy fault! France hath in thee found out","A nest of hollow bosoms, which he fills","With treacherous crowns; and three corrupted men,","One, Richard Earl of Cambridge, and the second,","Henry Lord Scroop of Masham, and the third,","Sir Thomas Grey, knight of Northumberland,","Have, for the gilt of France,--O guilt indeed!--","Confirm'd conspiracy with fearful France;","And by their hands this grace of kings must die,","If hell and treason hold their promises,","Ere he take ship for France, and in Southampton.","Linger your patience on, and we'll digest","The abuse of distance, force a play.","The sum is paid; the traitors are agreed;","The King is set from London; and the scene","Is now transported, gentles, to Southampton.","There is the playhouse now, there must you sit;","And thence to France shall we convey you safe,","And bring you back, charming the narrow seas","To give you gentle pass; for, if we may,","We'll not offend one stomach with our play.","But, till the King come forth, and not till then,","Unto Southampton do we shift our scene."],"id":69,"m":"Now all the young men of England are on fire, and silk party clothes are left in the wardrobe. Now armourers do good business, and the thought of honour rules alone in every man's heart. They sell their pasture land to buy a horse, following the model of all Christian kings, with winged heels, like English versions of Mercury. For Expectation now sits in the air and hides a sword from hilt to point with crowns of kings, crowns and coronets, promised to Harry and his followers. The French, well informed by their spies about this terrible preparation, shake with fear and with pale cunning try to turn the English from their purpose. Oh England! You are a small model of your inner greatness, like a little body with a mighty heart. What might you not do that honour would have you do, if all your children were loving and loyal! But see your fault! France has found in you a nest of hollow hearts, which it fills with treacherous gold coins; and three corrupted men, first, Richard Earl of Cambridge, second, Henry Lord Scroop of Masham, and third, Sir Thomas Grey, knight of Northumberland, have, for the gilt of France (oh, guilt indeed!), sealed a conspiracy with frightened France; and by their hands this finest of kings must die, if hell and treason keep their promises, before he takes ship for France, in Southampton. Keep your patience a little longer, and we'll make you swallow the jumps in place and force them into a play. The bribe is paid, the traitors have agreed, the King has left London, and the scene now moves, gentlemen, to Southampton. That is the playhouse now, there you must sit, and from there we'll carry you safely to France and back, charming the narrow sea to give you a smooth crossing, for, if we can help it, we won't make a single stomach seasick with our play. But not until the King comes out, and not until then, will we shift our scene to Southampton."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":70},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Corporal Nym and Lieutenant Bardolph.","id":71},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Well met, Corporal Nym."],"id":72,"m":"Well met, Corporal Nym."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Good morrow, Lieutenant Bardolph."],"id":73,"m":"Good morning, Lieutenant Bardolph."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["What, are Ancient Pistol and you friends yet?"],"id":74,"m":"Well, are Ensign Pistol and you friends yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["For my part, I care not. I say little; but when time shall","serve, there shall be smiles; but that shall be as it may. I dare","not fight, but I will wink and hold out mine iron. It is a simple","one, but what though? It will toast cheese, and it will endure","cold as another man's sword will; and there's an end."],"id":75,"m":"As far as I'm concerned, I don't care. I say little, but when the time comes there shall be smiles; but that shall be as it may. I don't dare fight, but I'll shut my eyes and hold out my sword. It's a plain one, but so what? It will toast cheese, and it will stand the cold as well as any man's sword; and that's the end of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["I will bestow a breakfast to make you friends; and we'll","be all three sworn brothers to France. Let it be so, good","Corporal Nym."],"id":76,"m":"I'll pay for a breakfast to make you friends, and we'll all three be sworn brothers going to France. Let it be so, good Corporal Nym."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Faith, I will live so long as I may, that's the certain of it; and","when I cannot live any longer, I will do as I may. That is my rest,","that is the rendezvous of it."],"id":77,"m":"Honestly, I'll live as long as I can, that's certain; and when I can't live any longer, I'll do as I can. That's my last word, that's the end of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["It is certain, corporal, that he is married to Nell Quickly; and","certainly she did you wrong, for you were troth-plight to her."],"id":78,"m":"It's certain, corporal, that he's married Nell Quickly; and she certainly wronged you, because you were engaged to her."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I cannot tell. Things must be as they may. Men may sleep, and","they may have their throats about them at that time; and some say","knives have edges. It must be as it may. Though patience be a","tired mare, yet she will plod. There must be conclusions. Well, I","cannot tell."],"id":79,"m":"I can't say. Things must be as they may. Men may sleep, and they may have their throats with them at the time; and some say knives have edges. It must be as it may. Though patience is a tired old mare, she still plods on. There must be an end. Well, I can't say."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pistol and Hostess.","id":80},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Here comes Ancient Pistol and his wife. Good Corporal, be","patient here. How now, mine host Pistol!"],"id":81,"m":"Here comes Ensign Pistol and his wife. Good Corporal, keep your temper here. Hello there, landlord Pistol!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Base tike, call'st thou me host?","Now, by this hand, I swear I scorn the term;","Nor shall my Nell keep lodgers."],"id":82,"m":"Low mongrel, are you calling me landlord? By this hand, I swear I scorn the word, and my Nell won't keep lodgers either."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["No, by my troth, not long; for we cannot lodge and board a","dozen or fourteen gentlewomen that live honestly by the prick of","their needles, but it will be thought we keep a bawdy house","straight. [Nym and Pistol draw] O well a day, Lady, if he be not","drawn now! We shall see wilful adultery and murder committed."],"id":83,"m":"No, honestly, not for long; because we can't give room and board to twelve or fourteen respectable ladies who live honestly by sewing without people at once thinking we keep a brothel. (Nym and Pistol draw their swords) Oh dear, by Our Lady, he's drawn his sword! We'll see deliberate adultery and murder done here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Good Lieutenant! good corporal! offer nothing here."],"id":84,"m":"Good Lieutenant! Good Corporal! Don't start anything here."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Pish!"],"id":85,"m":"Pish!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Pish for thee, Iceland dog! thou prick-ear'd cur of Iceland!"],"id":86,"m":"Pish to you, you Iceland lapdog! You prick-eared cur from Iceland!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Good Corporal Nym, show thy valour, and put up your sword."],"id":87,"m":"Good Corporal Nym, show how brave you are and put your sword away."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Will you shog off? I would have you solus."],"id":88,"m":"Will you clear off? I want you alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["\"Solus,\" egregious dog! O viper vile!","The \"solus\" in thy most mervailous face;","The \"solus\" in thy teeth, and in thy throat,","And in thy hateful lungs, yea, in thy maw, perdy,","And, which is worse, within thy nasty mouth!","I do retort the \"solus\" in thy bowels;","For I can take, and Pistol's cock is up,","And flashing fire will follow."],"id":89,"m":"\"Alone\", you outrageous dog! Oh vile viper! I throw \"alone\" in your most astonishing face, in your teeth and in your throat, in your hateful lungs, yes, in your belly, by God, and, what's worse, in your nasty mouth! I hurl \"alone\" back into your guts; for I can strike, and Pistol's cock is raised, and flashing fire will follow."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I am not Barbason; you cannot conjure me. I have an humour to","knock you indifferently well. If you grow foul with me, Pistol, I","will scour you with my rapier, as I may, in fair terms. If you","would walk off, I would prick your guts a little, in good terms,","as I may; and that's the humour of it."],"id":90,"m":"I'm not the devil Barbason; you can't drive me off with spells. I have a mind to beat you fairly well. If you get nasty with me, Pistol, I'll clean you out with my rapier, as I may, in fair terms. If you'll walk off with me, I'll prick your guts a little, in good terms, as I may; and that's the humour of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["O braggart vile and damned furious wight!","The grave doth gape, and doting death is near,","Therefore exhale."],"id":91,"m":"Oh vile braggart and damned furious creature! The grave is gaping, and doting death is near; so breathe your last."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Hear me, hear me what I say. He that strikes the first","stroke I'll run him up to the hilts, as I am a soldier."],"id":92,"m":"Listen to me, listen to what I say. Whoever strikes the first blow, I'll run him through up to the hilt, as I'm a soldier."},{"t":"sd","text":"Draws.","id":93},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["An oath of mickle might; and fury shall abate.","Give me thy fist, thy fore-foot to me give.","Thy spirits are most tall."],"id":94,"m":"An oath of great power; and fury shall calm down. Give me your fist, give me your front paw. Your spirit is very brave."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I will cut thy throat, one time or other, in fair terms:","that is the humour of it."],"id":95,"m":"I'll cut your throat some time or other, in fair terms; that's the humour of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["\"Couple a gorge!\"","That is the word. I thee defy again.","O hound of Crete, think'st thou my spouse to get?","No! to the spital go,","And from the powdering tub of infamy","Fetch forth the lazar kite of Cressid's kind,","Doll Tearsheet she by name, and her espouse.","I have, and I will hold, the quondam Quickly","For the only she; and--pauca, there's enough.","Go to."],"id":96,"m":"\"Coupe la gorge!\", \"cut the throat!\", that's the word. I defy you again. Oh hound of Crete, do you think you'll get my wife? No! Go to the hospital, and from the sweating tub of disgrace fetch out that diseased bird of Cressida's kind, Doll Tearsheet by name, and marry her. I have, and I will keep, the former Quickly as my one and only; and, in short, that's enough. Go on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Boy.","id":97},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Mine host Pistol, you must come to my master, and you,","hostess. He is very sick, and would to bed. Good Bardolph, put","thy face between his sheets, and do the office of a warming-pan.","Faith, he's very ill."],"id":98,"m":"Landlord Pistol, you must come to my master, and you too, hostess. He's very sick and wants to go to bed. Good Bardolph, put your red face between his sheets and do the job of a warming-pan. Honestly, he's very ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Away, you rogue!"],"id":99,"m":"Get away, you rascal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["By my troth, he'll yield the crow a pudding one of these days.","The King has kill'd his heart.","Good husband, come home presently."],"id":100,"m":"Honestly, he'll be giving the crows a meal one of these days. The King has broken his heart. Good husband, come home at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Hostess and Boy.","id":101},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Come, shall I make you two friends? We must to France","together; why the devil should we keep knives to cut one","another's throats?"],"id":102,"m":"Come on, shall I make you two friends? We have to go to France together; why the devil should we keep knives to cut each other's throats?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Let floods o'erswell, and fiends for food howl on!"],"id":103,"m":"Let floods overflow and devils howl for food!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["You'll pay me the eight shillings I won of you at betting?"],"id":104,"m":"Will you pay me the eight shillings I won from you in a bet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Base is the slave that pays."],"id":105,"m":"Only a low slave pays."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["That now I will have: that's the humour of it."],"id":106,"m":"I'll have that now; that's the humour of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["As manhood shall compound. Push home."],"id":107,"m":"As our manhood shall settle it. Thrust away."},{"t":"sd","text":"They draw.","id":108},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["By this sword, he that makes the first thrust, I'll kill","him; by this sword, I will."],"id":109,"m":"By this sword, whoever makes the first thrust, I'll kill him; by this sword, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Sword is an oath, and oaths must have their course."],"id":110,"m":"\"By this sword\" is an oath, and oaths must take their course."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Corporal Nym, and thou wilt be friends, be friends; an","thou wilt not, why, then, be enemies with me too. Prithee,","put up."],"id":111,"m":"Corporal Nym, if you'll be friends, be friends; if you won't, then be my enemy too. Please, put it away."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I shall have my eight shillings I won from you at betting?"],"id":112,"m":"Will I get the eight shillings I won from you in the bet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["A noble shalt thou have, and present pay;","And liquor likewise will I give to thee,","And friendship shall combine, and brotherhood.","I'll live by Nym, and Nym shall live by me.","Is not this just? For I shall sutler be","Unto the camp, and profits will accrue.","Give me thy hand."],"id":113,"m":"You shall have a noble, paid on the spot, and drink as well, and friendship and brotherhood will join us. I'll live by Nym, and Nym shall live by me. Isn't that fair? For I'm going to be a supplier of food and drink to the army, and there will be profits. Give me your hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I shall have my noble?"],"id":114,"m":"Will I get my noble?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["In cash most justly paid."],"id":115,"m":"In cash, paid very fairly."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Well, then, that's the humour of't."],"id":116,"m":"Well then, that's the humour of it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Hostess.","id":117},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["As ever you come of women, come in quickly to Sir John.","Ah, poor heart! he is so shak'd of a burning quotidian tertian,","that it is most lamentable to behold. Sweet men, come to him."],"id":118,"m":"As surely as you were born of women, come in quickly to Sir John. Oh, poor soul! He's so shaken by a burning fever that it's terribly sad to see. Sweet men, come to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["The King hath run bad humours on the knight; that's the even","of it."],"id":119,"m":"The King has given the knight a bad time; that's the long and short of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Nym, thou hast spoke the right.","His heart is fracted and corroborate."],"id":120,"m":"Nym, you've spoken the truth. His heart is fractured and corroborated."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["The King is a good king; but it must be as it may; he","passes some humours and careers."],"id":121,"m":"The King is a good king, but it must be as it may; he has his moods and his wild moments."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Let us condole the knight; for, lambkins, we will live."],"id":122,"m":"Let us go and grieve with the knight; for, my lambs, we will live."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":123},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Southampton. A council-chamber.","id":124},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Exeter, Bedford, and Westmoreland.","id":125},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["'Fore God, his Grace is bold, to trust these traitors."],"id":126,"m":"By God, his Grace is bold to trust these traitors."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["They shall be apprehended by and by."],"id":127,"m":"They will be arrested soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["How smooth and even they do bear themselves!","As if allegiance in their bosoms sat","Crowned with faith and constant loyalty."],"id":128,"m":"How smooth and calm they look! As if loyalty sat in their hearts, crowned with faith and constant allegiance."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["The King hath note of all that they intend,","By interception which they dream not of."],"id":129,"m":"The King knows everything they plan, from intercepted messages they never dream of."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Nay, but the man that was his bed-fellow,","Whom he hath dull'd and cloy'd with gracious favours,","That he should, for a foreign purse, so sell","His sovereign's life to death and treachery."],"id":130,"m":"Yes, but think of the man who was his bedfellow, whom he has filled to the brim with gracious favours, that he should sell his king's life to death and treachery for a foreign purse!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets sound. Enter King Henry, Scroop, Cambridge, and Grey.","id":131},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Now sits the wind fair, and we will aboard.","My Lord of Cambridge, and my kind Lord of Masham,","And you, my gentle knight, give me your thoughts.","Think you not that the powers we bear with us","Will cut their passage through the force of France,","Doing the execution and the act","For which we have in head assembled them?"],"id":132,"m":"Now the wind is fair, and we'll go aboard. My Lord of Cambridge, my kind Lord of Masham, and you, my gentle knight, tell me your thoughts. Don't you think the forces we take with us will cut their way through the power of France and carry out the work we have raised them for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["No doubt, my liege, if each man do his best."],"id":133,"m":"No doubt of it, my lord, if every man does his best."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I doubt not that, since we are well persuaded","We carry not a heart with us from hence","That grows not in a fair consent with ours,","Nor leave not one behind that doth not wish","Success and conquest to attend on us."],"id":134,"m":"I don't doubt that, since we are sure we take no heart with us from here that does not agree with ours, and leave none behind that does not wish us success and conquest."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMBRIDGE","lines":["Never was monarch better fear'd and lov'd","Than is your Majesty. There's not, I think, a subject","That sits in heart-grief and uneasiness","Under the sweet shade of your government."],"id":135,"m":"No monarch was ever more respected and loved than your Majesty. I don't think there is one subject who lives in grief and unease under the sweet shade of your rule."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREY","lines":["True; those that were your father's enemies","Have steep'd their galls in honey, and do serve you","With hearts create of duty and of zeal."],"id":136,"m":"True; even those who were your father's enemies have soaked their bitterness in honey and serve you with hearts made of duty and devotion."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We therefore have great cause of thankfulness,","And shall forget the office of our hand","Sooner than quittance of desert and merit","According to the weight and worthiness."],"id":137,"m":"So we have great reason to be thankful, and we would sooner forget how to use our hand than forget to reward service and merit according to its weight and worth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["So service shall with steeled sinews toil,","And labour shall refresh itself with hope,","To do your Grace incessant services."],"id":138,"m":"Then service will toil with muscles of steel, and labour will refresh itself with hope, to serve your Grace without rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We judge no less. Uncle of Exeter,","Enlarge the man committed yesterday,","That rail'd against our person. We consider","It was excess of wine that set him on,","And on his more advice we pardon him."],"id":139,"m":"We expect nothing less. Uncle Exeter, release the man arrested yesterday who shouted insults against us. We think it was too much wine that set him on, and now that he is sober we pardon him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["That's mercy, but too much security.","Let him be punish'd, sovereign, lest example","Breed, by his sufferance, more of such a kind."],"id":140,"m":"That's merciful, but too trusting. Let him be punished, my king, in case letting him off breeds more of the same kind."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["O, let us yet be merciful."],"id":141,"m":"Oh, let us still be merciful."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMBRIDGE","lines":["So may your Highness, and yet punish too."],"id":142,"m":"Your Highness can be, and still punish too."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREY","lines":["Sir,","You show great mercy if you give him life","After the taste of much correction."],"id":143,"m":"Sir, you show great mercy if you let him live after giving him a good taste of punishment."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Alas, your too much love and care of me","Are heavy orisons 'gainst this poor wretch!","If little faults, proceeding on distemper,","Shall not be wink'd at, how shall we stretch our eye","When capital crimes, chew'd, swallow'd, and digested,","Appear before us? We'll yet enlarge that man,","Though Cambridge, Scroop, and Grey, in their dear care","And tender preservation of our person,","Would have him punish'd. And now to our French causes.","Who are the late commissioners?"],"id":144,"m":"Alas, your great love and care for me are heavy prayers against this poor wretch! If little faults that come from drunkenness are not to be overlooked, how wide shall we open our eyes when crimes worthy of death, chewed, swallowed and digested, come before us? We'll release that man all the same, though Cambridge, Scroop and Grey, in their loving care and tender protection of our person, would have him punished. And now to our French business. Who are the newly appointed commissioners?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMBRIDGE","lines":["I one, my lord.","Your Highness bade me ask for it to-day."],"id":145,"m":"I am one, my lord. Your Highness told me to ask for my commission today."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["So did you me, my liege."],"id":146,"m":"So did you tell me, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREY","lines":["And I, my royal sovereign."],"id":147,"m":"And me, my royal king."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Then, Richard Earl of Cambridge, there is yours;","There yours, Lord Scroop of Masham; and, sir knight,","Grey of Northumberland, this same is yours.","Read them, and know I know your worthiness.","My Lord of Westmoreland, and uncle Exeter,","We will aboard to-night.--Why, how now, gentlemen!","What see you in those papers that you lose","So much complexion?--Look ye, how they change!","Their cheeks are paper.--Why, what read you there,","That have so cowarded and chas'd your blood","Out of appearance?"],"id":148,"m":"Then, Richard Earl of Cambridge, there is yours; there's yours, Lord Scroop of Masham; and, sir knight, Grey of Northumberland, this one is yours. Read them, and know that I know what you are worth. My Lord of Westmoreland and Uncle Exeter, we'll go aboard tonight. Why, what's the matter, gentlemen? What do you see in those papers that makes you lose so much colour? Look how they change! Their cheeks are as white as paper. Why, what are you reading there that has made your blood run so cowardly out of sight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMBRIDGE","lines":["I do confess my fault,","And do submit me to your Highness' mercy."],"id":149,"m":"I confess my crime and throw myself on your Highness's mercy."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREY AND SCROOP","lines":["To which we all appeal."],"id":150,"m":"Which we all appeal to."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["The mercy that was quick in us but late,","By your own counsel is suppress'd and kill'd.","You must not dare, for shame, to talk of mercy,","For your own reasons turn into your bosoms,","As dogs upon their masters, worrying you.","See you, my princes and my noble peers,","These English monsters! My Lord of Cambridge here,","You know how apt our love was to accord","To furnish him with all appertinents","Belonging to his honour; and this man","Hath, for a few light crowns, lightly conspir'd","And sworn unto the practices of France","To kill us here in Hampton; to the which","This knight, no less for bounty bound to us","Than Cambridge is, hath likewise sworn. But, O","What shall I say to thee, Lord Scroop? thou cruel,","Ingrateful, savage, and inhuman creature!","Thou that didst bear the key of all my counsels,","That knew'st the very bottom of my soul,","That almost mightst have coin'd me into gold,","Wouldst thou have practis'd on me for thy use,--","May it be possible that foreign hire","Could out of thee extract one spark of evil","That might annoy my finger? 'Tis so strange,","That, though the truth of it stands off as gross","As black and white, my eye will scarcely see it.","Treason and murder ever kept together,","As two yoke-devils sworn to either's purpose,","Working so grossly in a natural cause","That admiration did not whoop at them;","But thou, 'gainst all proportion, didst bring in","Wonder to wait on treason and on murder;","And whatsoever cunning fiend it was","That wrought upon thee so preposterously","Hath got the voice in hell for excellence;","And other devils that suggest by treasons","Do botch and bungle up damnation","With patches, colours, and with forms being fetch'd","From glist'ring semblances of piety.","But he that temper'd thee bade thee stand up,","Gave thee no instance why thou shouldst do treason,","Unless to dub thee with the name of traitor.","If that same demon that hath gull'd thee thus","Should with his lion gait walk the whole world,","He might return to vasty Tartar back,","And tell the legions, \"I can never win","A soul so easy as that Englishman's.\"","O, how hast thou with jealousy infected","The sweetness of affiance! Show men dutiful?","Why, so didst thou. Seem they grave and learned?","Why, so didst thou. Come they of noble family?","Why, so didst thou. Seem they religious?","Why, so didst thou. Or are they spare in diet,","Free from gross passion or of mirth or anger,","Constant in spirit, not swerving with the blood,","Garnish'd and deck'd in modest complement,","Not working with the eye without the ear,","And but in purged judgement trusting neither?","Such and so finely bolted didst thou seem.","And thus thy fall hath left a kind of blot","To mark the full-fraught man and best indued","With some suspicion. I will weep for thee;","For this revolt of thine, methinks, is like","Another fall of man. Their faults are open.","Arrest them to the answer of the law;","And God acquit them of their practices!"],"id":151,"m":"The mercy that was alive in us just now has been suppressed and killed by your own advice. For shame, you must not dare to talk of mercy, because your own arguments turn back on your hearts like dogs on their masters, tearing at you. Look, my princes and noble lords, at these English monsters! My Lord of Cambridge here, you know how ready our love was to give him everything that belonged to his rank; and this man, for a few light coins, has lightly plotted and sworn to the schemes of France to kill us here in Southampton. And this knight, no less bound to us by our generosity than Cambridge is, has sworn the same. But oh, what shall I say to you, Lord Scroop? You cruel, ungrateful, savage and inhuman creature! You who held the key to all my secrets, who knew the very bottom of my soul, who could almost have turned me into gold for your own use, could you have plotted against me for your own profit? Is it possible that foreign pay could squeeze out of you one spark of evil that might hurt my finger? It is so strange that, though the truth of it stands out as plain as black and white, my eye can hardly see it. Treason and murder have always gone together, like two devils in one yoke, each sworn to the other's purpose, working so obviously for their natural end that no one cried out in amazement at them. But you, against all reason, brought amazement in to wait on treason and murder; and whatever cunning devil worked on you so unnaturally has won the vote in hell as the best. Other devils who tempt men to treason patch together damnation with excuses, colours and shapes borrowed from glittering appearances of holiness. But the one who moulded you just told you to rise up, and gave you no reason to commit treason except to dub you with the name of traitor. If that same demon who fooled you like this were to stride the whole world with his lion's step, he might go back to vast hell and tell its legions, \"I can never win a soul as easily as that Englishman's.\" Oh, how you have infected the sweetness of trust with suspicion! Do men seem dutiful? Why, so did you. Do they seem serious and learned? Why, so did you. Do they come of noble family? Why, so did you. Do they seem religious? Why, so did you. Or are they moderate in diet, free of coarse passions of joy or anger, steady in spirit, not carried away by impulse, dressed and adorned in modest manners, not trusting the eye without the ear, and trusting neither except after clear judgement? You seemed exactly that, so finely sifted. And so your fall has left a kind of stain that marks even the most complete and gifted man with some suspicion. I will weep for you; for this rebellion of yours seems to me like a second fall of man. Their crimes are plain. Arrest them to answer to the law; and may God forgive them for their plots!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["I arrest thee of high treason, by the name of Richard Earl of","Cambridge. I arrest thee of high treason, by the name of Henry","Lord Scroop of Masham. I arrest thee of high treason, by the name","of Thomas Grey, knight, of Northumberland."],"id":152,"m":"I arrest you for high treason, in the name of Richard Earl of Cambridge. I arrest you for high treason, in the name of Henry Lord Scroop of Masham. I arrest you for high treason, in the name of Thomas Grey, knight, of Northumberland."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["Our purposes God justly hath discover'd,","And I repent my fault more than my death,","Which I beseech your Highness to forgive,","Although my body pay the price of it."],"id":153,"m":"God has justly uncovered our plans, and I regret my crime more than my death, which I beg your Highness to forgive, even though my body pays the price for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMBRIDGE","lines":["For me, the gold of France did not seduce,","Although I did admit it as a motive","The sooner to effect what I intended.","But God be thanked for prevention,","Which I in sufferance heartily will rejoice,","Beseeching God and you to pardon me."],"id":154,"m":"As for me, the gold of France did not tempt me, though I accepted it as a means to bring about sooner what I intended. But thank God it was prevented, which I will heartily rejoice at even while I suffer, begging God and you to pardon me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREY","lines":["Never did faithful subject more rejoice","At the discovery of most dangerous treason","Than I do at this hour joy o'er myself,","Prevented from a damned enterprise.","My fault, but not my body, pardon, sovereign."],"id":155,"m":"No loyal subject ever rejoiced more at the discovery of a most dangerous treason than I do at this moment for myself, stopped from a damned enterprise. Pardon my crime, my king, but not my body."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["God quit you in his mercy! Hear your sentence.","You have conspir'd against our royal person,","Join'd with an enemy proclaim'd, and from his coffers","Received the golden earnest of our death;","Wherein you would have sold your king to slaughter,","His princes and his peers to servitude,","His subjects to oppression and contempt,","And his whole kingdom into desolation.","Touching our person seek we no revenge;","But we our kingdom's safety must so tender,","Whose ruin you have sought, that to her laws","We do deliver you. Get you therefore hence,","Poor miserable wretches, to your death,","The taste whereof God of his mercy give","You patience to endure, and true repentance","Of all your dear offences! Bear them hence."],"id":156,"m":"May God reward you with his mercy! Hear your sentence. You have plotted against our royal person, joined a declared enemy, and taken from his treasury the gold down payment for our death. In doing so you would have sold your king to slaughter, his princes and lords to slavery, his subjects to oppression and contempt, and his whole kingdom to ruin. For our own person we seek no revenge, but we must care so much for our kingdom's safety, whose ruin you have sought, that we hand you over to its laws. So go away, poor miserable wretches, to your death; and may God in his mercy give you patience to bear it and true repentance for all your grievous crimes! Take them away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Cambridge, Scroop, and Grey, guarded.","id":157},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Now, lords, for France; the enterprise whereof","Shall be to you, as us, like glorious.","We doubt not of a fair and lucky war,","Since God so graciously hath brought to light","This dangerous treason lurking in our way","To hinder our beginnings. We doubt not now","But every rub is smoothed on our way.","Then forth, dear countrymen! Let us deliver","Our puissance into the hand of God,","Putting it straight in expedition.","Cheerly to sea! The signs of war advance!","No king of England, if not king of France!"],"id":158,"m":"Now, lords, to France; the enterprise will be as glorious for you as for us. We have no doubt of a fair and lucky war, since God has so graciously brought to light this dangerous treason lurking in our path to stop us at the start. We're sure now that every obstacle on our way has been smoothed out. So onward, dear countrymen! Let us put our strength into the hands of God and set it moving at once. Cheerfully to sea! Raise the banners of war! No king of England, if not king of France!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish.","id":159},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":160},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"London. Before a tavern.","id":161},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pistol, Nym, Bardolph, Boy, and Hostess.","id":162},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Prithee, honey, sweet husband, let me bring thee to Staines."],"id":163,"m":"Please, honey, sweet husband, let me go with you as far as Staines."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["No; for my manly heart doth yearn.","Bardolph, be blithe; Nym, rouse thy vaunting veins;","Boy, bristle thy courage up; for Falstaff he is dead,","And we must yearn therefore."],"id":164,"m":"No; for my manly heart grieves. Bardolph, be cheerful; Nym, stir up your boasting spirit; boy, bristle up your courage; for Falstaff is dead, and so we must grieve."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Would I were with him, wheresome'er he is, either in","heaven or in hell!"],"id":165,"m":"I wish I were with him, wherever he is, whether in heaven or in hell!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Nay, sure, he's not in hell. He's in Arthur's bosom, if ever","man went to Arthur's bosom. 'A made a finer end and went","away an it had been any christom child. 'A parted even just","between twelve and one, even at the turning o' the tide: for","after I saw him fumble with the sheets, and play with flowers,","and smile upon his fingers' ends, I knew there was but one way;","for his nose was as sharp as a pen, and 'a babbled of green","fields. \"How now, Sir John!\" quoth I; \"what, man! be o' good","cheer.\" So 'a cried out, \"God, God, God!\" three or four times.","Now I, to comfort him, bid him 'a should not think of God; I","hop'd there was no need to trouble himself with any such thoughts","yet. So 'a bade me lay more clothes on his feet. I put my hand","into the bed and felt them, and they were as cold as any stone;","then I felt to his knees, and they were as cold as any stone;","and so upward and upward, and all was as cold as any stone."],"id":166,"m":"No, he's surely not in hell. He's in Arthur's bosom, if any man ever went to Arthur's bosom. He made a finer end and went away as if he were a baby just christened. He died just between twelve and one, right at the turn of the tide. For after I saw him fumble with the sheets and play with flowers and smile at his fingertips, I knew there was only one way he was going; for his nose was as sharp as a pen, and he babbled of green fields. \"How are you, Sir John!\" I said. \"Come on, man, cheer up.\" Then he cried out \"God, God, God!\" three or four times. Now I, to comfort him, told him he shouldn't think of God; I hoped there was no need for him to trouble himself with such thoughts yet. Then he asked me to put more blankets on his feet. I put my hand into the bed and felt them, and they were as cold as any stone; then I felt up to his knees, and they were as cold as any stone; and so up and up, and everything was as cold as any stone."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["They say he cried out of sack."],"id":167,"m":"They say he cried out against sack."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Ay, that 'a did."],"id":168,"m":"Yes, he did."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["And of women."],"id":169,"m":"And against women."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Nay, that 'a did not."],"id":170,"m":"No, he didn't."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Yes, that 'a did; and said they were devils incarnate."],"id":171,"m":"Yes, he did, and said they were devils incarnate."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["'A could never abide carnation; 'twas a colour he never liked."],"id":172,"m":"He could never stand carnation; it was a colour he never liked."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["'A said once, the devil would have him about women."],"id":173,"m":"He said once the devil would get him because of women."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["'A did in some sort, indeed, handle women; but then he was","rheumatic, and talk'd of the whore of Babylon."],"id":174,"m":"In a way he did handle women, it's true; but then he was rheumatic, and talked about the whore of Babylon."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Do you not remember, 'a saw a flea stick upon Bardolph's nose,","and 'a said it was a black soul burning in hell-fire?"],"id":175,"m":"Don't you remember, he saw a flea stuck on Bardolph's nose and said it was a black soul burning in hell-fire?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Well, the fuel is gone that maintain'd that fire. That's all the","riches I got in his service."],"id":176,"m":"Well, the fuel that kept that fire going is gone. That's all the riches I got in his service."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Shall we shog? The King will be gone from Southampton."],"id":177,"m":"Shall we get moving? The King will have left Southampton."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Come, let's away. My love, give me thy lips.","Look to my chattels and my movables.","Let senses rule; the word is \"Pitch and Pay.\"","Trust none;","For oaths are straws, men's faiths are wafer-cakes","And hold-fast is the only dog, my duck;","Therefore, Caveto be thy counsellor.","Go, clear thy crystals. Yoke-fellows in arms,","Let us to France; like horse-leeches, my boys,","To suck, to suck, the very blood to suck!"],"id":178,"m":"Come, let's go. My love, give me your lips. Look after my goods and furniture. Use your sense; the rule is \"cash down\". Trust no one; for oaths are straws, men's promises are thin as wafers, and Hold-fast is the only dog worth having, my duck; so let Caution be your adviser. Go, dry your eyes. Comrades in arms, let's go to France, like horse-leeches, my boys, to suck, to suck, to suck the very blood!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["And that's but unwholesome food, they say."],"id":179,"m":"And that's unhealthy food, they say."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Touch her soft mouth, and march."],"id":180,"m":"Touch her soft mouth and march."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Farewell, hostess."],"id":181,"m":"Farewell, hostess."},{"t":"sd","text":"Kissing her.","id":182},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I cannot kiss; that is the humour of it; but, adieu."],"id":183,"m":"I can't kiss, that's the humour of it; but goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Let housewifery appear. Keep close, I thee command."],"id":184,"m":"Be a good housekeeper. Stay at home, I order you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Farewell; adieu."],"id":185,"m":"Farewell; goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":186},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"France. The King's palace.","id":187},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter the French King, the Dauphin, the Dukes of Berri and Bretagne the Constable, and others.","id":188},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Thus comes the English with full power upon us,","And more than carefully it us concerns","To answer royally in our defences.","Therefore the Dukes of Berri and of Bretagne,","Of Brabant and of Orleans, shall make forth,","And you, Prince Dauphin, with all swift dispatch,","To line and new repair our towns of war","With men of courage and with means defendant;","For England his approaches makes as fierce","As waters to the sucking of a gulf.","It fits us then to be as provident","As fears may teach us out of late examples","Left by the fatal and neglected English","Upon our fields."],"id":189,"m":"So the English are coming at us in full strength, and it matters more than ever that we answer with royal defences. So the Dukes of Berri and Brittany, of Brabant and of Orleans, shall set out, and you, Prince Dauphin, with all speed, to man and repair our fortified towns with brave soldiers and the means of defence. For England's advance is as fierce as water rushing into a whirlpool. So it suits us to be as careful as fear can teach us from recent examples left on our fields by the deadly English we underestimated."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["My most redoubted father,","It is most meet we arm us 'gainst the foe;","For peace itself should not so dull a kingdom,","Though war nor no known quarrel were in question,","But that defences, musters, preparations,","Should be maintain'd, assembled, and collected,","As were a war in expectation.","Therefore, I say, 'tis meet we all go forth","To view the sick and feeble parts of France.","And let us do it with no show of fear;","No, with no more than if we heard that England","Were busied with a Whitsun morris-dance;","For, my good liege, she is so idly king'd,","Her sceptre so fantastically borne","By a vain, giddy, shallow, humorous youth,","That fear attends her not."],"id":190,"m":"My most honoured father, it is right that we arm against the enemy; for even peace should not make a kingdom so dull, even if no war or quarrel were in question, that defences, musters and supplies are not kept up, gathered and collected as if a war were expected. So I say it is right that we all go out to inspect the weak and feeble parts of France. And let's do it without showing any fear; no, with no more fear than if we heard that England were busy with a Whitsun morris dance. For, my good lord, she has such an idle king, her sceptre is carried so fancifully by a vain, giddy, shallow, moody youth, that there is nothing to fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["O peace, Prince Dauphin!","You are too much mistaken in this king.","Question your Grace the late ambassadors","With what great state he heard their embassy,","How well supplied with noble counsellors,","How modest in exception, and withal","How terrible in constant resolution,","And you shall find his vanities forespent","Were but the outside of the Roman Brutus,","Covering discretion with a coat of folly;","As gardeners do with ordure hide those roots","That shall first spring and be most delicate."],"id":191,"m":"Oh, quiet, Prince Dauphin! You are badly mistaken about this king. Ask the ambassadors who have just returned, your Grace, with what great dignity he heard their message, how well supplied he was with noble advisers, how moderate in his objections, and at the same time how terrifying in his firm resolve. And you'll find his past follies were only the outward disguise of the Roman Brutus, covering wisdom with a coat of foolishness, as gardeners hide with manure the roots that will spring up first and be the most delicate."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Well, 'tis not so, my Lord High Constable;","But though we think it so, it is no matter.","In cases of defence 'tis best to weigh","The enemy more mighty than he seems,","So the proportions of defence are fill'd;","Which, of a weak and niggardly projection,","Doth, like a miser, spoil his coat with scanting","A little cloth."],"id":192,"m":"Well, it isn't so, my Lord High Constable; but even if we think it is, it doesn't matter. In matters of defence it's best to judge the enemy stronger than he seems, so that our defences are fully supplied; skimping on them, like a miser, spoils the coat to save a little cloth."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Think we King Harry strong;","And, Princes, look you strongly arm to meet him.","The kindred of him hath been flesh'd upon us;","And he is bred out of that bloody strain","That haunted us in our familiar paths.","Witness our too much memorable shame","When Cressy battle fatally was struck,","And all our princes captiv'd by the hand","Of that black name, Edward, Black Prince of Wales;","Whiles that his mountain sire, on mountain standing,","Up in the air, crown'd with the golden sun,","Saw his heroical seed, and smil'd to see him,","Mangle the work of nature and deface","The patterns that by God and by French fathers","Had twenty years been made. This is a stem","Of that victorious stock; and let us fear","The native mightiness and fate of him."],"id":193,"m":"Let us think King Harry strong; and, princes, make sure you arm strongly to meet him. His family has tasted our blood before, and he is bred from that bloody stock that haunted us on our own home ground. Remember our all too memorable shame when the battle of Crecy was so fatally fought, and all our princes were captured by that dreaded name, Edward, the Black Prince of Wales, while his mountain of a father, standing on a mountain high in the air, crowned with the golden sun, watched his heroic son and smiled to see him mangle the work of nature and destroy the men whom God and French fathers had spent twenty years making. This is a branch of that victorious tree; and let us fear his inborn strength and destiny."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":194},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Ambassadors from Harry King of England","Do crave admittance to your Majesty."],"id":195,"m":"Ambassadors from Harry, King of England, ask to be admitted to your Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["We'll give them present audience. Go, and bring them."],"id":196,"m":"We'll see them at once. Go and bring them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Messenger and certain Lords.","id":197},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","cont":1,"lines":["You see this chase is hotly follow'd, friends."],"id":198,"m":"You see this hunt is being hotly pursued, friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Turn head and stop pursuit; for coward dogs","Most spend their mouths when what they seem to threaten","Runs far before them. Good my sovereign,","Take up the English short, and let them know","Of what a monarchy you are the head.","Self-love, my liege, is not so vile a sin","As self-neglecting."],"id":199,"m":"Turn round and stop the chase; for cowardly dogs bark loudest when what they seem to threaten runs far ahead of them. My good king, cut the English short and let them know what a great monarchy you are head of. Self-love, my lord, is not so vile a sin as self-neglect."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EXETER.","id":200},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["From our brother of England?"],"id":201,"m":"From our brother of England?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["From him; and thus he greets your Majesty:","He wills you, in the name of God Almighty,","That you divest yourself, and lay apart","The borrowed glories that by gift of heaven,","By law of nature and of nations, longs","To him and to his heirs; namely, the crown","And all wide-stretched honours that pertain","By custom and the ordinance of times","Unto the crown of France. That you may know","'Tis no sinister nor no awkward claim","Pick'd from the worm-holes of long-vanish'd days,","Nor from the dust of old oblivion rak'd,","He sends you this most memorable line,","In every branch truly demonstrative;","Willing you overlook this pedigree;","And when you find him evenly deriv'd","From his most fam'd of famous ancestors,","Edward the Third, he bids you then resign","Your crown and kingdom, indirectly held","From him, the native and true challenger."],"id":202,"m":"From him; and this is how he greets your Majesty. He orders you, in the name of God Almighty, to strip off and lay aside the borrowed glories that, by gift of heaven and by the law of nature and of nations, belong to him and his heirs: namely, the crown and all the wide honours that belong by custom and long tradition to the crown of France. So that you may know it is no wicked or false claim picked out of the worm-holes of long-gone days or raked out of the dust of forgotten times, he sends you this most memorable family tree, true in every branch, asking you to study it; and when you find him descended directly from his most famous of famous ancestors, Edward the Third, he orders you to give up your crown and kingdom, which you hold unlawfully from him, the born and true claimant."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Or else what follows?"],"id":203,"m":"Or else what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Bloody constraint; for if you hide the crown","Even in your hearts, there will he rake for it.","Therefore in fierce tempest is he coming,","In thunder and in earthquake, like a Jove,","That, if requiring fail, he will compel;","And bids you, in the bowels of the Lord,","Deliver up the crown, and to take mercy","On the poor souls for whom this hungry war","Opens his vasty jaws; and on your head","Turning the widows' tears, the orphans' cries,","The dead men's blood, the pining maidens' groans,","For husbands, fathers, and betrothed lovers,","That shall be swallowed in this controversy.","This is his claim, his threat'ning, and my message;","Unless the Dauphin be in presence here,","To whom expressly I bring greeting too."],"id":204,"m":"Bloody force; for if you hide the crown even in your hearts, he will dig there for it. So he is coming in a fierce storm, in thunder and earthquake, like Jove, so that if asking fails, he will force you. And he asks you, by the mercy of the Lord, to give up the crown and take pity on the poor souls for whom this hungry war is opening its huge jaws; and he turns onto your head the widows' tears, the orphans' cries, the dead men's blood and the grieving girls' groans for husbands, fathers and promised lovers who will be swallowed up in this quarrel. That is his claim, his threat and my message; except for the Dauphin, if he is here, to whom I bring a particular greeting too."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["For us, we will consider of this further.","To-morrow shall you bear our full intent","Back to our brother of England."],"id":205,"m":"As for us, we will think about this further. Tomorrow you shall take our full answer back to our brother of England."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["For the Dauphin,","I stand here for him. What to him from England?"],"id":206,"m":"As for the Dauphin, I'm here for him. What message does England have for him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Scorn and defiance. Slight regard, contempt,","And anything that may not misbecome","The mighty sender, doth he prize you at.","Thus says my king: an if your father's Highness","Do not, in grant of all demands at large,","Sweeten the bitter mock you sent his Majesty,","He'll call you to so hot an answer of it","That caves and womby vaultages of France","Shall chide your trespass and return your mock","In second accent of his ordinance."],"id":207,"m":"Scorn and defiance, low regard and contempt, and anything that does not shame the mighty sender: that is what he thinks you worth. My king says this: unless your royal father sweetens the bitter joke you sent his Majesty by granting all his demands in full, he'll call you to such a hot answer for it that the caves and hollow vaults of France will rebuke your offence and throw your joke back at you in the echo of his cannon."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Say, if my father render fair return,","It is against my will; for I desire","Nothing but odds with England. To that end,","As matching to his youth and vanity,","I did present him with the Paris balls."],"id":208,"m":"Tell him that if my father gives a fair answer, it is against my will; for I want nothing but a quarrel with England. That's why, to suit his youth and vanity, I sent him the Paris tennis balls."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["He'll make your Paris Louvre shake for it,","Were it the mistress-court of mighty Europe;","And, be assur'd, you'll find a difference,","As we his subjects have in wonder found,","Between the promise of his greener days","And these he masters now. Now he weighs time","Even to the utmost grain. That you shall read","In your own losses, if he stay in France."],"id":209,"m":"He'll make your Paris Louvre shake for it, even if it were the chief court of mighty Europe. And, believe me, you'll find a difference, as we his subjects have found with amazement, between the promise of his younger days and the man he is now. Now he weighs every moment to the last grain. You'll read that in your own losses if he stays in France."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["To-morrow shall you know our mind at full."],"id":210,"m":"Tomorrow you shall know our mind in full."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish.","id":211},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Dispatch us with all speed, lest that our king","Come here himself to question our delay;","For he is footed in this land already."],"id":212,"m":"Send us back as fast as possible, or our king may come here himself to ask why we're delayed; for he has already set foot in this country."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["You shall be soon dispatch'd with fair conditions.","A night is but small breath and little pause","To answer matters of this consequence."],"id":213,"m":"You'll soon be sent back with fair terms. A night is only a short breath and a small pause to answer matters as important as this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":214},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"France. Before Harfleur.","id":215},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter Chorus.","id":216},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","lines":["Thus with imagin'd wing our swift scene flies,","In motion of no less celerity","Than that of thought. Suppose that you have seen","The well-appointed king at Hampton pier","Embark his royalty, and his brave fleet","With silken streamers the young Phoebus fanning.","Play with your fancies; and in them behold","Upon the hempen tackle ship-boys climbing;","Hear the shrill whistle which doth order give","To sounds confus'd; behold the threaden sails,","Borne with the invisible and creeping wind,","Draw the huge bottoms through the furrow'd sea,","Breasting the lofty surge. O, do but think","You stand upon the rivage and behold","A city on the inconstant billows dancing;","For so appears this fleet majestical,","Holding due course to Harfleur. Follow, follow!","Grapple your minds to sternage of this navy,","And leave your England, as dead midnight still,","Guarded with grandsires, babies, and old women,","Either past or not arriv'd to pith and puissance.","For who is he, whose chin is but enrich'd","With one appearing hair, that will not follow","These cull'd and choice-drawn cavaliers to France?","Work, work your thoughts, and therein see a siege;","Behold the ordnance on their carriages,","With fatal mouths gaping on girded Harfleur.","Suppose the ambassador from the French comes back,","Tells Harry that the King doth offer him","Katharine his daughter, and with her, to dowry,","Some petty and unprofitable dukedoms.","The offer likes not; and the nimble gunner","With linstock now the devilish cannon touches,"],"id":217,"m":"Our quick scene flies on the wings of imagination, moving no slower than thought. Imagine you have seen the well-equipped king put his royal person aboard at the pier in Southampton, and his brave fleet fanning the young sun with silk streamers. Let your imaginations play, and in them see ship-boys climbing the rope rigging; hear the shrill whistle that gives order to the confused noise; see the woven sails, filled by the invisible, creeping wind, drawing the huge hulls through the furrowed sea, breasting the high waves. Oh, just think that you stand on the shore and see a city dancing on the restless waves; for that is how this majestic fleet looks, holding its course for Harfleur. Follow, follow! Hook your minds to the sterns of this navy and leave your England as still as dead midnight, guarded by grandfathers, babies and old women, either past or not yet come to strength and power. For who is there, with just one hair showing on his chin, who will not follow these hand-picked knights to France? Work, work your thoughts, and in them see a siege. See the cannons on their wheeled carriages, their deadly mouths gaping at encircled Harfleur. Imagine the French ambassador comes back and tells Harry that the King offers him his daughter Katharine and, as her dowry, some small, worthless dukedoms. The offer does not please, and the quick gunner now touches the devilish cannon with his match,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum, and chambers go off.","id":218},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","cont":1,"lines":["And down goes all before them. Still be kind,","And eke out our performance with your mind."],"id":219,"m":"and everything in front of them goes down. Keep on being kind, and make up for our performance with your minds."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":220},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter King Henry, Exeter, Bedford, Gloucester, and Soldiers, with scaling-ladders.","id":221},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Once more unto the breach, dear friends, once more,","Or close the wall up with our English dead.","In peace there's nothing so becomes a man","As modest stillness and humility;","But when the blast of war blows in our ears,","Then imitate the action of the tiger;","Stiffen the sinews, summon up the blood,","Disguise fair nature with hard-favour'd rage;","Then lend the eye a terrible aspect;","Let it pry through the portage of the head","Like the brass cannon; let the brow o'erwhelm it","As fearfully as does a galled rock","O'erhang and jutty his confounded base,","Swill'd with the wild and wasteful ocean.","Now set the teeth and stretch the nostril wide,","Hold hard the breath, and bend up every spirit","To his full height. On, on, you noblest English,","Whose blood is fet from fathers of war-proof!","Fathers that, like so many Alexanders,","Have in these parts from morn till even fought,","And sheath'd their swords for lack of argument.","Dishonour not your mothers; now attest","That those whom you call'd fathers did beget you.","Be copy now to men of grosser blood,","And teach them how to war. And you, good yeomen,","Whose limbs were made in England, show us here","The mettle of your pasture; let us swear","That you are worth your breeding, which I doubt not;","For there is none of you so mean and base,","That hath not noble lustre in your eyes.","I see you stand like greyhounds in the slips,","Straining upon the start. The game's afoot!","Follow your spirit, and upon this charge","Cry, \"God for Harry! England and Saint George!\""],"id":222,"m":"Once more into the gap in the wall, dear friends, once more, or fill the wall up with our English dead! In peace nothing suits a man so well as modest quietness and humility; but when the blast of war blows in our ears, then imitate the action of the tiger. Stiffen your muscles, call up your blood, disguise your gentle nature with grim-faced rage. Then give the eye a terrible look; let it glare through the head's gunports like a brass cannon; let the brow hang over it as fearfully as a worn-away rock overhangs and juts out over its battered base, swamped by the wild and destructive ocean. Now clench your teeth and flare your nostrils, hold your breath hard, and stretch every spirit to its full height. On, on, you noblest English, whose blood comes from fathers proved in war! Fathers who, like so many Alexanders, fought in these lands from morning till evening and put away their swords only because there was no one left to fight. Don't dishonour your mothers; prove now that the men you called fathers really were your fathers. Be an example now to men of coarser blood and teach them how to fight. And you, good yeomen, whose limbs were grown in England, show us here the quality of your feeding. Let us swear that you are worth your upbringing, which I don't doubt; for there is not one of you so low and humble that he does not have a noble shine in his eyes. I see you standing like greyhounds on the leash, straining to start. The game is on! Follow your spirit, and as you charge, cry \"God for Harry! England and Saint George!\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt. Alarum, and chambers go off.","id":223},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"France. Before Harfleur.","id":224},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Nym, Bardolph, Pistol, and Boy.","id":225},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["On, on, on, on, on! To the breach, to the breach!"],"id":226,"m":"On, on, on, on, on! To the breach, to the breach!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Pray thee, corporal, stay. The knocks are too hot; and, for","mine own part, I have not a case of lives. The humour of it is","too hot; that is the very plain-song of it."],"id":227,"m":"Please, corporal, wait. The blows are too hot, and as for me, I don't have a spare set of lives. The humour of it is too hot; that's the plain tune of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["The plain-song is most just, for humours do abound.","\"Knocks go and come; God's vassals drop and die;","And sword and shield,","In bloody field,","Doth win immortal fame.\""],"id":228,"m":"The plain tune is very right, for there are moods aplenty. (Sings) \"Blows come and go; God's servants drop and die; and sword and shield, in bloody field, win everlasting fame.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Would I were in an alehouse in London! I would give all my","fame for a pot of ale and safety."],"id":229,"m":"I wish I were in an alehouse in London! I'd give all my fame for a pot of ale and safety."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["And I.","\"If wishes would prevail with me,","My purpose should not fail with me,","But thither would I hie.\""],"id":230,"m":"And so would I. (Sings) \"If wishes could win for me, my aim would not fail me, but there I'd hurry.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["\"As duly, but not as truly,","As bird doth sing on bough.\""],"id":231,"m":"(Sings) \"As eagerly, but not as truly, as a bird sings on a branch.\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fluellen.","id":232},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Up to the breach, you dogs! Avaunt, you cullions!"],"id":233,"m":"Up to the breach, you dogs! Get on, you scoundrels!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Driving them forward.","id":234},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Be merciful, great Duke, to men of mould.","Abate thy rage, abate thy manly rage,","Abate thy rage, great Duke!","Good bawcock, bate thy rage; use lenity, sweet chuck!"],"id":235,"m":"Have mercy, great Duke, on men made of earth. Calm your rage, calm your manly rage, calm your rage, great Duke! Good fine fellow, calm your rage; be gentle, sweet chick!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["These be good humours! Your honour wins bad humours."],"id":236,"m":"These are fine moods! Your honour has foul moods."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Boy.","id":237},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["As young as I am, I have observ'd these three swashers. I am","boy to them all three; but all they three, though they would","serve me, could not be man to me; for indeed three such antics","do not amount to a man. For Bardolph, he is white-liver'd and","red-fac'd; by the means whereof 'a faces it out, but fights not.","For Pistol, he hath a killing tongue and a quiet sword; by the","means whereof 'a breaks words, and keeps whole weapons. For","Nym, he hath heard that men of few words are the best men; and","therefore he scorns to say his prayers, lest 'a should be thought","a coward. But his few bad words are match'd with as few good","deeds; for 'a never broke any man's head but his own, and that","was against a post when he was drunk. They will steal anything,","and call it purchase. Bardolph stole a lute-case, bore it twelve","leagues, and sold it for three half-pence. Nym and Bardolph are","sworn brothers in filching, and in Calais they stole a","fire-shovel.","I knew by that piece of service the men would carry coals. They","would have me as familiar with men's pockets as their gloves or","their handkerchers; which makes much against my manhood, if I","should take from another's pocket to put into mine; for it is","plain pocketing up of wrongs. I must leave them, and seek some","better service. Their villainy goes against my weak stomach,","and therefore I must cast it up."],"id":238,"m":"Young as I am, I've watched these three swaggerers. I'm servant boy to all three of them; but all three together, even if they served me, couldn't be a man to me, because three such clowns don't add up to one man. As for Bardolph, he is white-livered and red-faced, which is why he brazens it out but doesn't fight. As for Pistol, he has a killing tongue and a quiet sword, which is why he breaks his word and keeps his weapons whole. As for Nym, he has heard that men of few words are the best men, and so he refuses even to say his prayers, in case he's thought a coward. But his few bad words are matched by just as few good deeds; for he never broke any man's head but his own, and that was against a post when he was drunk. They'll steal anything and call it a bargain. Bardolph stole a lute-case, carried it thirty-six miles and sold it for three halfpence. Nym and Bardolph are sworn brothers in pilfering, and in Calais they stole a fire-shovel. I knew by that bit of work that they would put up with anything. They wanted me to be as at home in men's pockets as in their gloves or handkerchiefs, which goes badly against my manhood, if I take from another's pocket to put in mine; for it's plainly pocketing up wrongs. I must leave them and look for a better position. Their villainy turns my weak stomach, so I must throw it up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":239},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gower and Fluellen.","id":240},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Captain Fluellen, you must come presently to the mines.","The Duke of Gloucester would speak with you."],"id":241,"m":"Captain Fluellen, you must come at once to the mines. The Duke of Gloucester wants to speak with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["To the mines! Tell you the Duke, it is not so good to come","to the mines; for, look you, the mines is not according to the","disciplines of the war. The concavities of it is not sufficient;","for, look you, the athversary, you may discuss unto the Duke,","look you, is digt himself four yard under the countermines. By","Cheshu, I think 'a will plow up all, if there is not better","directions."],"id":242,"m":"To the mines! You tell the Duke it's not so good to come to the mines; for, look you, the mines are not according to the rules of war. The tunnels are not deep enough; for, look you, the enemy, you may explain to the Duke, look you, has dug himself four yards under the countermines. By Jesus, I think he'll blow everything up if there aren't better orders."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["The Duke of Gloucester, to whom the order of the siege is","given, is altogether directed by an Irishman, a very valiant","gentleman, i' faith."],"id":243,"m":"The Duke of Gloucester, who has been given command of the siege, is completely guided by an Irishman, a very brave gentleman, honestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["It is Captain Macmorris, is it not?"],"id":244,"m":"That's Captain Macmorris, isn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["I think it be."],"id":245,"m":"I think so."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["By Cheshu, he is an ass, as in the world. I will verify as","much in his beard. He has no more directions in the true","disciplines of the wars, look you, of the Roman disciplines,","than is a puppy-dog."],"id":246,"m":"By Jesus, he's as big an ass as any in the world. I'll prove as much to his face. He knows no more about the true rules of war, look you, the Roman rules, than a puppy does."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Macmorris and Captain Jamy.","id":247},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Here 'a comes; and the Scots captain, Captain Jamy, with him."],"id":248,"m":"Here he comes, and the Scottish captain, Captain Jamy, with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Captain Jamy is a marvellous falorous gentleman, that is","certain; and of great expedition and knowledge in the aunchient","wars, upon my particular knowledge of his directions. By Cheshu,","he will maintain his argument as well as any military man in the","world, in the disciplines of the pristine wars of the Romans."],"id":249,"m":"Captain Jamy is a marvellously valiant gentleman, that's certain, and of great energy and knowledge in the ancient wars, from my own knowledge of his instructions. By Jesus, he'll hold his own in an argument as well as any soldier in the world on the rules of the ancient Roman wars."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAMY","lines":["I say gud-day, Captain Fluellen."],"id":250,"m":"I say good day, Captain Fluellen."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["God-den to your worship, good Captain James."],"id":251,"m":"Good evening to your worship, good Captain James."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["How now, Captain Macmorris! have you quit the mines?","Have the pioneers given o'er?"],"id":252,"m":"Well, Captain Macmorris, have you left the mines? Have the diggers given up?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MACMORRIS","lines":["By Chrish, la! 'tish ill done! The work ish give over, the","trompet sound the retreat. By my hand I swear, and my","father's soul, the work ish ill done; it ish give over. I would","have blowed up the town, so Chrish save me, la! in an hour.","O, 'tish ill done, 'tish ill done; by my hand, 'tish ill done!"],"id":253,"m":"By Christ, ah, it's badly done! The work is given up; the trumpet sounded the retreat. I swear by my hand and my father's soul, the work is badly done; it's given up. I would have blown up the town in an hour, so Christ save me! Oh, it's badly done, it's badly done; by my hand, it's badly done!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Captain Macmorris, I beseech you now, will you voutsafe me,","look you, a few disputations with you, as partly touching or","concerning the disciplines of the war, the Roman wars, in the way","of argument, look you, and friendly communication; partly to","satisfy my opinion, and partly for the satisfaction, look you, of","my mind, as touching the direction of the military discipline;","that is the point."],"id":254,"m":"Captain Macmorris, I beg you now, will you allow me, look you, a few discussions with you, partly about or concerning the rules of war, the Roman wars, by way of argument, look you, and friendly conversation; partly to satisfy my opinion, and partly for the satisfaction, look you, of my mind, about the direction of military discipline; that's the point."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAMY","lines":["It sall be vary gud, gud feith, gud captains bath: and I sall","quit you with gud leve, as I may pick occasion; that sall I,","marry."],"id":255,"m":"That will be very good, by my faith, good captains both; and I'll answer you, with your permission, when I get the chance; that I will, indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"MACMORRIS","lines":["It is no time to discourse, so Chrish save me. The day is hot,","and the weather, and the wars, and the King, and the Dukes. It","is no time to discourse. The town is beseech'd, and the trumpet","call us to the breach, and we talk, and, be Chrish, do nothing.","'Tis shame for us all. So God sa' me, 'tis shame to stand still;","it is shame, by my hand; and there is throats to be cut, and works","to be done; and there ish nothing done, so Chrish sa' me, la!"],"id":256,"m":"It's no time to talk, so Christ save me. The day is hot, and the weather, and the wars, and the King, and the Dukes. It's no time to talk. The town is besieged, and the trumpet calls us to the breach, and we talk and, by Christ, do nothing. It's a shame for all of us. So God save me, it's a shame to stand still; it's a shame, by my hand; and there are throats to cut and work to do, and nothing is being done, so Christ save me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"JAMY","lines":["By the mess, ere theise eyes of mine take themselves to slomber,","I'll de gud service, or I'll lig i' the grund for it; ay, or go to","death; and I'll pay't as valorously as I may, that sall I suerly do,","that is the breff and the long. Marry, I wad full fain heard some","question 'tween you tway."],"id":257,"m":"By the Mass, before these eyes of mine go to sleep, I'll do good service or lie in the ground for it; yes, or go to my death; and I'll pay it as bravely as I can, that I surely will, that's the short and long of it. Indeed, I'd very much like to hear some debate between you two."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Captain Macmorris, I think, look you, under your correction, there","is not many of your nation--"],"id":258,"m":"Captain Macmorris, I think, look you, if you'll allow me to say so, there are not many of your nation..."},{"t":"sp","who":"MACMORRIS","lines":["Of my nation! What ish my nation? Ish a villain, and a bastard,","and a knave, and a rascal? What ish my nation? Who talks of my","nation?"],"id":259,"m":"Of my nation! What is my nation? Is it a villain, and a bastard, and a knave, and a rascal? What is my nation? Who's talking about my nation?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Look you, if you take the matter otherwise than is meant, Captain","Macmorris, peradventure I shall think you do not use me with that","affability as in discretion you ought to use me, look you, being","as good a man as yourself, both in the disciplines of war, and in","the derivation of my birth, and in other particularities."],"id":260,"m":"Look you, if you take the matter differently from how it's meant, Captain Macmorris, perhaps I shall think you don't treat me with the courtesy you should sensibly use towards me, look you, since I'm as good a man as you, both in the rules of war and in my family descent and in other details."},{"t":"sp","who":"MACMORRIS","lines":["I do not know you so good a man as myself. So Chrish save me,","I will cut off your head."],"id":261,"m":"I don't know that you're as good a man as me. So Christ save me, I'll cut your head off."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Gentlemen both, you will mistake each other."],"id":262,"m":"Gentlemen both, you are misunderstanding each other."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAMY","lines":["Ah! that's a foul fault."],"id":263,"m":"Ah, that's a bad mistake."},{"t":"sd","text":"A parley sounded.","id":264},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["The town sounds a parley."],"id":265,"m":"The town is calling for a truce to talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Captain Macmorris, when there is more better opportunity to be","required, look you, I will be so bold as to tell you I know the","disciplines of war; and there is an end."],"id":266,"m":"Captain Macmorris, when there's a better opportunity, look you, I'll be so bold as to tell you that I know the rules of war; and that's the end of it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":267},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"France. Before the gates of Harfleur.","id":268},{"t":"sd","text":"The Governor and some citizens on the walls; the English forces below. Enter King Henry and his train.","id":269},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["How yet resolves the governor of the town?","This is the latest parle we will admit;","Therefore to our best mercy give yourselves,","Or like to men proud of destruction","Defy us to our worst; for, as I am a soldier,","A name that in my thoughts becomes me best,","If I begin the battery once again,","I will not leave the half-achieved Harfleur","Till in her ashes she lie buried.","The gates of mercy shall be all shut up,","And the flesh'd soldier, rough and hard of heart,","In liberty of bloody hand shall range","With conscience wide as hell, mowing like grass","Your fresh fair virgins and your flow'ring infants.","What is it then to me, if impious War,","Array'd in flames like to the prince of fiends,","Do with his smirch'd complexion all fell feats","Enlink'd to waste and desolation?","What is't to me, when you yourselves are cause,","If your pure maidens fall into the hand","Of hot and forcing violation?","What rein can hold licentious wickedness","When down the hill he holds his fierce career?","We may as bootless spend our vain command","Upon the enraged soldiers in their spoil","As send precepts to the leviathan","To come ashore. Therefore, you men of Harfleur,","Take pity of your town and of your people,","Whiles yet my soldiers are in my command,","Whiles yet the cool and temperate wind of grace","O'erblows the filthy and contagious clouds","Of heady murder, spoil, and villainy.","If not, why, in a moment look to see","The blind and bloody soldier with foul hand","Defile the locks of your shrill-shrieking daughters;","Your fathers taken by the silver beards,","And their most reverend heads dash'd to the walls;","Your naked infants spitted upon pikes,","Whiles the mad mothers with their howls confus'd","Do break the clouds, as did the wives of Jewry","At Herod's bloody-hunting slaughtermen.","What say you? Will you yield, and this avoid,","Or, guilty in defence, be thus destroy'd?"],"id":270,"m":"What has the governor of the town decided? This is the last truce we will allow; so either surrender to our best mercy or, like men proud to be destroyed, defy us to do our worst. For, as I am a soldier, a name that I think suits me best, if I begin the bombardment again I will not leave half-conquered Harfleur until she lies buried in her ashes. The gates of mercy will all be shut, and the soldier who has tasted blood, rough and hard-hearted, will roam freely with bloody hand and a conscience as wide as hell, mowing down your fresh, fair virgins and your blossoming babies like grass. What is it to me, then, if wicked War, dressed in flames like the prince of devils, with his blackened face, does all the cruel deeds that go with destruction and ruin? What is it to me, when you yourselves are the cause, if your pure daughters fall into the hands of hot and violent rape? What rein can hold back unbridled wickedness when it charges fiercely downhill? We might as well waste our useless orders on the enraged soldiers in their looting as send commands to the whale to come ashore. So, you men of Harfleur, take pity on your town and your people while my soldiers are still under my command, while the cool, calm wind of mercy still blows away the filthy, infectious clouds of reckless murder, plunder and villainy. If not, then expect in a moment to see the blind and bloody soldier with his foul hand defile the hair of your shrieking daughters; your fathers seized by their silver beards and their most respected heads smashed against the walls; your naked babies speared on pikes, while their mad mothers break the clouds with their confused howls, as the women of Judea did at Herod's bloodthirsty butchers. What do you say? Will you surrender and avoid this, or, guilty of resisting, be destroyed like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOVERNOR","lines":["Our expectation hath this day an end.","The Dauphin, whom of succours we entreated,","Returns us that his powers are yet not ready","To raise so great a siege. Therefore, great King,","We yield our town and lives to thy soft mercy.","Enter our gates; dispose of us and ours;","For we no longer are defensible."],"id":271,"m":"Our hopes end today. The Dauphin, whom we begged for help, sends back word that his forces are not yet ready to lift so great a siege. So, great King, we surrender our town and our lives to your gentle mercy. Enter our gates; do what you will with us and ours; for we can no longer defend ourselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Open your gates. Come, uncle Exeter,","Go you and enter Harfleur; there remain,","And fortify it strongly 'gainst the French.","Use mercy to them all. For us, dear uncle,","The winter coming on, and sickness growing","Upon our soldiers, we will retire to Calais.","To-night in Harfleur will we be your guest;","To-morrow for the march are we addrest."],"id":272,"m":"Open your gates. Come, Uncle Exeter, go and enter Harfleur; stay there and fortify it strongly against the French. Be merciful to them all. As for us, dear uncle, with winter coming on and sickness spreading among our soldiers, we will withdraw to Calais. Tonight we will be your guest in Harfleur; tomorrow we are ready to march."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. The King and his train enter the town.","id":273},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"Rouen. A room in the French King's palace.","id":274},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Katharine and Alice, an old Gentlewoman.","id":275},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Alice, tu as ete en Angleterre, et tu parles bien le langage."],"id":276,"m":"(In French) Alice, you have been in England, and you speak the language well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Un peu, madame."],"id":277,"m":"(In French) A little, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Je te prie, m'enseignez; il faut que j'apprenne a parler.","Comment appelez-vous la main en Anglois?"],"id":278,"m":"(In French) Please teach me; I must learn to speak it. What do you call \"la main\" in English?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["La main? Elle est appelee de hand."],"id":279,"m":"(In French) La main? It is called \"de hand\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["De hand. Et les doigts?"],"id":280,"m":"(In French) \"De hand\". And \"les doigts\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Les doigts? Ma foi, j'oublie les doigts; mais je me","souviendrai. Les doigts? Je pense qu'ils sont appeles de","fingres; oui, de fingres."],"id":281,"m":"(In French) Les doigts? Goodness, I forget les doigts; but I'll remember. Les doigts? I think they're called \"de fingres\"; yes, \"de fingres\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["La main, de hand; les doigts, de fingres. Je pense que","je suis le bon ecolier; j'ai gagne deux mots d'Anglois","vitement. Comment appelez-vous les ongles?"],"id":282,"m":"(In French) La main, \"de hand\"; les doigts, \"de fingres\". I think I'm a good student; I've learned two words of English quickly. What do you call \"les ongles\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Les ongles? Nous les appelons de nails."],"id":283,"m":"(In French) Les ongles? We call them \"de nails\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["De nails. Ecoutez; dites-moi, si je parle bien: de hand,","de fingres, et de nails."],"id":284,"m":"(In French) \"De nails\". Listen; tell me if I say it well: \"de hand, de fingres, and de nails\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["C'est bien dit, madame; il est fort bon Anglois."],"id":285,"m":"(In French) Well said, madam; that's very good English."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Dites-moi l'Anglois pour le bras."],"id":286,"m":"(In French) Tell me the English for \"le bras\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["De arm, madame."],"id":287,"m":"(In French) \"De arm\", madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Et le coude?"],"id":288,"m":"(In French) And \"le coude\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["D'elbow."],"id":289,"m":"(In French) \"D'elbow\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["D'elbow. Je m'en fais la repetition de tous les mots","que vous m'avez appris des a present."],"id":290,"m":"(In French) \"D'elbow\". I'll repeat all the words you've taught me so far."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Il est trop difficile, madame, comme je pense."],"id":291,"m":"(In French) It's too hard, madam, I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Excusez-moi, Alice; ecoutez: d'hand, de fingres, de","nails, d'arma, de bilbow."],"id":292,"m":"(In French) Excuse me, Alice; listen: \"d'hand, de fingres, de nails, d'arma, de bilbow\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["D'elbow, madame."],"id":293,"m":"(In French) \"D'elbow\", madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["O Seigneur Dieu, je m'en oublie! D'elbow.","Comment appelez-vous le col?"],"id":294,"m":"(In French) Oh Lord God, I forget! \"D'elbow\". What do you call \"le col\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["De nick, madame."],"id":295,"m":"(In French) \"De nick\", madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["De nick. Et le menton?"],"id":296,"m":"(In French) \"De nick\". And \"le menton\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["De chin."],"id":297,"m":"(In French) \"De chin\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["De sin. Le col, de nick; le menton, de sin."],"id":298,"m":"(In French) \"De sin\". Le col, \"de nick\"; le menton, \"de sin\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Oui. Sauf votre honneur, en verite, vous prononcez les","mots aussi droit que les natifs d'Angleterre."],"id":299,"m":"(In French) Yes. With respect, truly, you pronounce the words as correctly as the natives of England."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Je ne doute point d'apprendre, par la grace de Dieu,","et en peu de temps."],"id":300,"m":"(In French) I have no doubt I'll learn, by the grace of God, and in a short time."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["N'avez-vous pas deja oublie ce que je vous ai enseigne?"],"id":301,"m":"(In French) Haven't you already forgotten what I taught you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Non, je reciterai a vous promptement: d'hand, de","fingres, de mails,--"],"id":302,"m":"(In French) No, I'll recite it to you straight away: \"d'hand, de fingres, de mails\","},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["De nails, madame."],"id":303,"m":"(In French) \"De nails\", madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["De nails, de arm, de ilbow."],"id":304,"m":"(In French) \"De nails, de arm, de ilbow.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Sauf votre honneur, de elbow."],"id":305,"m":"(In French) With respect, \"de elbow\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Ainsi dis-je; d'elbow, de nick, et de sin. Comment","appelez-vous le pied et la robe?"],"id":306,"m":"(In French) That's what I said: \"d'elbow, de nick, and de sin\". What do you call \"le pied\" and \"la robe\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["De foot, madame; et de coun."],"id":307,"m":"(In French) \"De foot\", madam, and \"de coun\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["De foot et de coun! O Seigneur Dieu! ce sont mots de son","mauvais, corruptible, gros, et impudique, et non pour les","dames d'honneur d'user. Je ne voudrais prononcer ces mots","devant les seigneurs de France pour tout le monde. Foh! le","foot et le coun! Neanmoins, je reciterai une autre fois ma lecon","ensemble: d' hand, de fingres, de nails, d'arm, d'elbow, de","nick, de sin, de foot, de coun."],"id":308,"m":"(In French) \"De foot\" and \"de coun\"! Oh Lord God! Those are bad-sounding words, corrupting, coarse and indecent, not for ladies of honour to use. I wouldn't say those words in front of the lords of France for all the world. Ugh! \"Le foot\" and \"le coun\"! All the same, I'll recite my whole lesson once more: \"d'hand, de fingres, de nails, d'arm, d'elbow, de nick, de sin, de foot, de coun\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Excellent, madame!"],"id":309,"m":"(In French) Excellent, madam!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["C'est assez pour une fois: allons-nous a diner."],"id":310,"m":"(In French) That's enough for one time; let's go to dinner."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":311},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"V","loc":"Rouen. The French King's palace.","id":312},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King of France, the Dauphin, the Duke of Bourbon, the Constable of France, and others.","id":313},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["'Tis certain he hath pass'd the river Somme."],"id":314,"m":"It is certain he has crossed the river Somme."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["And if he be not fought withal, my lord,","Let us not live in France; let us quit all","And give our vineyards to a barbarous people."],"id":315,"m":"And if we don't fight him, my lord, let's not live in France; let's give up everything and hand our vineyards to a race of barbarians."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["O Dieu vivant! shall a few sprays of us,","The emptying of our fathers' luxury,","Our scions put in wild and savage stock,","Spirt up so suddenly into the clouds,","And overlook their grafters?"],"id":316,"m":"Living God! Shall a few offshoots of ours, the spilt seed of our ancestors' lust, our shoots grafted onto wild and savage stock, spurt up so suddenly into the clouds and look down on those who grafted them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOURBON","lines":["Normans, but bastard Normans, Norman bastards!","Mort de ma vie! if they march along","Unfought withal, but I will sell my dukedom,","To buy a slobbery and a dirty farm","In that nook-shotten isle of Albion."],"id":317,"m":"Normans, but bastard Normans, Norman bastards! Death of my life! If they march on without a fight, I'll sell my dukedom to buy a muddy, dirty farm on that ragged, corner-cut island of Britain."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Dieu de batailles! where have they this mettle?","Is not their climate foggy, raw, and dull,","On whom, as in despite, the sun looks pale,","Killing their fruit with frowns? Can sodden water,","A drench for sur-rein'd jades, their barley-broth,","Decoct their cold blood to such valiant heat?","And shall our quick blood, spirited with wine,","Seem frosty? O, for honour of our land,","Let us not hang like roping icicles","Upon our houses' thatch, whiles a more frosty people","Sweat drops of gallant youth in our rich fields!","Poor we may call them in their native lords."],"id":318,"m":"God of battles! Where do they get this spirit? Isn't their climate foggy, raw and dull, where the sun looks pale on them as if in spite, killing their fruit with its frowns? Can boiled water, a medicine for worn-out horses, their barley-broth beer, heat their cold blood to such brave fire? And shall our lively blood, spirited by wine, seem frosty? Oh, for the honour of our country, let's not hang like dripping icicles from the thatch of our houses, while a more frosty people sweat drops of brave youth in our rich fields! We may call our fields poor in the lords they were born with."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["By faith and honour,","Our madams mock at us, and plainly say","Our mettle is bred out, and they will give","Their bodies to the lust of English youth","To new-store France with bastard warriors."],"id":319,"m":"By my faith and honour, our ladies mock us and say plainly that our spirit has been bred out of us, and that they will give their bodies to the lust of English youths to restock France with bastard warriors."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOURBON","lines":["They bid us to the English dancing-schools,","And teach lavoltas high, and swift corantos;","Saying our grace is only in our heels,","And that we are most lofty runaways."],"id":320,"m":"They tell us to go to the English dancing schools and teach high lavoltas and quick corantos, saying our only skill is in our heels, and that we are very high-flying runaways."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Where is Montjoy the herald? Speed him hence.","Let him greet England with our sharp defiance.","Up, princes! and, with spirit of honour edged","More sharper than your swords, hie to the field!","Charles Delabreth, High Constable of France;","You Dukes of Orleans, Bourbon, and of Berri,","Alencon, Brabant, Bar, and Burgundy;","Jacques Chatillon, Rambures, Vaudemont,","Beaumont, Grandpre, Roussi, and Fauconberg,","Foix, Lestrale, Bouciqualt, and Charolois;","High dukes, great princes, barons, lords, and knights,","For your great seats now quit you of great shames.","Bar Harry England, that sweeps through our land","With pennons painted in the blood of Harfleur.","Rush on his host, as doth the melted snow","Upon the valleys, whose low vassal seat","The Alps doth spit and void his rheum upon.","Go down upon him, you have power enough,","And in a captive chariot into Rouen","Bring him our prisoner."],"id":321,"m":"Where is Montjoy the herald? Send him off quickly. Let him greet England with our sharp defiance. Up, princes, and with a sense of honour sharper than your swords, hurry to the field! Charles Delabreth, High Constable of France; you Dukes of Orleans, Bourbon and Berri, Alencon, Brabant, Bar and Burgundy; Jacques Chatillon, Rambures, Vaudemont, Beaumont, Grandpre, Roussi and Fauconberg, Foix, Lestrale, Bouciqualt and Charolois; high dukes, great princes, barons, lords and knights, for the sake of your great estates, now free yourselves of great shame. Stop Harry of England, who sweeps through our land with banners painted in the blood of Harfleur. Rush on his army as melted snow rushes on the valleys, the low servant ground on which the Alps spit and pour their spittle. Fall on him, you have power enough, and bring him to Rouen as our prisoner in a captive's cart."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["This becomes the great.","Sorry am I his numbers are so few,","His soldiers sick and famish'd in their march;","For I am sure, when he shall see our army,","He'll drop his heart into the sink of fear","And for achievement offer us his ransom."],"id":322,"m":"This is worthy of the great. I'm sorry his numbers are so small, his soldiers sick and starving on their march; for I'm sure that when he sees our army his heart will drop into the pit of fear, and instead of victory he'll offer us his ransom."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Therefore, Lord Constable, haste on Montjoy,","And let him say to England that we send","To know what willing ransom he will give.","Prince Dauphin, you shall stay with us in Rouen."],"id":323,"m":"So, Lord Constable, hurry Montjoy on, and let him tell England that we send to ask what ransom he will willingly pay. Prince Dauphin, you shall stay with us in Rouen."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Not so, I do beseech your Majesty."],"id":324,"m":"No, I beg your Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Be patient, for you shall remain with us.","Now forth, Lord Constable and princes all,","And quickly bring us word of England's fall."],"id":325,"m":"Be patient, for you shall stay with us. Now go, Lord Constable and all you princes, and quickly bring us news of England's fall."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":326},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"VI","loc":"The English camp in Picardy.","id":327},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gower and Fluellen, meeting.","id":328},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["How now, Captain Fluellen! come you from the bridge?"],"id":329,"m":"Well, Captain Fluellen! Have you come from the bridge?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["I assure you, there is very excellent services committed at the","bridge."],"id":330,"m":"I assure you, very excellent service has been done at the bridge."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Is the Duke of Exeter safe?"],"id":331,"m":"Is the Duke of Exeter safe?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["The Duke of Exeter is as magnanimous as Agamemnon; and a","man that I love and honour with my soul, and my heart, and my","duty, and my live, and my living, and my uttermost power. He","is not--God be praised and blessed!--any hurt in the world; but","keeps the bridge most valiantly, with excellent discipline. There","is an aunchient lieutenant there at the pridge, I think in my","very conscience he is as valiant a man as Mark Antony; and he is","a man of no estimation in the world, but I did see him do as","gallant service."],"id":332,"m":"The Duke of Exeter is as great-hearted as Agamemnon, and a man that I love and honour with my soul, and my heart, and my duty, and my life, and my living, and my utmost power. He is not, God be praised and blessed, hurt in the least, but holds the bridge most bravely, with excellent discipline. There's an ensign there at the bridge, I think in my very conscience he's as brave a man as Mark Antony; and he's a man of no reputation in the world, but I saw him do fine service."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["What do you call him?"],"id":333,"m":"What's his name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["He is call'd Aunchient Pistol."],"id":334,"m":"He's called Ensign Pistol."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["I know him not."],"id":335,"m":"I don't know him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pistol.","id":336},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Here is the man."],"id":337,"m":"Here's the man."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Captain, I thee beseech to do me favours.","The Duke of Exeter doth love thee well."],"id":338,"m":"Captain, I beg you to do me a favour. The Duke of Exeter thinks well of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Ay, I praise God; and I have merited some love at his hands."],"id":339,"m":"Yes, I praise God, and I have deserved some regard from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Bardolph, a soldier, firm and sound of heart,","And of buxom valour, hath by cruel fate","And giddy Fortune's furious fickle wheel,","That goddess blind,","That stands upon the rolling restless stone--"],"id":340,"m":"Bardolph, a soldier firm and sound of heart and of lively courage, has, by cruel fate and giddy Fortune's furious fickle wheel, that blind goddess who stands on the rolling, restless stone..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["By your patience, Aunchient Pistol. Fortune is painted","blind, with a muffler afore his eyes, to signify to you that","Fortune is blind; and she is painted also with a wheel, to","signify to you, which is the moral of it, that she is turning,","and inconstant, and mutability, and variation; and her foot,","look you, is fixed upon a spherical stone, which rolls, and","rolls, and rolls. In good truth, the poet makes a most excellent","description of it. Fortune is an excellent moral."],"id":341,"m":"Excuse me, Ensign Pistol. Fortune is painted blind, with a scarf over his eyes, to signify to you that Fortune is blind; and she is also painted with a wheel, to signify to you, which is the moral of it, that she is turning and inconstant, and changeable and variable; and her foot, look you, is fixed on a round stone, which rolls, and rolls, and rolls. In good truth, the poet makes a most excellent description of it. Fortune is an excellent moral."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Fortune is Bardolph's foe, and frowns on him;","For he hath stolen a pax, and hanged must 'a be,--","A damned death!","Let gallows gape for dog; let man go free,","And let not hemp his windpipe suffocate.","But Exeter hath given the doom of death","For pax of little price.","Therefore, go speak; the Duke will hear thy voice;","And let not Bardolph's vital thread be cut","With edge of penny cord and vile reproach.","Speak, captain, for his life, and I will thee requite."],"id":342,"m":"Fortune is Bardolph's enemy and frowns on him; for he has stolen a pax, a holy tablet, and he must be hanged. A damned death! Let gallows gape for dogs; let men go free, and let no rope choke his windpipe. But Exeter has given the sentence of death for a pax of little value. So go and speak; the Duke will listen to you; and don't let Bardolph's thread of life be cut with a penny rope and vile disgrace. Speak for his life, captain, and I'll repay you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Aunchient Pistol, I do partly understand your meaning."],"id":343,"m":"Ensign Pistol, I partly understand what you mean."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Why then, rejoice therefore."],"id":344,"m":"Why then, rejoice at it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Certainly, aunchient, it is not a thing to rejoice at; for if,","look you, he were my brother, I would desire the Duke","to use his good pleasure, and put him to execution; for","discipline ought to be used."],"id":345,"m":"Certainly, ensign, it's nothing to rejoice at; for if, look you, he were my brother, I would want the Duke to do as he pleases and have him executed; for discipline must be kept."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Die and be damn'd! and figo for thy friendship!"],"id":346,"m":"Die and be damned! And a fig for your friendship!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["It is well."],"id":347,"m":"Very well."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["The fig of Spain."],"id":348,"m":"The fig of Spain!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":349},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Very good."],"id":350,"m":"Very good."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Why, this is an arrant counterfeit rascal. I remember","him now; a bawd, a cutpurse."],"id":351,"m":"Why, this is an out-and-out fake, a rascal. I remember him now: a pimp, a pickpocket."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["I'll assure you, 'a uttered as prave words at the pridge as you","shall see in a summer's day. But it is very well; what he has","spoke to me, that is well, I warrant you, when time is serve."],"id":352,"m":"I assure you, he spoke as brave words at the bridge as you'll see on a summer's day. But it is very well; what he has said to me, that is well, I promise you, when the time comes."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Why, 't is a gull, a fool, a rogue, that now and then goes to","the wars, to grace himself at his return into London under the","form of a soldier. And such fellows are perfect in the great","commanders' names; and they will learn you by rote where services","were done; at such and such a sconce, at such a breach, at such a","convoy; who came off bravely, who was shot, who disgrac'd, what","terms the enemy stood on; and this they con perfectly in the","phrase of war, which they trick up with new-tuned oaths: and what","a beard of the general's cut and a horrid suit of the camp will","do among foaming bottles and ale-wash'd wits, is wonderful to be","thought on. But you must learn to know such slanders of the age,","or else you may be marvellously mistook."],"id":353,"m":"Why, he's a dupe, a fool, a rogue, who now and then goes to the wars so that he can show off on his return to London dressed as a soldier. Fellows like that know the great commanders' names perfectly, and they'll recite to you where the actions were fought: at such and such a fort, at such a breach, at such a convoy; who came off bravely, who was shot, who was disgraced, what terms the enemy held out for; and they learn this perfectly in military jargon, which they dress up with newly fashioned oaths. And what a beard trimmed like the general's and a shabby, battle-worn uniform will do among frothing bottles and ale-soaked minds is amazing to think of. But you must learn to recognise these disgraces of our time, or you can be badly fooled."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["I tell you what, Captain Gower; I do perceive he is not the man","that he would gladly make show to the world he is. If I find a","hole in his coat, I will tell him my mind. [Drum heard] Hark","you, the King is coming, and I must speak with him from the pridge."],"id":354,"m":"I'll tell you what, Captain Gower; I can see he isn't the man he would like to show the world he is. If I find a hole in his coat, I'll tell him what I think. (Drum heard) Listen, the King is coming, and I must give him news from the bridge."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum and colours. Enter King Henry, Gloucester, and his poor soldiers.","id":355},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","cont":1,"lines":["God bless your Majesty!"],"id":356,"m":"God bless your Majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["How now, Fluellen! cam'st thou from the bridge?"],"id":357,"m":"Well, Fluellen! Have you come from the bridge?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Ay, so please your Majesty. The Duke of Exeter has very","gallantly maintain'd the pridge. The French is gone off, look","you; and there is gallant and most prave passages. Marry, th'","athversary was have possession of the pridge; but he is enforced","to retire, and the Duke of Exeter is master of the pridge. I can","tell your Majesty, the Duke is a prave man."],"id":358,"m":"Yes, if it please your Majesty. The Duke of Exeter has held the bridge very gallantly. The French have gone off, look you, and there have been gallant and most brave actions. Indeed, the enemy had taken the bridge, but he was forced to retreat, and the Duke of Exeter is master of the bridge. I can tell your Majesty, the Duke is a brave man."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What men have you lost, Fluellen?"],"id":359,"m":"How many men have you lost, Fluellen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["The perdition of the athversary hath been very great, reasonable","great. Marry, for my part, I think the Duke hath lost never a","man, but one that is like to be executed for robbing a church, one","Bardolph, if your Majesty know the man. His face is all bubukles,","and whelks, and knobs, and flames o' fire; and his lips blows at","his nose, and it is like a coal of fire, sometimes plue and","sometimes red; but his nose is executed, and his fire's out."],"id":360,"m":"The enemy's losses have been very great, reasonably great. Indeed, for my part, I think the Duke has not lost a single man, except one who is likely to be executed for robbing a church, one Bardolph, if your Majesty knows the man. His face is all boils and pimples and lumps and flames of fire; and his lips blow at his nose, and it's like a burning coal, sometimes blue and sometimes red; but his nose is executed, and his fire's out."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We would have all such offenders so cut off; and we give express","charge, that in our marches through the country, there be nothing","compell'd from the villages, nothing taken but paid for, none of","the French upbraided or abused in disdainful language; for when","lenity and cruelty play for a kingdom, the gentler gamester is the","soonest winner."],"id":361,"m":"We would have all such offenders cut off like that; and we give strict orders that on our marches through the country nothing is to be taken from the villages by force, nothing taken without being paid for, and none of the French insulted or abused with scornful words; for when gentleness and cruelty gamble for a kingdom, the gentler player wins soonest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Tucket. Enter Montjoy.","id":362},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["You know me by my habit."],"id":363,"m":"You know who I am by my uniform."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Well then I know thee. What shall I know of thee?"],"id":364,"m":"Well then, I know you. What shall I learn from you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["My master's mind."],"id":365,"m":"My master's mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Unfold it."],"id":366,"m":"Reveal it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["Thus says my King: Say thou to Harry of England: Though we","seem'd dead, we did but sleep; advantage is a better soldier","than rashness. Tell him we could have rebuk'd him at Harfleur,","but that we thought not good to bruise an injury till it were","full ripe. Now we speak upon our cue, and our voice is imperial.","England shall repent his folly, see his weakness, and admire our","sufferance. Bid him therefore consider of his ransom; which must","proportion the losses we have borne, the subjects we have lost,","the disgrace we have digested; which in weight to re-answer, his","pettishness would bow under. For our losses, his exchequer is too","poor; for the effusion of our blood, the muster of his kingdom","too faint a number; and for our disgrace, his own person, kneeling","at our feet, but a weak and worthless satisfaction. To this add","defiance; and tell him, for conclusion, he hath betrayed his","followers, whose condemnation is pronounc'd. So far my King and","master; so much my office."],"id":367,"m":"This is what my King says: Tell Harry of England that though we seemed dead, we were only asleep; waiting for the right moment is a better soldier than rashness. Tell him we could have punished him at Harfleur, but we thought it best not to lance an injury until it was fully ripe. Now we speak on our cue, and our voice is royal. England shall regret his folly, see his weakness and wonder at our patience. So tell him to think about his ransom, which must match the losses we have suffered, the subjects we have lost, the disgrace we have swallowed; to repay that in full, his small power would collapse. For our losses his treasury is too poor; for the blood we have shed, the whole muster of his kingdom is too small a number; and for our disgrace, his own person kneeling at our feet would be only a weak and worthless compensation. Add defiance to this, and tell him finally that he has betrayed his followers, whose doom is already pronounced. So far my King and master; that is my duty done."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What is thy name? I know thy quality."],"id":368,"m":"What is your name? I know your rank."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["Montjoy."],"id":369,"m":"Montjoy."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Thou dost thy office fairly. Turn thee back,","And tell thy King I do not seek him now,","But could be willing to march on to Calais","Without impeachment; for, to say the sooth,","Though 'tis no wisdom to confess so much","Unto an enemy of craft and vantage,","My people are with sickness much enfeebled,","My numbers lessen'd, and those few I have","Almost no better than so many French;","Who when they were in health, I tell thee, herald,","I thought upon one pair of English legs","Did march three Frenchmen. Yet, forgive me, God,","That I do brag thus! This your air of France","Hath blown that vice in me. I must repent.","Go therefore, tell thy master here I am;","My ransom is this frail and worthless trunk,","My army but a weak and sickly guard;","Yet, God before, tell him we will come on,","Though France himself and such another neighbour","Stand in our way. There's for thy labour, Montjoy.","Go, bid thy master well advise himself.","If we may pass, we will; if we be hind'red,","We shall your tawny ground with your red blood","Discolour; and so, Montjoy, fare you well.","The sum of all our answer is but this:","We would not seek a battle, as we are;","Nor, as we are, we say we will not shun it.","So tell your master."],"id":370,"m":"You do your job well. Go back and tell your King I am not looking for him now, but would be happy to march on to Calais without being stopped; for, to tell the truth, though it isn't wise to admit so much to a cunning enemy with the advantage, my men are badly weakened by sickness, my numbers reduced, and the few I have are hardly better than so many Frenchmen; though when they were healthy, I tell you, herald, I thought one pair of English legs could march as well as three Frenchmen. Yet, God forgive me for boasting like this! This French air of yours has blown that vice into me. I must repent. So go and tell your master here I am. My ransom is this frail and worthless body, my army only a weak and sickly guard. Yet, with God leading, tell him we will come on, even if France himself and another neighbour like him stand in our way. There's something for your trouble, Montjoy. Go, tell your master to think carefully. If we may pass, we will; if we are stopped, we will stain your brown earth with your red blood. And so, Montjoy, farewell. Our whole answer comes to this: as we are, we would not seek a battle; nor, as we are, do we say we will avoid one. Tell your master that."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["I shall deliver so. Thanks to your Highness."],"id":371,"m":"I'll deliver it so. Thank you, your Highness."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":372},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["I hope they will not come upon us now."],"id":373,"m":"I hope they won't attack us now."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["We are in God's hands, brother, not in theirs.","March to the bridge; it now draws toward night.","Beyond the river we'll encamp ourselves,","And on to-morrow bid them march away."],"id":374,"m":"We are in God's hands, brother, not in theirs. March to the bridge; night is coming on. We'll camp beyond the river, and tomorrow we'll order them to march on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":375},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"VII","loc":"The French camp, near Agincourt.","id":376},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Constable of France, the Lord Rambures, Orleans, Dauphin, with others.","id":377},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Tut! I have the best armour of the world.","Would it were day!"],"id":378,"m":"Nonsense! I have the best armour in the world. I wish it were day!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["You have an excellent armour; but let my horse have his due."],"id":379,"m":"You have excellent armour; but give my horse his due."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["It is the best horse of Europe."],"id":380,"m":"It's the best horse in Europe."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["Will it never be morning?"],"id":381,"m":"Will it never be morning?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["My Lord of Orleans, and my Lord High Constable, you talk of","horse and armour?"],"id":382,"m":"My Lord of Orleans and my Lord High Constable, are you talking about horses and armour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["You are as well provided of both as any prince in the world."],"id":383,"m":"You are as well supplied with both as any prince in the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["What a long night is this! I will not change my horse with","any that treads but on four pasterns. Ca, ha! he bounds from the","earth, as if his entrails were hairs; le cheval volant, the","Pegasus, chez les narines de feu! When I bestride him, I soar, I","am a hawk. he trots the air; the earth sings when he touches it;","the basest horn of his hoof is more musical than the pipe of Hermes."],"id":384,"m":"What a long night this is! I wouldn't swap my horse for any animal that walks on four hooves. Ha! He leaps off the ground as if he were stuffed with hair, like a tennis ball; the flying horse, Pegasus, with nostrils of fire! When I ride him, I soar, I'm a hawk; he trots on the air; the earth sings when he touches it; the lowest horn of his hoof is more musical than the pipe of Hermes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["He's of the colour of the nutmeg."],"id":385,"m":"He's the colour of nutmeg."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["And of the heat of the ginger. It is a beast for Perseus. He is","pure air and fire; and the dull elements of earth and water never","appear in him, but only in patient stillness while his rider mounts","him. He is indeed a horse, and all other jades you may call beasts."],"id":386,"m":"And as hot as ginger. He's a beast fit for Perseus. He's pure air and fire; the dull elements of earth and water never show in him, except in his patient stillness while his rider mounts. He is truly a horse, and you may call all other nags mere beasts."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Indeed, my lord, it is a most absolute and excellent horse."],"id":387,"m":"Indeed, my lord, it's a perfect and excellent horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["It is the prince of palfreys; his neigh is like the bidding of a","monarch, and his countenance enforces homage."],"id":388,"m":"He's the prince of riding horses; his neigh is like a king's command, and his face compels respect."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["No more, cousin."],"id":389,"m":"No more, cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Nay, the man hath no wit that cannot, from the rising of the","lark to the lodging of the lamb, vary deserved praise on my","palfrey. It is a theme as fluent as the sea; turn the sands into","eloquent tongues, and my horse is argument for them all. 'Tis","a subject for a sovereign to reason on, and for a sovereign's","sovereign to ride on; and for the world, familiar to us and","unknown, to lay apart their particular functions and wonder at","him. I once writ a sonnet in his praise and began thus: \"Wonder","of nature,\"--"],"id":390,"m":"No, the man has no wit who can't, from the lark's rising to the lamb's bedtime, vary his well-deserved praise of my horse. It's a theme as flowing as the sea; turn the grains of sand into eloquent tongues, and my horse gives them all a subject. He's a subject for a king to talk about, and for a king's king to ride; and for the whole world, known and unknown, to put aside their own business and wonder at him. I once wrote a sonnet in his praise that began, \"Wonder of nature\","},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["I have heard a sonnet begin so to one's mistress."],"id":391,"m":"I've heard a sonnet to someone's mistress begin like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Then did they imitate that which I compos'd to my courser,","for my horse is my mistress."],"id":392,"m":"Then they copied the one I wrote to my horse, for my horse is my mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["Your mistress bears well."],"id":393,"m":"Your mistress carries well."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Me well; which is the prescript praise and perfection of a","good and particular mistress."],"id":394,"m":"Carries me well; which is the proper praise and perfection of a good mistress who belongs to one man."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Nay, for methought yesterday your mistress shrewdly shook","your back."],"id":395,"m":"No, because I thought yesterday your mistress shook your back badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["So perhaps did yours."],"id":396,"m":"So perhaps did yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Mine was not bridled."],"id":397,"m":"Mine wasn't bridled."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["O then belike she was old and gentle; and you rode, like a","kern of Ireland, your French hose off, and in your strait","strossers."],"id":398,"m":"Oh, then perhaps she was old and gentle, and you rode like an Irish foot-soldier, with your French breeches off and in your tight trousers."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["You have good judgment in horsemanship."],"id":399,"m":"You know a lot about riding."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Be warn'd by me, then; they that ride so and ride not warily,","fall into foul bogs. I had rather have my horse to my mistress."],"id":400,"m":"Then take a warning from me: those who ride like that and don't take care fall into foul bogs. I'd rather have my horse as my mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["I had as lief have my mistress a jade."],"id":401,"m":"I'd just as soon have my mistress be a nag."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["I tell thee, Constable, my mistress wears his own hair."],"id":402,"m":"I tell you, Constable, my mistress wears her own hair."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["I could make as true a boast as that, if I had a sow to","my mistress."],"id":403,"m":"I could make as true a boast as that if I had a sow as my mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["\"Le chien est retourne a son propre vomissement, et la","truie lavee au bourbier.\" Thou mak'st use of anything."],"id":404,"m":"\"The dog has returned to its own vomit, and the washed sow to the mud.\" You make use of anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Yet do I not use my horse for my mistress, or any such","proverb so little kin to the purpose."],"id":405,"m":"Yet I don't use my horse as my mistress, or any proverb so unrelated to the point."},{"t":"sp","who":"RAMBURES","lines":["My Lord Constable, the armour that I saw in your tent","to-night, are those stars or suns upon it?"],"id":406,"m":"My Lord Constable, the armour I saw in your tent tonight, are those stars or suns on it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Stars, my lord."],"id":407,"m":"Stars, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Some of them will fall to-morrow, I hope."],"id":408,"m":"Some of them will fall tomorrow, I hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["And yet my sky shall not want."],"id":409,"m":"And still my sky won't be short of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["That may be, for you bear a many superfluously, and 'twere","more honour some were away."],"id":410,"m":"That may be, because you carry a lot more than you need, and it would be more honourable if some were gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Even as your horse bears your praises; who would trot as","well, were some of your brags dismounted."],"id":411,"m":"Just as your horse carries your praises; he would trot just as well if some of your boasts were dismounted."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Would I were able to load him with his desert! Will it never","be day? I will trot to-morrow a mile, and my way shall be","paved with English faces."],"id":412,"m":"I wish I could load him with all he deserves! Will it never be day? Tomorrow I'll trot a mile, and my way will be paved with English faces."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["I will not say so, for fear I should be fac'd out of my way.","But I would it were morning; for I would fain be about","the ears of the English."],"id":413,"m":"I won't say that, in case I'm faced down and driven off my way. But I wish it were morning, because I long to be at the English."},{"t":"sp","who":"RAMBURES","lines":["Who will go to hazard with me for twenty prisoners?"],"id":414,"m":"Who'll gamble with me for twenty prisoners?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["You must first go yourself to hazard, ere you have them."],"id":415,"m":"You must first risk yourself before you get them."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["'Tis midnight; I'll go arm myself."],"id":416,"m":"It's midnight; I'll go and put on my armour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":417},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["The Dauphin longs for morning."],"id":418,"m":"The Dauphin longs for morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"RAMBURES","lines":["He longs to eat the English."],"id":419,"m":"He longs to eat the English."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["I think he will eat all he kills."],"id":420,"m":"I think he'll eat all he kills."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["By the white hand of my lady, he's a gallant prince."],"id":421,"m":"By the white hand of my lady, he's a gallant prince."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Swear by her foot that she may tread out the oath."],"id":422,"m":"Swear by her foot, so that she can stamp out the oath."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["He is simply the most active gentleman of France."],"id":423,"m":"He is simply the most active gentleman in France."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Doing is activity; and he will still be doing."],"id":424,"m":"Doing is activity, and he'll always be doing something."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["He never did harm, that I heard of."],"id":425,"m":"He never did any harm that I've heard of."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Nor will do none to-morrow. He will keep that good","name still."],"id":426,"m":"And he won't do any tomorrow. He'll keep that good name."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["I know him to be valiant."],"id":427,"m":"I know he's brave."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["I was told that by one that knows him better than you."],"id":428,"m":"I was told that by someone who knows him better than you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["What's he?"],"id":429,"m":"Who's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Marry, he told me so himself; and he said he car'd not","who knew it."],"id":430,"m":"Why, he told me so himself; and he said he didn't care who knew it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["He needs not; it is no hidden virtue in him."],"id":431,"m":"He doesn't need to; it's no hidden virtue in him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["By my faith, sir, but it is; never anybody saw it but his","lackey. 'Tis a hooded valour; and when it appears, it will","bate."],"id":432,"m":"By my faith, sir, it is; nobody ever saw it except his servant. It's a hooded courage, like a hawk's; and when the hood comes off, it will flutter away."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["\"Ill will never said well.\""],"id":433,"m":"\"Ill will never spoke well.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["I will cap that proverb with \"There is flattery in friendship.\""],"id":434,"m":"I'll top that proverb with \"There is flattery in friendship.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["And I will take up that with \"Give the devil his due.\""],"id":435,"m":"And I'll answer that with \"Give the devil his due.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Well plac'd. There stands your friend for the devil; have at","the very eye of that proverb with \"A pox of the devil.\""],"id":436,"m":"Well placed: there stands your friend as the devil. I'll hit the bull's-eye of that proverb with \"A plague on the devil.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["You are the better at proverbs, by how much \"A fool's","bolt is soon shot.\""],"id":437,"m":"You are better at proverbs to the extent that \"A fool's arrow is soon shot.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["You have shot over."],"id":438,"m":"You've shot over the target."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["'Tis not the first time you were overshot."],"id":439,"m":"It's not the first time you've been shot past."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":440},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My Lord High Constable, the English lie within fifteen","hundred paces of your tents."],"id":441,"m":"My Lord High Constable, the English are camped within fifteen hundred paces of your tents."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Who hath measur'd the ground?"],"id":442,"m":"Who has measured the ground?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The Lord Grandpre."],"id":443,"m":"Lord Grandpre."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["A valiant and most expert gentleman. Would it were day!","Alas, poor Harry of England, he longs not for the dawning as","we do."],"id":444,"m":"A brave and very expert gentleman. I wish it were day! Alas, poor Harry of England, he isn't longing for the dawn as we are."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["What a wretched and peevish fellow is this King of England,","to mope with his fat-brain'd followers so far out of his","knowledge!"],"id":445,"m":"What a wretched and foolish fellow this King of England is, to wander gloomily with his thick-headed followers so far out of his depth!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["If the English had any apprehension, they would run away."],"id":446,"m":"If the English had any sense, they would run away."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["That they lack; for if their heads had any intellectual armour,","they could never wear such heavy head-pieces."],"id":447,"m":"That's what they lack; for if their heads had any intellectual armour, they could never wear such heavy helmets."},{"t":"sp","who":"RAMBURES","lines":["That island of England breeds very valiant creatures. Their","mastiffs are of unmatchable courage."],"id":448,"m":"That island of England breeds very brave creatures. Their mastiffs are of unmatched courage."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["Foolish curs, that run winking into the mouth of a Russian bear","and have their heads crush'd like rotten apples! You may as well","say, that's a valiant flea that dare eat his breakfast on the lip","of a lion."],"id":449,"m":"Foolish curs that run with their eyes shut into the mouth of a Russian bear and have their heads crushed like rotten apples! You might as well say that's a brave flea that dares to eat its breakfast on a lion's lip."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Just, just; and the men do sympathize with the mastiffs in","robustious and rough coming on, leaving their wits with their wives;","and then, give them great meals of beef and iron and steel, they","will eat like wolves and fight like devils."],"id":450,"m":"Exactly, exactly; and the men are like the mastiffs in charging roughly and heavily, leaving their brains with their wives; and then, give them great meals of beef and iron and steel, and they'll eat like wolves and fight like devils."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["Ay, but these English are shrewdly out of beef."],"id":451,"m":"Yes, but these English are badly short of beef."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Then shall we find to-morrow they have only stomachs to","eat and none to fight. Now is it time to arm. Come, shall we","about it?"],"id":452,"m":"Then we'll find tomorrow that they only have the stomach to eat and none to fight. Now it's time to arm. Come, shall we get on with it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["It is now two o'clock; but, let me see, by ten","We shall have each a hundred Englishmen."],"id":453,"m":"It is now two o'clock; but, let me see, by ten we'll each have a hundred Englishmen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":454},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"The English camp at Agincourt.","id":455},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Chorus.","id":456},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","lines":["Now entertain conjecture of a time","When creeping murmur and the poring dark","Fills the wide vessel of the universe.","From camp to camp through the foul womb of night","The hum of either army stilly sounds,","That the fix'd sentinels almost receive","The secret whispers of each other's watch;","Fire answers fire, and through their paly flames","Each battle sees the other's umber'd face;","Steed threatens steed, in high and boastful neighs","Piercing the night's dull ear; and from the tents","The armourers, accomplishing the knights,","With busy hammers closing rivets up,","Give dreadful note of preparation.","The country cocks do crow, the clocks do toll,","And the third hour of drowsy morning name.","Proud of their numbers and secure in soul,","The confident and over-lusty French","Do the low-rated English play at dice;","And chide the cripple tardy-gaited Night","Who, like a foul and ugly witch, doth limp","So tediously away. The poor condemned English,","Like sacrifices, by their watchful fires","Sit patiently and inly ruminate","The morning's danger; and their gesture sad,","Investing lank-lean cheeks and war-worn coats,","Presented them unto the gazing moon","So many horrid ghosts. O now, who will behold","The royal captain of this ruin'd band","Walking from watch to watch, from tent to tent,","Let him cry, \"Praise and glory on his head!\"","For forth he goes and visits all his host,","Bids them good morrow with a modest smile,","And calls them brothers, friends, and countrymen.","Upon his royal face there is no note","How dread an army hath enrounded him;","Nor doth he dedicate one jot of colour","Unto the weary and all-watched night,","But freshly looks, and over-bears attaint","With cheerful semblance and sweet majesty;","That every wretch, pining and pale before,","Beholding him, plucks comfort from his looks.","A largess universal like the sun","His liberal eye doth give to every one,","Thawing cold fear, that mean and gentle all","Behold, as may unworthiness define,","A little touch of Harry in the night.","And so our scene must to the battle fly,","Where--O for pity!--we shall much disgrace","With four or five most vile and ragged foils,","Right ill-dispos'd in brawl ridiculous,","The name of Agincourt. Yet sit and see,","Minding true things by what their mock'ries be."],"id":457,"m":"Now imagine a time when creeping murmurs and the straining dark fill the wide bowl of the universe. From camp to camp, through the foul womb of night, the hum of each army sounds quietly, so that the posted sentries can almost hear the secret whispers of each other's watch. Fire answers fire, and through their pale flames each army sees the other's shadowed faces. Horse threatens horse with high, boastful neighs piercing the dull ear of night; and from the tents the armourers, fitting out the knights, closing up rivets with busy hammers, give a dreadful sound of preparation. The country cocks crow, the clocks strike and announce three o'clock in the drowsy morning. Proud of their numbers and confident in spirit, the cocky, over-eager French gamble at dice for the low-valued English, and scold the crippled, slow-footed Night, who limps away so tediously like a foul, ugly witch. The poor condemned English sit patiently like sacrifices by their watch-fires and brood inwardly on the morning's danger; and their sad bearing, along with their thin, hollow cheeks and war-worn coats, make them look to the staring moon like so many horrible ghosts. Oh, now whoever sees the royal captain of this ruined band walking from watch to watch, from tent to tent, let him cry \"Praise and glory on his head!\" For out he goes and visits all his army, wishes them good morning with a modest smile, and calls them brothers, friends and countrymen. On his royal face there is no sign of what a terrible army surrounds him, nor does he give up one bit of colour to the weary, sleepless night; he looks fresh and overcomes exhaustion with a cheerful look and sweet majesty, so that every wretch, pining and pale before, takes comfort from his looks when he sees him. Like the sun, his generous eye gives a gift to everyone, thawing cold fear, so that high and low alike see, as far as our poor skill can show it, a little touch of Harry in the night. And so our scene must fly to the battle, where, oh, for pity's sake, we will badly disgrace the name of Agincourt with four or five vile, ragged swords, clumsily handled in a ridiculous brawl. Still, sit and watch, imagining the real things from our imitations of them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":458},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King Henry, Bedford, and Gloucester.","id":459},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Gloucester, 'tis true that we are in great danger;","The greater therefore should our courage be.","Good morrow, brother Bedford. God Almighty!","There is some soul of goodness in things evil,","Would men observingly distil it out;","For our bad neighbour makes us early stirrers,","Which is both healthful and good husbandry.","Besides, they are our outward consciences,","And preachers to us all, admonishing","That we should dress us fairly for our end.","Thus may we gather honey from the weed,","And make a moral of the devil himself."],"id":460,"m":"Gloucester, it's true that we are in great danger, so our courage must be all the greater. Good morning, brother Bedford. God Almighty! There is some spirit of good in bad things, if men would carefully distil it out. For our bad neighbour makes us get up early, which is both healthy and good management. Besides, they are like our consciences outside us, and preachers to us all, warning us to prepare ourselves well for our end. In this way we can gather honey from the weed and draw a moral lesson from the devil himself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Erpingham.","id":461},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Good morrow, old Sir Thomas Erpingham:","A good soft pillow for that good white head","Were better than a churlish turf of France."],"id":462,"m":"Good morning, old Sir Thomas Erpingham. A good soft pillow for that good white head would be better than a rough clod of French earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"ERPINGHAM","lines":["Not so, my liege; this lodging likes me better,","Since I may say, \"Now lie I like a king.\""],"id":463,"m":"Not so, my lord; I like this bed better, since I can say, \"Now I'm lying like a king.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["'Tis good for men to love their present pains","Upon example; so the spirit is eased;","And when the mind is quick'ned, out of doubt,","The organs, though defunct and dead before,","Break up their drowsy grave and newly move,","With casted slough and fresh legerity.","Lend me thy cloak, Sir Thomas. Brothers both,","Commend me to the princes in our camp;","Do my good morrow to them, and anon","Desire them all to my pavilion."],"id":464,"m":"It's good for men to love their present hardships by following an example like that; it eases the spirit. And when the mind is quickened, no doubt the body's organs, though dead and sluggish before, break out of their drowsy grave and move again, like a snake that has shed its skin, with fresh nimbleness. Lend me your cloak, Sir Thomas. Brothers both, give my greetings to the princes in our camp; wish them good morning from me, and ask them all to come to my tent soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["We shall, my liege."],"id":465,"m":"We shall, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ERPINGHAM","lines":["Shall I attend your Grace?"],"id":466,"m":"Shall I go with your Grace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["No, my good knight;","Go with my brothers to my lords of England.","I and my bosom must debate a while,","And then I would no other company."],"id":467,"m":"No, my good knight; go with my brothers to my lords of England. My heart and I must debate a while, and then I want no other company."},{"t":"sp","who":"ERPINGHAM","lines":["The Lord in heaven bless thee, noble Harry!"],"id":468,"m":"The Lord in heaven bless you, noble Harry!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but King.","id":469},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["God-a-mercy, old heart! thou speak'st cheerfully."],"id":470,"m":"Thank you, old heart! You speak cheerfully."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pistol.","id":471},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Qui va la?"],"id":472,"m":"(In French) Who goes there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["A friend."],"id":473,"m":"A friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Discuss unto me; art thou officer?","Or art thou base, common, and popular?"],"id":474,"m":"Explain yourself to me: are you an officer, or are you low, common and of the people?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I am a gentleman of a company."],"id":475,"m":"I am a gentleman serving in a company."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Trail'st thou the puissant pike?"],"id":476,"m":"Do you trail the mighty pike?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Even so. What are you?"],"id":477,"m":"Exactly. What are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["As good a gentleman as the Emperor."],"id":478,"m":"As good a gentleman as the Emperor."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Then you are a better than the King."],"id":479,"m":"Then you're better than the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["The King's a bawcock, and a heart of gold,","A lad of life, an imp of fame;","Of parents good, of fist most valiant.","I kiss his dirty shoe, and from heart-string","I love the lovely bully. What is thy name?"],"id":480,"m":"The King's a fine fellow and a heart of gold, a lively lad, a child of fame, of good parents, with a most brave fist. I kiss his dirty shoe, and with all my heart I love the lovely fellow. What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Harry le Roy."],"id":481,"m":"Harry le Roy."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Le Roy! a Cornish name. Art thou of Cornish crew?"],"id":482,"m":"Le Roy! A Cornish name. Are you one of the Cornish lot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["No, I am a Welshman."],"id":483,"m":"No, I'm a Welshman."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Know'st thou Fluellen?"],"id":484,"m":"Do you know Fluellen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Yes."],"id":485,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Tell him I'll knock his leek about his pate","Upon Saint Davy's day."],"id":486,"m":"Tell him I'll knock his leek about his head on Saint David's Day."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Do not you wear your dagger in your cap that day, lest","he knock that about yours."],"id":487,"m":"Don't wear your dagger in your cap that day, or he'll knock that about yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Art thou his friend?"],"id":488,"m":"Are you his friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["And his kinsman too."],"id":489,"m":"And his relative too."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["The figo for thee, then!"],"id":490,"m":"Then a fig for you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I thank you. God be with you!"],"id":491,"m":"Thank you. God be with you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["My name is Pistol call'd."],"id":492,"m":"My name is Pistol."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":493},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["It sorts well with your fierceness."],"id":494,"m":"It suits your fierceness well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fluellen and Gower.","id":495},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Captain Fluellen!"],"id":496,"m":"Captain Fluellen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["So! in the name of Jesu Christ, speak lower. It is the greatest","admiration in the universal world, when the true and aunchient","prerogatifes and laws of the wars is not kept. If you would take","the pains but to examine the wars of Pompey the Great, you","shall find, I warrant you, that there is no tiddle taddle nor","pibble pabble in Pompey's camp. I warrant you, you shall find the","ceremonies of the wars, and the cares of it, and the forms of it,","and the sobriety of it, and the modesty of it, to be otherwise."],"id":497,"m":"Hush! In the name of Jesus Christ, speak lower. It is the greatest wonder in the whole world when the true and ancient privileges and laws of war are not kept. If you would only take the trouble to examine the wars of Pompey the Great, you will find, I promise you, that there's no tittle-tattle or babble in Pompey's camp. I promise you, you will find the ceremonies of the wars, and the cares of them, and the forms of them, and the seriousness of them, and the modesty of them, to be different."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Why, the enemy is loud; you hear him all night."],"id":498,"m":"Why, the enemy is loud; you can hear him all night."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["If the enemy is an ass and a fool and a prating coxcomb, is it","meet, think you, that we should also, look you, be an ass and a","fool and a prating coxcomb? In your own conscience, now?"],"id":499,"m":"If the enemy is an ass and a fool and a chattering idiot, is it right, do you think, that we should also, look you, be an ass and a fool and a chattering idiot? In your own conscience, now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["I will speak lower."],"id":500,"m":"I'll speak lower."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["I pray you and beseech you that you will."],"id":501,"m":"I beg and beseech you to."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gower and Fluellen.","id":502},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Though it appear a little out of fashion,","There is much care and valour in this Welshman."],"id":503,"m":"Though it seems a little old-fashioned, there is a lot of care and courage in this Welshman."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter three soldiers, John Bates, Alexander Court, and Michael Williams.","id":504},{"t":"sp","who":"COURT","lines":["Brother John Bates, is not that the morning which breaks","yonder?"],"id":505,"m":"Brother John Bates, isn't that the morning breaking over there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BATES","lines":["I think it be; but we have no great cause to desire the","approach of day."],"id":506,"m":"I think so; but we haven't much reason to want the day to come."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["We see yonder the beginning of the day, but I think","we shall never see the end of it. Who goes there?"],"id":507,"m":"We see the beginning of the day over there, but I don't think we'll ever see the end of it. Who goes there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["A friend."],"id":508,"m":"A friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Under what captain serve you?"],"id":509,"m":"Which captain do you serve under?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Under Sir Thomas Erpingham."],"id":510,"m":"Sir Thomas Erpingham."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["A good old commander and a most kind gentleman. I","pray you, what thinks he of our estate?"],"id":511,"m":"A good old commander and a very kind gentleman. Tell me, what does he think of our situation?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Even as men wreck'd upon a sand, that look to be","wash'd off the next tide."],"id":512,"m":"Just like men shipwrecked on a sandbank, who expect to be washed off by the next tide."},{"t":"sp","who":"BATES","lines":["He hath not told his thought to the King?"],"id":513,"m":"He hasn't told the King what he thinks?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["No; nor it is not meet he should. For though I speak it to you,","I think the King is but a man as I am. The violet smells to him","as it doth to me; the element shows to him as it doth to me; all","his senses have but human conditions. His ceremonies laid by,","in his nakedness he appears but a man; and though his affections","are higher mounted than ours, yet, when they stoop, they stoop","with the like wing. Therefore, when he sees reason of fears as we","do, his fears, out of doubt, be of the same relish as ours are;","yet, in reason, no man should possess him with any appearance of","fear, lest he, by showing it, should dishearten his army."],"id":514,"m":"No, and it isn't right that he should. For, though I say it to you, I think the King is only a man, like me. The violet smells the same to him as to me; the sky looks the same to him as to me; all his senses work only as human senses do. With his ceremonies put aside, naked he appears only a man; and though his feelings fly higher than ours, when they swoop down, they swoop on the same kind of wing. So when he sees reason to be afraid, as we do, his fears no doubt taste the same as ours; yet, reasonably, no one should give him any sign of fear, in case he shows it and disheartens his army."},{"t":"sp","who":"BATES","lines":["He may show what outward courage he will; but I believe, as","cold a night as 'tis, he could wish himself in Thames up to the","neck; and so I would he were, and I by him, at all adventures, so","we were quit here."],"id":515,"m":"He can put on whatever outward courage he likes; but I believe that, cold as the night is, he would wish himself in the Thames up to his neck; and I wish he were, and I beside him, whatever the risk, so long as we were out of here."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["By my troth, I will speak my conscience of the King: I think he","would not wish himself anywhere but where he is."],"id":516,"m":"Honestly, I'll tell you what I really think of the King: I think he wouldn't want to be anywhere but where he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"BATES","lines":["Then I would he were here alone; so should he be sure to be","ransomed, and a many poor men's lives saved."],"id":517,"m":"Then I wish he were here alone; then he'd be sure to be ransomed, and many poor men's lives would be saved."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I dare say you love him not so ill, to wish him here alone,","howsoever you speak this to feel other men's minds. Methinks","I could not die anywhere so contented as in the King's company,","his cause being just and his quarrel honourable."],"id":518,"m":"I dare say you don't dislike him so much as to wish him here alone, however you talk to test other men's minds. I think I could not die anywhere so contented as in the King's company, his cause being just and his quarrel honourable."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["That's more than we know."],"id":519,"m":"That's more than we know."},{"t":"sp","who":"BATES","lines":["Ay, or more than we should seek after; for we know enough, if","we know we are the King's subjects. If his cause be wrong, our","obedience to the King wipes the crime of it out of us."],"id":520,"m":"Yes, or more than we should try to find out; for we know enough if we know we are the King's subjects. If his cause is wrong, our obedience to the King wipes the crime of it off us."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["But if the cause be not good, the King himself hath a heavy","reckoning to make, when all those legs and arms and heads, chopp'd","off in a battle, shall join together at the latter day and cry all,","\"We died at such a place\"; some swearing, some crying for a","surgeon, some upon their wives left poor behind them, some upon the","debts they owe, some upon their children rawly left. I am afeard","there are few die well that die in a battle; for how can they","charitably dispose of anything, when blood is their argument?","Now, if these men do not die well, it will be a black matter","for the King that led them to it; who to disobey were against","all proportion of subjection."],"id":521,"m":"But if the cause isn't good, the King himself has a heavy account to settle, when all those legs and arms and heads chopped off in a battle join together on Judgement Day and all cry out, \"We died at such a place\": some swearing, some crying for a surgeon, some for the wives they left poor behind them, some for the debts they owe, some for their children left unprovided for. I'm afraid few men die well who die in a battle; for how can they settle anything in a Christian spirit when blood is their business? Now, if these men don't die well, it will be a black matter for the King who led them to it, since to disobey him would be against all the duty of a subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["So, if a son that is by his father sent about merchandise do","sinfully miscarry upon the sea, the imputation of his wickedness,","by your rule, should be imposed upon his father that sent him; or","if a servant, under his master's command transporting a sum of","money, be assailed by robbers and die in many irreconcil'd","iniquities, you may call the business of the master the author of","the servant's damnation. But this is not so. The King is not","bound to answer the particular endings of his soldiers, the father","of his son, nor the master of his servant; for they purpose not","their death, when they purpose their services. Besides, there is","no king, be his cause never so spotless, if it come to the","arbitrement of swords, can try it out with all unspotted soldiers.","Some peradventure have on them the guilt of premeditated and","contrived murder; some, of beguiling virgins with the broken seals","of perjury; some, making the wars their bulwark, that have before","gored the gentle bosom of Peace with pillage and robbery. Now, if","these men have defeated the law and outrun native punishment,","though they can outstrip men, they have no wings to fly from God.","War is his beadle, war is his vengeance; so that here men are","punish'd for before-breach of the King's laws in now the King's","quarrel. Where they feared the death, they have borne life away;","and where they would be safe, they perish. Then if they die","unprovided, no more is the King guilty of their damnation than he","was before guilty of those impieties for the which they are now","visited. Every subject's duty is the King's; but every subject's","soul is his own. Therefore should every soldier in the wars do as","every sick man in his bed, wash every mote out of his conscience;","and dying so, death is to him advantage; or not dying, the time was","blessedly lost wherein such preparation was gained; and in him that","escapes, it were not sin to think that, making God so free an","offer, He let him outlive that day to see His greatness and to","teach others how they should prepare."],"id":522,"m":"So, if a son who is sent by his father on a trading voyage dies at sea in a state of sin, by your rule the blame for his wickedness should be laid on the father who sent him; or if a servant carrying a sum of money under his master's orders is attacked by robbers and dies with many unconfessed sins, you could call the master's business the cause of the servant's damnation. But that is not so. The King is not bound to answer for the particular ends of his soldiers, nor the father of his son, nor the master of his servant; for they don't intend their deaths when they ask for their services. Besides, there is no king, however spotless his cause, who, if it comes to settlement by the sword, can fight it out with soldiers who are all spotless. Some perhaps carry the guilt of planned and plotted murder; some of seducing virgins with broken promises; some make the wars their shelter, having earlier wounded the gentle breast of Peace with looting and robbery. Now if these men have cheated the law and escaped punishment at home, though they can outrun men, they have no wings to fly from God. War is his officer, war is his vengeance; so that here men are punished for earlier breaches of the King's laws in what is now the King's quarrel. Where they feared death, they escaped with their lives; and where they think they are safe, they die. So if they die unprepared, the King is no more guilty of their damnation than he was guilty before of the sins for which they are now punished. Every subject's duty belongs to the King, but every subject's soul is his own. So every soldier in the wars should do what every sick man in his bed does: wash every speck out of his conscience. Dying like that, death is a gain to him; or if he doesn't die, the time was blessedly spent in which such readiness was gained; and of the man who escapes, it would be no sin to think that, since he made God so free an offer, God let him outlive that day to see His greatness and to teach others how to prepare themselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["'Tis certain, every man that dies ill, the ill upon his own head,","the King is not to answer for it."],"id":523,"m":"It's certain that for every man who dies in sin the sin is on his own head; the King doesn't have to answer for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BATES","lines":["I do not desire he should answer for me; and yet I determine to","fight lustily for him."],"id":524,"m":"I don't want him to answer for me; and yet I'm determined to fight hard for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I myself heard the King say he would not be ransom'd."],"id":525,"m":"I heard the King say myself that he would not be ransomed."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Ay, he said so, to make us fight cheerfully; but when our","throats are cut, he may be ransom'd, and we ne'er the wiser."],"id":526,"m":"Yes, he said so to make us fight cheerfully; but when our throats are cut, he may be ransomed, and we'd be none the wiser."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["If I live to see it, I will never trust his word after."],"id":527,"m":"If I live to see it, I'll never trust his word again."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["You pay him then. That's a perilous shot out of an elder-gun,","that a poor and a private displeasure can do against a monarch!","You may as well go about to turn the sun to ice with fanning in","his face with a peacock's feather. You'll never trust his word","after! Come, 'tis a foolish saying."],"id":528,"m":"So you'll pay him back! That's a dangerous shot from a pop-gun, what one poor private man's displeasure can do to a king! You might as well try to turn the sun to ice by fanning its face with a peacock's feather. You'll never trust his word again! Come on, that's a foolish thing to say."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Your reproof is something too round. I should be angry with","you, if the time were convenient."],"id":529,"m":"Your criticism is a bit too blunt. I would be angry with you if this were a suitable time."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Let it be a quarrel between us if you live."],"id":530,"m":"Let it be a quarrel between us, if you live."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I embrace it."],"id":531,"m":"I accept."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["How shall I know thee again?"],"id":532,"m":"How will I know you again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Give me any gage of thine, and I will wear it in my bonnet;","then, if ever thou dar'st acknowledge it, I will make it my","quarrel."],"id":533,"m":"Give me some token of yours, and I'll wear it in my cap; then, if you ever dare to acknowledge it, I'll make it my quarrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Here's my glove; give me another of thine."],"id":534,"m":"Here's my glove; give me one of yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["There."],"id":535,"m":"There."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["This will I also wear in my cap. If ever thou come to me","and say, after to-morrow, \"This is my glove,\" by this hand I","will take thee a box on the ear."],"id":536,"m":"I'll wear this in my cap too. If you ever come to me after tomorrow and say \"This is my glove\", by this hand I'll give you a box on the ear."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["If ever I live to see it, I will challenge it."],"id":537,"m":"If I live to see it, I'll challenge you for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Thou dar'st as well be hang'd."],"id":538,"m":"You'd as soon dare to be hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Well, I will do it, though I take thee in the King's company."],"id":539,"m":"Well, I'll do it, even if I find you in the King's company."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Keep thy word; fare thee well."],"id":540,"m":"Keep your word; farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"BATES","lines":["Be friends, you English fools, be friends. We have","French quarrels enow, if you could tell how to reckon."],"id":541,"m":"Be friends, you English fools, be friends. We have enough quarrels with the French, if you could count them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt soldiers.","id":542},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Indeed, the French may lay twenty French crowns to one","they will beat us, for they bear them on their shoulders; but it","is no English treason to cut French crowns, and to-morrow the","King himself will be a clipper.","Upon the King! Let us our lives, our souls,","Our debts, our careful wives,","Our children, and our sins lay on the King!","We must bear all. O hard condition,","Twin-born with greatness, subject to the breath","Of every fool, whose sense no more can feel","But his own wringing! What infinite heart's-ease","Must kings neglect, that private men enjoy!","And what have kings, that privates have not too,","Save ceremony, save general ceremony?","And what art thou, thou idol Ceremony?","What kind of god art thou, that suffer'st more","Of mortal griefs than do thy worshippers?","What are thy rents? What are thy comings in?","O Ceremony, show me but thy worth!","What is thy soul of adoration?","Art thou aught else but place, degree, and form,","Creating awe and fear in other men?","Wherein thou art less happy being fear'd","Than they in fearing.","What drink'st thou oft, instead of homage sweet,","But poison'd flattery? O, be sick, great greatness,","And bid thy Ceremony give thee cure!","Think'st thou the fiery fever will go out","With titles blown from adulation?","Will it give place to flexure and low bending?","Canst thou, when thou command'st the beggar's knee,","Command the health of it? No, thou proud dream,","That play'st so subtly with a king's repose;","I am a king that find thee, and I know","'Tis not the balm, the sceptre, and the ball,","The sword, the mace, the crown imperial,","The intertissued robe of gold and pearl,","The farced title running 'fore the King,","The throne he sits on, nor the tide of pomp","That beats upon the high shore of this world,","No, not all these, thrice-gorgeous Ceremony,--","Not all these, laid in bed majestical,","Can sleep so soundly as the wretched slave,","Who with a body fill'd and vacant mind","Gets him to rest, cramm'd with distressful bread,","Never sees horrid night, the child of hell,","But, like a lackey, from the rise to set","Sweats in the eye of Phoebus, and all night","Sleeps in Elysium; next day after dawn,","Doth rise and help Hyperion to his horse,","And follows so the ever-running year,","With profitable labour, to his grave:","And, but for ceremony, such a wretch,","Winding up days with toil and nights with sleep,","Had the fore-hand and vantage of a king.","The slave, a member of the country's peace,","Enjoys it, but in gross brain little wots","What watch the King keeps to maintain the peace,","Whose hours the peasant best advantages."],"id":543,"m":"Indeed, the French can bet twenty French crowns to one that they'll beat us, since they carry them on their shoulders. But it's no treason in England to cut French crowns, and tomorrow the King himself will be a coin-clipper. Lay it all on the King! Let us lay our lives, our souls, our debts, our worried wives, our children and our sins on the King! We must carry everything. Oh, hard condition, born twin with greatness, at the mercy of the talk of every fool who can feel nothing but his own stomach-ache! What endless peace of mind must kings go without that private men enjoy! And what do kings have that private men do not have too, except ceremony, public ceremony? And what are you, you idol Ceremony? What kind of god are you, who suffers more human griefs than your worshippers do? What are your rents? What is your income? Oh Ceremony, just show me your worth! What is the essence of the worship you get? Are you anything but rank, status and outward form, creating awe and fear in other men? In which you, being feared, are less happy than they are in fearing. What do you often drink, instead of sweet respect, except poisoned flattery? Oh, be sick, great greatness, and tell your Ceremony to cure you! Do you think the burning fever will go out at titles puffed up by flattery? Will it give way to curtseys and low bows? When you command the beggar's knee, can you command its health? No, you proud dream that plays so cunningly with a king's rest. I am a king who sees through you, and I know that it is not the holy oil, the sceptre and the orb, the sword, the mace, the imperial crown, the robe woven with gold and pearl, the stuffed-up titles announced before the King, the throne he sits on, or the tide of pomp that beats on the high shore of this world; no, not all these, thrice-gorgeous Ceremony, not all these, laid in a royal bed, can sleep as soundly as the wretched slave who, with full belly and empty mind, goes to rest, stuffed with bread earned by hard toil. He never sees horrible night, the child of hell, but like a servant sweats in the sun's eye from sunrise to sunset, and all night sleeps in paradise; next day after dawn he gets up and helps the sun god onto his horse, and so follows the ever-running year with useful labour to his grave. And except for ceremony, such a wretch, filling his days with toil and his nights with sleep, would have the upper hand and the advantage over a king. The slave, a member of the peaceful country, enjoys its peace, but in his dull brain has little idea what sleepless watch the King keeps to protect that peace, whose hours benefit the peasant most of all."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Erpingham.","id":544},{"t":"sp","who":"ERPINGHAM","lines":["My lord, your nobles, jealous of your absence,","Seek through your camp to find you."],"id":545,"m":"My lord, your nobles, worried by your absence, are searching your camp for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Good old knight,","Collect them all together at my tent.","I'll be before thee."],"id":546,"m":"Good old knight, gather them all at my tent. I'll be there before you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ERPINGHAM","lines":["I shall do't, my lord."],"id":547,"m":"I'll do it, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":548},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["O God of battles! steel my soldiers' hearts.","Possess them not with fear. Take from them now","The sense of reckoning, if the opposed numbers","Pluck their hearts from them. Not to-day, O Lord,","O, not to-day, think not upon the fault","My father made in compassing the crown!","I Richard's body have interred new,","And on it have bestow'd more contrite tears","Than from it issued forced drops of blood.","Five hundred poor I have in yearly pay,","Who twice a day their wither'd hands hold up","Toward heaven, to pardon blood; and I have built","Two chantries, where the sad and solemn priests","Sing still for Richard's soul. More will I do;","Though all that I can do is nothing worth,","Since that my penitence comes after all,","Imploring pardon."],"id":549,"m":"Oh God of battles, steel my soldiers' hearts! Don't fill them with fear. Take from them now the ability to count, in case the numbers against them pluck their hearts out. Not today, oh Lord, oh, not today, don't think about the crime my father committed in seizing the crown! I have buried Richard's body again, and shed more repentant tears on it than drops of blood were forced out of it. I pay five hundred poor people a year, who twice a day hold up their withered hands to heaven to ask pardon for that blood; and I have built two chapels where sad, solemn priests continually sing for Richard's soul. I will do more, though all I can do is worth nothing, since my repentance comes after all, begging forgiveness."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gloucester.","id":550},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["My liege!"],"id":551,"m":"My lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My brother Gloucester's voice? Ay;","I know thy errand, I will go with thee.","The day, my friends, and all things stay for me."],"id":552,"m":"My brother Gloucester's voice? Yes, I know your errand; I'll go with you. The day, my friends and everything are waiting for me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":553},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"The French camp.","id":554},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Dauphin, Orleans, Rambures, and others.","id":555},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["The sun doth gild our armour; up, my lords!"],"id":556,"m":"The sun is gilding our armour; up, my lords!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Montez a cheval! My horse, varlet! lackey! ha!"],"id":557,"m":"(In French) To horse! My horse, servant! Lackey! Ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["O brave spirit!"],"id":558,"m":"Oh, brave spirit!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Via! les eaux et la terre."],"id":559,"m":"(In French) Away, over water and earth!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["Rien puis? L'air et le feu."],"id":560,"m":"(In French) Nothing more? Air and fire, too."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Ciel, cousin Orleans."],"id":561,"m":"(In French) Heaven, cousin Orleans."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Constable.","id":562},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","cont":1,"lines":["Now, my Lord Constable!"],"id":563,"m":"Now, my Lord Constable!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Hark, how our steeds for present service neigh!"],"id":564,"m":"Listen how our horses neigh to go into action now!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Mount them, and make incision in their hides,","That their hot blood may spin in English eyes,","And dout them with superfluous courage, ha!"],"id":565,"m":"Mount them, and cut into their hides with your spurs, so that their hot blood may spurt into English eyes and put them out with extra courage, ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RAMBURES","lines":["What, will you have them weep our horses' blood?","How shall we, then, behold their natural tears?"],"id":566,"m":"What, will you have them weep our horses' blood? Then how shall we see their own tears?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":567},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The English are embattl'd, you French peers."],"id":568,"m":"The English are drawn up for battle, you French lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["To horse, you gallant princes! straight to horse!","Do but behold yon poor and starved band,","And your fair show shall suck away their souls,","Leaving them but the shales and husks of men.","There is not work enough for all our hands;","Scarce blood enough in all their sickly veins","To give each naked curtle-axe a stain,","That our French gallants shall to-day draw out,","And sheathe for lack of sport. Let us but blow on them,","The vapour of our valour will o'erturn them.","'Tis positive 'gainst all exceptions, lords,","That our superfluous lackeys and our peasants,","Who in unnecessary action swarm","About our squares of battle, were enow","To purge this field of such a hilding foe,","Though we upon this mountain's basis by","Took stand for idle speculation,","But that our honours must not. What's to say?","A very little little let us do,","And all is done. Then let the trumpets sound","The tucket sonance and the note to mount;","For our approach shall so much dare the field","That England shall crouch down in fear and yield."],"id":569,"m":"To horse, you gallant princes! Straight to horse! Just look at that poor, starving band over there, and your fine appearance will suck the souls out of them, leaving them only the shells and husks of men. There isn't work enough for all our hands; there's hardly blood enough in all their sickly veins to stain each bare cutlass that our French gallants will draw today and sheathe again for lack of sport. We only have to blow on them, and the breath of our courage will knock them over. It is certain beyond any objection, lords, that our spare servants and our peasants, who swarm uselessly around our battle formations, would be enough to clear this field of such a worthless enemy, while we stood by at the foot of this hill watching idly; but our honour won't allow it. What more is there to say? Let us do a very little, and it's all done. Then let the trumpets sound the call to mount; for our advance will so terrify the field that England will crouch down in fear and surrender."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Grandpre.","id":570},{"t":"sp","who":"GRANDPRE","lines":["Why do you stay so long, my lords of France?","Yond island carrions, desperate of their bones,","Ill-favouredly become the morning field.","Their ragged curtains poorly are let loose,","And our air shakes them passing scornfully.","Big Mars seems bankrupt in their beggar'd host,","And faintly through a rusty beaver peeps;","The horsemen sit like fixed candlesticks","With torch-staves in their hand; and their poor jades","Lob down their heads, drooping the hides and hips,","The gum down-roping from their pale-dead eyes,","And in their pale dull mouths the gimmal bit","Lies foul with chew'd grass, still, and motionless;","And their executors, the knavish crows,","Fly o'er them, all impatient for their hour.","Description cannot suit itself in words","To demonstrate the life of such a battle,","In life so lifeless as it shows itself."],"id":571,"m":"Why are you waiting so long, my lords of France? Those island corpses over there, hopeless of saving their bones, make an ugly sight on the morning field. Their ragged banners hang down poorly, and our air shakes them very scornfully as it passes. Mighty Mars seems bankrupt in their beggarly army and peeps feebly through a rusty visor. The horsemen sit like fixed candlesticks with lances in their hands, and their poor nags droop their heads, their skin and hips sagging, the gum dripping from their pale, dead eyes, and in their pale, dull mouths the jointed bit lies foul with chewed grass, still and motionless; and their executioners, the rascally crows, fly over them, impatient for their hour. No words can describe the life of such an army, which looks so lifeless while still alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["They have said their prayers, and they stay for death."],"id":572,"m":"They've said their prayers, and they're waiting for death."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Shall we go send them dinners and fresh suits","And give their fasting horses provender,","And after fight with them?"],"id":573,"m":"Shall we send them dinners and new clothes, and give their starving horses feed, and fight them afterwards?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["I stay but for my guard; on to the field!","I will the banner from a trumpet take,","And use it for my haste. Come, come, away!","The sun is high, and we outwear the day."],"id":574,"m":"I'm only waiting for my standard-bearer; on to the field! I'll take a banner from a trumpeter and use it in my hurry. Come, come, away! The sun is high, and we're wasting the day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":575},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"The English camp.","id":576},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gloucester, Bedford, Exeter, Erpingham, with all his host: Salisbury and Westmoreland.","id":577},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Where is the King?"],"id":578,"m":"Where is the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["The King himself is rode to view their battle."],"id":579,"m":"The King himself has ridden out to look at their army."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Of fighting men they have full three-score thousand."],"id":580,"m":"They have a full sixty thousand fighting men."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["There's five to one; besides, they all are fresh."],"id":581,"m":"That's five to one; and besides, they are all fresh."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["God's arm strike with us! 'tis a fearful odds.","God be wi' you, princes all; I'll to my charge.","If we no more meet till we meet in heaven,","Then, joyfully, my noble Lord of Bedford,","My dear Lord Gloucester, and my good Lord Exeter,","And my kind kinsman, warriors all, adieu!"],"id":582,"m":"May God's arm strike on our side! These are terrible odds. God be with you, princes all; I'll go to my command. If we don't meet again until we meet in heaven, then, joyfully, my noble Lord of Bedford, my dear Lord Gloucester, my good Lord Exeter, and my kind kinsman, warriors all, goodbye!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Farewell, good Salisbury, and good luck go with thee!"],"id":583,"m":"Farewell, good Salisbury, and good luck go with you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Farewell, kind lord; fight valiantly to-day!","And yet I do thee wrong to mind thee of it,","For thou art fram'd of the firm truth of valour."],"id":584,"m":"Farewell, kind lord; fight bravely today! And yet I wrong you by reminding you, for you are made of the solid substance of courage."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Salisbury.","id":585},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["He is as full of valour as of kindness,","Princely in both."],"id":586,"m":"He is as full of courage as of kindness, princely in both."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King.","id":587},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["O that we now had here","But one ten thousand of those men in England","That do no work to-day!"],"id":588,"m":"Oh, if only we had here now just ten thousand of those men in England who are doing no work today!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What's he that wishes so?","My cousin Westmoreland? No, my fair cousin.","If we are mark'd to die, we are enow","To do our country loss; and if to live,","The fewer men, the greater share of honour.","God's will! I pray thee, wish not one man more.","By Jove, I am not covetous for gold,","Nor care I who doth feed upon my cost;","It yearns me not if men my garments wear;","Such outward things dwell not in my desires;","But if it be a sin to covet honour,","I am the most offending soul alive.","No, faith, my coz, wish not a man from England.","God's peace! I would not lose so great an honour","As one man more, methinks, would share from me","For the best hope I have. O, do not wish one more!","Rather proclaim it, Westmoreland, through my host,","That he which hath no stomach to this fight,","Let him depart. His passport shall be made,","And crowns for convoy put into his purse.","We would not die in that man's company","That fears his fellowship to die with us.","This day is call'd the feast of Crispian.","He that outlives this day, and comes safe home,","Will stand a tip-toe when this day is named,","And rouse him at the name of Crispian.","He that shall live this day, and see old age,","Will yearly on the vigil feast his neighbours,","And say, \"To-morrow is Saint Crispian.\"","Then will he strip his sleeve and show his scars,","And say, \"These wounds I had on Crispian's day.\"","Old men forget; yet all shall be forgot,","But he'll remember with advantages","What feats he did that day. Then shall our names,","Familiar in his mouth as household words,","Harry the King, Bedford, and Exeter,","Warwick and Talbot, Salisbury and Gloucester,","Be in their flowing cups freshly rememb'red.","This story shall the good man teach his son;","And Crispin Crispian shall ne'er go by,","From this day to the ending of the world,","But we in it shall be remembered,","We few, we happy few, we band of brothers.","For he to-day that sheds his blood with me","Shall be my brother; be he ne'er so vile,","This day shall gentle his condition;","And gentlemen in England now a-bed","Shall think themselves accurs'd they were not here,","And hold their manhoods cheap whiles any speaks","That fought with us upon Saint Crispin's day."],"id":589,"m":"Who is it who wishes that? My cousin Westmoreland? No, my fair cousin. If we are marked out to die, there are enough of us for our country to lose; and if we are to live, the fewer men, the greater share of honour. By God's will, I beg you, don't wish for one man more. By Jove, I am not greedy for gold, and I don't care who eats at my expense; it doesn't trouble me if men wear my clothes; such outward things are not what I desire. But if it is a sin to long for honour, I am the most sinful soul alive. No, honestly, cousin, don't wish for one more man from England. God's peace! I would not lose as great a share of honour as one more man would take from me, I think, for the best hope I have. Oh, don't wish for one more! Rather announce through my army, Westmoreland, that whoever has no stomach for this fight may leave. His passport will be made out, and money for his journey put in his purse. We would not die in the company of a man who is afraid to die alongside us. Today is called the feast of Crispian. He who outlives this day and comes home safe will stand on tiptoe when this day is named and rouse himself at the name of Crispian. He who lives through this day and sees old age will feast his neighbours every year on the eve of it and say, \"Tomorrow is Saint Crispian's.\" Then he will roll up his sleeve and show his scars and say, \"I got these wounds on Crispin's day.\" Old men forget; yet even when everything else is forgotten, he'll remember, with a few additions, what deeds he did that day. Then our names, as familiar on his lips as everyday words, Harry the King, Bedford and Exeter, Warwick and Talbot, Salisbury and Gloucester, will be freshly remembered over their overflowing cups. The good man will teach this story to his son; and Crispin Crispian shall never go by, from this day to the end of the world, without our being remembered in it: we few, we happy few, we band of brothers. For whoever sheds his blood with me today shall be my brother; however low his birth, this day will raise him to a gentleman. And gentlemen in England now in bed will think themselves cursed that they were not here, and think their manhood cheap while anyone speaks who fought with us on Saint Crispin's day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Salisbury.","id":590},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["My sovereign lord, bestow yourself with speed.","The French are bravely in their battles set,","And will with all expedience charge on us."],"id":591,"m":"My sovereign lord, take your place quickly. The French are splendidly drawn up in their battalions and will charge us at any moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["All things are ready, if our minds be so."],"id":592,"m":"Everything is ready, if our minds are."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Perish the man whose mind is backward now!"],"id":593,"m":"Death to any man whose mind is unwilling now!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Thou dost not wish more help from England, coz?"],"id":594,"m":"You don't wish for more help from England, cousin?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["God's will! my liege, would you and I alone,","Without more help, could fight this royal battle!"],"id":595,"m":"By God's will, my lord, I wish you and I alone, without more help, could fight this royal battle!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Why, now thou hast unwish'd five thousand men,","Which likes me better than to wish us one.","You know your places. God be with you all!"],"id":596,"m":"Why, now you have unwished five thousand men, which pleases me more than wishing us one more. You know your places. God be with you all!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Tucket. Enter Montjoy.","id":597},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["Once more I come to know of thee, King Harry,","If for thy ransom thou wilt now compound,","Before thy most assured overthrow;","For certainly thou art so near the gulf,","Thou needs must be englutted. Besides, in mercy,","The Constable desires thee thou wilt mind","Thy followers of repentance; that their souls","May make a peaceful and a sweet retire","From off these fields, where, wretches, their poor bodies","Must lie and fester."],"id":598,"m":"Once more I have come to learn from you, King Harry, whether you will now agree on your ransom before your certain defeat; for you are surely so close to the whirlpool that you must be swallowed up. Besides, out of mercy, the Constable asks you to remind your followers to repent, so that their souls may make a peaceful and sweet withdrawal from these fields, where their poor bodies, wretches, must lie and rot."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Who hath sent thee now?"],"id":599,"m":"Who has sent you now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["The Constable of France."],"id":600,"m":"The Constable of France."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I pray thee, bear my former answer back:","Bid them achieve me and then sell my bones.","Good God! why should they mock poor fellows thus?","The man that once did sell the lion's skin","While the beast liv'd, was kill'd with hunting him.","A many of our bodies shall no doubt","Find native graves, upon the which, I trust,","Shall witness live in brass of this day's work;","And those that leave their valiant bones in France,","Dying like men, though buried in your dunghills,","They shall be fam'd; for there the sun shall greet them,","And draw their honours reeking up to heaven;","Leaving their earthly parts to choke your clime,","The smell whereof shall breed a plague in France.","Mark then abounding valour in our English,","That being dead, like to the bullet's grazing,","Break out into a second course of mischief,","Killing in relapse of mortality.","Let me speak proudly: tell the Constable","We are but warriors for the working-day.","Our gayness and our gilt are all besmirch'd","With rainy marching in the painful field;","There's not a piece of feather in our host--","Good argument, I hope, we will not fly--","And time hath worn us into slovenry;","But, by the mass, our hearts are in the trim;","And my poor soldiers tell me, yet ere night","They'll be in fresher robes, or they will pluck","The gay new coats o'er the French soldiers' heads","And turn them out of service. If they do this--","As, if God please, they shall,--my ransom then","Will soon be levied. Herald, save thou thy labour.","Come thou no more for ransom, gentle herald.","They shall have none, I swear, but these my joints;","Which if they have as I will leave 'em them,","Shall yield them little, tell the Constable."],"id":601,"m":"Please take my earlier answer back: tell them to capture me first and then sell my bones. Good God, why do they mock poor men like this? The man who once sold the lion's skin while the beast was still alive was killed hunting him. Many of our bodies, no doubt, will find graves at home, on which, I trust, brass memorials will record this day's work; and those who leave their brave bones in France, dying like men, though buried in your dunghills, will be famous; for the sun will greet them there and draw their honours steaming up to heaven, leaving their earthly parts to choke your air, and the smell will breed a plague in France. So note the overflowing courage of our English, who even when dead, like a bullet bouncing on, break out into a second round of damage, killing as they rot. Let me speak proudly: tell the Constable we are only warriors in our working clothes. Our finery and gilding are all soiled from marching in the rain over the hard field; there isn't a feather in our army (good proof, I hope, that we won't fly away), and time has worn us into scruffiness. But, by the Mass, our hearts are in good trim. And my poor soldiers tell me that before night they'll be in fresher clothes, or they'll pull the gay new coats over the French soldiers' heads and dismiss them from service. If they do this, as they will if God pleases, my ransom will soon be raised. Herald, save yourself the trouble. Don't come for ransom any more, gentle herald. They'll get none, I swear, except these limbs of mine; and if they get them in the state I'll leave them in, they'll be worth little to them. Tell the Constable that."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["I shall, King Harry. And so fare thee well;","Thou never shalt hear herald any more."],"id":602,"m":"I shall, King Harry. And so farewell; you will never hear a herald again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":603},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I fear thou'lt once more come again for ransom."],"id":604,"m":"I'm afraid you'll come once more for ransom."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter York.","id":605},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["My lord, most humbly on my knee I beg","The leading of the vaward."],"id":606,"m":"My lord, most humbly on my knees I beg to lead the front line."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Take it, brave York. Now, soldiers, march away;","And how thou pleasest, God, dispose the day!"],"id":607,"m":"Take it, brave York. Now, soldiers, march on; and, God, decide the day as you please!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":608},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"The field of battle at Agincourt.","id":609},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Excursions. Enter Pistol, French Soldier, and Boy.","id":610},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Yield, cur!"],"id":611,"m":"Surrender, cur!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["Je pense que vous etes le gentilhomme de bonne qualite."],"id":612,"m":"(In French) I think you are a gentleman of high rank."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Qualitie calmie custure me! Art thou a gentleman?","What is thy name? Discuss."],"id":613,"m":"\"Quality\"? (Repeats a nonsense refrain) Are you a gentleman? What's your name? Explain."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["O Seigneur Dieu!"],"id":614,"m":"(In French) Oh Lord God!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["O, Signieur Dew should be a gentleman.","Perpend my words, O Signieur Dew, and mark:","O Signieur Dew, thou diest on point of fox,","Except, O signieur, thou do give to me","Egregious ransom."],"id":615,"m":"Oh, Signieur Dew must be a gentleman. Weigh my words, oh Signieur Dew, and take note: oh Signieur Dew, you die on the point of my sword, unless, oh signieur, you give me an outrageous ransom."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["O, prenez misericorde! ayez pitie de moi!"],"id":616,"m":"(In French) Oh, have mercy! Take pity on me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Moy shall not serve; I will have forty moys,","Or I will fetch thy rim out at thy throat","In drops of crimson blood."],"id":617,"m":"\"Moy\" won't do; I'll have forty moys, or I'll pull your guts out through your throat in drops of crimson blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["Est-il impossible d'echapper la force de ton bras?"],"id":618,"m":"(In French) Is it impossible to escape the strength of your arm?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Brass, cur!","Thou damned and luxurious mountain goat,","Offer'st me brass?"],"id":619,"m":"Brass, cur! You damned, lecherous mountain goat, are you offering me brass?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["O pardonnez moi!"],"id":620,"m":"(In French) Oh, pardon me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Say'st thou me so? Is that a ton of moys?","Come hither, boy; ask me this slave in French","What is his name."],"id":621,"m":"Is that what you say? Is that a ton of moys? Come here, boy; ask this slave in French what his name is."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Ecoutez: comment etes-vous appele?"],"id":622,"m":"(In French) Listen: what is your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["Monsieur le Fer."],"id":623,"m":"(In French) Monsieur le Fer."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["He says his name is Master Fer."],"id":624,"m":"He says his name is Master Fer."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Master Fer! I'll fer him, and firk him, and ferret him.","Discuss the same in French unto him."],"id":625,"m":"Master Fer! I'll fer him, and firk him, and ferret him. Explain that to him in French."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["I do not know the French for fer, and ferret, and firk."],"id":626,"m":"I don't know the French for \"fer\", \"ferret\" and \"firk\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Bid him prepare; for I will cut his throat."],"id":627,"m":"Tell him to get ready, because I'm going to cut his throat."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["Que dit-il, monsieur?"],"id":628,"m":"(In French) What does he say, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Il me commande a vous dire que vous faites vous pret; car","ce soldat ici est dispose tout a cette heure de couper votre","gorge."],"id":629,"m":"(In French) He orders me to tell you to get ready, because this soldier here is inclined to cut your throat at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Owy, cuppele gorge, permafoy,","Peasant, unless thou give me crowns, brave crowns;","Or mangled shalt thou be by this my sword."],"id":630,"m":"Yes, cut the throat, by my faith, peasant, unless you give me crowns, fine crowns; or you'll be mangled by this sword of mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["O, je vous supplie, pour l'amour de Dieu, me pardonner!","Je suis gentilhomme de bonne maison; gardez ma vie, et","je vous donnerai deux cents ecus."],"id":631,"m":"(In French) Oh, I beg you, for the love of God, pardon me! I'm a gentleman of a good family; spare my life, and I'll give you two hundred crowns."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["What are his words?"],"id":632,"m":"What's he saying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["He prays you to save his life. He is a gentleman of a good","house; and for his ransom he will give you two hundred","crowns."],"id":633,"m":"He begs you to spare his life. He's a gentleman of a good family, and for his ransom he'll give you two hundred crowns."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Tell him my fury shall abate, and I","The crowns will take."],"id":634,"m":"Tell him my fury shall calm down, and I'll take the crowns."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["Petit monsieur, que dit-il?"],"id":635,"m":"(In French) Little sir, what does he say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Encore qu'il est contre son jurement de pardonner aucun","prisonnier; neanmoins, pour les ecus que vous l'avez promis, il","est content de vous donner la liberte, le franchisement."],"id":636,"m":"(In French) That although it is against his oath to pardon any prisoner, nevertheless, for the crowns you have promised him, he is content to give you your liberty, your freedom."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH SOLDIER","lines":["Sur mes genoux je vous donne mille remercimens; et je m'estime","heureux que je suis tombe entre les mains d'un chevalier, je","pense, le plus brave, vaillant, et tres distingue seigneur","d'Angleterre."],"id":637,"m":"(In French) On my knees I give you a thousand thanks; and I count myself lucky that I have fallen into the hands of a knight who is, I think, the bravest, most valiant and most distinguished lord in England."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Expound unto me, boy."],"id":638,"m":"Explain it to me, boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["He gives you upon his knees, a thousand thanks; and he esteems","himself happy that he hath fallen into the hands of one, as he","thinks, the most brave, valorous, and thrice-worthy signieur of","England."],"id":639,"m":"He gives you a thousand thanks on his knees, and he counts himself lucky that he has fallen into the hands of the bravest, most valiant and thrice-worthy lord in England, as he thinks."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["As I suck blood, I will some mercy show.","Follow me!"],"id":640,"m":"As I suck blood, I'll show some mercy. Follow me!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":641},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Suivez-vous le grand capitaine."],"id":642,"m":"(In French) Follow the great captain."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Pistol, and French Soldier.","id":643},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","cont":1,"lines":["I did never know so full a voice issue from so empty a heart; but","the saying is true, \"The empty vessel makes the greatest sound.\"","Bardolph and Nym had ten times more valour than this roaring","devil i' the old play, that every one may pare his nails with a","wooden dagger; and they are both hang'd; and so would this be,","if he durst steal anything adventurously. I must stay with the","lackeys with the luggage of our camp. The French might have a","good prey of us, if he knew of it; for there is none to guard it","but boys."],"id":644,"m":"I never knew such a loud voice come out of such an empty heart; but the saying is true, \"The empty pot makes the most noise.\" Bardolph and Nym had ten times more courage than this roaring devil from the old plays, whose nails anyone can pare with a wooden dagger; and they've both been hanged, and so would he be if he dared to steal anything boldly. I must stay with the servants and the baggage of our camp. The French could make an easy catch of us if they knew about it, because there's nobody guarding it but boys."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":645},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"Another part of the field.","id":646},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Constable, Orleans, Bourbon, Dauphin, and Rambures.","id":647},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["O diable!"],"id":648,"m":"(In French) Oh, the devil!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["O Seigneur! le jour est perdu, tout est perdu!"],"id":649,"m":"(In French) Oh Lord! The day is lost, all is lost!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["Mort de ma vie! all is confounded, all!","Reproach and everlasting shame","Sits mocking in our plumes."],"id":650,"m":"Death of my life! Everything is ruined, everything! Disgrace and everlasting shame sit mocking in the plumes of our helmets."},{"t":"sd","text":"A short alarum.","id":651},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","cont":1,"lines":["O mechante fortune! Do not run away."],"id":652,"m":"(In French) Oh, wicked fortune! Don't run away."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Why, all our ranks are broke."],"id":653,"m":"Why, all our ranks are broken."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAUPHIN","lines":["O perdurable shame! let's stab ourselves,","Be these the wretches that we play'd at dice for?"],"id":654,"m":"Oh, everlasting shame! Let's stab ourselves. Are these the wretches we played dice for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["Is this the king we sent to for his ransom?"],"id":655,"m":"Is this the king we sent to about his ransom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOURBON","lines":["Shame and eternal shame, nothing but shame!","Let's die in honour! Once more back again!","And he that will not follow Bourbon now,","Let him go hence, and with his cap in hand,","Like a base pandar, hold the chamber door","Whilst by a slave, no gentler than my dog,","His fairest daughter is contaminated."],"id":656,"m":"Shame, and eternal shame, nothing but shame! Let's die with honour! Back once more! And whoever won't follow Bourbon now, let him go away, and, cap in hand like a low pimp, hold the bedroom door while his fairest daughter is defiled by a slave no nobler than my dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSTABLE","lines":["Disorder, that hath spoil'd us, friend us now!","Let us on heaps go offer up our lives."],"id":657,"m":"May disorder, which has ruined us, help us now! Let's go in a mass and offer up our lives."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORLEANS","lines":["We are enow yet living in the field","To smother up the English in our throngs,","If any order might be thought upon."],"id":658,"m":"There are still enough of us alive in the field to smother the English in our crowds, if only some order could be worked out."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOURBON","lines":["The devil take order now! I'll to the throng.","Let life be short, else shame will be too long."],"id":659,"m":"The devil take order now! I'm going into the thick of it. Let life be short, or shame will be too long."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":660},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VI","loc":"Another part of the field.","id":661},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter King Henry and his train, with prisoners.","id":662},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Well have we done, thrice valiant countrymen.","But all's not done; yet keep the French the field."],"id":663,"m":"We have done well, thrice brave countrymen. But it's not all done; the French still hold the field."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["The Duke of York commends him to your Majesty."],"id":664,"m":"The Duke of York sends his respects to your Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Lives he, good uncle? Thrice within this hour","I saw him down; thrice up again, and fighting.","From helmet to the spur all blood he was."],"id":665,"m":"Is he alive, good uncle? Three times within this hour I saw him down, three times up again and fighting. He was all blood from helmet to spur."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["In which array, brave soldier, doth he lie,","Larding the plain; and by his bloody side,","Yoke-fellow to his honour-owing wounds,","The noble Earl of Suffolk also lies.","Suffolk first died; and York, all haggled over,","Comes to him, where in gore he lay insteeped,","And takes him by the beard; kisses the gashes","That bloodily did yawn upon his face.","He cries aloud, \"Tarry, my cousin Suffolk!","My soul shall thine keep company to heaven;","Tarry, sweet soul, for mine, then fly abreast,","As in this glorious and well-foughten field","We kept together in our chivalry.\"","Upon these words I came and cheer'd him up.","He smil'd me in the face, raught me his hand,","And, with a feeble gripe, says, \"Dear my lord,","Commend my service to my sovereign.\"","So did he turn and over Suffolk's neck","He threw his wounded arm and kiss'd his lips;","And so espous'd to death, with blood he seal'd","A testament of noble-ending love.","The pretty and sweet manner of it forc'd","Those waters from me which I would have stopp'd;","But I had not so much of man in me,","And all my mother came into mine eyes","And gave me up to tears."],"id":666,"m":"And dressed like that, brave soldier, he lies, enriching the ground with his blood; and by his bloody side, partner of his honourable wounds, lies the noble Earl of Suffolk too. Suffolk died first; and York, all hacked about, came to him where he lay steeped in blood, took him by the beard, and kissed the gashes that gaped bloodily in his face. He cried aloud, \"Wait, my cousin Suffolk! My soul will keep yours company to heaven. Wait for mine, sweet soul, then fly side by side, as we kept together as knights in this glorious and hard-fought field.\" At these words I came and comforted him. He smiled up into my face, reached me his hand, and with a feeble grip said, \"My dear lord, commend my service to my king.\" Then he turned and threw his wounded arm over Suffolk's neck and kissed his lips; and so, married to death, he sealed with his blood a promise of love that ended nobly. The beautiful, sweet way of it forced from me those tears I would have held back; but I didn't have enough of the man in me, and all my mother's softness came into my eyes and gave me up to tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I blame you not;","For, hearing this, I must perforce compound","With mistful eyes, or they will issue too."],"id":667,"m":"I don't blame you; for, hearing this, I must come to terms with misty eyes, or they will weep too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum.","id":668},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["But hark! what new alarum is this same?","The French have reinforc'd their scatter'd men.","Then every soldier kill his prisoners;","Give the word through."],"id":669,"m":"But listen! What is this new call to arms? The French have regrouped their scattered men. Then let every soldier kill his prisoners. Pass the order on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":670},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VII","loc":"Another part of the field.","id":671},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fluellen and Gower.","id":672},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Kill the poys and the luggage! 'Tis expressly against the","law of arms. 'Tis as arrant a piece of knavery, mark you now,","as can be offer't; in your conscience, now, is it not?"],"id":673,"m":"Kill the boys and the baggage! It's expressly against the law of arms. It's as out-and-out a piece of villainy, mark you now, as could be done; in your conscience now, isn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["'Tis certain there's not a boy left alive; and the cowardly","rascals that ran from the battle ha' done this slaughter.","Besides, they have burned and carried away all that was in the","King's tent; wherefore the King, most worthily, hath caus'd every","soldier to cut his prisoner's throat. O, 'tis a gallant king!"],"id":674,"m":"It's certain there's not a boy left alive; and the cowardly rascals who ran from the battle did this slaughter. Besides, they have burned and carried away everything that was in the King's tent; so the King, most rightly, has ordered every soldier to cut his prisoner's throat. Oh, he's a gallant king!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Ay, he was porn at Monmouth, Captain Gower. What call you","the town's name where Alexander the Pig was born?"],"id":675,"m":"Yes, he was born at Monmouth, Captain Gower. What do you call the name of the town where Alexander the Pig was born?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Alexander the Great."],"id":676,"m":"Alexander the Great."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Why, I pray you, is not pig great? The pig, or the great, or the","mighty, or the huge, or the magnanimous, are all one reckonings,","save the phrase is a little variations."],"id":677,"m":"Why, please, isn't \"pig\" great? The pig, or the great, or the mighty, or the huge, or the magnanimous all come to the same thing, except that the wording is a little different."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["I think Alexander the Great was born in Macedon. His father","was called Philip of Macedon, as I take it."],"id":678,"m":"I think Alexander the Great was born in Macedon. His father was called Philip of Macedon, I believe."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["I think it is in Macedon where Alexander is porn. I tell you,","Captain, if you look in the maps of the 'orld, I warrant you","sall find, in the comparisons between Macedon and Monmouth,","that the situations, look you, is both alike. There is a river in","Macedon; and there is also moreover a river at Monmouth; it is","call'd Wye at Monmouth; but it is out of my prains what is the","name of the other river; but 'tis all one, 'tis alike as my fingers","is to my fingers, and there is salmons in both. If you mark","Alexander's life well, Harry of Monmouth's life is come after it","indifferent well; for there is figures in all things. Alexander,","God knows, and you know, in his rages, and his furies, and his","wraths, and his cholers, and his moods, and his displeasures, and","his indignations, and also being a little intoxicates in his prains,","did, in his ales and his angers, look you, kill his best friend,","Cleitus."],"id":679,"m":"I think it is in Macedon that Alexander was born. I tell you, captain, if you look at the maps of the world, I promise you'll find, in comparing Macedon and Monmouth, that the positions, look you, are both alike. There's a river in Macedon, and there's also a river at Monmouth; it's called the Wye at Monmouth, but I can't remember the name of the other river; but it's all the same, it's as alike as my fingers are to my fingers, and there are salmon in both. If you look carefully at Alexander's life, Harry of Monmouth's life follows it fairly well; for there are parallels in all things. Alexander, God knows, and you know, in his rages and his furies and his wraths and his angers and his moods and his displeasures and his indignations, and also being a little drunk in his brain, did, in his ale and his anger, look you, kill his best friend, Cleitus."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Our King is not like him in that. He never kill'd any of","his friends."],"id":680,"m":"Our King isn't like him in that. He never killed any of his friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["It is not well done, mark you now, to take the tales out","of my mouth, ere it is made and finished. I speak but in the","figures and comparisons of it. As Alexander kill'd his friend","Cleitus, being in his ales and his cups; so also Harry Monmouth,","being in his right wits and his good judgements, turn'd away the","fat knight with the great belly doublet. He was full of jests,","and gipes, and knaveries, and mocks; I have forgot his name."],"id":681,"m":"It isn't right, mark you now, to take the story out of my mouth before it's made and finished. I'm only speaking of the parallels and comparisons. As Alexander killed his friend Cleitus when he was in his ale and his cups, so also Harry of Monmouth, being in his right mind and his good judgement, turned away the fat knight with the great belly-doublet. He was full of jokes and jibes and tricks and mockery; I've forgotten his name."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Sir John Falstaff."],"id":682,"m":"Sir John Falstaff."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["That is he. I'll tell you there is good men porn at Monmouth."],"id":683,"m":"That's him. I tell you, there are good men born at Monmouth."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Here comes his Majesty."],"id":684,"m":"Here comes his Majesty."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter King Henry and forces; Warwick, Gloucester, Exeter, with prisoners. Flourish.","id":685},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I was not angry since I came to France","Until this instant. Take a trumpet, herald;","Ride thou unto the horsemen on yond hill.","If they will fight with us, bid them come down,","Or void the field; they do offend our sight.","If they'll do neither, we will come to them,","And make them skirr away, as swift as stones","Enforced from the old Assyrian slings.","Besides, we'll cut the throats of those we have,","And not a man of them that we shall take","Shall taste our mercy. Go and tell them so."],"id":686,"m":"I have not been angry since I came to France until this moment. Take a trumpeter, herald, and ride to the horsemen on that hill over there. If they want to fight us, tell them to come down, or leave the field; they offend our sight. If they'll do neither, we'll come to them and make them scurry away as fast as stones shot from the old Assyrian slings. Besides, we'll cut the throats of the prisoners we have, and not a man of those we take shall taste our mercy. Go and tell them so."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Montjoy.","id":687},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Here comes the herald of the French, my liege."],"id":688,"m":"Here comes the French herald, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["His eyes are humbler than they us'd to be."],"id":689,"m":"His eyes are humbler than they used to be."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["How now! what means this, herald? Know'st thou not","That I have fin'd these bones of mine for ransom?","Com'st thou again for ransom?"],"id":690,"m":"Well! What does this mean, herald? Don't you know that I have fixed these bones of mine as my ransom? Have you come for ransom again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["No, great King;","I come to thee for charitable license,","That we may wander o'er this bloody field","To book our dead, and then to bury them;","To sort our nobles from our common men.","For many of our princes--woe the while!--","Lie drown'd and soak'd in mercenary blood;","So do our vulgar drench their peasant limbs","In blood of princes; and their wounded steeds","Fret fetlock deep in gore, and with wild rage","Yerk out their armed heels at their dead masters,","Killing them twice. O, give us leave, great King,","To view the field in safety, and dispose","Of their dead bodies!"],"id":691,"m":"No, great King; I come to ask your charitable permission to walk over this bloody field to record our dead and then bury them, and to sort our nobles from our common men. For many of our princes, alas, lie drowned and soaked in the blood of hired soldiers; and our common men soak their peasant limbs in the blood of princes; and their wounded horses struggle fetlock-deep in gore and in wild rage kick out with their armoured heels at their dead masters, killing them twice. Oh, give us permission, great King, to look over the field in safety and dispose of their dead bodies!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I tell thee truly, herald,","I know not if the day be ours or no;","For yet a many of your horsemen peer","And gallop o'er the field."],"id":692,"m":"I tell you truly, herald, I don't know if the day is ours or not; for many of your horsemen still appear and gallop over the field."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["The day is yours."],"id":693,"m":"The day is yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Praised be God, and not our strength, for it!","What is this castle call'd that stands hard by?"],"id":694,"m":"Praise God for it, and not our strength! What is that castle called that stands close by?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTJOY","lines":["They call it Agincourt."],"id":695,"m":"They call it Agincourt."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Then call we this the field of Agincourt,","Fought on the day of Crispin Crispianus."],"id":696,"m":"Then we'll call this the field of Agincourt, fought on the day of Crispin Crispianus."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Your grandfather of famous memory, an't please your","Majesty, and your great-uncle Edward the Plack Prince of","Wales, as I have read in the chronicles, fought a most prave","pattle here in France."],"id":697,"m":"Your grandfather of famous memory, if it please your Majesty, and your great-uncle Edward the Black Prince of Wales, as I have read in the chronicles, fought a most brave battle here in France."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["They did, Fluellen."],"id":698,"m":"They did, Fluellen."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Your Majesty says very true. If your Majesties is rememb'red of","it, the Welshmen did good service in garden where leeks did grow,","wearing leeks in their Monmouth caps; which, your Majesty know,","to this hour is an honourable badge of the service; and I do","believe your Majesty takes no scorn to wear the leek upon Saint","Tavy's day."],"id":699,"m":"Your Majesty says very true. If your Majesty remembers it, the Welshmen did good service in a garden where leeks grew, wearing leeks in their Monmouth caps; which, as your Majesty knows, is to this day an honourable badge of the service; and I believe your Majesty is not too proud to wear the leek on Saint David's Day."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I wear it for a memorable honour;","For I am Welsh, you know, good countryman."],"id":700,"m":"I wear it as an honour to remember; for I am Welsh, you know, good countryman."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["All the water in Wye cannot wash your Majesty's Welsh plood out","of your pody, I can tell you that. Got pless it and preserve it,","as long as it pleases His grace, and His majesty too!"],"id":701,"m":"All the water in the Wye can't wash your Majesty's Welsh blood out of your body, I can tell you that. God bless it and preserve it, as long as it pleases His grace, and His majesty too!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Thanks, good my countryman."],"id":702,"m":"Thank you, my good countryman."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["By Jeshu, I am your Majesty's countryman, I care not who know it.","I will confess it to all the 'orld. I need not be asham'd of your","Majesty, praised be God, so long as your Majesty is an honest man."],"id":703,"m":"By Jesus, I am your Majesty's countryman, I don't care who knows it. I'll confess it to the whole world. I needn't be ashamed of your Majesty, praise God, as long as your Majesty is an honest man."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["God keep me so!"],"id":704,"m":"God keep me so!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Williams.","id":705},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Our heralds go with him;","Bring me just notice of the numbers dead","On both our parts. Call yonder fellow hither."],"id":706,"m":"Let our heralds go with him; bring me an exact count of the numbers dead on both sides. Call that fellow over here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Heralds with Montjoy.","id":707},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Soldier, you must come to the King."],"id":708,"m":"Soldier, you must come to the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Soldier, why wear'st thou that glove in thy cap?"],"id":709,"m":"Soldier, why are you wearing that glove in your cap?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["An't please your Majesty, 'tis the gage of one that I","should fight withal, if he be alive."],"id":710,"m":"If it please your Majesty, it's the token of a man I'm supposed to fight, if he's alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["An Englishman?"],"id":711,"m":"An Englishman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["An't please your Majesty, a rascal that swagger'd with me","last night; who, if alive and ever dare to challenge this","glove, I have sworn to take him a box o' the ear; or if I can","see my glove in his cap, which he swore, as he was a soldier,","he would wear if alive, I will strike it out soundly."],"id":712,"m":"If it please your Majesty, a rascal who swaggered at me last night; if he's alive and ever dares to claim this glove, I've sworn to give him a box on the ear; or if I see my glove in his cap, which he swore as a soldier he would wear if he lived, I'll knock it out soundly."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What think you, Captain Fluellen? Is it fit this soldier keep","his oath?"],"id":713,"m":"What do you think, Captain Fluellen? Is it right for this soldier to keep his oath?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["He is a craven and a villain else, an't please your Majesty, in","my conscience."],"id":714,"m":"He's a coward and a villain otherwise, if it please your Majesty, in my conscience."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["It may be his enemy is a gentleman of great sort, quite from","the answer of his degree."],"id":715,"m":"It may be that his enemy is a gentleman of high rank, quite above answering a challenge from a man of his class."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Though he be as good a gentleman as the devil is, as Lucifier","and Belzebub himself, it is necessary, look your Grace, that he","keep his vow and his oath. If he be perjur'd, see you now, his","reputation is as arrant a villain and a Jacksauce, as ever his","black shoe trod upon God's ground and His earth, in my","conscience, la!"],"id":716,"m":"Even if he's as good a gentleman as the devil is, as Lucifer and Beelzebub himself, it is necessary, look your Grace, that he keeps his vow and his oath. If he breaks it, see you now, his reputation is that of as out-and-out a villain and a saucy knave as ever trod on God's ground and His earth with his black shoe, in my conscience, la!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Then keep thy vow, sirrah, when thou meet'st the fellow."],"id":717,"m":"Then keep your vow, fellow, when you meet the man."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["So I will, my liege, as I live."],"id":718,"m":"I will, my lord, as I live."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Who serv'st thou under?"],"id":719,"m":"Who do you serve under?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Under Captain Gower, my liege."],"id":720,"m":"Under Captain Gower, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Gower is a good captain, and is good knowledge and","literatured in the wars."],"id":721,"m":"Gower is a good captain, and is well informed and well read in the wars."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Call him hither to me, soldier."],"id":722,"m":"Call him here to me, soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["I will, my liege."],"id":723,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":724},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Here, Fluellen; wear thou this favour for me and stick it in thy","cap. When Alencon and myself were down together, I pluck'd","this glove from his helm. If any man challenge this, he is a","friend to Alencon, and an enemy to our person. If thou encounter","any such, apprehend him, an thou dost me love."],"id":725,"m":"Here, Fluellen, wear this token for me and stick it in your cap. When Alencon and I were down on the ground together, I pulled this glove off his helmet. If any man challenges it, he is a friend of Alencon's and an enemy of ours. If you meet anyone like that, arrest him, if you love me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Your Grace doo's me as great honours as can be desir'd in the","hearts of his subjects. I would fain see the man, that has but","two legs, that shall find himself aggrief'd at this glove; that","is all. But I would fain see it once, an please God of His grace","that I might see."],"id":726,"m":"Your Grace does me as great an honour as any of his subjects could wish for in their hearts. I would like to see the man with only two legs who will take offence at this glove, that's all. But I would like to see it once, if it please God of His grace that I might."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Know'st thou Gower?"],"id":727,"m":"Do you know Gower?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["He is my dear friend, an please you."],"id":728,"m":"He's my dear friend, if you please."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Pray thee, go seek him, and bring him to my tent."],"id":729,"m":"Please go and find him and bring him to my tent."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["I will fetch him."],"id":730,"m":"I'll fetch him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":731},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My Lord of Warwick, and my brother Gloucester,","Follow Fluellen closely at the heels.","The glove which I have given him for a favour","May haply purchase him a box o' the ear.","It is the soldier's; I by bargain should","Wear it myself. Follow, good cousin Warwick.","If that the soldier strike him, as I judge","By his blunt bearing he will keep his word,","Some sudden mischief may arise of it;","For I do know Fluellen valiant","And, touch'd with choler, hot as gunpowder,","And quickly will return an injury.","Follow, and see there be no harm between them.","Go you with me, uncle of Exeter."],"id":732,"m":"My Lord of Warwick and my brother Gloucester, follow close on Fluellen's heels. The glove I've given him as a token may well earn him a box on the ear. It's the soldier's; by our bargain I should be wearing it myself. Follow, good cousin Warwick. If the soldier strikes him, and I judge from his blunt manner that he'll keep his word, some sudden trouble may come of it; for I know Fluellen is brave, and when stirred to anger is as hot as gunpowder and quick to return an injury. Follow, and make sure no harm comes between them. Come with me, Uncle Exeter."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":733},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VIII","loc":"Before King Henry's pavilion.","id":734},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gower and Williams.","id":735},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["I warrant it is to knight you, Captain."],"id":736,"m":"I bet it's to knight you, Captain."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fluellen.","id":737},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["God's will and his pleasure, captain, I beseech you now,","come apace to the King. There is more good toward you","peradventure than is in your knowledge to dream of."],"id":738,"m":"By God's will and pleasure, captain, I beg you now, come quickly to the King. There may be more good coming to you than you could dream of."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Sir, know you this glove?"],"id":739,"m":"Sir, do you know this glove?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Know the glove! I know the glove is a glove."],"id":740,"m":"Know the glove! I know the glove is a glove."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["I know this; and thus I challenge it."],"id":741,"m":"I know this one; and this is how I challenge it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Strikes him.","id":742},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["'Sblood! an arrant traitor as any is in the universal","world, or in France, or in England!"],"id":743,"m":"By God's blood! As out-and-out a traitor as any in the whole world, or in France, or in England!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["How now, sir! you villain!"],"id":744,"m":"What's this, sir! You villain!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Do you think I'll be forsworn?"],"id":745,"m":"Do you think I'll break my oath?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Stand away, Captain Gower. I will give treason his","payment into plows, I warrant you."],"id":746,"m":"Stand back, Captain Gower. I'll pay treason what it's owed in blows, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["I am no traitor."],"id":747,"m":"I'm no traitor."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["That's a lie in thy throat. I charge you in his Majesty's","name, apprehend him; he's a friend of the Duke Alencon's."],"id":748,"m":"That's a lie in your throat. I order you in his Majesty's name, arrest him; he's a friend of the Duke of Alencon's."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Warwick and Gloucester.","id":749},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["How now, how now! what's the matter?"],"id":750,"m":"What's this, what's this! What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["My lord of Warwick, here is--praised be God for it!--a most","contagious treason come to light, look you, as you shall","desire in a summer's day. Here is his Majesty."],"id":751,"m":"My Lord of Warwick, here is, praise God for it, a most infectious treason brought to light, look you, as fine as you could wish on a summer's day. Here is his Majesty."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King Henry and Exeter.","id":752},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["How now! what's the matter?"],"id":753,"m":"What's this! What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["My liege, here is a villain and a traitor, that, look your Grace,","has struck the glove which your Majesty is take out of the","helmet of Alencon."],"id":754,"m":"My lord, here is a villain and a traitor who, look your Grace, has struck the glove which your Majesty took out of Alencon's helmet."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["My liege, this was my glove; here is the fellow of it; and he","that I gave it to in change promis'd to wear it in his cap. I","promis'd to strike him, if he did. I met this man with my","glove in his cap, and I have been as good as my word."],"id":755,"m":"My lord, this was my glove; here's its partner; and the man I gave it to in exchange promised to wear it in his cap. I promised to hit him if he did. I met this man with my glove in his cap, and I've been as good as my word."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Your Majesty hear now, saving your Majesty's manhood,","what an arrant, rascally, beggarly, lousy knave it is. I hope","your Majesty is pear me testimony and witness, and will","avouchment, that this is the glove of Alencon that your","Majesty is give me; in your conscience, now?"],"id":756,"m":"Now your Majesty hears, saving your Majesty's manhood, what an out-and-out, rascally, beggarly, lousy rogue this is. I hope your Majesty will bear me witness and testify that this is the glove of Alencon that your Majesty gave me; in your conscience, now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Give me thy glove, soldier. Look, here is the fellow of it.","'Twas I, indeed, thou promisedst to strike;","And thou hast given me most bitter terms."],"id":757,"m":"Give me your glove, soldier. Look, here is its partner. It was me, in fact, you promised to hit, and you gave me some very harsh words."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["An it please your Majesty, let his neck answer for it, if","there is any martial law in the world."],"id":758,"m":"If it please your Majesty, let his neck answer for it, if there's any military law in the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["How canst thou make me satisfaction?"],"id":759,"m":"How can you make amends to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["All offences, my lord, come from the heart. Never came","any from mine that might offend your Majesty."],"id":760,"m":"All offences, my lord, come from the heart. None ever came from mine that could offend your Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["It was ourself thou didst abuse."],"id":761,"m":"It was us you insulted."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["Your Majesty came not like yourself. You appear'd to me","but as a common man; witness the night, your garments, your","lowliness; and what your Highness suffer'd under that shape, I","beseech you take it for your own fault and not mine; for had you","been as I took you for, I made no offence; therefore, I beseech","your Highness, pardon me."],"id":762,"m":"Your Majesty didn't come as yourself. You seemed to me only a common man; witness the darkness, your clothes, your humble manner; and whatever your Highness suffered in that shape, I beg you to take it as your own fault and not mine; for if you had been what I took you for, I did nothing wrong. So I beg your Highness, pardon me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Here, uncle Exeter, fill this glove with crowns,","And give it to this fellow. Keep it, fellow;","And wear it for an honour in thy cap","Till I do challenge it. Give him his crowns;","And, captain, you must needs be friends with him."],"id":763,"m":"Here, Uncle Exeter, fill this glove with crowns and give it to this fellow. Keep it, fellow, and wear it as an honour in your cap until I challenge it. Give him his crowns. And, captain, you must make friends with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["By this day and this light, the fellow has mettle enough in his","belly. Hold, there is twelve pence for you; and I pray you to","serve God, and keep you out of prawls, and prabbles, and","quarrels, and dissensions, and, I warrant you, it is the better","for you."],"id":764,"m":"By this day and this light, the fellow has courage enough in his belly. Here, there's twelve pence for you; and I beg you to serve God and keep out of brawls and squabbles and quarrels and arguments, and, I promise you, you'll be the better for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAMS","lines":["I will none of your money."],"id":765,"m":"I don't want your money."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["It is with a good will; I can tell you, it will serve you to mend","your shoes. Come, wherefore should you be so pashful? Your","shoes is not so good. 'Tis a good silling, I warrant you, or I","will change it."],"id":766,"m":"It's given with goodwill; I can tell you, it will do to mend your shoes. Come, why should you be so shy? Your shoes aren't that good. It's a good shilling, I promise you, or I'll change it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter an English Herald.","id":767},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Now, herald, are the dead numb'red?"],"id":768,"m":"Now, herald, are the dead counted?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERALD","lines":["Here is the number of the slaught'red French."],"id":769,"m":"Here is the number of the slaughtered French."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What prisoners of good sort are taken, uncle?"],"id":770,"m":"What prisoners of high rank have been taken, uncle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Charles Duke of Orleans, nephew to the King;","John Duke of Bourbon, and Lord Bouciqualt:","Of other lords and barons, knights and squires,","Full fifteen hundred, besides common men."],"id":771,"m":"Charles Duke of Orleans, nephew to the King; John Duke of Bourbon, and Lord Bouciqualt; of other lords and barons, knights and squires, a full fifteen hundred, as well as common men."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["This note doth tell me of ten thousand French","That in the field lie slain; of princes, in this number,","And nobles bearing banners, there lie dead","One hundred twenty-six; added to these,","Of knights, esquires, and gallant gentlemen,","Eight thousand and four hundred; of the which,","Five hundred were but yesterday dubb'd knights;","So that, in these ten thousand they have lost,","There are but sixteen hundred mercenaries;","The rest are princes, barons, lords, knights, squires,","And gentlemen of blood and quality.","The names of those their nobles that lie dead:","Charles Delabreth, High Constable of France;","Jacques of Chatillon, Admiral of France;","The master of the cross-bows, Lord Rambures;","Great Master of France, the brave Sir Guichard Dauphin,","John Duke of Alencon, Anthony Duke of Brabant,","The brother to the Duke of Burgundy,","And Edward Duke of Bar; of lusty earls,","Grandpre and Roussi, Fauconberg and Foix,","Beaumont and Marle, Vaudemont and Lestrale.","Here was a royal fellowship of death!","Where is the number of our English dead?"],"id":772,"m":"This list tells me of ten thousand French who lie dead on the field. Of princes and nobles with their own banners, a hundred and twenty-six lie dead among them; and besides these, eight thousand four hundred knights, squires and gallant gentlemen, five hundred of whom were knighted only yesterday. So among these ten thousand they have lost, there are only sixteen hundred hired soldiers; the rest are princes, barons, lords, knights, squires and gentlemen of good birth and rank. These are the names of their nobles who lie dead: Charles Delabreth, High Constable of France; Jacques of Chatillon, Admiral of France; the master of the crossbowmen, Lord Rambures; the Great Master of France, brave Sir Guichard Dauphin; John Duke of Alencon; Anthony Duke of Brabant, the brother of the Duke of Burgundy; and Edward Duke of Bar; and of vigorous earls, Grandpre and Roussi, Fauconberg and Foix, Beaumont and Marle, Vaudemont and Lestrale. This was a royal company of death! Where is the count of our English dead?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Herald shows him another paper.","id":773},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Edward the Duke of York, the Earl of Suffolk,","Sir Richard Ketly, Davy Gam, esquire;","None else of name; and of all other men","But five and twenty.--O God, thy arm was here;","And not to us, but to thy arm alone,","Ascribe we all! When, without stratagem,","But in plain shock and even play of battle,","Was ever known so great and little loss","On one part and on the other? Take it, God,","For it is none but thine!"],"id":774,"m":"Edward the Duke of York, the Earl of Suffolk, Sir Richard Ketly, Davy Gam, squire; no one else of rank; and of all other men only twenty-five. Oh God, your arm was here; and we give all the credit not to ourselves but to your arm alone! When, without any trick, but in a straight clash and fair fight of battle, was so great a loss on one side and so little on the other ever known? Take it, God, for it is yours alone!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["'Tis wonderful!"],"id":775,"m":"It's amazing!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Come, go we in procession to the village;","And be it death proclaimed through our host","To boast of this or take that praise from God","Which is His only."],"id":776,"m":"Come, let's go to the village in procession; and let it be announced through our army that it is death to boast of this or take from God the praise that belongs to Him alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Is it not lawful, an please your Majesty, to tell how","many is kill'd?"],"id":777,"m":"Isn't it lawful, if it please your Majesty, to say how many were killed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Yes, Captain; but with this acknowledgment,","That God fought for us."],"id":778,"m":"Yes, captain, but with this acknowledgement: that God fought for us."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Yes, my conscience, He did us great good."],"id":779,"m":"Yes, on my conscience, He did us great good."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Do we all holy rites.","Let there be sung Non nobis and Te Deum,","The dead with charity enclos'd in clay,","And then to Calais; and to England then,","Where ne'er from France arriv'd more happy men."],"id":780,"m":"Let us perform all the holy rites. Let \"Non nobis\" and \"Te Deum\" be sung, the dead buried lovingly in the earth, and then to Calais, and then to England, where happier men never arrived from France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":781},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"France. The English camp.","id":782},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Chorus.","id":783},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","lines":["Vouchsafe to those that have not read the story,","That I may prompt them; and of such as have,","I humbly pray them to admit the excuse","Of time, of numbers, and due course of things,","Which cannot in their huge and proper life","Be here presented. Now we bear the King","Toward Calais; grant him there; there seen,","Heave him away upon your winged thoughts","Athwart the sea. Behold, the English beach","Pales in the flood with men, with wives and boys,","Whose shouts and claps out-voice the deep-mouth'd sea,","Which like a mighty whiffler 'fore the King","Seems to prepare his way. So let him land,","And solemnly see him set on to London.","So swift a pace hath thought that even now","You may imagine him upon Blackheath,","Where that his lords desire him to have borne","His bruised helmet and his bended sword","Before him through the city. He forbids it,","Being free from vainness and self-glorious pride;","Giving full trophy, signal, and ostent","Quite from himself to God. But now behold,","In the quick forge and working-house of thought,","How London doth pour out her citizens!","The mayor and all his brethren in best sort,","Like to the senators of the antique Rome,","With the plebeians swarming at their heels,","Go forth and fetch their conquering Caesar in;","As, by a lower but loving likelihood,","Were now the general of our gracious empress,","As in good time he may, from Ireland coming,","Bringing rebellion broached on his sword,","How many would the peaceful city quit,","To welcome him! Much more, and much more cause,","Did they this Harry. Now in London place him;","As yet the lamentation of the French","Invites the King of England's stay at home,--","The Emperor's coming in behalf of France,","To order peace between them;--and omit","All the occurrences, whatever chanc'd,","Till Harry's back-return again to France.","There must we bring him; and myself have play'd","The interim, by rememb'ring you 'tis past.","Then brook abridgment, and your eyes advance","After your thoughts, straight back again to France."],"id":784,"m":"Allow those who have not read the story to let me prompt them; and as for those who have, I humbly ask them to excuse our handling of time, of numbers and of the proper course of events, which cannot be shown here in their huge, true life. Now we carry the King towards Calais. Imagine him there; and having seen him there, lift him on your winged thoughts across the sea. Look, the English beach is fenced in at the water's edge with men, wives and boys, whose shouts and clapping drown out the deep-voiced sea, which, like a mighty usher clearing a path before the King, seems to prepare his way. So let him land, and see him set off solemnly for London. Thought moves so fast that even now you can imagine him on Blackheath, where his lords want him to have his dented helmet and his bent sword carried before him through the city. He forbids it, being free of vanity and self-glorifying pride, and gives all the trophies, signs and display away from himself to God. But now see, in the quick forge and workshop of thought, how London pours out her citizens! The mayor and all his fellow aldermen in their best clothes, like the senators of ancient Rome, with the common people swarming at their heels, go out and bring in their conquering Caesar. Just as, to take a lesser but loving comparison, if the general of our gracious queen were now coming from Ireland, as in good time he may, with rebellion skewered on his sword, how many would leave the peaceful city to welcome him! Much more, and with much more reason, did they welcome this Harry. Now place him in London, while the grief of the French still keeps the King of England at home, with the Emperor coming on France's behalf to arrange peace between them; and skip over everything that happened, whatever it was, until Harry's return to France. That's where we must bring him; and I have acted out the interval by reminding you that it has passed. So put up with the shortening, and let your eyes follow your thoughts straight back again to France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":785},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fluellen and Gower.","id":786},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Nay, that's right; but why wear you your leek to-day?","Saint Davy's day is past."],"id":787,"m":"Yes, that's right; but why are you wearing your leek today? Saint David's Day is over."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["There is occasions and causes why and wherefore in all","things. I will tell you asse my friend, Captain Gower. The","rascally, scald, beggarly, lousy, pragging knave, Pistol, which","you and yourself and all the world know to be no petter than a","fellow, look you now, of no merits, he is come to me and prings","me pread and salt yesterday, look you, and bid me eat my leek.","It was in a place where I could not breed no contention with him;","but I will be so bold as to wear it in my cap till I see him once","again, and then I will tell him a little piece of my desires."],"id":788,"m":"There are occasions and causes, why and wherefore, in all things. I'll tell you, as my friend, Captain Gower. The rascally, scabby, beggarly, lousy, bragging rogue Pistol, whom you and yourself and all the world know to be no better than a fellow, look you now, of no merit, came to me yesterday and brought me bread and salt, look you, and told me to eat my leek. It was in a place where I couldn't start a quarrel with him; but I'll be so bold as to wear it in my cap until I see him again, and then I'll tell him a little of what I'd like."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pistol.","id":789},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Why, here he comes, swelling like a turkey-cock."],"id":790,"m":"Why, here he comes, puffed up like a turkey-cock."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["'Tis no matter for his swellings nor his turkey-cocks. God","pless you, Aunchient Pistol! you scurvy, lousy knave, God","pless you!"],"id":791,"m":"His puffings and his turkey-cocks don't matter. God bless you, Ensign Pistol! You scurvy, lousy rogue, God bless you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Ha! art thou bedlam? Dost thou thirst, base Troyan,","To have me fold up Parca's fatal web?","Hence! I am qualmish at the smell of leek."],"id":792,"m":"Ha! Are you mad? Do you thirst, you low Trojan, to have me fold up the fatal web of the Fates? Away! The smell of leek makes me sick."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["I peseech you heartily, scurfy, lousy knave, at my desires,","and my requests, and my petitions, to eat, look you, this","leek. Because, look you, you do not love it, nor your","affections and your appetites and your digestions doo's not","agree with it, I would desire you to eat it."],"id":793,"m":"I beg you heartily, scurvy, lousy rogue, at my wish, my request and my petition, to eat, look you, this leek. Because, look you, you don't love it, and your tastes and your appetites and your digestion don't agree with it, I would like you to eat it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Not for Cadwallader and all his goats."],"id":794,"m":"Not for Cadwallader and all his goats."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["There is one goat for you. [Strikes him] Will you be so","good, scald knave, as eat it?"],"id":795,"m":"There's one goat for you. (Hits him) Will you be so good, you scabby rogue, as to eat it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Base Troyan, thou shalt die."],"id":796,"m":"Low Trojan, you shall die."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["You say very true, scald knave, when God's will is. I will","desire you to live in the mean time, and eat your victuals.","Come, there is sauce for it. [Strikes him] You call'd me","yesterday mountain-squire; but I will make you to-day a","squire of low degree. I pray you, fall to; if you can mock","a leek, you can eat a leek."],"id":797,"m":"You say very true, scabby rogue, when God wills it. I'd like you to live in the meantime and eat your food. Come, here's some sauce for it. (Hits him) Yesterday you called me a mountain squire, but today I'll make you a squire of low degree. Please, tuck in; if you can mock a leek, you can eat a leek."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Enough, captain; you have astonish'd him."],"id":798,"m":"Enough, captain; you've stunned him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["I say, I will make him eat some part of my leek, or I will","peat his pate four days. Bite, I pray you; it is good for","your green wound and your ploody coxcomb."],"id":799,"m":"I tell you, I'll make him eat some of my leek, or I'll beat his head for four days. Bite, please; it's good for your fresh wound and your bloody skull."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Must I bite?"],"id":800,"m":"Must I bite?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Yes, certainly, and out of doubt and out of question","too, and ambiguities."],"id":801,"m":"Yes, certainly, and beyond doubt, and beyond question too, and ambiguities."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["By this leek, I will most horribly revenge. I eat and","eat, I swear--"],"id":802,"m":"By this leek, I will take horrible revenge. I eat, and eat, I swear..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Eat, I pray you. Will you have some more sauce to","your leek? There is not enough leek to swear by."],"id":803,"m":"Eat, please. Do you want more sauce with your leek? There's not enough leek to swear by."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Quiet thy cudgel; thou dost see I eat."],"id":804,"m":"Keep your stick still; you can see I'm eating."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Much good do you, scald knave, heartily. Nay, pray you,","throw none away; the skin is good for your broken coxcomb.","When you take occasions to see leeks hereafter, I pray you,","mock at 'em; that is all."],"id":805,"m":"May it do you much good, scabby rogue, heartily. No, please, don't throw any away; the skin is good for your broken head. When you get the chance to see leeks in future, please, mock them; that's all."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Good."],"id":806,"m":"Good."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Ay, leeks is good. Hold you, there is a groat to heal","your pate."],"id":807,"m":"Yes, leeks are good. Here, there's fourpence to heal your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Me a groat!"],"id":808,"m":"Fourpence to me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["Yes, verily and in truth you shall take it; or I have","another leek in my pocket, which you shall eat."],"id":809,"m":"Yes, truly and indeed you shall take it; or I have another leek in my pocket, which you shall eat."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["I take thy groat in earnest of revenge."],"id":810,"m":"I take your fourpence as a down payment on revenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUELLEN","lines":["If I owe you anything I will pay you in cudgels. You","shall be a woodmonger, and buy nothing of me but cudgels.","God be wi' you, and keep you, and heal your pate."],"id":811,"m":"If I owe you anything, I'll pay you in cudgels. You can be a timber merchant and buy nothing from me but cudgels. God be with you, and keep you, and heal your head."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":812},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["All hell shall stir for this."],"id":813,"m":"All hell will be stirred up for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["Go, go; you are a counterfeit cowardly knave. Will you mock","at an ancient tradition, begun upon an honourable respect, and","worn as a memorable trophy of predeceased valour, and dare not","avouch in your deeds any of your words? I have seen you gleeking","and galling at this gentleman twice or thrice. You thought,","because he could not speak English in the native garb, he could","not therefore handle an English cudgel. You find it otherwise;","and henceforth let a Welsh correction teach you a good English","condition. Fare ye well."],"id":814,"m":"Go on, go on; you're a fake, cowardly rogue. Will you mock an ancient tradition, begun for an honourable reason and worn as a memorable trophy of the courage of the dead, and not dare to back up any of your words with deeds? I've seen you jeering and taunting this gentleman two or three times. You thought, because he couldn't speak English the way natives do, he couldn't handle an English cudgel. You find otherwise; and from now on let a Welsh beating teach you good English manners. Farewell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":815},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Doth Fortune play the huswife with me now?","News have I, that my Doll is dead i' the spital","Of malady of France;","And there my rendezvous is quite cut off.","Old I do wax; and from my weary limbs","Honour is cudgell'd. Well, bawd I'll turn,","And something lean to cutpurse of quick hand.","To England will I steal, and there I'll steal;","And patches will I get unto these cudgell'd scars,","And swear I got them in the Gallia wars."],"id":816,"m":"Is Fortune playing the hussy with me now? I've had news that my Doll has died in the hospital of the French disease, and so my refuge there is quite cut off. I'm growing old, and honour has been beaten out of my tired limbs. Well, I'll become a pimp, and turn a bit to quick-handed pickpocketing. I'll sneak back to England, and there I'll steal; and I'll put patches on these cudgel scars and swear I got them in the French wars."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":817},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"France. A royal palace at Troyes.","id":818},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, at one door, King Henry, Exeter, Bedford, Gloucester, Warwick, Westmoreland, and other Lords; at another, the French King, Queen Isabel, the Princess Katharine, Alice, and other Ladies; the Duke of Burgundy, and other French.","id":819},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Peace to this meeting, wherefore we are met!","Unto our brother France, and to our sister,","Health and fair time of day; joy and good wishes","To our most fair and princely cousin Katharine;","And, as a branch and member of this royalty,","By whom this great assembly is contriv'd,","We do salute you, Duke of Burgundy;","And, princes French, and peers, health to you all!"],"id":820,"m":"Peace to this meeting, which is why we have met! To our brother of France and to our sister, health and good day; joy and good wishes to our most fair and princely cousin Katharine; and, as a branch and member of this royal family, by whom this great assembly was arranged, we greet you, Duke of Burgundy; and, French princes and lords, health to you all!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Right joyous are we to behold your face,","Most worthy brother England; fairly met!","So are you, princes English, every one."],"id":821,"m":"We are delighted to see your face, most worthy brother of England; well met! And so are you, English princes, every one."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ISABEL","lines":["So happy be the issue, brother England,","Of this good day and of this gracious meeting","As we are now glad to behold your eyes;","Your eyes, which hitherto have borne in them","Against the French that met them in their bent","The fatal balls of murdering basilisks.","The venom of such looks, we fairly hope,","Have lost their quality; and that this day","Shall change all griefs and quarrels into love."],"id":822,"m":"May the outcome of this good day and this gracious meeting be as happy, brother of England, as we are glad now to see your eyes; your eyes, which until now have carried, against the French who met their aim, the deadly glances of murderous basilisks. We truly hope the poison of such looks has lost its power, and that this day will change all griefs and quarrels into love."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["To cry amen to that, thus we appear."],"id":823,"m":"We have come here to say amen to that."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ISABEL","lines":["You English princes all, I do salute you."],"id":824,"m":"I greet you, all you English princes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["My duty to you both, on equal love,","Great Kings of France and England! That I have labour'd,","With all my wits, my pains, and strong endeavours,","To bring your most imperial Majesties","Unto this bar and royal interview,","Your mightiness on both parts best can witness.","Since then my office hath so far prevail'd","That, face to face and royal eye to eye,","You have congreeted, let it not disgrace me","If I demand, before this royal view,","What rub or what impediment there is,","Why that the naked, poor, and mangled Peace,","Dear nurse of arts, plenties, and joyful births,","Should not in this best garden of the world,","Our fertile France, put up her lovely visage?","Alas, she hath from France too long been chas'd,","And all her husbandry doth lie on heaps,","Corrupting in it own fertility.","Her vine, the merry cheerer of the heart,","Unpruned dies; her hedges even-pleach'd,","Like prisoners wildly overgrown with hair,","Put forth disorder'd twigs; her fallow leas","The darnel, hemlock, and rank fumitory,","Doth root upon, while that the coulter rusts","That should deracinate such savagery;","The even mead, that erst brought sweetly forth","The freckled cowslip, burnet, and green clover,","Wanting the scythe, all uncorrected, rank,","Conceives by idleness, and nothing teems","But hateful docks, rough thistles, kexes, burs,","Losing both beauty and utility;","And as our vineyards, fallows, meads, and hedges,","Defective in their natures, grow to wildness.","Even so our houses and ourselves and children","Have lost, or do not learn for want of time,","The sciences that should become our country;","But grow like savages,--as soldiers will","That nothing do but meditate on blood,--","To swearing and stern looks, diffus'd attire,","And everything that seems unnatural.","Which to reduce into our former favour","You are assembled; and my speech entreats","That I may know the let, why gentle Peace","Should not expel these inconveniences","And bless us with her former qualities."],"id":825,"m":"My duty to you both, with equal love, great Kings of France and England! Your mightinesses on both sides can best witness that I have worked, with all my wits, my effort and strong endeavours, to bring your most royal Majesties to this court and royal meeting. Since, then, my work has succeeded so far that you have greeted each other face to face and royal eye to eye, don't think it a disgrace in me if I ask, before this royal company, what obstacle or hindrance there is to stop poor, naked, mangled Peace, dear nurse of the arts, of plenty and of joyful births, from showing her lovely face in this best garden of the world, our fertile France. Alas, she has been chased out of France too long, and all her farming lies in heaps, rotting in its own richness. Her vine, the merry cheerer of the heart, dies unpruned; her hedges, once neatly woven, put out disordered twigs like prisoners wildly overgrown with hair; on her unploughed fields darnel, hemlock and rank fumitory take root, while the ploughshare that should uproot such wild growth rusts. The level meadow, which once sweetly produced the speckled cowslip, burnet and green clover, lacking the scythe, all untended and overgrown, breeds through idleness and produces nothing but hateful docks, rough thistles, hemlock stalks and burrs, losing both beauty and usefulness. And just as our vineyards, fallow fields, meadows and hedges, failing in their nature, grow wild, so our households and we and our children have lost, or do not learn for lack of time, the skills that should grace our country, but grow like savages, as soldiers do who think of nothing but blood, to swearing and grim looks, untidy clothes and everything that seems unnatural. You are assembled to restore us to our former grace; and my speech begs to know the obstacle that stops gentle Peace from driving out these harms and blessing us with her former gifts."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["If, Duke of Burgundy, you would the peace,","Whose want gives growth to the imperfections","Which you have cited, you must buy that peace","With full accord to all our just demands;","Whose tenours and particular effects","You have enschedul'd briefly in your hands."],"id":826,"m":"If you want peace, Duke of Burgundy, the lack of which breeds the faults you have listed, you must buy that peace by fully agreeing to all our just demands, whose terms and particular details you have briefly written down in your hands."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["The King hath heard them; to the which as yet","There is no answer made."],"id":827,"m":"The King has heard them, and so far has given no answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Well, then, the peace,","Which you before so urg'd, lies in his answer."],"id":828,"m":"Well then, the peace you urged so strongly just now depends on his answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["I have but with a cursorary eye","O'erglanc'd the articles. Pleaseth your Grace","To appoint some of your council presently","To sit with us once more, with better heed","To re-survey them, we will suddenly","Pass our accept and peremptory answer."],"id":829,"m":"I have only glanced over the articles with a hasty eye. If it please your Grace to appoint some of your council at once to sit with us again and go over them more carefully, we will quickly give our acceptance and final answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Brother, we shall. Go, uncle Exeter,","And brother Clarence, and you, brother Gloucester,","Warwick, and Huntington, go with the King;","And take with you free power to ratify,","Augment, or alter, as your wisdoms best","Shall see advantageable for our dignity,","Anything in or out of our demands,","And we'll consign thereto. Will you, fair sister,","Go with the princes, or stay here with us?"],"id":830,"m":"Brother, we shall. Go, Uncle Exeter, and brother Clarence, and you, brother Gloucester, Warwick and Huntingdon, go with the King; and take with you full power to confirm, add to or change anything in or outside our demands, as your wisdom sees best for our dignity, and we'll put our seal to it. Will you, fair sister, go with the princes, or stay here with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ISABEL","lines":["Our gracious brother, I will go with them.","Haply a woman's voice may do some good,","When articles too nicely urg'd be stood on."],"id":831,"m":"Our gracious brother, I'll go with them. Perhaps a woman's voice may do some good when they argue over articles too fussily."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Yet leave our cousin Katharine here with us:","She is our capital demand, compris'd","Within the fore-rank of our articles."],"id":832,"m":"But leave our cousin Katharine here with us. She is our chief demand, placed in the front rank of our articles."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ISABEL","lines":["She hath good leave."],"id":833,"m":"She has our permission."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all except Henry, Katharine and Alice.","id":834},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Fair Katharine, and most fair,","Will you vouchsafe to teach a soldier terms","Such as will enter at a lady's ear","And plead his love-suit to her gentle heart?"],"id":835,"m":"Fair Katharine, and most fair, will you be so kind as to teach a soldier words that will enter a lady's ear and plead his love to her gentle heart?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Your Majesty shall mock me; I cannot speak your","England."],"id":836,"m":"Your Majesty will laugh at me; I can't speak your England."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["O fair Katharine, if you will love me soundly with your","French heart, I will be glad to hear you confess it brokenly","with your English tongue. Do you like me, Kate?"],"id":837,"m":"Oh, fair Katharine, if you will love me soundly with your French heart, I'll be glad to hear you admit it in broken English. Do you like me, Kate?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Pardonnez-moi, I cannot tell wat is \"like me.\""],"id":838,"m":"Excuse me, I don't know what \"like me\" is."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["An angel is like you, Kate, and you are like an angel."],"id":839,"m":"An angel is like you, Kate, and you are like an angel."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Que dit-il? Que je suis semblable a les anges?"],"id":840,"m":"(In French) What does he say? That I am like the angels?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Oui, vraiment, sauf votre grace, ainsi dit-il."],"id":841,"m":"(In French) Yes, truly, saving your grace, that's what he says."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I said so, dear Katharine; and I must not blush to affirm it."],"id":842,"m":"I said so, dear Katharine, and I mustn't blush to say it again."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["O bon Dieu! les langues des hommes sont pleines de tromperies."],"id":843,"m":"(In French) Oh good God! The tongues of men are full of deceits."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What says she, fair one? That the tongues of men are full of","deceits?"],"id":844,"m":"What does she say, fair lady? That the tongues of men are full of deceit?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Oui, dat de tongues of de mans is be full of deceits: dat is de","Princess."],"id":845,"m":"Yes, that the tongues of the men are full of deceits: that is what the Princess says."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["The Princess is the better Englishwoman. I' faith, Kate, my","wooing is fit for thy understanding: I am glad thou canst","speak no better English; for if thou couldst, thou wouldst","find me such a plain king that thou wouldst think I had sold my","farm to buy my crown. I know no ways to mince it in love, but","directly to say, \"I love you\"; then if you urge me farther than","to say, \"Do you in faith?\" I wear out my suit. Give me your","answer; i' faith, do; and so clap hands and a bargain. How say","you, lady?"],"id":846,"m":"The Princess is the better Englishwoman. Honestly, Kate, my wooing suits your understanding: I'm glad you can't speak better English; for if you could, you'd find me such a plain king that you'd think I had sold my farm to buy my crown. I know no way to dress it up in love except to say straight out, \"I love you.\" Then if you push me further than \"Do you really?\", my wooing is worn out. Give me your answer; honestly, do; and then shake hands on a bargain. What do you say, lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Sauf votre honneur, me understand well."],"id":847,"m":"Saving your honour, me understand well."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Marry, if you would put me to verses, or to dance for your","sake, Kate, why you undid me; for the one, I have neither","words nor measure, and for the other I have no strength in","measure, yet a reasonable measure in strength. If I could win a","lady at leap-frog, or by vaulting into my saddle with my armour","on my back, under the correction of bragging be it spoken, I","should quickly leap into a wife. Or if I might buffet for my","love, or bound my horse for her favours, I could lay on like a","butcher and sit like a jack-an-apes, never off. But, before God,","Kate, I cannot look greenly, nor gasp out my eloquence, nor I","have no cunning in protestation; only downright oaths, which I","never use till urg'd, nor never break for urging. If thou canst","love a fellow of this temper, Kate, whose face is not worth","sunburning, that never looks in his glass for love of anything","he sees there, let thine eye be thy cook. I speak to thee plain","soldier. If thou canst love me for this, take me; if not, to say","to thee that I shall die, is true; but for thy love, by the Lord,","no; yet I love thee too. And while thou liv'st, dear Kate, take a","fellow of plain and uncoined constancy; for he perforce must do","thee right, because he hath not the gift to woo in other places;","for these fellows of infinite tongue, that can rhyme themselves","into ladies' favours, they do always reason themselves out again.","What! a speaker is but a prater: a rhyme is but a ballad. A good","leg will fall; a straight back will stoop; a black beard will turn","white; a curl'd pate will grow bald; a fair face will wither; a","full eye will wax hollow; but a good heart, Kate, is the sun and","the moon; or rather the sun and not the moon; for it shines bright","and never changes, but keeps his course truly. If thou would have","such a one, take me; and take me, take a soldier; take a soldier,","take a king. And what say'st thou then to my love? Speak, my fair,","and fairly, I pray thee."],"id":848,"m":"Indeed, if you made me write verses or dance for your sake, Kate, you'd ruin me; for the first, I have neither the words nor the metre, and for the second, I have no strength in measure, though a reasonable measure of strength. If I could win a lady at leapfrog, or by vaulting into my saddle with my armour on my back, I'd quickly leap into a wife, if I may say so without boasting. Or if I could box for my love, or make my horse prance for her favours, I could lay on like a butcher and sit as tight as a monkey, never thrown. But, before God, Kate, I can't look lovesick or gasp out my eloquence, and I have no skill in making vows; only plain oaths, which I never use till I'm pushed, and never break however I'm pushed. If you can love a fellow of this kind, Kate, whose face isn't worth sunburning, who never looks in his mirror for love of anything he sees there, let your eye dress me up like a cook. I speak to you as a plain soldier. If you can love me for this, take me; if not, to tell you I'll die is true, but for love of you, by the Lord, no; and yet I do love you. And as long as you live, dear Kate, take a man of plain, uncounterfeited constancy; for he has to treat you right, because he hasn't the gift to woo elsewhere. These fellows with endless tongues, who can rhyme their way into ladies' favours, always reason their way out again. What! A talker is only a chatterer; a rhyme is only a ballad. A good leg will shrink; a straight back will bend; a black beard will turn white; a curly head will go bald; a fair face will wither; a bright eye will grow hollow; but a good heart, Kate, is the sun and the moon, or rather the sun and not the moon, for it shines bright and never changes, but keeps its course truly. If you want such a one, take me; and if you take me, you take a soldier; take a soldier, take a king. And what do you say then to my love? Speak, my fair one, and kindly, I beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Is it possible dat I should love de enemy of France?"],"id":849,"m":"Is it possible that I should love the enemy of France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["No; it is not possible you should love the enemy of France, Kate;","but, in loving me, you should love the friend of France; for I","love France so well that I will not part with a village of it, I","will have it all mine; and, Kate, when France is mine and I am","yours, then yours is France and you are mine."],"id":850,"m":"No, it isn't possible for you to love the enemy of France, Kate; but in loving me you would love the friend of France; for I love France so much that I won't part with a single village of it; I'll have it all as mine. And, Kate, when France is mine and I am yours, then France is yours and you are mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["I cannot tell wat is dat."],"id":851,"m":"I don't know what that is."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["No, Kate? I will tell thee in French; which I am sure will hang","upon my tongue like a new-married wife about her husband's","neck, hardly to be shook off. Je quand sur le possession de","France, et quand vous avez le possession de moi,--let me see,","what then? Saint Denis be my speed!--donc votre est France","et vous etes mienne. It is as easy for me, Kate, to conquer the","kingdom as to speak so much more French. I shall never move","thee in French, unless it be to laugh at me."],"id":852,"m":"No, Kate? I'll tell you in French, which I'm sure will hang on my tongue like a newly married wife round her husband's neck, hard to shake off. (In bad French) \"When I have the possession of France, and when you have the possession of me\", let me see, what then? Saint Denis help me! \"then France is yours and you are mine.\" It would be as easy for me, Kate, to conquer the kingdom as to speak that much more French. I'll never move you in French, except to laugh at me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Sauf votre honneur, le Francais que vous parlez, il est meilleur","que l'Anglois lequel je parle."],"id":853,"m":"(In French) Saving your honour, the French you speak is better than the English I speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["No, faith, is't not, Kate; but thy speaking of my tongue, and I","thine, most truly-falsely, must needs be granted to be much at","one. But, Kate, dost thou understand thus much English: canst","thou love me?"],"id":854,"m":"No, honestly, it isn't, Kate; but your speaking of my language and mine of yours, very truly wrong, must be admitted to be much the same. But, Kate, do you understand this much English: can you love me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["I cannot tell."],"id":855,"m":"I cannot tell."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Can any of your neighbours tell, Kate? I'll ask them. Come, I","know thou lovest me; and at night, when you come into your","closet, you'll question this gentlewoman about me; and I know,","Kate, you will to her dispraise those parts in me that you love","with your heart. But, good Kate, mock me mercifully; the","rather, gentle princess, because I love thee cruelly. If ever","thou beest mine, Kate, as I have a saving faith within me tells","me thou shalt, I get thee with scambling, and thou must therefore","needs prove a good soldier-breeder. Shall not thou and I, between","Saint Denis and Saint George, compound a boy, half French, half","English, that shall go to Constantinople and take the Turk by the","beard? Shall we not? What say'st thou, my fair flower-de-luce?"],"id":856,"m":"Can any of your neighbours tell, Kate? I'll ask them. Come, I know you love me; and at night, when you go into your private room, you'll ask this lady about me; and I know, Kate, you'll criticise to her the parts of me that you love in your heart. But, good Kate, mock me mercifully, all the more, gentle princess, because I love you cruelly. If you are ever mine, Kate, as a saving faith inside me tells me you will be, I'll have won you by fighting, so you are bound to be a good mother of soldiers. Shall you and I, between Saint Denis and Saint George, make a boy, half French, half English, who will go to Constantinople and take the Turk by the beard? Shan't we? What do you say, my fair lily?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["I do not know dat."],"id":857,"m":"I don't know that."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["No; 'tis hereafter to know, but now to promise. Do but now","promise, Kate, you will endeavour for your French part of","such a boy; and for my English moiety, take the word of a king","and a bachelor. How answer you, la plus belle Katherine du monde,","mon tres cher et divin deesse?"],"id":858,"m":"No; that's for later to know, but now to promise. Just promise now, Kate, that you'll do your best for the French half of such a boy; and for my English half, take the word of a king and a bachelor. What do you answer, (in French) the most beautiful Katharine in the world, my very dear and divine goddess?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Your Majestee ave fausse French enough to deceive de most","sage damoiselle dat is en France."],"id":859,"m":"Your Majesty has false French enough to deceive the wisest young lady in France."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Now, fie upon my false French! By mine honour, in true English,","I love thee, Kate; by which honour I dare not swear thou lovest","me; yet my blood begins to flatter me that thou dost,","notwithstanding the poor and untempering effect of my visage.","Now, beshrew my father's ambition! he was thinking of civil wars","when he got me; therefore was I created with a stubborn outside,","with an aspect of iron, that, when I come to woo ladies, I fright","them. But, in faith, Kate, the elder I wax, the better I shall","appear. My comfort is, that old age, that ill layer up of beauty,","can do no more spoil upon my face. Thou hast me, if thou hast me,","at the worst; and thou shalt wear me, if thou wear me, better and","better; and therefore tell me, most fair Katharine, will you have","me? Put off your maiden blushes; avouch the thoughts of your heart","with the looks of an empress; take me by the hand, and say, Harry","of England, I am thine; which word thou shalt no sooner bless mine","ear withal, but I will tell thee aloud, England is thine, Ireland","is thine, France is thine, and Henry Plantagenet is thine; who,","though I speak it before his face, if he be not fellow with the","best king, thou shalt find the best king of good fellows.","Come, your answer in broken music; for thy voice is music and thy","English broken; therefore, queen of all, Katharine, break thy mind","to me in broken English. Wilt thou have me?"],"id":860,"m":"Now, shame on my false French! On my honour, in true English, I love you, Kate; by that honour I don't dare swear you love me, and yet my blood begins to flatter me that you do, in spite of the poor and unappealing effect of my face. Now, curse my father's ambition! He was thinking of civil wars when he fathered me, so I was made with a stubborn outside and an iron face, so that when I come to woo ladies I frighten them. But honestly, Kate, the older I get, the better I'll look. My comfort is that old age, that bad keeper of beauty, can do no more damage to my face. You have me, if you have me, at my worst; and you'll wear me, if you wear me, better and better. So tell me, most fair Katharine, will you have me? Put off your girlish blushes; declare the thoughts of your heart with the looks of an empress; take me by the hand and say, \"Harry of England, I am yours\"; and no sooner will you bless my ear with that word than I'll tell you aloud, \"England is yours, Ireland is yours, France is yours, and Henry Plantagenet is yours\", who, though I say it to his face, if he isn't equal to the best king, you'll find him the best king of good fellows. Come, your answer in broken music; for your voice is music and your English is broken; so, queen of all, Katharine, open your mind to me in broken English. Will you have me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Dat is as it shall please de roi mon pere."],"id":861,"m":"That is as it shall please (in French) the king my father."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Nay, it will please him well, Kate; it shall please him, Kate."],"id":862,"m":"No, it will please him well, Kate; it shall please him, Kate."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Den it sall also content me."],"id":863,"m":"Then it shall also content me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Upon that I kiss your hand, and call you my queen."],"id":864,"m":"On that I kiss your hand and call you my queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Laissez, mon seigneur, laissez, laissez! Ma foi, je ne veux point","que vous abaissez votre grandeur en baisant la main d'une indigne","serviteur. Excusez-moi, je vous supplie, mon tres-puissant seigneur."],"id":865,"m":"(In French) Stop, my lord, stop, stop! Goodness, I don't want you to lower your greatness by kissing the hand of an unworthy servant. Excuse me, I beg you, my most powerful lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Then I will kiss your lips, Kate."],"id":866,"m":"Then I'll kiss your lips, Kate."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Les dames et demoiselles pour etre baisees devant leur noces, il","n'est pas la coutume de France."],"id":867,"m":"(In French) For ladies and young women to be kissed before their weddings is not the custom in France."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Madame my interpreter, what says she?"],"id":868,"m":"Madam my interpreter, what does she say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Dat it is not be de fashion pour les ladies of France,--I cannot","tell wat is baiser en Anglish."],"id":869,"m":"That it is not the fashion for the ladies of France... I don't know what \"baiser\" is in English."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["To kiss."],"id":870,"m":"To kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Your Majestee entendre bettre que moi."],"id":871,"m":"Your Majesty understands better than me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["It is not a fashion for the maids in France to kiss before they","are married, would she say?"],"id":872,"m":"Does she mean it isn't the fashion for girls in France to kiss before they're married?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALICE","lines":["Oui, vraiment."],"id":873,"m":"(In French) Yes, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["O Kate, nice customs curtsy to great kings. Dear Kate, you and I","cannot be confined within the weak list of a country's fashion.","We are the makers of manners, Kate; and the liberty that follows","our places stops the mouth of all find-faults, as I will do yours,","for upholding the nice fashion of your country in denying me a kiss;","therefore, patiently and yielding. [Kissing her] You have","witchcraft in your lips, Kate; there is more eloquence in a sugar","touch of them than in the tongues of the French council; and they","should sooner persuade Harry of England than a general petition of","monarchs. Here comes your father."],"id":874,"m":"Oh Kate, fussy customs bow to great kings. Dear Kate, you and I can't be confined within the weak limits of a country's fashion. We are the makers of manners, Kate; and the freedom that goes with our position stops the mouths of all fault-finders, as I will stop yours for keeping up the fussy custom of your country in refusing me a kiss; so, be patient and give in. (Kisses her) You have witchcraft in your lips, Kate; there is more eloquence in a sweet touch of them than in the tongues of the whole French council; and they would sooner persuade Harry of England than a joint petition from all the kings. Here comes your father."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter the French Power and the English Lords.","id":875},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["God save your Majesty! My royal cousin, teach you our princess","English?"],"id":876,"m":"God save your Majesty! My royal cousin, are you teaching our princess English?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I would have her learn, my fair cousin, how perfectly I love her;","and that is good English."],"id":877,"m":"I would have her learn, my fair cousin, how perfectly I love her; and that is good English."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Is she not apt?"],"id":878,"m":"Isn't she quick to learn?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Our tongue is rough, coz, and my condition is not smooth; so","that, having neither the voice nor the heart of flattery about","me, I cannot so conjure up the spirit of love in her, that he","will appear in his true likeness."],"id":879,"m":"Our language is rough, cousin, and my nature isn't smooth; so, having neither the voice nor the heart of a flatterer, I can't conjure up the spirit of love in her so that he appears in his true shape."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Pardon the frankness of my mirth, if I answer you for that. If","you would conjure in her, you must make a circle; if conjure up","Love in her in his true likeness, he must appear naked and blind.","Can you blame her then, being a maid yet ros'd over with the virgin","crimson of modesty, if she deny the appearance of a naked blind boy","in her naked seeing self? It were, my lord, a hard condition for a","maid to consign to."],"id":880,"m":"Forgive my frank joking if I answer you on that. If you want to conjure in her, you must draw a magic circle; if you want to conjure up Love in her in his true shape, he must appear naked and blind. Can you blame her, then, being still a virgin, rosy with the crimson of maiden modesty, if she refuses to let a naked blind boy appear in her naked, seeing self? It would be a hard condition, my lord, for a virgin to agree to."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Yet they do wink and yield, as love is blind and enforces."],"id":881,"m":"Yet they do shut their eyes and give in, since love is blind and forceful."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["They are then excus'd, my lord, when they see not what they do."],"id":882,"m":"Then they are excused, my lord, when they don't see what they are doing."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Then, good my lord, teach your cousin to consent winking."],"id":883,"m":"Then, my good lord, teach your cousin to consent with her eyes shut."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["I will wink on her to consent, my lord, if you will teach her to","know my meaning; for maids, well summer'd and warm kept, are like","flies at Bartholomew-tide, blind, though they have their eyes; and","then they will endure handling, which before would not abide","looking on."],"id":884,"m":"I'll wink at her to consent, my lord, if you'll teach her to understand my meaning; for girls well summered and kept warm are like flies at the end of August, blind though they have eyes; and then they will put up with being handled, when before they couldn't bear to be looked at."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["This moral ties me over to time and a hot summer; and so I shall","catch the fly, your cousin, in the latter end, and she must be blind","too."],"id":885,"m":"That lesson ties me to time and a hot summer; so I shall catch the fly, your cousin, at the end of it, and she must be blind too."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["As love is, my lord, before it loves."],"id":886,"m":"As love is, my lord, before it loves."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["It is so; and you may, some of you, thank love for my blindness,","who cannot see many a fair French city for one fair French maid","that stands in my way."],"id":887,"m":"That's true; and some of you can thank love for my blindness, since I can't see many a fair French city because of one fair French girl standing in my way."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Yes, my lord, you see them perspectively, the cities turn'd into","a maid; for they are all girdled with maiden walls that war hath","never ent'red."],"id":888,"m":"Yes, my lord, you see them as in a trick picture, the cities turned into a girl; for they are all surrounded by virgin walls that war has never entered."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Shall Kate be my wife?"],"id":889,"m":"Shall Kate be my wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["So please you."],"id":890,"m":"If you please."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I am content, so the maiden cities you talk of may wait on her;","so the maid that stood in the way for my wish shall show me the","way to my will."],"id":891,"m":"I am content, as long as the virgin cities you speak of come with her; so the girl who stood in the way of my wish shall show me the way to what I want."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["We have consented to all terms of reason."],"id":892,"m":"We have agreed to all reasonable terms."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Is't so, my lords of England?"],"id":893,"m":"Is that so, my lords of England?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["The king hath granted every article;","His daughter first, and then in sequel all,","According to their firm proposed natures."],"id":894,"m":"The King has granted every article: his daughter first, and then all the rest in order, exactly as they were firmly proposed."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Only he hath not yet subscribed this: where your Majesty demands,","that the King of France, having any occasion to write for matter","of grant, shall name your Highness in this form and with this","addition, in French, Notre tres-cher fils Henri, Roi d'Angleterre,","Heritier de France; and thus in Latin, Praeclarissimus filius noster","Henricus, Rex Angliae et Haeres Franciae."],"id":895,"m":"Only he has not yet signed this: where your Majesty demands that the King of France, whenever he has occasion to write about grants of land, shall name your Highness in this form and with this title, in French, \"Our very dear son Henry, King of England, Heir of France\", and in Latin, \"Our most illustrious son Henry, King of England and Heir of France\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Nor this I have not, brother, so denied","But our request shall make me let it pass."],"id":896,"m":"I have not refused this so firmly, brother, that I won't let it pass at your request."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I pray you then, in love and dear alliance,","Let that one article rank with the rest;","And thereupon give me your daughter."],"id":897,"m":"Then I beg you, in love and dear alliance, let that one article stand with the rest; and then give me your daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCH KING","lines":["Take her, fair son, and from her blood raise up","Issue to me; that the contending kingdoms","Of France and England, whose very shores look pale","With envy of each other's happiness,","May cease their hatred; and this dear conjunction","Plant neighbourhood and Christian-like accord","In their sweet bosoms, that never war advance","His bleeding sword 'twixt England and fair France."],"id":898,"m":"Take her, fair son, and from her blood raise up children for me, so that the rival kingdoms of France and England, whose very shores look pale with envy of each other's happiness, may end their hatred; and may this dear union plant neighbourliness and Christian harmony in their sweet hearts, so that war never raises his bleeding sword between England and fair France."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORDS","lines":["Amen!"],"id":899,"m":"Amen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Now, welcome, Kate; and bear me witness all,","That here I kiss her as my sovereign queen."],"id":900,"m":"Now welcome, Kate; and all of you bear witness that here I kiss her as my royal queen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish.","id":901},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ISABEL","lines":["God, the best maker of all marriages,","Combine your hearts in one, your realms in one!","As man and wife, being two, are one in love,","So be there 'twixt your kingdoms such a spousal,","That never may ill office, or fell jealousy,","Which troubles oft the bed of blessed marriage,","Thrust in between the paction of these kingdoms,","To make divorce of their incorporate league;","That English may as French, French Englishmen,","Receive each other. God speak this Amen!"],"id":902,"m":"May God, the best maker of all marriages, join your hearts in one and your kingdoms in one! As man and wife, being two, are one in love, so let there be such a marriage between your kingdoms that no evil act or cruel suspicion, which often troubles the bed of blessed marriage, may ever push in between the agreement of these kingdoms to divorce their united alliance; so that the English may accept the French, and the French the English, as each other's own. May God say Amen to this!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Amen!"],"id":903,"m":"Amen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Prepare we for our marriage; on which day,","My Lord of Burgundy, we'll take your oath,","And all the peers', for surety of our leagues,","Then shall I swear to Kate, and you to me;","And may our oaths well kept and prosperous be!"],"id":904,"m":"Let us prepare for our marriage, on which day, my Lord of Burgundy, we'll take your oath and all the lords' oaths as a guarantee of our alliance. Then I shall swear to Kate, and you to me; and may our oaths be well kept and prosper!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Sennet. Exeunt.","id":905},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Chorus.","id":906},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","lines":["Thus far, with rough and all-unable pen,","Our bending author hath pursu'd the story,","In little room confining mighty men,","Mangling by starts the full course of their glory.","Small time, but in that small most greatly lived","This star of England. Fortune made his sword,","By which the world's best garden he achieved,","And of it left his son imperial lord.","Henry the Sixth, in infant bands crown'd King","Of France and England, did this king succeed;","Whose state so many had the managing,","That they lost France and made his England bleed:","Which oft our stage hath shown; and, for their sake,","In your fair minds let this acceptance take."],"id":907,"m":"So far, with a rough and quite inadequate pen, our struggling author has followed the story, confining mighty men in a little room and mangling the full course of their glory by jumping from moment to moment. This star of England lived only a short time, but in that short time he lived most greatly. Fortune made his sword, with which he won the world's best garden and left his son its royal lord. Henry the Sixth, crowned King of France and England as a baby in swaddling clothes, succeeded this king; and so many people had the running of his state that they lost France and made his England bleed, which our stage has often shown; and for the sake of those plays, let this one find acceptance in your kind minds."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":908}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"King Henry V","group":"The English court","desc":"The former Prince Hal of the Henry IV plays, now newly crowned and, the bishops say, transformed overnight from wild youth to model king. He asks for a careful legal case before claiming France, answers the Dauphin's tennis balls with a terrible threat, unmasks and condemns the Southampton traitors, and leads a sick, shrinking army through France. He can be warm, witty and humble, walking among his soldiers in disguise on the eve of Agincourt, praying for pardon for his father's crime, and inspiring his men with the Crispin's day speech. He can also be ruthless: he threatens Harfleur with rape and massacre, lets his old friend Bardolph hang, and orders the French prisoners killed. After the victory he gives the glory to God and woos the French princess in plain prose."},{"name":"Chorus","group":"The English court","desc":"The narrator who opens each act and closes the play. He apologises for the 'wooden O' of the theatre and asks the audience to use their imaginations to fill it with armies, fleets and battles. He praises Henry as 'the mirror of all Christian kings' and describes 'a little touch of Harry in the night'. What he promises is sometimes different from what the scenes then show."},{"name":"Duke of Exeter","group":"The English court","desc":"Henry's uncle and most trusted lord. He arrests the traitors, carries Henry's claim and defiance to the French court, holds the bridge on the march, and tells Henry how York and Suffolk died together at Agincourt, weeping as he does so. He is one of the commissioners at the peace talks."},{"name":"Archbishop of Canterbury","group":"The English court","desc":"The senior churchman. With Ely he fears a bill that would strip the Church of its lands, and offers Henry a huge sum for the French war. He sets out the Salic law case for Henry's claim at great length, urges him on with memories of Edward III and the Black Prince, and gives the fable of the honey-bees."},{"name":"Bishop of Ely","group":"The English court","desc":"Canterbury's ally, who agrees that the King's reformation is wonderful ('the strawberry grows underneath the nettle') and urges Henry to renew the feats of his ancestors."},{"name":"Henry's brothers and lords","group":"The English court","desc":"Bedford and Gloucester, Henry's younger brothers; the Earls of Westmoreland, Salisbury and Warwick; the Duke of York, who begs to lead the vanguard and dies at Agincourt; and old Sir Thomas Erpingham, who lends Henry his cloak. Westmoreland's wish for 'ten thousand of those men in England' prompts the Crispin's day speech."},{"name":"Cambridge, Scroop and Grey","group":"The traitors","desc":"Richard Earl of Cambridge, Henry Lord Scroop of Masham and Sir Thomas Grey, bribed by France to kill Henry at Southampton. Scroop was Henry's bedfellow and confidant. They urge harsh punishment for a drunk who insulted the King, then are handed the evidence of their own treason and sentenced to death."},{"name":"Fluellen","group":"The captains","desc":"A Welsh captain, wordy, pedantic and brave, devoted to the 'disciplines of the wars' of the Romans and to Henry, his fellow Welshman. He argues with Macmorris, refuses to plead for Bardolph, compares Henry to Alexander the Great, is struck by Williams in Henry's glove trick, and makes Pistol eat a leek for mocking the Welsh."},{"name":"Gower","group":"The captains","desc":"An English captain, Fluellen's friend. He sees through Pistol as a fake soldier and at the end tells Pistol that a Welsh beating should teach him good English manners."},{"name":"Macmorris and Jamy","group":"The captains","desc":"An Irish and a Scottish captain at Harfleur. Hot-tempered Macmorris takes offence at Fluellen's 'your nation'; Jamy wants to hear them argue. With Fluellen and Gower they make a small picture of all four nations in Henry's army."},{"name":"Michael Williams","group":"The soldiers","desc":"A blunt common soldier who meets the disguised King on the night before Agincourt and argues that if the cause is bad, the King will answer for every soldier who dies. He quarrels with Henry and they swap gloves. When the glove trick is revealed he stands his ground, says the fault was the disguise, and is given the glove full of crowns."},{"name":"John Bates and Alexander Court","group":"The soldiers","desc":"Williams's fellow soldiers. Bates thinks a subject's obedience wipes out any guilt in the King's cause and tells them to save their quarrels for the French."},{"name":"Pistol","group":"The Eastcheap gang","desc":"Ancient (ensign) Pistol, a swaggering braggart from the Henry IV plays who talks in scraps of old plays. He has married the Hostess and goes to France to make money. He begs Fluellen to save Bardolph, captures a French soldier and ransoms him through the Boy, is beaten and made to eat a leek, and finally slinks home to be a thief, pretending his bruises are war wounds."},{"name":"Bardolph","group":"The Eastcheap gang","desc":"Falstaff's red-nosed follower, now a lieutenant. He makes peace between Nym and Pistol, leads the charge at the breach, and is hanged for stealing a pax from a church, with the King's approval."},{"name":"Nym","group":"The Eastcheap gang","desc":"A corporal who says little and ends every speech with 'that's the humour of it'. He had been engaged to the Hostess and quarrels with Pistol. The Boy reports that he too has been hanged for stealing."},{"name":"Boy","group":"The Eastcheap gang","desc":"Falstaff's former page, who goes to France with Pistol, Nym and Bardolph and sees through all three. He decides to leave them, translates for Pistol and the French soldier, and stays to guard the luggage, where the French kill all the boys."},{"name":"Hostess","group":"The Eastcheap gang","desc":"Nell Quickly, of the Boar's Head, now married to Pistol. She describes Falstaff's death with comic and moving detail. In this edition Pistol later hears that 'my Doll is dead'; many editors change this to 'my Nell', which would mean the Hostess has died."},{"name":"Sir John Falstaff","group":"The Eastcheap gang","desc":"Henry's old companion, who never appears. Nym and Pistol say the King has broken his heart; the Hostess reports his death; Fluellen recalls that Henry 'turned away the fat knight'."},{"name":"Charles VI, the French King","group":"The French court","desc":"An old king who remembers Crecy and takes Henry more seriously than his son does. He offers his daughter and some dukedoms, sends Montjoy with a demand for ransom, and at the end accepts Henry as his son and heir."},{"name":"The Dauphin","group":"The French court","desc":"Lewis, the French King's son, who sends the tennis balls, calls Henry a 'vain, giddy, shallow, humorous youth', and boasts endlessly about his horse. He longs for the battle, then, when it is lost, wants to stab himself."},{"name":"The Constable of France","group":"The French court","desc":"Charles Delabreth, commander of the French army. He warns the Dauphin that Henry's wild youth was a disguise, but on the eve of battle is as confident and scornful as the rest. He dies at Agincourt."},{"name":"Orleans, Bourbon, Rambures and Grandpre","group":"The French court","desc":"French lords who mock the English, gamble for prisoners and describe Henry's army as walking corpses. When the day is lost they go back to die in the throng. Orleans and Bourbon are captured; Rambures and Grandpre are killed."},{"name":"Montjoy","group":"The French court","desc":"The French herald. He twice demands Henry's ransom, then returns humbled to ask leave to bury the French dead and tells Henry 'The day is yours'."},{"name":"Katharine","group":"The French court","desc":"The French King's daughter. She has an English lesson with Alice, learning the names of parts of the body, and in the last scene is wooed by Henry in a mixture of English and French. She leaves the decision to her father and agrees to marry Henry as part of the peace."},{"name":"Alice","group":"The French court","desc":"Katharine's gentlewoman, who has been to England and teaches her mistress a little English, and later interprets during the wooing."},{"name":"Queen Isabel","group":"The French court","desc":"The French Queen, who appears only in the last scene. She hopes the meeting will turn quarrels into love and blesses the marriage of the two kingdoms."},{"name":"Duke of Burgundy","group":"The French court","desc":"The peacemaker who brings the two kings together and describes how war has turned France, 'this best garden of the world', into a wilderness."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"The Chorus asks the audience to imagine the fields of France and the armies of Agincourt inside the 'wooden O' of the theatre. In London, the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Bishop of Ely discuss a bill that would take away much of the Church's land. They marvel at how the wild Prince has become a wise and holy King, and Canterbury reveals that he has offered Henry a huge sum for his French war, more than the clergy ever gave before. The French ambassador is waiting to be heard.","key":["The Chorus makes the audience partners in the play, asking them to piece out its 'imperfections' with their thoughts.","The bishops praise Henry's sudden reformation, linking this play to the wild Hal of Henry IV.","The Church's money and the threat of the bill hang over the claim to France that follows."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Chorus ask the audience to do?","o":["Stay silent during the battle","Use their imaginations to fill the stage","Read the chronicles first","Cheer for the English"],"a":1},{"q":"Why are the bishops worried?","o":["The King plans to close the monasteries and sell their land to France","A bill would take away much of the Church's land","The French have invaded","The King has fallen ill"],"a":1},{"q":"What has Canterbury offered the King?","o":["His own lands in Kent and his palace at Lambeth for the King's use","A large sum from the clergy for the war in France","Soldiers from the Church's estates","A crown blessed by the Pope"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Henry asks Canterbury to explain, honestly and on his conscience, whether the Salic law bars his claim to France, warning him of the blood a war will cost. Canterbury argues at length that the law applies to a German territory and that French kings themselves claimed through the female line, then urges Henry to follow Edward III and the Black Prince. Ely, Exeter and Westmoreland agree, and Canterbury offers money and the fable of the honey-bees to show that England can defend itself against the Scots while a quarter of its army fights abroad. Henry resolves to bend France or break it. The Dauphin's ambassador delivers a mocking gift of tennis balls; Henry replies that he will turn them into gun-stones and that thousands will weep for the joke.","key":["Henry makes Canterbury take responsibility for the war's justice before he decides, but the decision clearly suits both of them.","The tennis balls recall Hal's wild youth; Henry turns the insult into a declaration of war.","Henry's reply mixes wit, cold fury and a claim that he acts in God's name."],"quiz":[{"q":"What law do the French use to bar Henry's claim?","o":["The law of Normandy","The Salic law","Canon law","The law of Calais"],"a":1},{"q":"What fable does Canterbury use to describe an ordered kingdom?","o":["The ant and the grasshopper","The honey-bees","The belly and the limbs","The lion and the mouse"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the Dauphin's gift?","o":["A gold crown for a boy king","A barrel of tennis balls","A sword with a broken blade","A map of the French coast"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"The Chorus describes England arming for war and three traitors, Cambridge, Scroop and Grey, bribed by France to kill Henry at Southampton. In London, Bardolph tries to make peace between Nym and Pistol, who have quarrelled because Pistol has married the Hostess, once engaged to Nym. Swords are drawn and sheathed several times. Falstaff's Boy brings news that his master is very sick, and the Hostess says the King has killed his heart. Pistol settles Nym's betting debt and the three go in to see Falstaff.","key":["The comic Eastcheap scenes bring the war down to the level of the people Henry left behind.","The Hostess's 'The King has killed his heart' reminds us of Hal's rejection of Falstaff at the end of 2 Henry IV.","The Chorus's 'O England! model to thy inward greatness' sits beside this picture of an England of quarrelling rogues."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why are Nym and Pistol quarrelling?","o":["Over a horse that Nym sold him","Pistol has married the Hostess, who was engaged to Nym","Over a bet on the King","Over Falstaff's money"],"a":1},{"q":"Who has the French bribed to kill Henry?","o":["Exeter, Bedford and Westmoreland","Cambridge, Scroop and Grey","Pistol, Nym and Bardolph","Canterbury and Ely"],"a":1},{"q":"According to the Hostess, what has happened to Falstaff?","o":["He has gone to France with the army","The King has killed his heart","He has married","He has been arrested"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"At Southampton Exeter, Bedford and Westmoreland reveal that the King knows all about the traitors' plot. Henry asks Cambridge, Scroop and Grey whether he should free a man who insulted him while drunk; they urge harsh punishment. He gives them papers they take for their commissions, which are in fact the evidence of their treason. They beg mercy, but Henry says their own advice has killed it. He reproaches Scroop, his closest friend, at length as a second fall of man, and sentences all three to death for the kingdom's sake, not his own. Taking the discovery as a sign of God's favour, he sails for France.","key":["Henry turns the traitors' own words against them: they refused mercy to a drunk, so they cannot ask for it.","His speech to Scroop shows personal hurt as well as public justice.","Henry separates his person from his kingdom: he seeks no revenge, but the law must act."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the traitors advise about the drunk who insulted the King?","o":["Free him","Punish him","Make him a soldier","Send him to France"],"a":1},{"q":"What papers does Henry give the traitors?","o":["Their commissions for France","Evidence of their treason","Letters from their wives","Maps of France"],"a":1},{"q":"Which traitor was Henry's 'bedfellow' and closest friend?","o":["Cambridge","Grey","Scroop","Westmoreland"],"a":2}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Outside the tavern in Eastcheap, the Hostess tells Pistol, Nym, Bardolph and the Boy how Falstaff died: he fumbled with the sheets, played with flowers and 'babbled of green fields', cried out to God three or four times, and grew cold from the feet up. They remember his jokes about sack, women and Bardolph's nose. Pistol kisses his wife goodbye, gives her advice on keeping the house, and leads his comrades off to France 'to suck, to suck, the very blood to suck'.","key":["The Hostess's account of Falstaff's death is comic in its slips and deeply moving.","Falstaff's death marks the end of Hal's old life and the old comic world.","Pistol's last line shows that the commoners see the war as a chance to steal."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does the Hostess say Falstaff died?","o":["Fighting the French on the beach at Harfleur","Peacefully, babbling of green fields","Cursing the King with his last breath","Drinking sack until the end"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does the Hostess say Falstaff is now?","o":["In hell","In Arthur's bosom","In France","In prison"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Pistol want from France?","o":["Honour and a name","Blood and profit","A French wife","Land"],"a":1}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"In France the French King orders his towns to be strengthened, remembering the English victory at Crecy. The Dauphin says there is nothing to fear from such a 'vain, giddy, shallow' young king, but the Constable warns him that Henry's wildness was a disguise like that of Brutus. Exeter arrives with Henry's pedigree and his demand that Charles give up the crown of France, or face a war whose widows' tears will be on his head. Exeter delivers scorn and defiance to the Dauphin for the tennis balls and warns that Henry is already in France. The King promises an answer the next day.","key":["The French are divided: the old King and the Constable fear Henry, the Dauphin despises him.","Exeter repeats Henry's habit of placing the blame for bloodshed on his enemy.","Exeter's 'Now he weighs time even to the utmost grain' insists that the Hal of the taverns is gone."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which battle does the French King remember with shame?","o":["Hastings","Crecy","Shrewsbury","Poitiers"],"a":1},{"q":"How does the Constable describe Henry's wild youth?","o":["As real wickedness that he cannot shake off","As a disguise, like Brutus's folly","As an illness","As the Dauphin's invention"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Exeter give the French King?","o":["A pedigree proving Henry's descent from Edward III","A ransom of gold for the King's prisoners","A box of jewels","A letter from Katharine"],"a":0}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"The Chorus describes the fleet sailing from Southampton to Harfleur 'like a city on the inconstant billows', the siege guns facing the town, and the French King's offer of Katharine and some small dukedoms, which Henry refuses. At the breach in the walls of Harfleur, Henry rallies his men: 'Once more unto the breach, dear friends'. He urges them to imitate the tiger, to prove themselves worthy of their warlike fathers, and calls the yeomen as noble as the gentlemen, ending with 'God for Harry! England and Saint George!'","key":["The speech turns ordinary men into heroes by appealing to family, nation and courage.","Its picture of war as a tiger's rage sits uneasily with the cost the play shows elsewhere.","The Chorus reports an offer of marriage that the play will return to in Act 5."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the French King offer Henry according to the Chorus?","o":["The crown of France","His daughter Katharine and some small dukedoms","A large ransom","Calais"],"a":1},{"q":"Which animal does Henry tell his soldiers to imitate?","o":["The lion","The tiger","The eagle","The wolf"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the speech's last cry?","o":["For France and for the glory of the King of England!","God for Harry! England and Saint George!","Remember Crecy!","Down with the Dauphin!"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Bardolph shouts 'To the breach!' but Nym, Pistol and the Boy would rather be in an alehouse in London. Fluellen drives them on with blows. Alone, the Boy describes his three masters as cowards and thieves who want to teach him to pick pockets, and decides to leave them. Fluellen complains to Gower that the mines are not dug according to the Roman disciplines of war. He meets the Irish captain Macmorris, who is furious that the digging has stopped, and the Scot Jamy. When Fluellen begins 'there is not many of your nation', Macmorris flares up, 'What ish my nation?', and they nearly fight. The town sounds a parley.","key":["The scene parodies the heroic speech that has just been made.","The Boy's soliloquy shows the war through the eyes of a child among thieves.","The four captains, Welsh, Irish, Scottish and English, show a British army that is united but quarrelsome."],"quiz":[{"q":"What would Nym, Pistol and the Boy rather be doing?","o":["Fighting the French on the walls of the town","Drinking in an alehouse in London","Guarding the camp at night","Digging mines"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Fluellen complain about?","o":["The food in the English camp, which is poor and running short","The mines are not dug by the Roman rules of war","The King's speech, which he thinks was far too long","The weather"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Macmorris take offence?","o":["Fluellen mentions his nation","Fluellen calls him a coward","Gower insults Ireland","Jamy laughs at him"],"a":0}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Before the gates of Harfleur Henry gives the Governor a last chance to surrender. If the siege begins again, he says, he will not be able to restrain his soldiers: they will rape the town's daughters, dash out the old men's brains and spit babies on pikes, and the guilt will be the townspeople's own. The Governor replies that the Dauphin cannot send help and surrenders. Henry tells Exeter to hold the town and use mercy to all, and plans to retreat to Calais because winter and sickness are weakening his army.","key":["The threats are among the most brutal in Shakespeare; Henry again blames his enemies for what his soldiers would do.","The threat works, and Henry then orders mercy, which can be read as strategy or as genuine restraint.","Sickness and winter show that the English campaign is in trouble."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Henry threaten if Harfleur does not surrender?","o":["To starve the town until the people eat their own horses","Rape, murder and destruction by his soldiers","To take hostages","To burn the churches only"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does the Governor surrender?","o":["He is bribed by the English King","The Dauphin cannot send help","His soldiers mutiny","The walls fall"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Henry plan to go next?","o":["Paris","Calais","Rouen","Back to England"],"a":1}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"In the French palace Princess Katharine asks her gentlewoman Alice, who has been to England, to teach her English. Speaking almost entirely in French, they work through the words for hand, fingers, nails, arm, elbow, neck and chin, Katharine getting most of them wrong. She is shocked by 'foot' and 'gown', which sound like rude French words, and declares that she would not say them before the lords of France for all the world. Then she recites the whole list again.","key":["The only scene in the play mostly in a foreign language makes the audience feel what it is to be outside a language.","Katharine is learning the language of the man who is invading her country, preparing for the wooing in Act 5.","The comedy of the body parts hints at the bodies that war will claim and that marriage will join."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is Alice teaching Katharine?","o":["Latin","English","Dancing","Music"],"a":1},{"q":"Which words shock Katharine?","o":["Hand and fingers","Foot and gown","Neck and chin","Arm and elbow"],"a":1},{"q":"In what language is most of the scene spoken?","o":["English","French","Welsh","Latin"],"a":1}]},"a3s5":{"sum":"Henry has crossed the river Somme. The French lords are outraged that the 'bastard Normans' of England march unchallenged; the Dauphin and Bourbon say their ladies mock them and offer themselves to English youths. The French King calls on a long roll of his nobles to stop Henry and bring him to Rouen as a prisoner. The Constable regrets only that the English are so few and sick. The King sends Montjoy to ask what ransom Henry will pay and orders the Dauphin to stay with him in Rouen.","key":["French pride is wounded by English success, but the French still expect an easy victory.","The roll call of French names will be read out again among the dead in 4.8.","The Constable's scorn for the small, sick English army sets up the reversal at Agincourt."],"quiz":[{"q":"What river has Henry crossed?","o":["The Seine","The Somme","The Loire","The Thames"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the French say their ladies are doing?","o":["Praying for them in the churches of Paris day and night","Mocking them and preferring English youths","Raising money for the war","Leaving for Paris"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is ordered to stay in Rouen?","o":["The Constable","The Dauphin","Montjoy","Orleans"],"a":1}]},"a3s6":{"sum":"Fluellen tells Gower that Exeter has bravely held a bridge, helped by an ensign as brave as Mark Antony: Pistol. Pistol arrives to beg Fluellen to plead for Bardolph, who is to be hanged for stealing a pax from a church. Fluellen refuses, saying discipline must be kept, and Pistol gives him the fig. Gower recognises Pistol as a fake soldier who will tell tavern war stories in London. Henry arrives; Fluellen reports that the only English loss is Bardolph, to be executed for robbing a church. Henry approves and orders that the French be paid for everything and not abused, since 'the gentler gamester is the soonest winner'. Montjoy demands ransom; Henry admits his army is sick and weak but says he will march on, neither seeking nor shunning battle.","key":["Henry lets his old companion Bardolph die without a word of their past, a sign of how far he has moved from Hal.","His order to treat the French gently mixes justice and policy.","His honest answer to Montjoy shows confidence in God rather than in numbers."],"quiz":[{"q":"What has Bardolph stolen?","o":["A horse","A pax from a church","The King's purse","French wine"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Fluellen say when Pistol asks him to save Bardolph?","o":["He agrees to help him at once and goes to the King","He refuses: discipline must be kept","He offers money","He asks the King himself"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Henry answer Montjoy's demand for ransom?","o":["He offers a great sum of money to buy a safe passage to Calais for his army","He says his ransom is his frail body and he will march on","He surrenders","He kills the herald"],"a":1}]},"a3s7":{"sum":"In the French camp the night before Agincourt, the Dauphin praises his horse as Pegasus, his mistress and the subject of a sonnet, and the Constable and Orleans mock him in a contest of proverbs after he leaves. Rambures offers to gamble for English prisoners. A messenger reports that the English are camped fifteen hundred paces away. The lords sneer at the 'fat-brained' English and their foolish king, mastiffs with no brains who fight only when fed on beef, and look forward to taking a hundred Englishmen each by ten in the morning.","key":["French boredom, boasting and mockery contrast with the English mood in the next scene.","The Dauphin's love for his horse makes him ridiculous, even to his own side.","The French confidence is dramatic irony: the audience knows how Agincourt ends."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Dauphin praise at length?","o":["His armour","His horse","His mistress's eyes","The King"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Rambures offer to gamble for?","o":["A bag of gold crowns","Twenty English prisoners","The Dauphin's horse","Henry's crown"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the French say the English need in order to fight?","o":["Wine","Great meals of beef","Their wives","Good weather"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"The Chorus describes the night before the battle: the camps so close the sentries hear each other, the over-confident French playing dice for the English, the English sitting like sacrifices by their fires, and Henry walking from tent to tent to give 'a little touch of Harry in the night'. Henry borrows Erpingham's cloak and walks the camp alone. Pistol, not knowing him, praises the King and gives 'Harry le Roy' the fig. Henry overhears Fluellen telling Gower to keep quiet. The soldiers Bates, Court and Williams argue with him about the King's responsibility for men who die in an unjust cause; Henry argues that every subject's soul is his own. Williams doubts the King's promise not to be ransomed, and they exchange gloves to settle their quarrel later. Alone, Henry reflects on the burden of kingship and the emptiness of 'ceremony', then prays to God to steel his soldiers and not to punish him today for his father's seizure of the crown.","key":["Henry hears honest opinions only when disguised; the argument with Williams goes to the heart of the play.","'Upon the King!' shows the loneliness and weight of kingship.","The prayer reminds us that Henry's crown came from Richard II's deposition and murder."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose cloak does Henry borrow?","o":["The Duke of Exeter's","Sir Thomas Erpingham's","Fluellen's","Bates's"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Williams argue?","o":["The King should surrender to the French before the battle begins","If the cause is bad, the King must answer for the dead","The French are cowards","Soldiers should desert"],"a":1},{"q":"What do Henry and Williams exchange?","o":["Swords","Gloves","Rings","Caps"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"At sunrise the French lords call for their horses. The Constable says the English are so few and starved that the French servants and peasants could defeat them alone; a little effort and all is done. Grandpre describes the English as an army of carrion, their horses drooping and the crows waiting overhead. The Dauphin mockingly suggests sending them dinners and fresh clothes before the fight. The Constable hurries to the field.","key":["The French certainty of victory is at its height just before their defeat.","Grandpre's grotesque description of the English matches the Chorus's 'horrid ghosts'.","The Constable is so impatient that he will take a trumpeter's banner rather than wait for his own."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who does the Constable say could defeat the English?","o":["The Dauphin alone, with his sword","Their servants and peasants","Their horses","Montjoy"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Grandpre describe the English army?","o":["As fierce lions","As carrion with crows waiting over them","As well fed","As hidden in the woods"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Dauphin mockingly suggest?","o":["Surrendering to the English King before the battle","Sending the English dinners and fresh clothes","Waiting until night","Asking for peace"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"The English lords learn they are outnumbered five to one; Salisbury says farewell before going to his post. When Westmoreland wishes for ten thousand more men from England, Henry answers that the fewer men, the greater share of honour. He offers any man who does not want to fight a passport home, and promises that those who fight on Saint Crispin's day will remember it all their lives, 'we few, we happy few, we band of brothers', while gentlemen in England will think themselves cursed not to have been there. Montjoy comes again for ransom; Henry tells him the French must win his bones before they sell them, and that his army, though worn and muddy, has hearts in trim. York asks to lead the vanguard.","key":["The Crispin's day speech turns fear of the odds into pride in them.","Henry promises that shedding blood with him will 'gentle the condition' of common men, a promise the play later tests.","His reply to Montjoy combines grim humour and defiance."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Westmoreland wish for?","o":["Better weather and dry ground for the fight","Ten thousand more men from England","A truce","More horses"],"a":1},{"q":"What feast day is the battle fought on?","o":["Saint George's Day, England's own","Saint Crispin's","Saint David's","Saint Denis's Day"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry offer men who do not want to fight?","o":["Pardon","A passport and money to go home","Double pay","A place guarding the luggage"],"a":1}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"In the battle Pistol captures a French soldier who thinks him a gentleman of high rank. Neither understands the other, so the Boy translates. Pistol threatens to cut the man's throat; the soldier, Monsieur le Fer, offers two hundred crowns for his life and thanks Pistol on his knees as the bravest lord in England. Alone, the Boy says Bardolph and Nym had ten times more courage than this roaring devil, and both have been hanged. He must stay with the luggage, which only boys are guarding.","key":["The only fighting the play shows on stage is a comic ransom deal, not a heroic duel.","Pistol's greed parodies the French demands for Henry's ransom.","The Boy's remark that only boys guard the luggage prepares for the killing of the boys."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who translates between Pistol and the French soldier?","o":["Fluellen","The Boy","Montjoy","Alice"],"a":1},{"q":"How much does the French soldier offer?","o":["Forty moys","Two hundred crowns","His fine horse and saddle","A thousand pounds"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Boy reveal about Nym?","o":["He has deserted to the French","He has been hanged","He is a hero","He has married"],"a":1}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"The French lords realise the day is lost: 'all is confounded, all!' The Dauphin talks of stabbing himself; Orleans asks whether this is the king they demanded ransom from. Bourbon calls for one last charge, and the Constable agrees that disorder, which ruined them, should now help them. They go back into the throng to die with honour.","key":["The French collapse is sudden and total, told in cries of shame.","Their mockery of the English is turned back on them line by line.","Their decision to die in the throng, without order, contrasts with English discipline."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the French realise?","o":["They are winning","The day is lost","Henry has fled","Reinforcements are coming"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Dauphin suggest?","o":["Surrender","Stabbing themselves","Running away","Asking Henry for terms"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Bourbon call for?","o":["A full retreat to Paris","One last charge","A parley with the English","Prayers"],"a":1}]},"a4s6":{"sum":"Henry praises his men, but the French still hold the field. Exeter describes how the Duke of York, covered in wounds, lay down beside the dead Earl of Suffolk, kissed his gashes, and died with his arm round Suffolk's neck, asking Exeter to commend his service to the King; Exeter wept as he tells it, and Henry too is near tears. A new alarm sounds: the French have regrouped. Henry orders every soldier to kill his prisoners.","key":["The death of York and Suffolk gives the battle a chivalric, tender image.","The order to kill the prisoners follows straight after the tears, a stark change of tone.","Henry gives the order because of a new French threat, before the attack on the boys is reported."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who dies beside the Earl of Suffolk?","o":["Salisbury","The Duke of York","Exeter","Warwick"],"a":1},{"q":"What makes Henry order the prisoners killed in this scene?","o":["News that the boys guarding the camp were killed","A new alarm: the French have regrouped","A French insult","A messenger"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Exeter feel telling the story?","o":["Angry at the French","Moved to tears","Amused","Bored"],"a":1}]},"a4s7":{"sum":"Fluellen is outraged that the French have killed the boys guarding the luggage, against the law of arms; Gower says the King has rightly ordered every prisoner's throat cut. Fluellen compares Henry, born at Monmouth, to Alexander the Great, born in Macedon: as Alexander killed his friend Cleitus in drink, so Henry, in his right wits, turned away the fat knight. Henry, angry for the first time in France, threatens the French horsemen and promises no mercy. Montjoy returns humbly to ask leave to bury the dead and tells Henry the day is his. Henry names it the field of Agincourt and gives praise to God. He meets Williams, who promises to strike whoever wears his glove, and gives Fluellen the glove, saying it came from Alencon's helmet, and sends Warwick and Gloucester after him to prevent harm.","key":["Fluellen's comparison is comic, but it reminds the audience of Falstaff's rejection at the moment of victory.","The order to kill prisoners is now linked to the killing of the boys.","The glove trick shows Henry's playful side returning after the battle."],"quiz":[{"q":"What have the French done to the luggage?","o":["Stolen nothing","Killed the boys and burned the King's tent","Surrendered it","Hidden it"],"a":1},{"q":"Who does Fluellen compare Henry to?","o":["Julius Caesar","Alexander the Great","Pompey","Hector of Troy"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry tell Fluellen about the glove?","o":["It belongs to the soldier Williams","It came from Alencon's helmet","It is Montjoy's, the herald","It is his own, from his tent"],"a":1}]},"a4s8":{"sum":"Williams recognises his glove in Fluellen's cap and strikes him. Fluellen calls him a traitor and a friend of Alencon. Henry arrives, produces the other glove, and reveals that he was the man Williams quarrelled with. Williams says he insulted a common man, not the King, and that the fault was Henry's disguise. Henry fills the glove with crowns for him and Fluellen offers a shilling. The herald brings the numbers: ten thousand French dead, including the Constable and many great lords, against four named English and twenty-five others. Henry gives all the glory to God, makes boasting a capital offence, and orders the Non nobis and Te Deum to be sung.","key":["Williams's defence of his own honesty earns Henry's respect and reward.","The lists of the dead show the scale of the French loss and the tiny English one.","Henry insists the victory is God's, turning triumph into worship."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Williams strike Fluellen?","o":["Fluellen insulted Wales","Fluellen is wearing Williams's glove","Fluellen stole from him","The King ordered it"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry give Williams?","o":["A knighthood for his courage at Agincourt","His glove filled with crowns","A horse","A pardon only, and no reward"],"a":1},{"q":"Roughly how many English are reported dead?","o":["Ten thousand","About twenty-nine","Fifteen hundred","Five hundred"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"The Chorus asks the audience to imagine Henry's return to England, the crowds at the shore, his refusal to have his dented helmet and bent sword carried before him, and London pouring out to welcome him like a Roman conqueror, compared with the hoped-for return of the Queen's general from Ireland. Time passes, and he returns to France. Fluellen, still wearing his leek after Saint David's Day, waits for Pistol, who mocked it. When Pistol arrives, Fluellen beats him with a cudgel until he eats the leek, then gives him a groat for his broken head. Gower scolds Pistol for mocking a Welshman's accent. Alone, Pistol hears that his Doll has died in the hospital, and decides to go home as a pimp and thief, passing off his bruises as war wounds.","key":["The Chorus links Henry's triumph to a living event, the Earl of Essex's Irish campaign of 1599.","The leek scene finishes the comic plot by humiliating the play's great braggart.","Pistol's exit, old, beaten and alone, is the last of the Eastcheap world."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Henry forbid on his return to London?","o":["A thanksgiving service in Saint Paul's Cathedral with all the lords and bishops","His bruised helmet and bent sword being carried before him","Bells being rung in every church of London","The Chorus speaking"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Fluellen make Pistol eat?","o":["Bread and salt","A leek","Cheese","A herring"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Pistol decide to do at the end?","o":["Join the French","Go back to England as a pimp and thief","Become a captain","Marry again"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"At the French palace Henry, the French King and Queen and the Duke of Burgundy meet for peace. Burgundy describes how war has ruined France, 'this best garden of the world', and its people. Henry says peace depends on his demands; the French King asks for time to study them, and the lords withdraw, leaving Katharine with Henry and Alice. Henry woos her in blunt prose and broken French, calling himself a plain soldier with a face not worth sunburning, and asks for a son who will take the Turk by the beard. She says it will be as her father pleases. He kisses her despite French custom. The lords return; Burgundy jokes with Henry about love, and the French King grants all the articles, including the title 'Heir of France'. Henry kisses Katharine as his queen, and Queen Isabel blesses the union. The Chorus ends the play: Henry lived a short time, and his son, Henry VI, lost France and made England bleed.","key":["The wooing is charming and comic, but Katharine has little real choice: she is 'our capital demand'.","Burgundy's speech shows the cost of the war to the French land and people.","The Epilogue undercuts the happy ending by reminding the audience that everything Henry won was soon lost."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Burgundy describe?","o":["Henry's victories in the field","How war has ruined France","His own family and their lands","The English court"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Katharine answer Henry's proposal?","o":["She refuses him outright in front of the court","It shall be as it pleases her father","She asks for time to think it over","She agrees at once in French"],"a":1},{"q":"According to the Epilogue, what happened under Henry VI?","o":["England conquered Spain and the Netherlands","France was lost and England bled","The crusade succeeded","Peace lasted a hundred years"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"The 'wooden O' and 'cockpit' are the round playhouse, probably the new Globe of 1599. The Chorus admits that the stage cannot show Agincourt and asks the audience to do the work.","5":"The bill had first been proposed in the eleventh year of Henry IV's reign (1410). Holinshed reports that the clergy feared it and encouraged the French war to distract the King.","13":"The bishops' praise of Henry's sudden change looks back to Hal's promise in 1 Henry IV to break out like the sun from clouds, and his rejection of Falstaff at the end of 2 Henry IV.","16":"A common belief was that strawberries grew best beside plants of a 'baser' kind. Ely suggests that Hal hid his good qualities under his wild behaviour.","21":"Edward III, Henry's great-grandfather, claimed France through his mother Isabella; this claim began the Hundred Years' War.","37":"Henry insists that Canterbury will be responsible for the bloodshed if his reading of the law is false. Many readers notice that the decision has already been half made, and that the Church has a motive.","38":"The Salic law said no woman could inherit in 'Salic land'. Canterbury's long list of dates and kings comes almost word for word from Holinshed and is often played for comedy, but its point is that French kings themselves claimed through women.","40":"Numbers 27:8: if a man dies without a son, his inheritance passes to his daughter. The Black Prince won the battle of Crecy (1346) while Edward III watched from a hill.","48":"The King of Scots captured in Edward III's time was David II, taken at Neville's Cross in 1346 while Edward was in France.","51":"The beehive was a standard image of an ordered commonwealth. Canterbury's 'king' bee was really a queen, which was not known until later.","59":"The tennis balls mock Hal's youth. 'Galliard' is a lively dance; the gift says Henry is fit for games, not dukedoms.","62":"Henry plays on tennis terms: a 'set', the 'hazard' (an opening in the court wall), a 'wrangler' (a tough opponent), 'courts' and 'chaces'. 'Gun-stones' were stone cannonballs.","69":"Mercury, messenger of the gods, had winged heels. The Chorus pictures England as a little body with a mighty heart, betrayed by three of its 'children'.","75":"'That's the humour of it' is Nym's catchphrase, from the fashion for comedies of humours. His speech is deliberately empty.","83":"The Hostess's malapropisms ('wilful adultery' for assault) are part of her comedy, as in the Henry IV plays.","96":"'Couple a gorge' is Pistol's mangled French for 'cut the throat'. The 'powdering tub' was the sweating tub used to treat venereal disease; Doll Tearsheet is the prostitute from 2 Henry IV.","100":"'Yield the crow a pudding' means die and become food for crows.","119":"Nym and Pistol blame the King: Falstaff's heart was broken when Hal, newly crowned, rejected him at the end of 2 Henry IV.","130":"Holinshed describes Scroop as so trusted that the King sometimes let him share his bed, an ordinary sign of close friendship and trust at the time.","144":"Henry's mercy to the drunk is a test: the traitors condemn themselves by refusing it.","151":"Henry compares Scroop's treason to 'another fall of man': the betrayal of trust is as shocking as Adam's sin.","154":"Cambridge hints at a darker motive: historically he hoped to put his brother-in-law, Edmund Mortimer, on the throne. His son became the father of Edward IV, which Shakespeare's audience would know.","166":"'Arthur's bosom' is the Hostess's mistake for Abraham's bosom, the resting place of the blessed. 'Babbled of green fields' is an editor's famous emendation (by Lewis Theobald) of a garbled Folio line.","172":"The Hostess mistakes 'incarnate' for 'carnation', a pinkish red.","178":"'Pitch and pay' means pay cash; 'Hold-fast is the only dog' is a proverb (Brag is a good dog, but Holdfast is better). 'Caveto' is Latin for beware.","191":"Lucius Junius Brutus pretended to be a fool to protect himself from the tyrant Tarquin, then led the revolt that founded the Roman republic.","193":"At Crecy in 1346 the Black Prince, aged sixteen, led the English vanguard while Edward III watched from a windmill on a hill.","202":"Exeter hands the French King Henry's family tree, showing descent from Edward III.","217":"Phoebus is the sun. The Chorus mentions the offer of Katharine and 'petty and unprofitable dukedoms', historically made before the invasion.","222":"'Once more unto the breach' is one of the most famous battle speeches in English. Notice how it moves from nobles ('noblest English') to the 'good yeomen', and from rage to the greyhounds of the final image.","227":"'Plain-song' is a simple melody without harmony: Nym means the plain truth.","235":"Pistol calls Fluellen 'great Duke' to flatter him. 'Bawcock' (fine fellow) and 'chuck' (chick) are affectionate terms.","238":"'Carry coals' means put up with insults, with a pun on the stolen fire-shovel. The Boy's soliloquy is a sharp outsider's view of the soldiers.","242":"Fluellen's Welsh accent turns b into p ('pridge') and d into t; the mines were tunnels dug under the walls, countermines the defenders' tunnels.","253":"Macmorris's Irish accent is shown by 'ish' for 'is' and 'Chrish' for 'Christ'. Jamy's Scots appears in 'gud' and 'sall'.","259":"Macmorris's outburst has been read as a sign of the tensions between England and Ireland in 1599, when Essex's army was fighting there.","270":"Harfleur surrendered in September 1415 after a five-week siege; Henry's threats here are Shakespeare's invention. Herod's slaughter of the innocents (Matthew 2) was a familiar image of tyranny.","272":"Historically about a third of Henry's army died or was sent home with dysentery after Harfleur.","276":"The French scene is a language lesson: some words ('de foot', 'de coun') sound like coarse French words, which is the joke.","308":"'Foot' sounds like the French 'foutre' (to have sex) and 'coun' (gown) like 'con' (a vulgar word for the female genitals).","316":"The Dauphin calls the English the bastard offspring of the Normans, who conquered England in 1066.","321":"The French roll of nobles comes from Holinshed's list of those who fought at Agincourt; many are listed again among the dead in 4.8.","332":"Agamemnon led the Greeks at Troy; Mark Antony was Caesar's great soldier. Fluellen's comparisons are grand and comic.","341":"Fortune was painted blind, with a wheel, standing on a rolling stone. Fluellen explains the emblem in detail.","342":"A pax was a small tablet with a holy image, kissed during Mass. Holinshed reports a soldier hanged for stealing one; Shakespeare gives the crime to Bardolph.","346":"The 'fig' (figo) was an obscene gesture, the thumb thrust between two fingers.","353":"Gower describes the type of fake soldier who learns the names of battles and generals to boast in taverns. 'Sconce' is a small fort.","360":"Fluellen describes Bardolph's famous red, pimpled face from the Henry IV plays. Henry does not comment on their old friendship.","361":"Holinshed records Henry's order that nothing be taken from the French without payment.","367":"Montjoy is the title of the chief French herald. A herald was protected by the laws of war.","384":"Pegasus was the winged horse; Hermes (Mercury) played the pipe. 'Ca, ha!' and 'le cheval volant' are the Dauphin's showy French.","404":"2 Peter 2:22: 'The dog is turned to his own vomit again; and the sow that was washed to her wallowing in the mire.'","457":"The Chorus pictures the night before the battle and Henry walking among his men. 'A little touch of Harry in the night' is one of the play's most famous phrases.","464":"Erpingham's cloak lets Henry walk unrecognised: the disguised king is a common motif in ballads and plays.","481":"'Harry le Roy' means Harry the King: Henry tells the truth in a language Pistol does not understand. Pistol thinks it is a Cornish name.","486":"On Saint David's Day (1 March) Welshmen wore leeks in their caps, a custom said to come from a battle in which Welsh soldiers wore leeks.","497":"Pompey the Great was a Roman general. Fluellen's praise of quiet discipline is sensible, which Henry recognises.","514":"Henry says the King is 'but a man as I am', which is true and also a joke the audience shares.","521":"Williams's picture of the limbs rejoining at the Last Judgement and crying out is one of the play's strongest challenges to the war.","522":"Henry's reply separates the King's responsibility for the cause from each soldier's responsibility for his own soul. Many critics note that he does not answer Williams's main point: whether the cause is just.","543":"'Crowns' puns on heads and coins; 'clipper' on cutting heads and clipping coins, a crime. The soliloquy on ceremony is in verse after the prose of the soldiers.","549":"Henry IV seized the crown from Richard II, who was then murdered. Historically Henry V reburied Richard in Westminster Abbey and founded religious houses.","569":"A 'curtle-axe' is a cutlass. The Constable says the French servants alone could beat the English.","589":"Saint Crispin and Saint Crispinian, brothers and shoemakers, were martyrs whose feast is 25 October, the date of Agincourt in 1415.","601":"Henry's story of the man who sold the lion's skin before killing the lion was proverbial.","612":"The French soldier speaks only French; Pistol hears 'qualite' and answers with a line from an Irish song, 'Calen o custure me'.","629":"The Boy speaks good French, which makes him the cleverest person in the scene.","644":"In the old morality plays the Vice beat the devil with a wooden dagger and threatened to pare his nails.","666":"Exeter's account of York and Suffolk is Shakespeare's invention, based on Holinshed's note that both died. York was Henry's cousin, the Duke of Aumerle of Richard II.","669":"Holinshed says Henry ordered the prisoners killed when he heard of a French attack on his camp and feared the prisoners would rise. Shakespeare gives two reasons in two scenes.","679":"Fluellen's comparison of Macedon and Monmouth is comic, but it brings up Falstaff at the height of victory.","681":"Cleitus saved Alexander's life in battle; Alexander killed him at a feast. Fluellen's 'fat knight' is Falstaff, rejected at the end of 2 Henry IV.","696":"The castle of Agincourt (Azincourt) gave the battle its name.","725":"The glove trick echoes Hal's tricks in the tavern in 1 Henry IV, and tests whether Williams will keep his word.","762":"Williams's defence is honest and brave: the King came disguised, so the offence was not to the King.","772":"The French dead in the chronicles were about ten thousand; English losses were reported as very small, though modern historians think they were higher.","774":"Davy Gam was a Welsh squire, killed at Agincourt.","780":"'Non nobis' (Psalm 115: 'Not unto us, O Lord') and the 'Te Deum' are hymns of thanks to God.","784":"'The general of our gracious empress' is usually taken to be the Earl of Essex, who left for Ireland in March 1599; this helps date the play to spring or summer 1599. Blackheath is just outside London.","788":"Bread and salt were traditionally offered to visitors, so Pistol was mocking Fluellen.","794":"Cadwallader was the last British king; goats were a joke about Welsh mountain country.","816":"The Folio reads 'my Doll is dead'; many editors change it to 'my Nell', meaning the Hostess. 'Malady of France' is syphilis.","825":"Burgundy's speech describes France as a neglected garden, a common image for a kingdom.","848":"Henry calls himself a plain soldier who cannot write verse or speak smoothly, though his prose here is very skilful.","856":"Historically the son of Henry and Katharine became Henry VI, who did not take the Turk by the beard but lost France.","860":"Henry says his father was thinking of civil wars when he fathered him: a joke about his own looks and a reminder of his father's troubles.","895":"'Heir of France' is the key term of the real Treaty of Troyes (1420): Henry would rule France after Charles VI's death.","907":"The Epilogue is a sonnet. It reminds the audience of Shakespeare's earlier Henry VI plays, in which France is lost and England falls into civil war."},"gloss":{"invention":"imagination, creative power","port":"bearing, manner","gentles":"ladies and gentlemen","scaffold":"stage","cockpit":"small round theatre","vasty":"vast","casques":"helmets","accompt":"account, sum","puissance":"power, army","scambling":"disorderly, turbulent","lazars":"lepers","Hydra-headed":"many-headed, like the Hydra monster","prelate":"bishop","Gordian knot":"a knot impossible to untie","crescive":"growing","exhibiters":"those who brought in the bill","severals":"details","embassy":"message","gloze":"interpret falsely","imbar":"bar, make safe","puissant":"powerful","pavilion'd":"camped in tents","spiritualty":"clergy","marches":"border lands","intendment":"intention","galling":"harassing","impounded":"shut up, as a stray animal","havoc":"destroy, plunder","Congreeing":"agreeing","mechanic":"manual labourer","empery":"rule, empire","galliard":"a lively dance","tun":"barrel","hazard":"opening in a tennis court; danger","wrangler":"opponent","chaces":"points in tennis; pursuits","gun-stones":"cannonballs","proportions":"forces, supplies","dalliance":"pleasure, flirtation","coronets":"small crowns worn by nobles","solus":"alone","egregious":"outrageous","humour":"mood, whim","tall":"brave","spital":"hospital","quondam":"former","pauca":"in short (Latin: few words)","sutler":"seller of food and drink to soldiers","quotidian tertian":"a fever (two kinds confused)","fracted":"broken","corroborate":"Pistol's nonsense for broken (it means strengthened)","bed-fellow":"close companion who shared a bed","cloy'd":"overfilled","quittance":"repayment, reward","enlarge":"set free","orisons":"prayers","distemper":"drunkenness, disorder","appertinents":"things belonging","Tartar":"Tartarus, hell","affiance":"trust","bolted":"sifted, refined","earnest":"down payment","christom":"newly christened","chattels":"goods, property","Caveto":"beware (Latin)","linstock":"staff holding a gunner's match","rivage":"shore","sternage":"sterns of ships","pith":"strength","breach":"gap in a wall","galled":"worn away","jutty":"jut over","fet":"fetched, descended","argument":"subject, opposition","yeomen":"small landowners, the common soldiers","mettle":"spirit, courage","slips":"leashes","cullions":"rascals","bawcock":"fine fellow","swashers":"swaggerers","antics":"clowns","purchase":"a bargain (thieves' slang for loot)","mines":"tunnels under the walls","countermines":"the defenders' tunnels","pioneers":"diggers, sappers","parley":"trumpet call for a truce to talk","flesh'd":"having tasted blood","leviathan":"whale, sea monster","defensible":"able to defend ourselves","sprays":"offshoots","scions":"shoots, grafts","nook-shotten":"full of corners, jagged","sodden":"boiled","jades":"worn-out horses","lavoltas":"a leaping dance","corantos":"a running dance","pennons":"small flags","rheum":"spit, mucus","aunchient":"ancient: ensign, standard-bearer","pax":"a holy tablet kissed at Mass","figo":"the fig, an obscene gesture","arrant":"out-and-out","cutpurse":"pickpocket","sconce":"small fort","bubukles":"Fluellen's word for pimples and boils","lenity":"gentleness","habit":"uniform, dress","impeachment":"hindrance","palfrey":"riding horse","pasterns":"hooves, ankles of a horse","kern":"Irish foot-soldier","strossers":"trousers","jade":"nag, worn-out horse; also a loose woman","hooded":"hidden, like a hooded hawk","bate":"flutter, flap (of a hawk)","peevish":"foolish","mastiffs":"large fighting dogs","umber'd":"shadowed, brown in the firelight","accomplishing":"equipping","attaint":"exhaustion","largess":"generous gift","foils":"light fencing swords","legerity":"nimbleness","slough":"a snake's skin","pibble pabble":"babble, chatter","coxcomb":"fool; head","rawly":"unprovided for","beadle":"parish officer who punished offenders","elder-gun":"a pop-gun made of elder wood","gage":"token, pledge","ceremony":"the outward show of royalty","balm":"consecrated oil used at a coronation","intertissued":"woven together","farced":"stuffed, padded","Phoebus":"the sun god","Hyperion":"the sun god","lackey":"servant","chantries":"chapels where priests sing for the dead","embattl'd":"drawn up for battle","curtle-axe":"cutlass","hilding":"worthless","tucket":"trumpet call","carrions":"dead flesh, corpses","beaver":"visor of a helmet","gimmal":"jointed","vaward":"vanguard, front line","englutted":"swallowed up","fox":"a kind of sword","firk":"beat","Larding":"enriching (with fat or blood)","haggled":"hacked","raught":"reached","compound":"come to terms","skirr":"scurry, rush","Jacksauce":"saucy fellow","literatured":"well read","apace":"quickly","Bartholomew-tide":"late August (St Bartholomew's day, 24 August)","whiffler":"officer who clears the way in a procession","scald":"scabby, contemptible","groat":"a fourpenny coin","cudgel":"club, heavy stick","woodmonger":"timber seller","gleeking":"mocking","huswife":"hussy","basilisks":"legendary serpents that killed with a look; also large cannon","darnel":"a weed","fumitory":"a weed","coulter":"blade of a plough","deracinate":"uproot","kexes":"dry hollow stalks","diffus'd":"disordered","cursorary":"hasty","consign":"agree","measure":"dance; metre in verse","flower-de-luce":"the lily, emblem of France","perspectively":"as in a trick picture","Praeclarissimus":"most illustrious (Latin)"}};
const PARTS={"2":[[0,3,"If only we had a Muse of fire, able to climb to the brightest heaven of imagination: a whole kingdom for a stage, princes as actors, and kings to watch the swelling scene!"],[4,7,"Then warlike Harry, true to himself, would take on the bearing of Mars, god of war, and at his heels famine, sword and fire would crouch like leashed hounds, waiting to be let loose. But forgive us, gentle audience,"],[8,13,"for the dull, uninspired minds that have dared to bring so great a subject onto this unworthy platform. Can this cockpit of a theatre hold the vast fields of France? Can we cram into this wooden O even the helmets that frightened the very air at Agincourt?"],[14,17,"Oh, forgive us! Since a little zero added to a figure can stand for a million in a small space, let us, mere zeros beside this great sum, work on your imaginations."],[18,21,"Imagine that within the circle of these walls two mighty kingdoms are now shut up, whose high, facing fronts are separated only by the dangerous narrow sea."],[22,26,"Make up for our shortcomings with your thoughts. Divide one man into a thousand and create an imaginary army. When we talk of horses, picture them pressing their proud hooves into the soft earth."],[27,33,"For it is your thoughts that must dress our kings now, carry them from place to place, leap over time, and squeeze the events of many years into an hour-glass. To help with that, let me be the Chorus to this history, and, like a prologue, humbly ask you to listen patiently and judge our play kindly."]],"7":[[0,6,"It needs thinking about. If it passes against us, we lose the better half of our property, because they would strip from us all the lands that devout men have left to the Church in their wills, valued like this: enough to keep, to the King's credit, fifteen earls and fifteen hundred knights,"],[7,12,"six thousand two hundred squires, and, to care for lepers and the old and needy who are past physical work, a hundred well-stocked almshouses; and on top of that a thousand pounds a year for the King's treasury. That's what the bill says."]],"13":[[0,7,"His behaviour as a youth did not promise it. But no sooner had the breath left his father's body than his wildness seemed to die too, killed off in him. Yes, at that very moment, reflection came like an angel and whipped the sinful Adam out of him, leaving his body like a paradise fit to hold heavenly spirits."],[8,13,"There was never such a sudden scholar made. Reform never came in such a headlong flood, scouring away faults, and never did many-headed stubbornness, like the Hydra, lose its throne so quickly and all at once as in this king."]],"15":[[0,6,"Just hear him reason about theology, and, full of admiration, you would secretly wish the King were a bishop. Hear him debate the affairs of state, and you would say he had studied nothing else. Listen to him talk about war, and you will hear a terrible battle turned into music."],[7,14,"Give him any political problem, and he will untie its Gordian knot as easily as his garter. When he speaks, the air, free as a licensed libertine, falls still, and silent wonder waits in men's ears to catch his sweet, honeyed sentences. It must be that experience and practice of life taught him this theory."],[15,21,"Which is a wonder, since how could his Grace have picked it up, when he was given to idle pursuits, his companions ignorant, rough and shallow, his hours filled with riots, feasts and games, and no one ever saw him study, or withdraw, or keep apart from public places and common people?"]],"19":[[0,2,"He seems neutral, or rather leans more towards our side than towards those who brought it in against us."],[3,9,"For I have made his Majesty an offer, on behalf of our church assembly and in view of the business now in hand, which I explained to him in full, concerning France: to give him a larger sum than the clergy have ever given at one time to any of his predecessors."]],"37":[[0,4,"Thank you, truly. My learned lord, please go on and explain fairly and devoutly whether the Salic law they have in France ought or ought not to bar us in our claim."],[5,12,"And God forbid, my dear and faithful lord, that you should twist, distort or bend your reading, or burden your conscience with subtle arguments for a false title that does not, in its true colours, match the truth. For God knows how many men now healthy will shed their blood to prove whatever your reverence urges us to do."],[13,20,"So take care how you commit our person, how you wake our sleeping sword of war. We order you, in God's name, take care. For never did two such kingdoms fight without great bloodshed, and every innocent drop is a grief, a bitter accusation against the man whose wrongs sharpen the swords that waste so many short lives."],[21,24,"Speak under this solemn charge, my lord, and we will listen, take note, and believe in our heart that what you say is washed as clean in your conscience as sin is in baptism."]],"38":[[0,9,"Then hear me, gracious king, and you lords who owe your selves, lives and service to this royal throne. There is no obstacle to your Highness's claim to France except this, which they quote from King Pharamond: \"In terram Salicam mulieres ne succedant\", \"No woman shall inherit in Salic land\". The French wrongly interpret this Salic land as the kingdom of France, and Pharamond as the founder of this law that bars women."],[10,20,"Yet their own historians honestly state that the Salic land is in Germany, between the rivers Saale and Elbe, where Charlemagne, after conquering the Saxons, left some Frenchmen settled. They despised the German women for their loose way of life, and so made this law that no woman should inherit in Salic land. That Salic land, as I said, between the Elbe and the Saale, is now called Meissen in Germany."],[21,31,"So it is clear the Salic law was not made for the kingdom of France. And the French did not hold the Salic land until four hundred and twenty-one years after the death of King Pharamond, wrongly supposed the founder of this law, who died in the year of our Lord 426; and Charlemagne conquered the Saxons and settled the French beyond the river Saale in the year 805. Besides, their writers say"],[32,35,"that King Pepin, who deposed Childeric, claimed the crown of France as general heir, because he was descended from Blithild, daughter of King Clothair."],[36,44,"Hugh Capet, too, who seized the crown from Charles, Duke of Lorraine, the only male heir of Charlemagne's true line, tried to give his title some show of truth, though in fact it was corrupt and worthless, by presenting himself as heir to Lady Lingare, daughter of Charlemain, who was son of the Emperor Louis, who was son of Charlemagne. Also King Louis the Tenth,"],[45,52,"who was sole heir of the usurper Capet, could not rest easy in his conscience wearing the crown of France until he was satisfied that his grandmother, fair Queen Isabel, descended from Lady Ermengare, daughter of that same Charles, Duke of Lorraine. By that marriage Charlemagne's line was joined to the crown of France again."],[53,62,"So, as clear as the summer sun, King Pepin's title, Hugh Capet's claim and King Louis's peace of mind all rest on descent through the female line. The kings of France do the same to this day, though they would hold up this Salic law to bar your Highness from claiming through the female, preferring to hide in a tangle of excuses rather than openly expose their crooked titles, stolen from you and your ancestors."]],"40":[[0,3,"Let the sin be on my head, dread king! For in the Book of Numbers it is written: when a man dies, let his inheritance pass to his daughter."],[4,13,"Gracious lord, stand up for what is yours! Unfurl your bloody battle flag! Look back to your mighty ancestors! Go, my dread lord, to the tomb of your great-grandfather, from whom you claim; call up his warlike spirit, and that of your great-uncle Edward the Black Prince, who staged a tragedy on French soil, defeating the whole army of France, while his mighty father stood smiling on a hill to watch his lion's cub feed on the blood of French nobles."],[14,17,"Oh, those noble English, who could take on the full pride of France with half their forces, while the other half stood by laughing, idle and cold for want of action!"]],"47":[[0,2,"We don't mean only the hit-and-run raiders; we fear a full invasion by the Scots, who have always been an unreliable neighbour to us."],[3,11,"For you will read that my great-grandfather never took his forces into France without the Scots pouring into his undefended kingdom like the tide through a gap in a sea wall, in full force, harrying the stripped land with fierce attacks and surrounding castles and towns with cruel sieges, so that England, empty of defenders, shook and trembled at such a bad neighbour."]],"48":[[0,3,"Then she was more frightened than hurt, my lord. Just take her own past as an example: when all her knights were in France and she was a widow mourning her absent nobles,"],[4,10,"she not only defended herself well but captured the King of Scots like a stray animal and sent him to France, to add captive kings to King Edward's fame, and to make her history as rich with praise as the ooze at the bottom of the sea is with sunken wrecks and countless treasure."]],"50":[[0,3,"So it follows that the cat must stay at home. But that is a forced conclusion, since we have locks to protect our goods and clever traps to catch petty thieves."],[4,9,"While the armed hand fights abroad, the wise head defends itself at home. For government, though made of high, low and lower ranks, each with its part, keeps in harmony, coming together in one full and natural ending, like music."]],"51":[[0,6,"That is why heaven divides human society into different roles, keeping effort always in motion, with obedience fixed as its aim or target. That is how honey-bees work, creatures that by natural law teach a populated kingdom how to be orderly."],[7,13,"They have a king and officers of different ranks: some, like magistrates, keep order at home; others, like merchants, trade abroad; others, like soldiers armed with their stings, raid the soft buds of summer and bring the plunder home in merry procession to the royal tent of their emperor."],[14,21,"He, busy with his royal duties, watches the singing masons building roofs of gold, the orderly citizens kneading the honey, the poor labourers crowding their heavy loads through his narrow gate, and the grave-eyed judge, with his stern hum, handing the lazy, yawning drone over to the pale executioners. From this I conclude:"],[22,30,"that many things, all directed to one agreed end, may work in different ways: as many arrows shot from different places hit one target, as many roads meet in one town, as many fresh streams flow into one salt sea, as many lines meet at the centre of a sundial, so a thousand actions, once begun, can end in one purpose and all succeed without failure."],[31,37,"So, to France, my lord! Divide your fortunate England into four, take one quarter into France, and with it you will make all France shake. If we, with three times that many left at home, can't defend our own doors from the dog, let us be savaged, and let our nation lose its name for courage and good government."]],"54":[[0,7,"Now we have made up our mind, and, with God's help and yours, the noble muscles of our power, since France is ours, we'll bend it to our authority or break it all to pieces. Either we'll sit there ruling in wide, full command over France and its almost royal dukedoms, or lay these bones in a humble urn, with no tomb and no memorial over them."],[8,11,"Either our history will speak loudly and freely of our deeds, or else our grave, like a Turkish mute with his tongue cut out, will have no voice and not even the honour of a wax epitaph."]],"59":[[0,3,"Then, in brief: your Highness recently sent to France claiming certain dukedoms by right of your great ancestor King Edward the Third."],[4,12,"In answer, the prince our master says that you smack too much of your youth, and advises you that nothing in France can be won by dancing a lively galliard; you cannot party your way into dukedoms there. So he sends you this barrel of treasure, more suitable to your character, and in return asks that the dukedoms you claim hear no more from you. That is the Dauphin's message."]],"62":[[0,7,"We are glad the Dauphin is so playful with us. We thank you for his present and your trouble. When we have matched our rackets to these balls, we will, by God's grace, play a set in France that will knock his father's crown into the hazard. Tell him he has made a match with such a tough opponent that all the courts of France will be disturbed with chases. And we understand"],[8,13,"him well, how he taunts us with our wilder days, without measuring what use we made of them. We never valued this humble throne of England, and so, living away from it, we gave ourselves up to wild freedom, as men are always merriest when they are away from home."],[14,21,"But tell the Dauphin I will keep my royal dignity, behave like a king, and show the full sail of my greatness when I rise up on my throne of France. For that I have set aside my majesty and plodded like a common working man; but I will rise there in such full glory that I will dazzle every eye in France, yes, strike the Dauphin blind to look at us."],[22,29,"And tell the playful prince that his joke has turned his balls into cannonballs, and his soul will carry the heavy guilt for the destructive vengeance that flies with them. This mockery of his will mock many thousand widows out of their dear husbands, mock mothers out of their sons, mock castles down; and some not yet conceived or born will have reason to curse the Dauphin's scorn."],[30,38,"But all this lies in the will of God, to whom I appeal, and in whose name tell the Dauphin I am coming to avenge myself as I can, and to stretch out my rightful hand in a holy cause. So go in peace, and tell the Dauphin his joke will seem shallow when thousands weep for it, more than ever laughed at it. (To attendants) Escort them safely. (To the Ambassadors) Farewell."]],"65":[[0,4,"We hope to make the sender blush for it. So, my lords, waste no good moment that could speed our expedition, for we now have no thought except France, apart from our thoughts of God, which come before our business."],[5,11,"So let our supplies for these wars be gathered quickly, and everything considered that might sensibly add feathers to our wings; for, with God leading, we'll scold this Dauphin at his father's door. So let every man now put his mind to it, so that this good enterprise may be set in motion."]],"69":[[0,6,"Now all the young men of England are on fire, and silk party clothes are left in the wardrobe. Now armourers do good business, and the thought of honour rules alone in every man's heart. They sell their pasture land to buy a horse, following the model of all Christian kings, with winged heels, like English versions of Mercury."],[7,10,"For Expectation now sits in the air and hides a sword from hilt to point with crowns of kings, crowns and coronets, promised to Harry and his followers."],[11,14,"The French, well informed by their spies about this terrible preparation, shake with fear and with pale cunning try to turn the English from their purpose."],[15,18,"Oh England! You are a small model of your inner greatness, like a little body with a mighty heart. What might you not do that honour would have you do, if all your children were loving and loyal!"],[19,29,"But see your fault! France has found in you a nest of hollow hearts, which it fills with treacherous gold coins; and three corrupted men, first, Richard Earl of Cambridge, second, Henry Lord Scroop of Masham, and third, Sir Thomas Grey, knight of Northumberland, have, for the gilt of France (oh, guilt indeed!), sealed a conspiracy with frightened France; and by their hands this finest of kings must die, if hell and treason keep their promises, before he takes ship for France, in Southampton."],[30,41,"Keep your patience a little longer, and we'll make you swallow the jumps in place and force them into a play. The bribe is paid, the traitors have agreed, the King has left London, and the scene now moves, gentlemen, to Southampton. That is the playhouse now, there you must sit, and from there we'll carry you safely to France and back, charming the narrow sea to give you a smooth crossing, for, if we can help it, we won't make a single stomach seasick with our play. But not until the King comes out, and not until then, will we shift our scene to Southampton."]],"96":[[0,6,"\"Coupe la gorge!\", \"cut the throat!\", that's the word. I defy you again. Oh hound of Crete, do you think you'll get my wife? No! Go to the hospital, and from the sweating tub of disgrace fetch out that diseased bird of Cressida's kind, Doll Tearsheet by name, and marry her."],[7,9,"I have, and I will keep, the former Quickly as my one and only; and, in short, that's enough. Go on."]],"144":[[0,4,"Alas, your great love and care for me are heavy prayers against this poor wretch! If little faults that come from drunkenness are not to be overlooked, how wide shall we open our eyes when crimes worthy of death, chewed, swallowed and digested, come before us?"],[5,9,"We'll release that man all the same, though Cambridge, Scroop and Grey, in their loving care and tender protection of our person, would have him punished. And now to our French business. Who are the newly appointed commissioners?"]],"148":[[0,3,"Then, Richard Earl of Cambridge, there is yours; there's yours, Lord Scroop of Masham; and, sir knight, Grey of Northumberland, this one is yours. Read them, and know that I know what you are worth."],[4,10,"My Lord of Westmoreland and Uncle Exeter, we'll go aboard tonight. Why, what's the matter, gentlemen? What do you see in those papers that makes you lose so much colour? Look how they change! Their cheeks are as white as paper. Why, what are you reading there that has made your blood run so cowardly out of sight?"]],"151":[[0,4,"The mercy that was alive in us just now has been suppressed and killed by your own advice. For shame, you must not dare to talk of mercy, because your own arguments turn back on your hearts like dogs on their masters, tearing at you."],[5,14,"Look, my princes and noble lords, at these English monsters! My Lord of Cambridge here, you know how ready our love was to give him everything that belonged to his rank; and this man, for a few light coins, has lightly plotted and sworn to the schemes of France to kill us here in Southampton. And this knight, no less bound to us by our generosity than Cambridge is, has sworn the same. But oh,"],[15,25,"what shall I say to you, Lord Scroop? You cruel, ungrateful, savage and inhuman creature! You who held the key to all my secrets, who knew the very bottom of my soul, who could almost have turned me into gold for your own use, could you have plotted against me for your own profit? Is it possible that foreign pay could squeeze out of you one spark of evil that might hurt my finger? It is so strange that, though the truth of it stands out as plain as black and white, my eye can hardly see it."],[26,38,"Treason and murder have always gone together, like two devils in one yoke, each sworn to the other's purpose, working so obviously for their natural end that no one cried out in amazement at them. But you, against all reason, brought amazement in to wait on treason and murder; and whatever cunning devil worked on you so unnaturally has won the vote in hell as the best. Other devils who tempt men to treason patch together damnation with excuses, colours and shapes borrowed from glittering appearances of holiness."],[39,46,"But the one who moulded you just told you to rise up, and gave you no reason to commit treason except to dub you with the name of traitor. If that same demon who fooled you like this were to stride the whole world with his lion's step, he might go back to vast hell and tell its legions, \"I can never win a soul as easily as that Englishman's.\""],[47,58,"Oh, how you have infected the sweetness of trust with suspicion! Do men seem dutiful? Why, so did you. Do they seem serious and learned? Why, so did you. Do they come of noble family? Why, so did you. Do they seem religious? Why, so did you. Or are they moderate in diet, free of coarse passions of joy or anger, steady in spirit, not carried away by impulse, dressed and adorned in modest manners, not trusting the eye without the ear, and trusting neither except after clear judgement? You seemed exactly that, so finely sifted."],[59,65,"And so your fall has left a kind of stain that marks even the most complete and gifted man with some suspicion. I will weep for you; for this rebellion of yours seems to me like a second fall of man. Their crimes are plain. Arrest them to answer to the law; and may God forgive them for their plots!"]],"156":[[0,7,"May God reward you with his mercy! Hear your sentence. You have plotted against our royal person, joined a declared enemy, and taken from his treasury the gold down payment for our death. In doing so you would have sold your king to slaughter, his princes and lords to slavery, his subjects to oppression and contempt, and his whole kingdom to ruin."],[8,15,"For our own person we seek no revenge, but we must care so much for our kingdom's safety, whose ruin you have sought, that we hand you over to its laws. So go away, poor miserable wretches, to your death; and may God in his mercy give you patience to bear it and true repentance for all your grievous crimes! Take them away."]],"158":[[0,6,"Now, lords, to France; the enterprise will be as glorious for you as for us. We have no doubt of a fair and lucky war, since God has so graciously brought to light this dangerous treason lurking in our path to stop us at the start. We're sure now that every obstacle on our way has been smoothed out."],[7,11,"So onward, dear countrymen! Let us put our strength into the hands of God and set it moving at once. Cheerfully to sea! Raise the banners of war! No king of England, if not king of France!"]],"166":[[0,3,"No, he's surely not in hell. He's in Arthur's bosom, if any man ever went to Arthur's bosom. He made a finer end and went away as if he were a baby just christened. He died just between twelve and one, right at the turn of the tide."],[4,10,"For after I saw him fumble with the sheets and play with flowers and smile at his fingertips, I knew there was only one way he was going; for his nose was as sharp as a pen, and he babbled of green fields. \"How are you, Sir John!\" I said. \"Come on, man, cheer up.\" Then he cried out \"God, God, God!\" three or four times. Now I, to comfort him, told him he shouldn't think of God; I hoped there was no need for him to trouble himself with such thoughts yet."],[11,14,"Then he asked me to put more blankets on his feet. I put my hand into the bed and felt them, and they were as cold as any stone; then I felt up to his knees, and they were as cold as any stone; and so up and up, and everything was as cold as any stone."]],"178":[[0,6,"Come, let's go. My love, give me your lips. Look after my goods and furniture. Use your sense; the rule is \"cash down\". Trust no one; for oaths are straws, men's promises are thin as wafers, and Hold-fast is the only dog worth having, my duck; so let Caution be your adviser."],[7,9,"Go, dry your eyes. Comrades in arms, let's go to France, like horse-leeches, my boys, to suck, to suck, to suck the very blood!"]],"189":[[0,7,"So the English are coming at us in full strength, and it matters more than ever that we answer with royal defences. So the Dukes of Berri and Brittany, of Brabant and of Orleans, shall set out, and you, Prince Dauphin, with all speed, to man and repair our fortified towns with brave soldiers and the means of defence."],[8,13,"For England's advance is as fierce as water rushing into a whirlpool. So it suits us to be as careful as fear can teach us from recent examples left on our fields by the deadly English we underestimated."]],"190":[[0,6,"My most honoured father, it is right that we arm against the enemy; for even peace should not make a kingdom so dull, even if no war or quarrel were in question, that defences, musters and supplies are not kept up, gathered and collected as if a war were expected."],[7,15,"So I say it is right that we all go out to inspect the weak and feeble parts of France. And let's do it without showing any fear; no, with no more fear than if we heard that England were busy with a Whitsun morris dance. For, my good lord, she has such an idle king, her sceptre is carried so fancifully by a vain, giddy, shallow, moody youth, that there is nothing to fear."]],"191":[[0,6,"Oh, quiet, Prince Dauphin! You are badly mistaken about this king. Ask the ambassadors who have just returned, your Grace, with what great dignity he heard their message, how well supplied he was with noble advisers, how moderate in his objections, and at the same time how terrifying in his firm resolve."],[7,11,"And you'll find his past follies were only the outward disguise of the Roman Brutus, covering wisdom with a coat of foolishness, as gardeners hide with manure the roots that will spring up first and be the most delicate."]],"193":[[0,4,"Let us think King Harry strong; and, princes, make sure you arm strongly to meet him. His family has tasted our blood before, and he is bred from that bloody stock that haunted us on our own home ground."],[5,13,"Remember our all too memorable shame when the battle of Crecy was so fatally fought, and all our princes were captured by that dreaded name, Edward, the Black Prince of Wales, while his mountain of a father, standing on a mountain high in the air, crowned with the golden sun, watched his heroic son and smiled to see him mangle the work of nature and destroy the men whom God and French fathers had spent twenty years making."],[14,16,"This is a branch of that victorious tree; and let us fear his inborn strength and destiny."]],"202":[[0,7,"From him; and this is how he greets your Majesty. He orders you, in the name of God Almighty, to strip off and lay aside the borrowed glories that, by gift of heaven and by the law of nature and of nations, belong to him and his heirs: namely, the crown and all the wide honours that belong by custom and long tradition to the crown of France."],[8,19,"So that you may know it is no wicked or false claim picked out of the worm-holes of long-gone days or raked out of the dust of forgotten times, he sends you this most memorable family tree, true in every branch, asking you to study it; and when you find him descended directly from his most famous of famous ancestors, Edward the Third, he orders you to give up your crown and kingdom, which you hold unlawfully from him, the born and true claimant."]],"204":[[0,4,"Bloody force; for if you hide the crown even in your hearts, he will dig there for it. So he is coming in a fierce storm, in thunder and earthquake, like Jove, so that if asking fails, he will force you."],[5,12,"And he asks you, by the mercy of the Lord, to give up the crown and take pity on the poor souls for whom this hungry war is opening its huge jaws; and he turns onto your head the widows' tears, the orphans' cries, the dead men's blood and the grieving girls' groans for husbands, fathers and promised lovers who will be swallowed up in this quarrel."],[13,15,"That is his claim, his threat and my message; except for the Dauphin, if he is here, to whom I bring a particular greeting too."]],"207":[[0,2,"Scorn and defiance, low regard and contempt, and anything that does not shame the mighty sender: that is what he thinks you worth."],[3,9,"My king says this: unless your royal father sweetens the bitter joke you sent his Majesty by granting all his demands in full, he'll call you to such a hot answer for it that the caves and hollow vaults of France will rebuke your offence and throw your joke back at you in the echo of his cannon."]],"217":[[0,5,"Our quick scene flies on the wings of imagination, moving no slower than thought. Imagine you have seen the well-equipped king put his royal person aboard at the pier in Southampton, and his brave fleet fanning the young sun with silk streamers."],[6,15,"Let your imaginations play, and in them see ship-boys climbing the rope rigging; hear the shrill whistle that gives order to the confused noise; see the woven sails, filled by the invisible, creeping wind, drawing the huge hulls through the furrowed sea, breasting the high waves. Oh, just think that you stand on the shore and see a city dancing on the restless waves; for that is how this majestic fleet looks, holding its course for Harfleur."],[16,23,"Follow, follow! Hook your minds to the sterns of this navy and leave your England as still as dead midnight, guarded by grandfathers, babies and old women, either past or not yet come to strength and power. For who is there, with just one hair showing on his chin, who will not follow these hand-picked knights to France?"],[24,32,"Work, work your thoughts, and in them see a siege. See the cannons on their wheeled carriages, their deadly mouths gaping at encircled Harfleur. Imagine the French ambassador comes back and tells Harry that the King offers him his daughter Katharine and, as her dowry, some small, worthless dukedoms. The offer does not please, and the quick gunner now touches the devilish cannon with his match,"]],"222":[[0,4,"Once more into the gap in the wall, dear friends, once more, or fill the wall up with our English dead! In peace nothing suits a man so well as modest quietness and humility; but when the blast of war blows in our ears,"],[5,13,"then imitate the action of the tiger. Stiffen your muscles, call up your blood, disguise your gentle nature with grim-faced rage. Then give the eye a terrible look; let it glare through the head's gunports like a brass cannon; let the brow hang over it as fearfully as a worn-away rock overhangs and juts out over its battered base, swamped by the wild and destructive ocean."],[14,20,"Now clench your teeth and flare your nostrils, hold your breath hard, and stretch every spirit to its full height. On, on, you noblest English, whose blood comes from fathers proved in war! Fathers who, like so many Alexanders, fought in these lands from morning till evening and put away their swords only because there was no one left to fight."],[21,23,"Don't dishonour your mothers; prove now that the men you called fathers really were your fathers. Be an example now to men of coarser blood and teach them how to fight."],[24,29,"And you, good yeomen, whose limbs were grown in England, show us here the quality of your feeding. Let us swear that you are worth your upbringing, which I don't doubt; for there is not one of you so low and humble that he does not have a noble shine in his eyes."],[30,33,"I see you standing like greyhounds on the leash, straining to start. The game is on! Follow your spirit, and as you charge, cry \"God for Harry! England and Saint George!\""]],"238":[[0,5,"Young as I am, I've watched these three swaggerers. I'm servant boy to all three of them; but all three together, even if they served me, couldn't be a man to me, because three such clowns don't add up to one man. As for Bardolph, he is white-livered and red-faced, which is why he brazens it out but doesn't fight. As for Pistol, he has a killing tongue and a quiet sword, which is why he breaks his word and keeps his weapons whole."],[6,10,"As for Nym, he has heard that men of few words are the best men, and so he refuses even to say his prayers, in case he's thought a coward. But his few bad words are matched by just as few good deeds; for he never broke any man's head but his own, and that was against a post when he was drunk."],[11,15,"They'll steal anything and call it a bargain. Bardolph stole a lute-case, carried it thirty-six miles and sold it for three halfpence. Nym and Bardolph are sworn brothers in pilfering, and in Calais they stole a fire-shovel."],[16,22,"I knew by that bit of work that they would put up with anything. They wanted me to be as at home in men's pockets as in their gloves or handkerchiefs, which goes badly against my manhood, if I take from another's pocket to put in mine; for it's plainly pocketing up wrongs. I must leave them and look for a better position. Their villainy turns my weak stomach, so I must throw it up."]],"270":[[0,8,"What has the governor of the town decided? This is the last truce we will allow; so either surrender to our best mercy or, like men proud to be destroyed, defy us to do our worst. For, as I am a soldier, a name that I think suits me best, if I begin the bombardment again I will not leave half-conquered Harfleur until she lies buried in her ashes."],[9,13,"The gates of mercy will all be shut, and the soldier who has tasted blood, rough and hard-hearted, will roam freely with bloody hand and a conscience as wide as hell, mowing down your fresh, fair virgins and your blossoming babies like grass."],[14,20,"What is it to me, then, if wicked War, dressed in flames like the prince of devils, with his blackened face, does all the cruel deeds that go with destruction and ruin? What is it to me, when you yourselves are the cause, if your pure daughters fall into the hands of hot and violent rape?"],[21,31,"What rein can hold back unbridled wickedness when it charges fiercely downhill? We might as well waste our useless orders on the enraged soldiers in their looting as send commands to the whale to come ashore. So, you men of Harfleur, take pity on your town and your people while my soldiers are still under my command, while the cool, calm wind of mercy still blows away the filthy, infectious clouds of reckless murder, plunder and villainy."],[32,40,"If not, then expect in a moment to see the blind and bloody soldier with his foul hand defile the hair of your shrieking daughters; your fathers seized by their silver beards and their most respected heads smashed against the walls; your naked babies speared on pikes, while their mad mothers break the clouds with their confused howls, as the women of Judea did at Herod's bloodthirsty butchers."],[41,42,"What do you say? Will you surrender and avoid this, or, guilty of resisting, be destroyed like this?"]],"318":[[0,5,"God of battles! Where do they get this spirit? Isn't their climate foggy, raw and dull, where the sun looks pale on them as if in spite, killing their fruit with its frowns? Can boiled water, a medicine for worn-out horses, their barley-broth beer, heat their cold blood to such brave fire?"],[6,11,"And shall our lively blood, spirited by wine, seem frosty? Oh, for the honour of our country, let's not hang like dripping icicles from the thatch of our houses, while a more frosty people sweat drops of brave youth in our rich fields! We may call our fields poor in the lords they were born with."]],"321":[[0,3,"Where is Montjoy the herald? Send him off quickly. Let him greet England with our sharp defiance. Up, princes, and with a sense of honour sharper than your swords, hurry to the field!"],[4,11,"Charles Delabreth, High Constable of France; you Dukes of Orleans, Bourbon and Berri, Alencon, Brabant, Bar and Burgundy; Jacques Chatillon, Rambures, Vaudemont, Beaumont, Grandpre, Roussi and Fauconberg, Foix, Lestrale, Bouciqualt and Charolois; high dukes, great princes, barons, lords and knights, for the sake of your great estates, now free yourselves of great shame."],[12,19,"Stop Harry of England, who sweeps through our land with banners painted in the blood of Harfleur. Rush on his army as melted snow rushes on the valleys, the low servant ground on which the Alps spit and pour their spittle. Fall on him, you have power enough, and bring him to Rouen as our prisoner in a captive's cart."]],"342":[[0,6,"Fortune is Bardolph's enemy and frowns on him; for he has stolen a pax, a holy tablet, and he must be hanged. A damned death! Let gallows gape for dogs; let men go free, and let no rope choke his windpipe. But Exeter has given the sentence of death for a pax of little value."],[7,10,"So go and speak; the Duke will listen to you; and don't let Bardolph's thread of life be cut with a penny rope and vile disgrace. Speak for his life, captain, and I'll repay you."]],"353":[[0,7,"Why, he's a dupe, a fool, a rogue, who now and then goes to the wars so that he can show off on his return to London dressed as a soldier. Fellows like that know the great commanders' names perfectly, and they'll recite to you where the actions were fought: at such and such a fort, at such a breach, at such a convoy; who came off bravely, who was shot, who was disgraced, what terms the enemy held out for; and they learn this perfectly in military jargon, which they dress up with newly fashioned oaths. And what"],[8,11,"a beard trimmed like the general's and a shabby, battle-worn uniform will do among frothing bottles and ale-soaked minds is amazing to think of. But you must learn to recognise these disgraces of our time, or you can be badly fooled."]],"367":[[0,6,"This is what my King says: Tell Harry of England that though we seemed dead, we were only asleep; waiting for the right moment is a better soldier than rashness. Tell him we could have punished him at Harfleur, but we thought it best not to lance an injury until it was fully ripe. Now we speak on our cue, and our voice is royal. England shall regret his folly, see his weakness and wonder at our patience. So tell him"],[7,12,"to think about his ransom, which must match the losses we have suffered, the subjects we have lost, the disgrace we have swallowed; to repay that in full, his small power would collapse. For our losses his treasury is too poor; for the blood we have shed, the whole muster of his kingdom is too small a number; and for our disgrace, his own person kneeling at our feet would be only a weak and worthless compensation. Add defiance"],[13,15,"to this, and tell him finally that he has betrayed his followers, whose doom is already pronounced. So far my King and master; that is my duty done."]],"370":[[0,8,"You do your job well. Go back and tell your King I am not looking for him now, but would be happy to march on to Calais without being stopped; for, to tell the truth, though it isn't wise to admit so much to a cunning enemy with the advantage, my men are badly weakened by sickness, my numbers reduced, and the few I have are hardly better than so many Frenchmen;"],[9,13,"though when they were healthy, I tell you, herald, I thought one pair of English legs could march as well as three Frenchmen. Yet, God forgive me for boasting like this! This French air of yours has blown that vice into me. I must repent."],[14,20,"So go and tell your master here I am. My ransom is this frail and worthless body, my army only a weak and sickly guard. Yet, with God leading, tell him we will come on, even if France himself and another neighbour like him stand in our way. There's something for your trouble, Montjoy. Go, tell your master to think carefully."],[21,27,"If we may pass, we will; if we are stopped, we will stain your brown earth with your red blood. And so, Montjoy, farewell. Our whole answer comes to this: as we are, we would not seek a battle; nor, as we are, do we say we will avoid one. Tell your master that."]],"457":[[0,6,"Now imagine a time when creeping murmurs and the straining dark fill the wide bowl of the universe. From camp to camp, through the foul womb of night, the hum of each army sounds quietly, so that the posted sentries can almost hear the secret whispers of each other's watch."],[7,13,"Fire answers fire, and through their pale flames each army sees the other's shadowed faces. Horse threatens horse with high, boastful neighs piercing the dull ear of night; and from the tents the armourers, fitting out the knights, closing up rivets with busy hammers, give a dreadful sound of preparation."],[14,21,"The country cocks crow, the clocks strike and announce three o'clock in the drowsy morning. Proud of their numbers and confident in spirit, the cocky, over-eager French gamble at dice for the low-valued English, and scold the crippled, slow-footed Night, who limps away so tediously like a foul, ugly witch."],[22,27,"The poor condemned English sit patiently like sacrifices by their watch-fires and brood inwardly on the morning's danger; and their sad bearing, along with their thin, hollow cheeks and war-worn coats, make them look to the staring moon like so many horrible ghosts."],[28,33,"Oh, now whoever sees the royal captain of this ruined band walking from watch to watch, from tent to tent, let him cry \"Praise and glory on his head!\" For out he goes and visits all his army, wishes them good morning with a modest smile, and calls them brothers, friends and countrymen."],[34,41,"On his royal face there is no sign of what a terrible army surrounds him, nor does he give up one bit of colour to the weary, sleepless night; he looks fresh and overcomes exhaustion with a cheerful look and sweet majesty, so that every wretch, pining and pale before, takes comfort from his looks when he sees him."],[42,46,"Like the sun, his generous eye gives a gift to everyone, thawing cold fear, so that high and low alike see, as far as our poor skill can show it, a little touch of Harry in the night."],[47,52,"And so our scene must fly to the battle, where, oh, for pity's sake, we will badly disgrace the name of Agincourt with four or five vile, ragged swords, clumsily handled in a ridiculous brawl. Still, sit and watch, imagining the real things from our imitations of them."]],"460":[[0,6,"Gloucester, it's true that we are in great danger, so our courage must be all the greater. Good morning, brother Bedford. God Almighty! There is some spirit of good in bad things, if men would carefully distil it out. For our bad neighbour makes us get up early, which is both healthy and good management."],[7,11,"Besides, they are like our consciences outside us, and preachers to us all, warning us to prepare ourselves well for our end. In this way we can gather honey from the weed and draw a moral lesson from the devil himself."]],"464":[[0,5,"It's good for men to love their present hardships by following an example like that; it eases the spirit. And when the mind is quickened, no doubt the body's organs, though dead and sluggish before, break out of their drowsy grave and move again, like a snake that has shed its skin, with fresh nimbleness."],[6,9,"Lend me your cloak, Sir Thomas. Brothers both, give my greetings to the princes in our camp; wish them good morning from me, and ask them all to come to my tent soon."]],"514":[[0,5,"No, and it isn't right that he should. For, though I say it to you, I think the King is only a man, like me. The violet smells the same to him as to me; the sky looks the same to him as to me; all his senses work only as human senses do. With his ceremonies put aside, naked he appears only a man; and though his feelings fly higher than ours, when they swoop down, they swoop on the same kind of wing."],[6,9,"So when he sees reason to be afraid, as we do, his fears no doubt taste the same as ours; yet, reasonably, no one should give him any sign of fear, in case he shows it and disheartens his army."]],"521":[[0,5,"But if the cause isn't good, the King himself has a heavy account to settle, when all those legs and arms and heads chopped off in a battle join together on Judgement Day and all cry out, \"We died at such a place\": some swearing, some crying for a surgeon, some for the wives they left poor behind them, some for the debts they owe, some for their children left unprovided for."],[6,10,"I'm afraid few men die well who die in a battle; for how can they settle anything in a Christian spirit when blood is their business? Now, if these men don't die well, it will be a black matter for the King who led them to it, since to disobey him would be against all the duty of a subject."]],"522":[[0,6,"So, if a son who is sent by his father on a trading voyage dies at sea in a state of sin, by your rule the blame for his wickedness should be laid on the father who sent him; or if a servant carrying a sum of money under his master's orders is attacked by robbers and dies with many unconfessed sins, you could call the master's business the cause of the servant's damnation. But that is not so."],[7,11,"The King is not bound to answer for the particular ends of his soldiers, nor the father of his son, nor the master of his servant; for they don't intend their deaths when they ask for their services. Besides, there is no king, however spotless his cause, who, if it comes to settlement by the sword, can fight it out with soldiers who are all spotless."],[12,17,"Some perhaps carry the guilt of planned and plotted murder; some of seducing virgins with broken promises; some make the wars their shelter, having earlier wounded the gentle breast of Peace with looting and robbery. Now if these men have cheated the law and escaped punishment at home, though they can outrun men, they have no wings to fly from God."],[18,24,"War is his officer, war is his vengeance; so that here men are punished for earlier breaches of the King's laws in what is now the King's quarrel. Where they feared death, they escaped with their lives; and where they think they are safe, they die. So if they die unprepared, the King is no more guilty of their damnation than he was guilty before of the sins for which they are now punished."],[25,31,"Every subject's duty belongs to the King, but every subject's soul is his own. So every soldier in the wars should do what every sick man in his bed does: wash every speck out of his conscience. Dying like that, death is a gain to him; or if he doesn't die, the time was blessedly spent in which such readiness was gained; and of the man who escapes, it would be no sin to think that, since he made God so free an offer, God let him outlive that day to see His greatness and to teach others how to prepare themselves."]],"543":[[0,3,"Indeed, the French can bet twenty French crowns to one that they'll beat us, since they carry them on their shoulders. But it's no treason in England to cut French crowns, and tomorrow the King himself will be a coin-clipper."],[4,13,"Lay it all on the King! Let us lay our lives, our souls, our debts, our worried wives, our children and our sins on the King! We must carry everything. Oh, hard condition, born twin with greatness, at the mercy of the talk of every fool who can feel nothing but his own stomach-ache! What endless peace of mind must kings go without that private men enjoy! And what do kings have that private men do not have too, except ceremony, public ceremony?"],[14,23,"And what are you, you idol Ceremony? What kind of god are you, who suffers more human griefs than your worshippers do? What are your rents? What is your income? Oh Ceremony, just show me your worth! What is the essence of the worship you get? Are you anything but rank, status and outward form, creating awe and fear in other men? In which you, being feared, are less happy than they are in fearing."],[24,32,"What do you often drink, instead of sweet respect, except poisoned flattery? Oh, be sick, great greatness, and tell your Ceremony to cure you! Do you think the burning fever will go out at titles puffed up by flattery? Will it give way to curtseys and low bows? When you command the beggar's knee, can you command its health? No, you proud dream that plays so cunningly with a king's rest."],[33,44,"I am a king who sees through you, and I know that it is not the holy oil, the sceptre and the orb, the sword, the mace, the imperial crown, the robe woven with gold and pearl, the stuffed-up titles announced before the King, the throne he sits on, or the tide of pomp that beats on the high shore of this world; no, not all these, thrice-gorgeous Ceremony, not all these, laid in a royal bed, can sleep as soundly as the wretched slave who, with full belly and empty mind, goes to rest, stuffed with bread earned by hard toil."],[45,54,"He never sees horrible night, the child of hell, but like a servant sweats in the sun's eye from sunrise to sunset, and all night sleeps in paradise; next day after dawn he gets up and helps the sun god onto his horse, and so follows the ever-running year with useful labour to his grave. And except for ceremony, such a wretch, filling his days with toil and his nights with sleep, would have the upper hand and the advantage over a king."],[55,58,"The slave, a member of the peaceful country, enjoys its peace, but in his dull brain has little idea what sleepless watch the King keeps to protect that peace, whose hours benefit the peasant most of all."]],"549":[[0,5,"Oh God of battles, steel my soldiers' hearts! Don't fill them with fear. Take from them now the ability to count, in case the numbers against them pluck their hearts out. Not today, oh Lord, oh, not today, don't think about the crime my father committed in seizing the crown!"],[6,13,"I have buried Richard's body again, and shed more repentant tears on it than drops of blood were forced out of it. I pay five hundred poor people a year, who twice a day hold up their withered hands to heaven to ask pardon for that blood; and I have built two chapels where sad, solemn priests continually sing for Richard's soul."],[14,16,"I will do more, though all I can do is worth nothing, since my repentance comes after all, begging forgiveness."]],"569":[[0,8,"To horse, you gallant princes! Straight to horse! Just look at that poor, starving band over there, and your fine appearance will suck the souls out of them, leaving them only the shells and husks of men. There isn't work enough for all our hands; there's hardly blood enough in all their sickly veins to stain each bare cutlass that our French gallants will draw today and sheathe again for lack of sport."],[9,17,"We only have to blow on them, and the breath of our courage will knock them over. It is certain beyond any objection, lords, that our spare servants and our peasants, who swarm uselessly around our battle formations, would be enough to clear this field of such a worthless enemy, while we stood by at the foot of this hill watching idly; but our honour won't allow it."],[18,22,"What more is there to say? Let us do a very little, and it's all done. Then let the trumpets sound the call to mount; for our advance will so terrify the field that England will crouch down in fear and surrender."]],"571":[[0,6,"Why are you waiting so long, my lords of France? Those island corpses over there, hopeless of saving their bones, make an ugly sight on the morning field. Their ragged banners hang down poorly, and our air shakes them very scornfully as it passes. Mighty Mars seems bankrupt in their beggarly army and peeps feebly through a rusty visor."],[7,14,"The horsemen sit like fixed candlesticks with lances in their hands, and their poor nags droop their heads, their skin and hips sagging, the gum dripping from their pale, dead eyes, and in their pale, dull mouths the jointed bit lies foul with chewed grass, still and motionless; and their executioners, the rascally crows, fly over them, impatient for their hour."],[15,17,"No words can describe the life of such an army, which looks so lifeless while still alive."]],"589":[[0,5,"Who is it who wishes that? My cousin Westmoreland? No, my fair cousin. If we are marked out to die, there are enough of us for our country to lose; and if we are to live, the fewer men, the greater share of honour. By God's will, I beg you, don't wish for one man more."],[6,11,"By Jove, I am not greedy for gold, and I don't care who eats at my expense; it doesn't trouble me if men wear my clothes; such outward things are not what I desire. But if it is a sin to long for honour, I am the most sinful soul alive."],[12,21,"No, honestly, cousin, don't wish for one more man from England. God's peace! I would not lose as great a share of honour as one more man would take from me, I think, for the best hope I have. Oh, don't wish for one more! Rather announce through my army, Westmoreland, that whoever has no stomach for this fight may leave. His passport will be made out, and money for his journey put in his purse. We would not die in the company of a man who is afraid to die alongside us."],[22,30,"Today is called the feast of Crispian. He who outlives this day and comes home safe will stand on tiptoe when this day is named and rouse himself at the name of Crispian. He who lives through this day and sees old age will feast his neighbours every year on the eve of it and say, \"Tomorrow is Saint Crispian's.\" Then he will roll up his sleeve and show his scars and say, \"I got these wounds on Crispin's day.\""],[31,37,"Old men forget; yet even when everything else is forgotten, he'll remember, with a few additions, what deeds he did that day. Then our names, as familiar on his lips as everyday words, Harry the King, Bedford and Exeter, Warwick and Talbot, Salisbury and Gloucester, will be freshly remembered over their overflowing cups."],[38,42,"The good man will teach this story to his son; and Crispin Crispian shall never go by, from this day to the end of the world, without our being remembered in it: we few, we happy few, we band of brothers."],[43,49,"For whoever sheds his blood with me today shall be my brother; however low his birth, this day will raise him to a gentleman. And gentlemen in England now in bed will think themselves cursed that they were not here, and think their manhood cheap while anyone speaks who fought with us on Saint Crispin's day."]],"598":[[0,4,"Once more I have come to learn from you, King Harry, whether you will now agree on your ransom before your certain defeat; for you are surely so close to the whirlpool that you must be swallowed up."],[5,9,"Besides, out of mercy, the Constable asks you to remind your followers to repent, so that their souls may make a peaceful and sweet withdrawal from these fields, where their poor bodies, wretches, must lie and rot."]],"601":[[0,4,"Please take my earlier answer back: tell them to capture me first and then sell my bones. Good God, why do they mock poor men like this? The man who once sold the lion's skin while the beast was still alive was killed hunting him."],[5,13,"Many of our bodies, no doubt, will find graves at home, on which, I trust, brass memorials will record this day's work; and those who leave their brave bones in France, dying like men, though buried in your dunghills, will be famous; for the sun will greet them there and draw their honours steaming up to heaven, leaving their earthly parts to choke your air, and the smell will breed a plague in France."],[14,17,"So note the overflowing courage of our English, who even when dead, like a bullet bouncing on, break out into a second round of damage, killing as they rot."],[18,25,"Let me speak proudly: tell the Constable we are only warriors in our working clothes. Our finery and gilding are all soiled from marching in the rain over the hard field; there isn't a feather in our army (good proof, I hope, that we won't fly away), and time has worn us into scruffiness. But, by the Mass, our hearts are in good trim."],[26,31,"And my poor soldiers tell me that before night they'll be in fresher clothes, or they'll pull the gay new coats over the French soldiers' heads and dismiss them from service. If they do this, as they will if God pleases, my ransom will soon be raised."],[32,35,"Herald, save yourself the trouble. Don't come for ransom any more, gentle herald. They'll get none, I swear, except these limbs of mine; and if they get them in the state I'll leave them in, they'll be worth little to them. Tell the Constable that."]],"666":[[0,7,"And dressed like that, brave soldier, he lies, enriching the ground with his blood; and by his bloody side, partner of his honourable wounds, lies the noble Earl of Suffolk too. Suffolk died first; and York, all hacked about, came to him where he lay steeped in blood, took him by the beard, and kissed the gashes that gaped bloodily in his face."],[8,12,"He cried aloud, \"Wait, my cousin Suffolk! My soul will keep yours company to heaven. Wait for mine, sweet soul, then fly side by side, as we kept together as knights in this glorious and hard-fought field.\""],[13,20,"At these words I came and comforted him. He smiled up into my face, reached me his hand, and with a feeble grip said, \"My dear lord, commend my service to my king.\" Then he turned and threw his wounded arm over Suffolk's neck and kissed his lips; and so, married to death, he sealed with his blood a promise of love that ended nobly."],[21,25,"The beautiful, sweet way of it forced from me those tears I would have held back; but I didn't have enough of the man in me, and all my mother's softness came into my eyes and gave me up to tears."]],"679":[[0,7,"I think it is in Macedon that Alexander was born. I tell you, captain, if you look at the maps of the world, I promise you'll find, in comparing Macedon and Monmouth, that the positions, look you, are both alike. There's a river in Macedon, and there's also a river at Monmouth; it's called the Wye at Monmouth, but I can't remember the name of the other river; but it's all the same, it's as alike as my fingers are to my fingers, and there are salmon in both."],[8,14,"If you look carefully at Alexander's life, Harry of Monmouth's life follows it fairly well; for there are parallels in all things. Alexander, God knows, and you know, in his rages and his furies and his wraths and his angers and his moods and his displeasures and his indignations, and also being a little drunk in his brain, did, in his ale and his anger, look you, kill his best friend, Cleitus."]],"686":[[0,4,"I have not been angry since I came to France until this moment. Take a trumpeter, herald, and ride to the horsemen on that hill over there. If they want to fight us, tell them to come down, or leave the field; they offend our sight."],[5,10,"If they'll do neither, we'll come to them and make them scurry away as fast as stones shot from the old Assyrian slings. Besides, we'll cut the throats of the prisoners we have, and not a man of those we take shall taste our mercy. Go and tell them so."]],"691":[[0,4,"No, great King; I come to ask your charitable permission to walk over this bloody field to record our dead and then bury them, and to sort our nobles from our common men."],[5,11,"For many of our princes, alas, lie drowned and soaked in the blood of hired soldiers; and our common men soak their peasant limbs in the blood of princes; and their wounded horses struggle fetlock-deep in gore and in wild rage kick out with their armoured heels at their dead masters, killing them twice."],[12,13,"Oh, give us permission, great King, to look over the field in safety and dispose of their dead bodies!"]],"732":[[0,5,"My Lord of Warwick and my brother Gloucester, follow close on Fluellen's heels. The glove I've given him as a token may well earn him a box on the ear. It's the soldier's; by our bargain I should be wearing it myself."],[6,13,"Follow, good cousin Warwick. If the soldier strikes him, and I judge from his blunt manner that he'll keep his word, some sudden trouble may come of it; for I know Fluellen is brave, and when stirred to anger is as hot as gunpowder and quick to return an injury. Follow, and make sure no harm comes between them. Come with me, Uncle Exeter."]],"772":[[0,10,"This list tells me of ten thousand French who lie dead on the field. Of princes and nobles with their own banners, a hundred and twenty-six lie dead among them; and besides these, eight thousand four hundred knights, squires and gallant gentlemen, five hundred of whom were knighted only yesterday. So among these ten thousand they have lost, there are only sixteen hundred hired soldiers; the rest are princes, barons, lords, knights, squires and gentlemen of good birth and rank."],[11,20,"These are the names of their nobles who lie dead: Charles Delabreth, High Constable of France; Jacques of Chatillon, Admiral of France; the master of the crossbowmen, Lord Rambures; the Great Master of France, brave Sir Guichard Dauphin; John Duke of Alencon; Anthony Duke of Brabant, the brother of the Duke of Burgundy; and Edward Duke of Bar; and of vigorous earls, Grandpre and Roussi, Fauconberg and Foix, Beaumont and Marle, Vaudemont and Lestrale."],[21,22,"This was a royal company of death! Where is the count of our English dead?"]],"774":[[0,3,"Edward the Duke of York, the Earl of Suffolk, Sir Richard Ketly, Davy Gam, squire; no one else of rank; and of all other men only twenty-five. Oh God, your arm was here;"],[4,9,"and we give all the credit not to ourselves but to your arm alone! When, without any trick, but in a straight clash and fair fight of battle, was so great a loss on one side and so little on the other ever known? Take it, God, for it is yours alone!"]],"784":[[0,5,"Allow those who have not read the story to let me prompt them; and as for those who have, I humbly ask them to excuse our handling of time, of numbers and of the proper course of events, which cannot be shown here in their huge, true life."],[6,13,"Now we carry the King towards Calais. Imagine him there; and having seen him there, lift him on your winged thoughts across the sea. Look, the English beach is fenced in at the water's edge with men, wives and boys, whose shouts and clapping drown out the deep-voiced sea, which, like a mighty usher clearing a path before the King, seems to prepare his way. So let him land, and see him set off solemnly for London."],[14,21,"Thought moves so fast that even now you can imagine him on Blackheath, where his lords want him to have his dented helmet and his bent sword carried before him through the city. He forbids it, being free of vanity and self-glorifying pride, and gives all the trophies, signs and display away from himself to God."],[22,27,"But now see, in the quick forge and workshop of thought, how London pours out her citizens! The mayor and all his fellow aldermen in their best clothes, like the senators of ancient Rome, with the common people swarming at their heels, go out and bring in their conquering Caesar."],[28,34,"Just as, to take a lesser but loving comparison, if the general of our gracious queen were now coming from Ireland, as in good time he may, with rebellion skewered on his sword, how many would leave the peaceful city to welcome him! Much more, and with much more reason, did they welcome this Harry."],[35,40,"Now place him in London, while the grief of the French still keeps the King of England at home, with the Emperor coming on France's behalf to arrange peace between them; and skip over everything that happened, whatever it was, until Harry's return to France."],[41,44,"That's where we must bring him; and I have acted out the interval by reminding you that it has passed. So put up with the shortening, and let your eyes follow your thoughts straight back again to France."]],"816":[[0,3,"Is Fortune playing the hussy with me now? I've had news that my Doll has died in the hospital of the French disease, and so my refuge there is quite cut off."],[4,9,"I'm growing old, and honour has been beaten out of my tired limbs. Well, I'll become a pimp, and turn a bit to quick-handed pickpocketing. I'll sneak back to England, and there I'll steal; and I'll put patches on these cudgel scars and swear I got them in the French wars."]],"825":[[0,5,"My duty to you both, with equal love, great Kings of France and England! Your mightinesses on both sides can best witness that I have worked, with all my wits, my effort and strong endeavours, to bring your most royal Majesties to this court and royal meeting."],[6,14,"Since, then, my work has succeeded so far that you have greeted each other face to face and royal eye to eye, don't think it a disgrace in me if I ask, before this royal company, what obstacle or hindrance there is to stop poor, naked, mangled Peace, dear nurse of the arts, of plenty and of joyful births, from showing her lovely face in this best garden of the world, our fertile France."],[15,24,"Alas, she has been chased out of France too long, and all her farming lies in heaps, rotting in its own richness. Her vine, the merry cheerer of the heart, dies unpruned; her hedges, once neatly woven, put out disordered twigs like prisoners wildly overgrown with hair; on her unploughed fields darnel, hemlock and rank fumitory take root, while the ploughshare that should uproot such wild growth rusts."],[25,30,"The level meadow, which once sweetly produced the speckled cowslip, burnet and green clover, lacking the scythe, all untended and overgrown, breeds through idleness and produces nothing but hateful docks, rough thistles, hemlock stalks and burrs, losing both beauty and usefulness."],[31,39,"And just as our vineyards, fallow fields, meadows and hedges, failing in their nature, grow wild, so our households and we and our children have lost, or do not learn for lack of time, the skills that should grace our country, but grow like savages, as soldiers do who think of nothing but blood, to swearing and grim looks, untidy clothes and everything that seems unnatural."],[40,44,"You are assembled to restore us to our former grace; and my speech begs to know the obstacle that stops gentle Peace from driving out these harms and blessing us with her former gifts."]],"848":[[0,8,"Indeed, if you made me write verses or dance for your sake, Kate, you'd ruin me; for the first, I have neither the words nor the metre, and for the second, I have no strength in measure, though a reasonable measure of strength. If I could win a lady at leapfrog, or by vaulting into my saddle with my armour on my back, I'd quickly leap into a wife, if I may say so without boasting. Or if I could box for my love, or make my horse prance for her favours, I could lay on like a butcher and sit as tight as a monkey, never thrown."],[9,17,"But, before God, Kate, I can't look lovesick or gasp out my eloquence, and I have no skill in making vows; only plain oaths, which I never use till I'm pushed, and never break however I'm pushed. If you can love a fellow of this kind, Kate, whose face isn't worth sunburning, who never looks in his mirror for love of anything he sees there, let your eye dress me up like a cook. I speak to you as a plain soldier. If you can love me for this, take me; if not, to tell you I'll die is true, but for love of you, by the Lord, no; and yet I do love you."],[18,27,"And as long as you live, dear Kate, take a man of plain, uncounterfeited constancy; for he has to treat you right, because he hasn't the gift to woo elsewhere. These fellows with endless tongues, who can rhyme their way into ladies' favours, always reason their way out again. What! A talker is only a chatterer; a rhyme is only a ballad. A good leg will shrink; a straight back will bend; a black beard will turn white; a curly head will go bald; a fair face will wither; a bright eye will grow hollow; but a good heart, Kate, is the sun and the moon, or rather the sun and not the moon, for it shines bright and never changes, but keeps its course truly."],[28,30,"If you want such a one, take me; and if you take me, you take a soldier; take a soldier, take a king. And what do you say then to my love? Speak, my fair one, and kindly, I beg you."]],"856":[[0,5,"Can any of your neighbours tell, Kate? I'll ask them. Come, I know you love me; and at night, when you go into your private room, you'll ask this lady about me; and I know, Kate, you'll criticise to her the parts of me that you love in your heart. But, good Kate, mock me mercifully, all the more, gentle princess, because I love you cruelly."],[6,11,"If you are ever mine, Kate, as a saving faith inside me tells me you will be, I'll have won you by fighting, so you are bound to be a good mother of soldiers. Shall you and I, between Saint Denis and Saint George, make a boy, half French, half English, who will go to Constantinople and take the Turk by the beard? Shan't we? What do you say, my fair lily?"]],"860":[[0,9,"Now, shame on my false French! On my honour, in true English, I love you, Kate; by that honour I don't dare swear you love me, and yet my blood begins to flatter me that you do, in spite of the poor and unappealing effect of my face. Now, curse my father's ambition! He was thinking of civil wars when he fathered me, so I was made with a stubborn outside and an iron face, so that when I come to woo ladies I frighten them. But honestly, Kate, the older I get, the better I'll look. My comfort is that old age, that bad keeper of beauty, can do no more damage to my face."],[10,18,"You have me, if you have me, at my worst; and you'll wear me, if you wear me, better and better. So tell me, most fair Katharine, will you have me? Put off your girlish blushes; declare the thoughts of your heart with the looks of an empress; take me by the hand and say, \"Harry of England, I am yours\"; and no sooner will you bless my ear with that word than I'll tell you aloud, \"England is yours, Ireland is yours, France is yours, and Henry Plantagenet is yours\", who, though I say it to his face, if he isn't equal to the best king, you'll find him the best king of good fellows."],[19,21,"Come, your answer in broken music; for your voice is music and your English is broken; so, queen of all, Katharine, open your mind to me in broken English. Will you have me?"]],"874":[[0,5,"Oh Kate, fussy customs bow to great kings. Dear Kate, you and I can't be confined within the weak limits of a country's fashion. We are the makers of manners, Kate; and the freedom that goes with our position stops the mouths of all fault-finders, as I will stop yours for keeping up the fussy custom of your country in refusing me a kiss; so, be patient and give in. (Kisses her)"],[6,9,"You have witchcraft in your lips, Kate; there is more eloquence in a sweet touch of them than in the tongues of the whole French council; and they would sooner persuade Harry of England than a joint petition from all the kings. Here comes your father."]],"902":[[0,5,"May God, the best maker of all marriages, join your hearts in one and your kingdoms in one! As man and wife, being two, are one in love, so let there be such a marriage between your kingdoms that no evil act or cruel suspicion, which often troubles the bed of blessed marriage,"],[6,9,"may ever push in between the agreement of these kingdoms to divorce their united alliance; so that the English may accept the French, and the French the English, as each other's own. May God say Amen to this!"]],"907":[[0,3,"So far, with a rough and quite inadequate pen, our struggling author has followed the story, confining mighty men in a little room and mangling the full course of their glory by jumping from moment to moment."],[4,7,"This star of England lived only a short time, but in that short time he lived most greatly. Fortune made his sword, with which he won the world's best garden and left his son its royal lord."],[8,13,"Henry the Sixth, crowned King of France and England as a baby in swaddling clothes, succeeded this king; and so many people had the running of his state that they lost France and made his England bleed, which our stage has often shown; and for the sake of those plays, let this one find acceptance in your kind minds."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"kingship":["Kingship","What makes a good king: public duty, private cost, ceremony, and the right to rule."],"war":["War and its cost","Glory and slaughter, sieges and prisoners, and the price paid by ordinary men and by France."],"nation":["England and Britain","National pride, the four nations in Henry's army, and English views of the French."],"justice":["Justice and mercy","Traitors, looters and prisoners: when Henry pardons and when he punishes."],"god":["God and the cause","Whether the war is just, who answers for it, and Henry's claim that God fights for England."],"theatre":["The Chorus and imagination","The 'wooden O', the Chorus's promises, and what the stage can and cannot show."],"youth":["The wild prince reformed","Hal's past, Falstaff's death, and how far Henry has changed."],"language":["Language and persuasion","Speeches that move armies, broken English and French, and talk as a weapon."],"common":["Common soldiers","Pistol, Nym, Bardolph, the Boy, Williams and Bates: the war from below."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"CHORUS","text":"O for a Muse of fire, that would ascend The brightest heaven of invention,","themes":["theatre"],"note":"The play's opening wish for a fiery inspiration equal to its subject."},{"id":"2","who":"CHORUS","text":"Can this cockpit hold The vasty fields of France?","themes":["theatre"],"note":"The Chorus admits the theatre is too small for the story."},{"id":"2","who":"CHORUS","text":"Within this wooden O the very casques That did affright the air at Agincourt?","themes":["theatre","war"],"note":"The 'wooden O' is the round playhouse."},{"id":"2","who":"CHORUS","text":"Piece out our imperfections with your thoughts:","themes":["theatre"],"note":"The audience must complete the play."},{"id":"2","who":"CHORUS","text":"Into a thousand parts divide one man,","themes":["theatre","war"],"note":"One actor must stand for a thousand soldiers."},{"id":"13","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"The courses of his youth promis'd it not.","themes":["youth"],"note":"Canterbury on Henry's wild youth."},{"id":"13","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"Consideration like an angel came And whipp'd the offending Adam out of him,","themes":["youth","god"],"note":"Henry's reform as a sudden religious conversion."},{"id":"15","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"Hear him but reason in divinity,","themes":["kingship","youth"],"note":"Canterbury praises Henry's learning."},{"id":"16","who":"ELY","text":"The strawberry grows underneath the nettle,","themes":["youth"],"note":"Ely's image: virtue hidden under wildness."},{"id":"17","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"for miracles are ceas'd,","themes":["youth","god"],"note":"There must be a natural explanation for Henry's change."},{"id":"37","who":"KING HENRY","text":"take heed how you impawn our person, How you awake our sleeping sword of war.","themes":["god","war","kingship"],"note":"Henry warns Canterbury of the weight of his advice."},{"id":"37","who":"KING HENRY","text":"For never two such kingdoms did contend Without much fall of blood,","themes":["war"],"note":"Henry knows the war will be bloody."},{"id":"37","who":"KING HENRY","text":"As pure as sin with baptism.","themes":["god"],"note":"Henry wants a conscience washed clean."},{"id":"38","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"No woman shall succeed in Salique land;","themes":["god","kingship"],"note":"The Salic law at the heart of the claim."},{"id":"38","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"So that, as clear as is the summer's sun,","themes":["language","god"],"note":"Canterbury claims his tangled argument is clear."},{"id":"39","who":"KING HENRY","text":"May I with right and conscience make this claim?","themes":["god","kingship"],"note":"Henry's direct question."},{"id":"40","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"The sin upon my head, dread sovereign!","themes":["god"],"note":"Canterbury takes the guilt on himself."},{"id":"40","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"Stand for your own! Unwind your bloody flag!","themes":["war","language"],"note":"Canterbury urges war."},{"id":"41","who":"ELY","text":"Is in the very May-morn of his youth,","themes":["youth","war"],"note":"Ely on Henry's youth as ready for action."},{"id":"49","who":"WESTMORELAND","text":"If that you will France win, Then with Scotland first begin.","themes":["nation"],"note":"Westmoreland's old saying about the Scots."},{"id":"50","who":"EXETER","text":"For government, though high and low and lower, Put into parts, doth keep in one consent,","themes":["kingship","nation"],"note":"Exeter's image of ordered government as music."},{"id":"51","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"for so work the honey-bees,","themes":["kingship","nation"],"note":"The beehive as a model kingdom."},{"id":"51","who":"CANTERBURY","text":"The lazy yawning drone.","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"The idle are punished in the hive."},{"id":"54","who":"KING HENRY","text":"France being ours, we'll bend it to our awe, Or break it all to pieces.","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Henry's decision."},{"id":"54","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Either our history shall with full mouth Speak freely of our acts,","themes":["kingship","theatre"],"note":"Henry thinks of how history will remember him."},{"id":"58","who":"KING HENRY","text":"We are no tyrant, but a Christian king,","themes":["kingship","god"],"note":"Henry's self-description."},{"id":"59","who":"AMBASSADOR","text":"You cannot revel into dukedoms there.","themes":["youth","language"],"note":"The Dauphin's mocking message."},{"id":"61","who":"EXETER","text":"Tennis-balls, my liege.","themes":["youth"],"note":"Exeter names the gift."},{"id":"62","who":"KING HENRY","text":"When we have match'd our rackets to these balls,","themes":["language","war"],"note":"Henry turns the tennis joke round."},{"id":"62","who":"KING HENRY","text":"How he comes o'er us with our wilder days, Not measuring what use we made of them.","themes":["youth"],"note":"Henry hints that his youth had a purpose."},{"id":"62","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Hath turn'd his balls to gun-stones,","themes":["war","language"],"note":"Tennis balls become cannonballs."},{"id":"62","who":"KING HENRY","text":"for many a thousand widows Shall this his mock mock out of their dear husbands,","themes":["war"],"note":"Henry blames the Dauphin for future deaths."},{"id":"62","who":"KING HENRY","text":"His jest will savour but of shallow wit, When thousands weep more than did laugh at it.","themes":["war","language"],"note":"Henry's closing couplet."},{"id":"65","who":"KING HENRY","text":"For we have now no thought in us but France,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's single-minded purpose."},{"id":"69","who":"CHORUS","text":"Now all the youth of England are on fire,","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"The Chorus on England arming."},{"id":"69","who":"CHORUS","text":"Following the mirror of all Christian kings,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The Chorus's praise of Henry."},{"id":"69","who":"CHORUS","text":"O England! model to thy inward greatness, Like little body with a mighty heart,","themes":["nation"],"note":"England as a small body with a big heart."},{"id":"69","who":"CHORUS","text":"Were all thy children kind and natural!","themes":["nation","justice"],"note":"England's flaw is traitors."},{"id":"69","who":"CHORUS","text":"for the gilt of France,--O guilt indeed!--","themes":["justice","language"],"note":"A pun on gilt and guilt."},{"id":"75","who":"NYM","text":"I dare not fight, but I will wink and hold out mine iron.","themes":["common"],"note":"Nym's comic cowardice."},{"id":"96","who":"PISTOL","text":"Couple a gorge!","themes":["common","language"],"note":"Pistol's mangled French."},{"id":"100","who":"HOSTESS","text":"The King has kill'd his heart.","themes":["youth","common"],"note":"The Hostess on Falstaff."},{"id":"119","who":"NYM","text":"The King hath run bad humours on the knight;","themes":["youth","common"],"note":"Nym blames the King."},{"id":"121","who":"NYM","text":"The King is a good king; but it must be as it may;","themes":["kingship","common"],"note":"Nym's double judgement."},{"id":"122","who":"PISTOL","text":"for, lambkins, we will live.","themes":["common"],"note":"Pistol's survival instinct."},{"id":"130","who":"EXETER","text":"Nay, but the man that was his bed-fellow,","themes":["justice"],"note":"Exeter on Scroop."},{"id":"141","who":"KING HENRY","text":"O, let us yet be merciful.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Henry's mercy to the drunk."},{"id":"144","who":"KING HENRY","text":"how shall we stretch our eye When capital crimes, chew'd, swallow'd, and digested, Appear before us?","themes":["justice"],"note":"Henry's argument for mercy to small faults."},{"id":"151","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The mercy that was quick in us but late, By your own counsel is suppress'd and kill'd.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The traitors have killed their own mercy."},{"id":"151","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Thou that didst bear the key of all my counsels,","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Henry to Scroop."},{"id":"151","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Another fall of man.","themes":["justice","god"],"note":"Scroop's treason compared to Adam's fall."},{"id":"156","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Touching our person seek we no revenge;","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Henry separates self and state."},{"id":"158","who":"KING HENRY","text":"No king of England, if not king of France!","themes":["kingship","nation"],"note":"Henry's rallying cry."},{"id":"166","who":"HOSTESS","text":"'A made a finer end and went away an it had been any christom child.","themes":["youth","common"],"note":"The Hostess on Falstaff's death."},{"id":"166","who":"HOSTESS","text":"and 'a babbled of green fields.","themes":["youth","common"],"note":"Falstaff's dying words."},{"id":"166","who":"HOSTESS","text":"and all was as cold as any stone.","themes":["common"],"note":"The end of Falstaff."},{"id":"176","who":"BARDOLPH","text":"That's all the riches I got in his service.","themes":["common","youth"],"note":"Bardolph on Falstaff."},{"id":"178","who":"PISTOL","text":"For oaths are straws, men's faiths are wafer-cakes","themes":["common","language"],"note":"Pistol's cynical advice."},{"id":"178","who":"PISTOL","text":"To suck, to suck, the very blood to suck!","themes":["common","war"],"note":"Pistol goes to war for profit."},{"id":"190","who":"DAUPHIN","text":"By a vain, giddy, shallow, humorous youth,","themes":["youth","nation"],"note":"The Dauphin's view of Henry."},{"id":"191","who":"CONSTABLE","text":"You are too much mistaken in this king.","themes":["youth","kingship"],"note":"The Constable warns the Dauphin."},{"id":"191","who":"CONSTABLE","text":"Were but the outside of the Roman Brutus, Covering discretion with a coat of folly;","themes":["youth"],"note":"Henry's youth as a disguise."},{"id":"193","who":"FRENCH KING","text":"Think we King Harry strong;","themes":["kingship","war"],"note":"The French King's caution."},{"id":"204","who":"EXETER","text":"Turning the widows' tears, the orphans' cries,","themes":["war","god"],"note":"Exeter blames France for the bloodshed."},{"id":"209","who":"EXETER","text":"Now he weighs time Even to the utmost grain.","themes":["youth","kingship"],"note":"Exeter on the new Henry."},{"id":"217","who":"CHORUS","text":"Thus with imagin'd wing our swift scene flies,","themes":["theatre"],"note":"The Chorus moves us to France."},{"id":"217","who":"CHORUS","text":"For who is he, whose chin is but enrich'd With one appearing hair, that will not follow","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"Every young man follows Henry."},{"id":"222","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Once more unto the breach, dear friends, once more,","themes":["war","language"],"note":"Henry at Harfleur."},{"id":"222","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Or close the wall up with our English dead.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"Fill the gap with bodies."},{"id":"222","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Then imitate the action of the tiger;","themes":["war"],"note":"War turns men into beasts."},{"id":"222","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Disguise fair nature with hard-favour'd rage;","themes":["war","theatre"],"note":"Warriors must act a part."},{"id":"222","who":"KING HENRY","text":"And you, good yeomen, Whose limbs were made in England, show us here The mettle of your pasture;","themes":["nation","common"],"note":"Henry speaks to the common soldiers."},{"id":"222","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I see you stand like greyhounds in the slips,","themes":["war","language"],"note":"The eager soldiers."},{"id":"222","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Cry, \"God for Harry! England and Saint George!\"","themes":["nation","god"],"note":"The battle cry."},{"id":"227","who":"NYM","text":"The knocks are too hot; and, for mine own part, I have not a case of lives.","themes":["common","war"],"note":"Nym after the speech."},{"id":"229","who":"BOY","text":"Would I were in an alehouse in London! I would give all my fame for a pot of ale and safety.","themes":["common","war"],"note":"The Boy's wish."},{"id":"238","who":"BOY","text":"for indeed three such antics do not amount to a man.","themes":["common"],"note":"The Boy on his masters."},{"id":"238","who":"BOY","text":"They will steal anything, and call it purchase.","themes":["common","justice"],"note":"The Boy on the thieves."},{"id":"249","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"in the disciplines of the pristine wars of the Romans.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"Fluellen's obsession."},{"id":"256","who":"MACMORRIS","text":"and there is throats to be cut, and works to be done;","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"Macmorris's impatience."},{"id":"259","who":"MACMORRIS","text":"Of my nation! What ish my nation?","themes":["nation"],"note":"Macmorris's outburst."},{"id":"270","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The gates of mercy shall be all shut up,","themes":["war","justice"],"note":"Henry threatens Harfleur."},{"id":"270","who":"KING HENRY","text":"mowing like grass Your fresh fair virgins and your flow'ring infants.","themes":["war"],"note":"Henry's terrible threat."},{"id":"270","who":"KING HENRY","text":"What is't to me, when you yourselves are cause,","themes":["war","god"],"note":"Henry blames the town."},{"id":"270","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Your naked infants spitted upon pikes,","themes":["war"],"note":"The most violent image of the play."},{"id":"272","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Use mercy to them all.","themes":["justice","war"],"note":"After the threats, mercy."},{"id":"272","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The winter coming on, and sickness growing Upon our soldiers,","themes":["war"],"note":"The army is weakening."},{"id":"308","who":"KATHARINE","text":"le foot et le coun!","themes":["language"],"note":"Katharine's shock."},{"id":"318","who":"CONSTABLE","text":"Dieu de batailles! where have they this mettle?","themes":["nation"],"note":"The Constable is puzzled by English courage."},{"id":"318","who":"CONSTABLE","text":"Is not their climate foggy, raw, and dull,","themes":["nation"],"note":"French scorn for England."},{"id":"319","who":"DAUPHIN","text":"Our madams mock at us,","themes":["nation"],"note":"French shame."},{"id":"321","who":"FRENCH KING","text":"Bar Harry England, that sweeps through our land With pennons painted in the blood of Harfleur.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"The French King's call to arms."},{"id":"332","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"The Duke of Exeter is as magnanimous as Agamemnon;","themes":["language","nation"],"note":"Fluellen's grand comparisons."},{"id":"341","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"Fortune is an excellent moral.","themes":["language"],"note":"Fluellen on Fortune's emblem."},{"id":"342","who":"PISTOL","text":"For he hath stolen a pax, and hanged must 'a be,--","themes":["justice","common"],"note":"Bardolph's crime."},{"id":"345","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"for discipline ought to be used.","themes":["justice","war"],"note":"Fluellen refuses to help Bardolph."},{"id":"353","who":"GOWER","text":"Why, 't is a gull, a fool, a rogue, that now and then goes to the wars,","themes":["common","war"],"note":"Gower on Pistol."},{"id":"360","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"but his nose is executed, and his fire's out.","themes":["justice","youth"],"note":"Fluellen on Bardolph's hanging."},{"id":"361","who":"KING HENRY","text":"We would have all such offenders so cut off;","themes":["justice","youth"],"note":"Henry approves Bardolph's death."},{"id":"361","who":"KING HENRY","text":"for when lenity and cruelty play for a kingdom, the gentler gamester is the soonest winner.","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Henry's policy."},{"id":"370","who":"KING HENRY","text":"My people are with sickness much enfeebled,","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Henry admits weakness to the enemy."},{"id":"370","who":"KING HENRY","text":"We would not seek a battle, as we are; Nor, as we are, we say we will not shun it.","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Henry's balanced reply."},{"id":"374","who":"KING HENRY","text":"We are in God's hands, brother, not in theirs.","themes":["god"],"note":"Henry trusts God."},{"id":"384","who":"DAUPHIN","text":"When I bestride him, I soar, I am a hawk.","themes":["nation","language"],"note":"The Dauphin on his horse."},{"id":"392","who":"DAUPHIN","text":"for my horse is my mistress.","themes":["nation"],"note":"The Dauphin's absurd love."},{"id":"412","who":"DAUPHIN","text":"and my way shall be paved with English faces.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"The Dauphin's boast."},{"id":"445","who":"ORLEANS","text":"to mope with his fat-brain'd followers so far out of his knowledge!","themes":["nation"],"note":"Orleans on the English."},{"id":"450","who":"CONSTABLE","text":"give them great meals of beef and iron and steel, they will eat like wolves and fight like devils.","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"The Constable on the English."},{"id":"453","who":"ORLEANS","text":"We shall have each a hundred Englishmen.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"Orleans's boast."},{"id":"457","who":"CHORUS","text":"The confident and over-lusty French Do the low-rated English play at dice;","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"The Chorus on the French."},{"id":"457","who":"CHORUS","text":"The poor condemned English, Like sacrifices, by their watchful fires","themes":["war","common"],"note":"The Chorus on the English."},{"id":"457","who":"CHORUS","text":"And calls them brothers, friends, and countrymen.","themes":["kingship","common"],"note":"Henry visits his men."},{"id":"457","who":"CHORUS","text":"A little touch of Harry in the night.","themes":["kingship","theatre"],"note":"The Chorus's famous phrase."},{"id":"457","who":"CHORUS","text":"With four or five most vile and ragged foils,","themes":["theatre","war"],"note":"The Chorus apologises for the stage battle."},{"id":"460","who":"KING HENRY","text":"There is some soul of goodness in things evil, Would men observingly distil it out;","themes":["god"],"note":"Henry finds good in danger."},{"id":"467","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I and my bosom must debate a while,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry wants to be alone."},{"id":"480","who":"PISTOL","text":"The King's a bawcock, and a heart of gold,","themes":["common","kingship"],"note":"Pistol praises the King to his face."},{"id":"503","who":"KING HENRY","text":"There is much care and valour in this Welshman.","themes":["nation","kingship"],"note":"Henry on Fluellen."},{"id":"507","who":"WILLIAMS","text":"We see yonder the beginning of the day, but I think we shall never see the end of it.","themes":["common","war"],"note":"Williams's fear."},{"id":"514","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I think the King is but a man as I am.","themes":["kingship","common"],"note":"Henry in disguise."},{"id":"514","who":"KING HENRY","text":"His ceremonies laid by, in his nakedness he appears but a man;","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Kingship as costume."},{"id":"518","who":"KING HENRY","text":"his cause being just and his quarrel honourable.","themes":["god","kingship"],"note":"Henry defends the cause."},{"id":"519","who":"WILLIAMS","text":"That's more than we know.","themes":["god","common"],"note":"Williams's reply."},{"id":"520","who":"BATES","text":"If his cause be wrong, our obedience to the King wipes the crime of it out of us.","themes":["god","common"],"note":"Bates on obedience."},{"id":"521","who":"WILLIAMS","text":"But if the cause be not good, the King himself hath a heavy reckoning to make,","themes":["god","kingship","war"],"note":"Williams's challenge."},{"id":"521","who":"WILLIAMS","text":"some crying for a surgeon, some upon their wives left poor behind them,","themes":["war","common"],"note":"The dead cry out."},{"id":"522","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Every subject's duty is the King's; but every subject's soul is his own.","themes":["god","kingship"],"note":"Henry's answer."},{"id":"528","who":"WILLIAMS","text":"That's a perilous shot out of an elder-gun,","themes":["common","kingship"],"note":"Williams mocks a private man's power."},{"id":"543","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Upon the King! Let us our lives, our souls, Our debts, our careful wives, Our children, and our sins lay on the King!","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's soliloquy begins."},{"id":"543","who":"KING HENRY","text":"And what have kings, that privates have not too, Save ceremony, save general ceremony?","themes":["kingship"],"note":"What separates king from subject."},{"id":"543","who":"KING HENRY","text":"O Ceremony, show me but thy worth!","themes":["kingship","theatre"],"note":"Henry questions ceremony."},{"id":"543","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Can sleep so soundly as the wretched slave,","themes":["kingship","common"],"note":"The peasant sleeps better."},{"id":"543","who":"KING HENRY","text":"What watch the King keeps to maintain the peace,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The king's sleepless duty."},{"id":"549","who":"KING HENRY","text":"O God of battles! steel my soldiers' hearts.","themes":["god","war"],"note":"Henry's prayer."},{"id":"549","who":"KING HENRY","text":"think not upon the fault My father made in compassing the crown!","themes":["god","kingship","youth"],"note":"Henry remembers Richard II's deposition."},{"id":"549","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Since that my penitence comes after all, Imploring pardon.","themes":["god"],"note":"Henry doubts his penance is enough."},{"id":"569","who":"CONSTABLE","text":"Leaving them but the shales and husks of men.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"The Constable's contempt."},{"id":"571","who":"GRANDPRE","text":"Big Mars seems bankrupt in their beggar'd host,","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"Grandpre on the English."},{"id":"581","who":"EXETER","text":"There's five to one; besides, they all are fresh.","themes":["war"],"note":"Exeter on the odds."},{"id":"588","who":"WESTMORELAND","text":"O that we now had here But one ten thousand of those men in England That do no work to-day!","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"Westmoreland's wish."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The fewer men, the greater share of honour.","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Henry's answer."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"But if it be a sin to covet honour, I am the most offending soul alive.","themes":["kingship","youth"],"note":"Henry's love of honour."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"That he which hath no stomach to this fight, Let him depart.","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Henry's offer."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"This day is call'd the feast of Crispian.","themes":["war","god"],"note":"The famous date."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Then will he strip his sleeve and show his scars,","themes":["war","common"],"note":"Veterans remember."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Familiar in his mouth as household words,","themes":["theatre","nation"],"note":"The names will be remembered."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"We few, we happy few, we band of brothers.","themes":["war","kingship","common"],"note":"The speech's heart."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"be he ne'er so vile, This day shall gentle his condition;","themes":["common","kingship"],"note":"Fighting will make men gentlemen."},{"id":"589","who":"KING HENRY","text":"And gentlemen in England now a-bed Shall think themselves accurs'd they were not here,","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"Those absent will envy them."},{"id":"592","who":"KING HENRY","text":"All things are ready, if our minds be so.","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Henry's calm."},{"id":"601","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Bid them achieve me and then sell my bones.","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Henry's reply to Montjoy."},{"id":"601","who":"KING HENRY","text":"We are but warriors for the working-day.","themes":["war","common"],"note":"Henry on his tired army."},{"id":"601","who":"KING HENRY","text":"But, by the mass, our hearts are in the trim;","themes":["war"],"note":"Spirit matters more than looks."},{"id":"607","who":"KING HENRY","text":"And how thou pleasest, God, dispose the day!","themes":["god"],"note":"Henry leaves the outcome to God."},{"id":"625","who":"PISTOL","text":"Master Fer! I'll fer him, and firk him, and ferret him.","themes":["common","language"],"note":"Pistol's word-play."},{"id":"644","who":"BOY","text":"\"The empty vessel makes the greatest sound.\"","themes":["common","language"],"note":"The Boy on Pistol."},{"id":"644","who":"BOY","text":"for there is none to guard it but boys.","themes":["war","common"],"note":"The Boy at the luggage."},{"id":"650","who":"DAUPHIN","text":"Mort de ma vie! all is confounded, all!","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"The Dauphin in defeat."},{"id":"656","who":"BOURBON","text":"Let's die in honour! Once more back again!","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"Bourbon's last charge."},{"id":"659","who":"BOURBON","text":"Let life be short, else shame will be too long.","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"Bourbon."},{"id":"666","who":"EXETER","text":"He cries aloud, \"Tarry, my cousin Suffolk!","themes":["war"],"note":"York's dying words."},{"id":"666","who":"EXETER","text":"And all my mother came into mine eyes And gave me up to tears.","themes":["war"],"note":"Exeter weeps."},{"id":"669","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Then every soldier kill his prisoners;","themes":["war","justice"],"note":"Henry's order."},{"id":"673","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"Kill the poys and the luggage! 'Tis expressly against the law of arms.","themes":["war","justice"],"note":"Fluellen on the French attack."},{"id":"674","who":"GOWER","text":"O, 'tis a gallant king!","themes":["kingship","war"],"note":"Gower praises the order."},{"id":"681","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"turn'd away the fat knight with the great belly doublet.","themes":["youth"],"note":"Fluellen remembers Falstaff."},{"id":"686","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I was not angry since I came to France Until this instant.","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Henry's anger."},{"id":"693","who":"MONTJOY","text":"The day is yours.","themes":["war"],"note":"Montjoy concedes."},{"id":"694","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Praised be God, and not our strength, for it!","themes":["god"],"note":"Henry gives God the credit."},{"id":"700","who":"KING HENRY","text":"For I am Welsh, you know, good countryman.","themes":["nation"],"note":"Henry claims Welsh blood."},{"id":"703","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"I need not be asham'd of your Majesty, praised be God, so long as your Majesty is an honest man.","themes":["nation","kingship"],"note":"Fluellen's loyalty."},{"id":"757","who":"KING HENRY","text":"'Twas I, indeed, thou promisedst to strike;","themes":["kingship","common"],"note":"Henry reveals himself."},{"id":"760","who":"WILLIAMS","text":"All offences, my lord, come from the heart.","themes":["common","justice"],"note":"Williams's defence."},{"id":"762","who":"WILLIAMS","text":"Your Majesty came not like yourself.","themes":["common","kingship"],"note":"Williams blames the disguise."},{"id":"772","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Here was a royal fellowship of death!","themes":["war"],"note":"Henry on the French dead."},{"id":"774","who":"KING HENRY","text":"O God, thy arm was here;","themes":["god","war"],"note":"Henry on the low English losses."},{"id":"774","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Take it, God, For it is none but thine!","themes":["god"],"note":"Henry gives glory to God."},{"id":"776","who":"KING HENRY","text":"And be it death proclaimed through our host To boast of this","themes":["god","justice"],"note":"Boasting forbidden."},{"id":"780","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Let there be sung Non nobis and Te Deum,","themes":["god"],"note":"Thanksgiving."},{"id":"784","who":"CHORUS","text":"Being free from vainness and self-glorious pride;","themes":["kingship","god"],"note":"The Chorus on Henry's humility."},{"id":"784","who":"CHORUS","text":"Go forth and fetch their conquering Caesar in;","themes":["kingship","theatre"],"note":"London welcomes Henry."},{"id":"797","who":"FLUELLEN","text":"if you can mock a leek, you can eat a leek.","themes":["nation","language"],"note":"Fluellen to Pistol."},{"id":"814","who":"GOWER","text":"and henceforth let a Welsh correction teach you a good English condition.","themes":["nation"],"note":"Gower's last word."},{"id":"816","who":"PISTOL","text":"Old I do wax; and from my weary limbs Honour is cudgell'd.","themes":["common","war"],"note":"Pistol's farewell."},{"id":"816","who":"PISTOL","text":"And swear I got them in the Gallia wars.","themes":["common","war"],"note":"Pistol will fake war wounds."},{"id":"825","who":"BURGUNDY","text":"Dear nurse of arts, plenties, and joyful births,","themes":["war"],"note":"Burgundy on Peace."},{"id":"825","who":"BURGUNDY","text":"Should not in this best garden of the world, Our fertile France, put up her lovely visage?","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"France as a ruined garden."},{"id":"832","who":"KING HENRY","text":"She is our capital demand,","themes":["kingship","war"],"note":"Henry on Katharine."},{"id":"846","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I know no ways to mince it in love, but directly to say, \"I love you\";","themes":["language"],"note":"Henry's plain wooing."},{"id":"848","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I speak to thee plain soldier.","themes":["language","kingship"],"note":"Henry's pose of plainness."},{"id":"848","who":"KING HENRY","text":"but a good heart, Kate, is the sun and the moon;","themes":["language"],"note":"Henry on true love."},{"id":"849","who":"KATHARINE","text":"Is it possible dat I should love de enemy of France?","themes":["nation","language"],"note":"Katharine's question."},{"id":"850","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I love France so well that I will not part with a village of it, I will have it all mine;","themes":["war","language"],"note":"Henry's answer."},{"id":"856","who":"KING HENRY","text":"compound a boy, half French, half English, that shall go to Constantinople and take the Turk by the beard?","themes":["nation","god"],"note":"Henry imagines a son."},{"id":"860","who":"KING HENRY","text":"he was thinking of civil wars when he got me;","themes":["youth","kingship"],"note":"Henry jokes about his father."},{"id":"861","who":"KATHARINE","text":"Dat is as it shall please de roi mon pere.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Katharine defers to her father."},{"id":"874","who":"KING HENRY","text":"We are the makers of manners, Kate;","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry above custom."},{"id":"874","who":"KING HENRY","text":"You have witchcraft in your lips, Kate;","themes":["language"],"note":"After the kiss."},{"id":"887","who":"KING HENRY","text":"who cannot see many a fair French city for one fair French maid that stands in my way.","themes":["war","language"],"note":"Henry's joke."},{"id":"898","who":"FRENCH KING","text":"Plant neighbourhood and Christian-like accord","themes":["god","nation"],"note":"The French King's hope."},{"id":"907","who":"CHORUS","text":"In little room confining mighty men,","themes":["theatre"],"note":"The Epilogue's apology."},{"id":"907","who":"CHORUS","text":"Small time, but in that small most greatly lived This star of England.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's short life."},{"id":"907","who":"CHORUS","text":"That they lost France and made his England bleed:","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"What happened next."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Henry V, once the wild Prince Hal of the Henry IV plays, is newly King of England, and the bishops marvel at his sudden reformation. Encouraged by the Archbishop of Canterbury, who wants to save the Church's lands from a hostile bill, Henry claims the crown of France through his great-great-grandmother. When the French Dauphin answers with a mocking gift of tennis balls, Henry promises to turn them into cannonballs. Before he sails he unmasks three traitors bribed by France, including his close friend Lord Scroop, and sends them to their deaths. In London his old companions mourn Falstaff, whose heart the King has broken, and set off to the war to steal what they can.","Henry besieges Harfleur ('Once more unto the breach') and threatens the town with horrors until it surrenders. His army, shrunk by sickness, marches towards Calais; his old friend Bardolph is hanged for robbing a church. A huge French army blocks the way at Agincourt. On the night before the battle Henry walks the camp in disguise, argues with the soldier Williams about whether the King is responsible for his men's souls, and reflects alone on the burden of kingship. In the morning, outnumbered five to one, he rouses his men with the Saint Crispin's day speech.","Against all odds the English win. Henry orders the French prisoners killed when the French regroup and attack the baggage, then gives the glory to God when he hears that ten thousand French have died against only twenty-nine English. In the last act Fluellen makes Pistol eat a leek, Henry woos the French princess Katharine in a mixture of English and French, and the peace treaty makes him heir of France. The Chorus, who has guided the audience's imagination throughout, ends by reminding them that Henry died young and his son lost everything he had won."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Life of King Henry the Fifth (First Folio, 1623); the 1600 quarto was called The Chronicle History of Henry the Fifth"],["Written","1599, probably in the spring or summer (the Chorus to Act 5 refers to the Earl of Essex's campaign in Ireland); a shorter quarto without the Chorus appeared in 1600, the full text in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","History (placed in the First Folio's Histories, after the two parts of Henry IV); the last play of the second tetralogy, with comic scenes and a romantic ending"],["Setting","England and France, 1414 to 1420: London, Southampton, the French court, Harfleur, the march through Picardy, the field of Agincourt and the palace at Troyes"],["Protagonist","King Henry V"],["Antagonists","The French, especially the Dauphin; within England, the Southampton traitors; morally, the doubts voiced by Williams"],["Inciting incident","The Dauphin's gift of tennis balls, which turns Henry's legal claim into a war (1.2)"],["Turning point","The night before Agincourt, when Henry walks among his soldiers and prays (4.1)"],["Climax","The battle of Agincourt and the Crispin's day speech (4.3 to 4.8)"],["Resolution","The Treaty of Troyes: Henry becomes heir of France and is betrothed to Katharine (5.2)"],["Time span","Historically from 1414 to 1420; the Chorus jumps over the gaps, including Henry's return to England between Agincourt and Troyes"],["Major conflicts","England against France; the King against traitors; the heroic view of war against its cost; Henry the public king against Harry the private man"],["Themes","Kingship, war and its cost, England and Britain, justice and mercy, God and the cause, the Chorus and imagination, the wild prince reformed, language and persuasion, common soldiers"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The Chorus asks the audience to imagine Agincourt in the theatre","id":2},{"s":"a1s1","t":"The bishops plan to fund the French war","id":19},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Canterbury explains the Salic law","id":38},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Henry resolves to conquer France","id":54},{"s":"a1s2","t":"The Dauphin's tennis balls","id":62},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The Chorus reveals the traitors' plot","id":69},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Nym and Pistol quarrel; Falstaff falls sick","id":100},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Henry unmasks Cambridge, Scroop and Grey","id":148},{"s":"a2s2","t":"The traitors are sentenced to death","id":156},{"s":"a2s3","t":"The Hostess describes Falstaff's death","id":166},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Exeter demands the French crown","id":202},{"s":"a3s1","t":"'Once more unto the breach'","id":222},{"s":"a3s2","t":"The four captains quarrel at Harfleur","id":259},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Henry threatens Harfleur, which surrenders","id":270},{"s":"a3s4","t":"Katharine's English lesson","id":308},{"s":"a3s5","t":"The French King sends his nobles against Henry","id":321},{"s":"a3s6","t":"Bardolph is hanged for stealing a pax","id":360},{"s":"a3s6","t":"Henry tells Montjoy he will march on","id":370},{"s":"a3s7","t":"The French lords boast on the eve of battle","id":453},{"s":"a4s1","t":"'A little touch of Harry in the night'","id":457},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Henry argues with Williams and exchanges gloves","id":534},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Henry's soliloquy on ceremony and his prayer","id":543},{"s":"a4s3","t":"The Crispin's day speech","id":589},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Pistol takes a prisoner","id":631},{"s":"a4s6","t":"Henry orders the prisoners killed","id":669},{"s":"a4s7","t":"Montjoy concedes: 'The day is yours'","id":693},{"s":"a4s8","t":"The glove trick revealed; the dead counted","id":774},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Fluellen makes Pistol eat the leek","id":802},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Henry woos Katharine","id":848},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The Epilogue: Henry VI lost France","id":907}],"irony":{"13":"The bishops praise Henry's holiness while plotting to protect their own wealth.","40":"Canterbury takes 'the sin upon my head', but it is the soldiers who will pay.","59":"The Dauphin mocks Henry's youth; Hal's youth was a disguise, as the Constable later warns.","134":"Henry tells the traitors he trusts every heart in his army, knowing they plan to kill him.","143":"The traitors urge punishment for a small offence and so condemn themselves.","190":"The Dauphin dismisses Henry as a 'giddy, shallow' youth just before Henry crushes France.","412":"The Dauphin plans to pave his way with English faces; he will be the one talking of suicide.","453":"Orleans expects a hundred English prisoners each; it is the French who will be taken.","480":"Pistol praises the King to the King's face without knowing it.","514":"'I think the King is but a man as I am': the audience knows the speaker is the King.","528":"Williams mocks the idea that a private man's displeasure can harm a king, unaware he is speaking to one.","533":"Henry says he will wear Williams's glove; he gives it to Fluellen to wear instead.","598":"Montjoy pities the English for their certain defeat.","681":"At the moment of victory Fluellen reminds everyone that Henry rejected Falstaff.","856":"Henry imagines his son taking Constantinople; the Epilogue tells us that son lost France.","907":"The play ends in triumph and marriage, and the Epilogue says it was all soon lost."},"devices":{"theatre":"The stage and acting","beasts":"Beasts and hunting","garden":"Gardens, plants and weeds","sport":"Sport and games","body":"The body and blood","sea":"The sea and flood"},"dev":{"2":[["theatre","The theatre as a cockpit and a 'wooden O' that cannot hold the battle.","Within this wooden O the very casques"]],"16":[["garden","Virtue growing hidden under wildness, like a strawberry under a nettle.","The strawberry grows underneath the nettle,"]],"47":[["sea","The Scots pour in like the tide through a breach in a sea wall.","Came pouring, like the tide into a breach,"]],"49":[["beasts","England as an eagle, Scotland as a weasel stealing eggs.","To her unguarded nest the weasel Scot"]],"51":[["beasts","The kingdom as a beehive with its king and its drones.","for so work the honey-bees,"]],"62":[["sport","Tennis terms become threats of war.","When we have match'd our rackets to these balls,"]],"69":[["body","England as a little body with a mighty heart.","Like little body with a mighty heart,"]],"151":[["beasts","The traitors' own arguments turn on them like dogs.","As dogs upon their masters, worrying you."]],"178":[["beasts","Soldiers as horse-leeches sucking blood.","like horse-leeches, my boys,"]],"204":[["body","War as a monster opening its jaws to swallow men.","Opens his vasty jaws;"]],"217":[["sea","The fleet as a city dancing on the waves.","A city on the inconstant billows dancing;"]],"222":[["beasts","Soldiers as tigers and greyhounds.","Then imitate the action of the tiger;"],["sea","The brow over the eye like a rock over the wild sea.","Swill'd with the wild and wasteful ocean."],["theatre","Rage as a disguise put on like a costume.","Disguise fair nature with hard-favour'd rage;"]],"270":[["garden","Soldiers mowing down virgins and infants like grass.","mowing like grass"]],"316":[["garden","The English as wild offshoots grafted from French stock.","Our scions put in wild and savage stock,"]],"321":[["sea","The French should fall on Henry like melted snow pouring down the Alps.","Rush on his host, as doth the melted snow"]],"449":[["beasts","English mastiffs running into the bear's mouth.","Foolish curs, that run winking into the mouth of a Russian bear"]],"457":[["theatre","The stage battle as 'four or five most vile and ragged foils'.","With four or five most vile and ragged foils,"]],"460":[["garden","Gathering honey from weeds: good from evil.","Thus may we gather honey from the weed,"]],"543":[["theatre","Ceremony as an idol and a proud dream.","And what art thou, thou idol Ceremony?"]],"571":[["beasts","The English as carrion, with crows waiting.","And their executors, the knavish crows,"]],"601":[["body","Dead English bodies breed a plague in France.","The smell whereof shall breed a plague in France."]],"666":[["body","York and Suffolk 'espoused to death' in blood.","And so espous'd to death, with blood he seal'd"]],"784":[["sea","The sea as a whiffler clearing the King's way.","Which like a mighty whiffler 'fore the King"],["theatre","The Chorus as the thing that fills the gap in time.","The interim, by rememb'ring you 'tis past."]],"825":[["garden","France as the best garden of the world, overgrown with weeds.","Should not in this best garden of the world,"]],"848":[["body","Ageing bodies against a good heart that never changes.","a good heart, Kate, is the sun and the moon;"]],"907":[["garden","France as 'the world's best garden'.","By which the world's best garden he achieved,"]]},"stage":{"palace":"A royal palace: the English court in London or the French court","room":"A room in the French King's palace at Rouen","street":"A street in London","hall":"A council chamber at Southampton","tavern":"Outside the Boar's Head tavern in Eastcheap","walls":"The walls and gates of Harfleur","camp":"An army camp in France","tent":"King Henry's pavilion","battle":"The battlefield at Agincourt"},"stagerules":[["a room","room"],["palace","palace"],["street","street"],["council","hall"],["tavern","tavern"],["harfleur","walls"],["pavilion","tent"],["field","battle"],["camp","camp"]],"place":{"a1s1":"The palace, London","a1s2":"The presence chamber, London","a2s1":"A street, London","a2s2":"Southampton","a2s3":"The Boar's Head, Eastcheap","a2s4":"The French court","a3s1":"Before Harfleur","a3s2":"Before Harfleur","a3s3":"The gates of Harfleur","a3s4":"The French palace, Rouen","a3s5":"The French palace, Rouen","a3s6":"The English camp in Picardy","a3s7":"The French camp near Agincourt","a4s1":"The English camp at Agincourt","a4s2":"The French camp","a4s3":"The English camp","a4s4":"The field of Agincourt","a4s5":"The field of Agincourt","a4s6":"The field of Agincourt","a4s7":"The field of Agincourt","a4s8":"Before Henry's pavilion","a5s1":"The English camp in France","a5s2":"The French palace at Troyes"},"arc":{"English fortunes":[5,7,6,7,5,6,7,6,8,7,6,5,4,4,3,6,6,9,8,9,10,9,10],"French fortunes":[7,6,6,5,6,7,5,5,4,5,6,7,9,8,9,8,7,2,3,1,1,2,4]},"arcnote":"English fortunes rise as Henry resolves on war and survives the Southampton plot, dip with Falstaff's death, and climb again with the fall of Harfleur. Then sickness, winter and the march through Picardy wear the army down; by the eve of Agincourt the English are few, hungry and expecting to die. French fortunes run the other way: alarmed by Harfleur, the French recover their confidence as their great army gathers, and they are at their most scornful on the night before the battle. The two lines cross at Agincourt, where the French collapse in 4.5 and the English win against all odds. In Act 5 England dictates the peace, but France recovers a little through the marriage, which promises union rather than conquest.","iago":[["a1s2","Henry asks Canterbury whether he can claim France with a clear conscience."],["a1s2","Henry resolves to bend France to his will or die trying."],["a1s2","The Dauphin's tennis balls: Henry vows to turn them into cannonballs."],["a2s2","At Southampton Henry unmasks and condemns the traitors bribed by France."],["a2s2","Henry sails: 'No king of England, if not king of France!'"],["a2s4","Exeter demands the French crown in Henry's name."],["a3s1","Henry leads the assault on Harfleur: 'Once more unto the breach'."],["a3s3","Henry threatens Harfleur into surrender and orders mercy."],["a3s6","Henry approves Bardolph's execution and forbids looting."],["a3s6","Henry tells Montjoy he will march on to Calais whatever the odds."],["a4s1","Henry walks the camp in disguise and argues with Williams."],["a4s1","Henry reflects on ceremony and prays before the battle."],["a4s3","Henry's Crispin's day speech rouses the outnumbered army."],["a4s3","Henry refuses Montjoy's last demand for ransom."],["a4s6","Henry orders the prisoners killed as the French regroup."],["a4s7","Montjoy concedes the day; Henry names the field of Agincourt."],["a4s8","Henry gives the victory to God: 'Take it, God, For it is none but thine!'"],["a5s2","The Treaty of Troyes makes Henry heir of France and Katharine his queen."]],"rel":{"1":[["KING HENRY","CANTERBURY","trust"],["CANTERBURY","KING HENRY","deceive"],["EXETER","KING HENRY","love"],["DAUPHIN","KING HENRY","hate"],["KING HENRY","DAUPHIN","hate"],["ELY","CANTERBURY","trust"]],"2":[["SCROOP","KING HENRY","deceive"],["CAMBRIDGE","KING HENRY","deceive"],["KING HENRY","SCROOP","hate"],["PISTOL","NYM","hate"],["PISTOL","HOSTESS","love"],["BARDOLPH","NYM","trust"],["CONSTABLE","DAUPHIN","hate"],["FRENCH KING","KING HENRY","hate"]],"3":[["FLUELLEN","MACMORRIS","hate"],["BOY","PISTOL","hate"],["FLUELLEN","PISTOL","deceive"],["PISTOL","FLUELLEN","hate"],["KING HENRY","BARDOLPH","hate"],["KATHARINE","ALICE","trust"],["CONSTABLE","DAUPHIN","deceive"],["FLUELLEN","KING HENRY","love"]],"4":[["KING HENRY","WILLIAMS","deceive"],["WILLIAMS","KING HENRY","hate"],["KING HENRY","FLUELLEN","trust"],["FLUELLEN","WILLIAMS","hate"],["EXETER","KING HENRY","love"],["PISTOL","FRENCH SOLDIER","deceive"],["MONTJOY","KING HENRY","trust"]],"5":[["FLUELLEN","PISTOL","hate"],["GOWER","PISTOL","hate"],["KING HENRY","KATHARINE","love"],["KATHARINE","KING HENRY","trust"],["BURGUNDY","KING HENRY","trust"],["FRENCH KING","KING HENRY","trust"],["QUEEN ISABEL","KATHARINE","love"]]},"context":[["Date","Written in 1599. The Chorus to Act 5 compares Henry's welcome in London to the welcome the 'general of our gracious empress' would get coming home from Ireland, which almost certainly means the Earl of Essex, who left for Ireland in March 1599 and came back in disgrace in September. The 'wooden O' may be the new Globe, opened that year."],["Sources","Shakespeare's main source was Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles (1587), with Edward Hall's chronicle and an old anonymous play, The Famous Victories of Henry the Fifth, which already contained the tennis balls, the wild prince and the wooing. The bishops' plot, the Salic law speech, Harfleur, the killing of prisoners and the lists of the dead all come from Holinshed."],["The real history","Henry landed in August 1415 and took Harfleur in September after a long siege that cost many lives through dysentery. He marched towards Calais and was intercepted at Agincourt, where on 25 October his small, tired army, mostly archers, defeated a much larger French force on a narrow, muddy field. He returned to France in 1417, conquered Normandy, and in 1420 the Treaty of Troyes made him heir of France and husband of Katherine. He died in 1422, aged 35, before he could inherit France."],["The Henriad","Henry V is the last of four plays (Richard II, Henry IV Parts 1 and 2, Henry V) that follow the house of Lancaster from the deposition of Richard II to Henry's triumph. The prayer before Agincourt recalls Richard's murder; the bishops, Falstaff's death and Fluellen's 'fat knight' recall Hal's youth. The Epilogue points forward to the Henry VI plays Shakespeare had already written, in which France is lost."],["Falstaff's absence","The Epilogue to 2 Henry IV promised to continue the story 'with Sir John in it'. Instead Shakespeare kills Falstaff offstage. His death is told by the Hostess, and his absence hangs over the play: Nym and Pistol blame the King, and Fluellen recalls his rejection at the height of the victory."],["Quarto and Folio","The 1600 quarto is much shorter than the Folio text. It has no Chorus, no 'Once more unto the breach' and no Macmorris or Jamy, and it cuts some of Henry's harshest moments. Scholars still debate whether it is a shortened acting version or a report of performance."],["The four nations","The captains at Harfleur are English (Gower), Welsh (Fluellen), Irish (Macmorris) and Scottish (Jamy). They suggest a united British army, but they also quarrel, and the Scots are feared as invaders in 1.2. In 1599 England was at war in Ireland and had no union with Scotland."],["Kingship and the disguised king","The idea of a king walking unknown among his subjects was popular in ballads and plays. Shakespeare uses it to let Henry hear what his soldiers really think, and the conversation does not go the way the comic tradition expects."],["The laws of war","Killing prisoners who had surrendered was against the customs of war, though Holinshed defends Henry's order as a response to a new threat. Killing the boys guarding the baggage was a worse crime, which Fluellen calls 'expressly against the law of arms'. Shakespeare gives both versions."],["The Chorus","No other Shakespeare history has a Chorus before every act. He apologises for the theatre, praises Henry, and fills the gaps of time and place. Many readers notice that what he says does not always match what the scenes show: he praises a united England just before we meet traitors and thieves."],["On stage and screen","Laurence Olivier's film (1944), made during the Second World War, cut the traitors, the threats at Harfleur and the killing of prisoners and celebrated national unity. Kenneth Branagh's film (1989) showed mud, blood and the hanging of Bardolph. Productions often reveal their view of war by what they cut."]],"critics":{"kingship":[["William Hazlitt, 'Characters of Shakespear's Plays' (1817)","Paraphrased: Hazlitt admired the poetry but disliked Henry, seeing him as a king who goes to war for glory and plunder and whom we like on stage only because he is not our king."],["W. B. Yeats, 'At Stratford-on-Avon' (1901)","Paraphrased: Yeats saw Henry as Shakespeare's picture of the successful, practical man, efficient and admired but coarse and without inner life, set against the failed poet-king Richard II."]],"war":[["Norman Rabkin, 'Rabbits, Ducks, and Henry V' (1977)","Paraphrased: Rabkin argued that the play is like an optical puzzle that can be seen as a duck or a rabbit: it presents Henry as an ideal Christian king and as a ruthless politician at the same time, and the audience cannot hold both views at once."],["Harold C. Goddard, 'The Meaning of Shakespeare' (1951)","Paraphrased: Goddard read the play ironically, arguing that the Chorus gives the popular, patriotic view while the scenes reveal the brutality and self-deception of the war."]],"nation":[["Jonathan Dollimore and Alan Sinfield, 'History and Ideology: the Instance of Henry V' (1985)","Paraphrased: they argued that the play works hard to show a united nation under a strong king, but that the effort reveals the anxieties it tries to hide, such as the traitors, the Irish captain's anger and the doubts of the soldiers."]],"theatre":[["Anne Barton, 'The King Disguised' (1975)","Paraphrased: Barton compared Henry's night walk with older plays and ballads in which a disguised king meets his subjects happily, and argued that Shakespeare deliberately lets the encounter with Williams go wrong, showing the distance between king and subject."]],"youth":[["Stephen Greenblatt, 'Invisible Bullets' (1985)","Paraphrased: Greenblatt argued that the Henry plays raise doubts about power, such as Hal's calculated use of his friends and Henry's ruthlessness, only to contain them, so that the audience's doubts end up strengthening its admiration for the king."]],"god":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, 'Shakespeare's History Plays' (1944)","Paraphrased: Tillyard read the histories as an epic of English order and divine providence leading to the Tudors, but found Henry V the weakest of the cycle because Henry has become a public type rather than a living character."]]},"symbols":{"God":"\\bgod\\b|\\bheaven\\b|\\blord\\b","Honour":"\\bhonou?rs?\\b|\\bglory\\b","Crown":"\\bcrowns?\\b|\\bking\\b|\\bthrone\\b","France":"\\bfrance\\b|\\bfrench\\w*","England":"\\bengland\\b|\\benglish\\w*","Ceremony":"\\bceremon\\w*|\\bmajesty\\b"},"symbols2":[["The tennis balls","The Dauphin's insult to Henry's youth. Henry turns them into gun-stones, and the whole war becomes his answer to the joke."],["The wooden O","The theatre itself, which the Chorus admits cannot hold Agincourt. It makes the audience part of the play's work of imagination."],["Erpingham's cloak","The disguise that lets Henry walk as a common man and hear what his soldiers think of their king."],["The gloves","The pledges exchanged by Henry and Williams. Henry's trick with them turns a serious quarrel into a joke and ends with a glove full of crowns."],["The leek","The Welsh badge worn on Saint David's Day. Fluellen makes Pistol eat it, a comic victory of Welsh pride over English mockery."],["The pax","The holy tablet Bardolph steals. His hanging for it shows Henry's justice and the end of his old friendships."],["The garden","Burgundy's 'best garden of the world', overrun by weeds: France ruined by war, and the peace that might restore it."],["Crowns","Gold coins and royal crowns: the French bribe the traitors with crowns, Henry jokes about cutting French crowns, and Williams's glove is filled with crowns."]],"solo":{"2":1,"69":1,"217":1,"238":1,"457":1,"543":1,"549":1,"644":1,"784":1,"816":1,"907":1},"why":["Opens the play with the Chorus's apology and ambition.","Makes the audience aware of the stage itself.","A famous image of the Globe.","Makes imagination a partner in the action.","Shows the gap between stage and history.","Links Henry to Hal of the Henry IV plays.","Presents the change as a miracle.","Shows how the Church flatters the King.","Explains the reformation as planned.","Adds doubt to the miracle.","Henry puts responsibility on the Church.","Shows awareness of war's cost from the start.","Religious language frames the claim.","The legal basis of the whole war.","Comic irony after a confusing speech.","The key question of the play's war.","Shifts responsibility from King to Church.","A churchman calling for blood.","Youth turned from vice to valour.","Shows English fears of Scotland.","The ideal of harmony between ranks.","Order and obedience as natural.","Order includes harsh justice.","Shows his total resolve.","Links action to reputation.","He claims self-control and Christian virtue.","The insult that provokes Henry.","A short, devastating line.","His wit becomes a threat.","Recalls 'I know you all' in 1 Henry IV.","A key image of the play.","The cost of war placed on the enemy.","A memorable warning.","Focus replaces his old idleness.","Pictures a united, eager nation.","Henry as a model king.","Patriotic image of the nation.","Introduces the Southampton plot.","French gold corrupts English hearts.","Undercuts the heroic Chorus.","Comic bravado.","Links Henry to Falstaff's rejection.","The commoners' view of Hal's change.","Loyalty mixed with resentment.","The commoners' realistic aim.","Prepares for Henry's personal hurt.","The test for the traitors.","He traps the traitors.","Henry's justice is logical and cold.","The pain of betrayal.","Gives the betrayal religious weight.","The king's two bodies: person and office.","All or nothing.","Comic and tender.","One of Shakespeare's most moving lines.","Simple, repeated, unforgettable.","Even grief is comic.","The commoners' view of loyalty.","War as theft.","The French mistake about Henry.","The wise French voice.","Echoes the Church's view of a planned reform.","He respects Henry's ancestors.","Henry's message repeats his method.","The idler has become exact.","The stage travels by imagination.","Patriotic exaggeration.","The play's most famous call to battle.","Glory and death side by side.","Courage as deliberate rage.","War as a performance.","He includes all ranks.","A vivid hunting image.","Joins God, King and country.","Parodies heroism straight away.","The ordinary view of glory.","Sharp outsider judgement.","Prepares for Bardolph's hanging.","Comic, but he values order.","The Irish captain wants action.","The tension within 'Britain'.","Mercy as a tool of war.","War's cruelty in plain view.","His habit of shifting guilt.","Shocks the audience.","Shows threat as strategy.","Prepares for the odds at Agincourt.","Comedy of language and the body.","French view of the English.","National stereotypes.","Wounded pride.","France roused at last.","Comic classical learning.","Pedantry as comedy.","An old friend condemned.","Discipline over friendship.","The fake soldier type.","Comic and cruel.","He shows no sign of their past.","Mercy as good politics.","Honesty that is also confidence.","Calm defiance.","Faith over numbers.","French vanity.","Mocked even by his own side.","Confidence before the fall.","French contempt.","A backhanded compliment.","Ironic before Agincourt.","Contrast of the two camps.","A picture of fear and patience.","Prepares for 'band of brothers'.","Henry's comforting presence.","The stage cannot show Agincourt.","His thoughtful faith.","The private man behind the king.","Comic irony.","Henry values all his nations.","The ordinary soldier's view.","The king as a human being.","Prepares for the ceremony soliloquy.","The question Williams challenges.","Short, sharp doubt.","One answer to the moral problem.","The play's strongest moral question.","War's human cost.","Separates duty from conscience.","Class and power.","The burden of the crown.","Ceremony as empty show.","Kingship as a costume.","The private cost of power.","Responsibility the people never see.","Faith before battle.","The guilt behind the crown.","Humility and unease.","French certainty.","The English look beaten.","The scale of the challenge.","Prompts the Crispin's day speech.","Turns weakness into glory.","Recalls Hotspur in 1 Henry IV.","Shows confidence.","Gives the battle a name and a feast.","War as a story told later.","Fame through storytelling.","Unity across ranks.","A promise the play tests.","Pride in the few.","Readiness is mental.","Grim humour.","Pride in plainness.","Defiance.","Faith before the battle.","Comic battle.","A proverb exposes the braggart.","Prepares for the boys' deaths.","The French collapse.","French honour at the end.","Death before shame.","Chivalric love in death.","Even soldiers weep.","The play's most debated act.","Breaking the rules of war.","How Henry's men see it.","The past returns at the moment of victory.","Emotion behind the order.","Victory in four words.","Humility in victory.","Unites the nations.","A condition on loyalty.","The joke ends.","Honest dignity.","Kingship depends on appearance.","Grim respect.","Victory as miracle.","Humility or policy?","God's credit enforced by law.","Victory as worship.","Henry refuses a triumph.","Henry as a Roman hero.","Comic justice for the braggart.","Welsh and English united.","The end of the Eastcheap world.","The truth behind war stories.","What war destroys.","The cost to France.","Marriage as a term of peace.","The soldier as lover.","A role he plays well.","Wit in the wooing.","The politics of the wooing.","Love and conquest joined.","Irony: their son lost France.","A reminder of his father's troubles.","She has little choice.","Royal power in private life.","A tender moment.","Cities and woman joined.","Peace through marriage.","Returns to the theme of the stage.","Praise mixed with loss.","Undercuts the happy ending."],"insults":[{"id":"59","f":"you savour too much of your youth","who":"The Dauphin (through his ambassador)","n":"The Dauphin's insult that starts the war."},{"id":"86","f":"Pish for thee, Iceland dog! thou prick-ear'd cur of Iceland!","who":"Pistol","n":"Pistol's comic abuse of Nym."},{"id":"89","f":"egregious dog! O viper vile!","who":"Pistol","n":"Mock-heroic insults from the old plays."},{"id":"96","f":"O hound of Crete","who":"Pistol","n":"More dog insults for Nym."},{"id":"151","f":"thou cruel, Ingrateful, savage, and inhuman creature!","who":"King Henry","n":"Henry's fury at Scroop's betrayal."},{"id":"190","f":"By a vain, giddy, shallow, humorous youth,","who":"The Dauphin","n":"The Dauphin's contempt for Henry."},{"id":"233","f":"Up to the breach, you dogs! Avaunt, you cullions!","who":"Fluellen","n":"Fluellen drives the cowards forward."},{"id":"246","f":"By Cheshu, he is an ass, as in the world.","who":"Fluellen","n":"Fluellen on Macmorris."},{"id":"259","f":"Ish a villain, and a bastard, and a knave, and a rascal?","who":"Macmorris","n":"Macmorris hears an insult to his nation."},{"id":"317","f":"Normans, but bastard Normans, Norman bastards!","who":"Bourbon","n":"French contempt for the English."},{"id":"346","f":"Die and be damn'd! and figo for thy friendship!","who":"Pistol","n":"Pistol's obscene gesture at Fluellen."},{"id":"351","f":"Why, this is an arrant counterfeit rascal.","who":"Gower","n":"Gower sees through Pistol."},{"id":"445","f":"What a wretched and peevish fellow is this King of England,","who":"Orleans","n":"French scorn the night before the battle."},{"id":"449","f":"Foolish curs,","who":"Orleans","n":"The English as stupid dogs."},{"id":"490","f":"The figo for thee, then!","who":"Pistol","n":"Pistol insults the disguised King."},{"id":"528","f":"Come, 'tis a foolish saying.","who":"Williams","n":"A common soldier rebukes the King without knowing it."},{"id":"571","f":"Yond island carrions,","who":"Grandpre","n":"The English as corpses."},{"id":"743","f":"'Sblood! an arrant traitor as any is in the universal world,","who":"Fluellen","n":"Fluellen's outrage at Williams."},{"id":"791","f":"you scurvy, lousy knave,","who":"Fluellen","n":"Fluellen's revenge on Pistol."},{"id":"797","f":"if you can mock a leek, you can eat a leek.","who":"Fluellen","n":"Comic justice."},{"id":"814","f":"you are a counterfeit cowardly knave.","who":"Gower","n":"Gower's final judgement on Pistol."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"58","f":"Tell us the Dauphin's mind.","n":"Henry uses the royal 'we' and 'us' in public: he speaks as the state."},{"id":"151","f":"What shall I say to thee, Lord Scroop?","n":"Henry turns to intimate 'thee' with Scroop: the hurt is personal."},{"id":"156","f":"You have conspir'd against our royal person,","n":"Back to formal 'you' and royal 'our' for the sentence."},{"id":"474","f":"art thou officer?","n":"Pistol 'thou's the disguised King as an equal or inferior."},{"id":"532","f":"How shall I know thee again?","n":"Williams uses 'thee' to the man he takes for a fellow soldier."},{"id":"762","f":"Your Majesty came not like yourself.","n":"Once Williams knows the King, he uses respectful 'your Majesty'."},{"id":"837","f":"Do you like me, Kate?","n":"Henry begins his wooing with 'you'."},{"id":"848","f":"I speak to thee plain soldier.","n":"He moves to loving 'thee' as the wooing warms."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["The bishops oppose the war","The bishops fund the war to protect their lands"],"a1s2":["Henry decides against war","Henry declares war after the tennis balls"],"a2s1":["Nym and Pistol fight to the death","They make up and go to see the dying Falstaff"],"a2s2":["The traitors kill Henry","Henry traps them with their own words"],"a2s3":["Falstaff recovers","The Hostess describes his death"],"a2s4":["The French King surrenders","He promises an answer the next day"],"a3s1":["Harfleur falls at once","Henry rallies his men at the breach"],"a3s2":["The commoners fight bravely","They hang back and Fluellen drives them on"],"a3s3":["Henry sacks Harfleur","Harfleur surrenders and Henry orders mercy"],"a3s4":["Katharine refuses to learn English","She learns English body words, badly"],"a3s5":["The French let Henry pass","They gather a great army to stop him"],"a3s6":["Henry saves Bardolph","Henry approves his execution"],"a3s7":["The French fear the battle","They boast and gamble for English prisoners"],"a4s1":["Henry sleeps before battle","He walks the camp in disguise"],"a4s2":["The French wait for the English to attack","They rush confidently to the field"],"a4s3":["Henry pays a ransom","He rallies his men and refuses ransom"],"a4s4":["Pistol is killed","Pistol captures a Frenchman for ransom"],"a4s5":["The French win","The French realise the day is lost"],"a4s6":["Henry spares the prisoners","Henry orders them killed"],"a4s7":["The French fight on","Montjoy concedes and asks to bury the dead"],"a4s8":["Henry punishes Williams","Henry rewards Williams"],"a5s1":["Pistol beats Fluellen","Fluellen makes Pistol eat a leek"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Henry and the Dauphin","pts":["Both are young princes; the Dauphin thinks Henry is still a wild boy.","The Dauphin boasts about his horse; Henry speaks plainly about his weak army.","Henry wins honour by action; the Dauphin talks and then wants to stab himself."],"ids":[62,384]},{"t":"Henry and Williams","pts":["Both speak in prose in the night scene, as equals.","Williams thinks the King answers for his soldiers' souls; Henry says each soul is its own.","Williams keeps his word when he knows who Henry is; Henry rewards honesty with crowns."],"ids":[521,522]},{"t":"Fluellen and Pistol","pts":["Both are comic and both talk too much.","Fluellen is brave, loyal and keeps the rules of war; Pistol is a coward who steals.","Fluellen's last scene beats Pistol into eating the leek he mocked."],"ids":[345,346]},{"t":"The English and French camps","pts":["The French spend the night boasting and gambling for prisoners.","The English sit 'like sacrifices' by their fires, and their King walks among them.","The French have numbers and fine horses; the English have discipline and unity."],"ids":[453,457]},{"t":"The Chorus and the scenes","pts":["The Chorus praises a united England; the next scene shows quarrelling thieves.","The Chorus promises glorious battles; the stage shows Pistol ransoming a Frenchman.","The Chorus ends in triumph, then reminds us it was all lost."],"ids":[69,75]}],"analysis":{"KING HENRY":["Henry is the play's hero and its puzzle. The bishops tell us that the wild Prince Hal of the Henry IV plays has been transformed overnight into a model king, wise in theology, politics and war. The Chorus calls him 'the mirror of all Christian kings'. Henry himself is careful to look just: he asks Canterbury whether he may claim France 'with right and conscience', and warns him that God will hold him responsible for every drop of blood. Yet the Church has its own reasons for wanting the war, and Henry seems to have made up his mind already.","Henry is a master of words. He turns the Dauphin's tennis balls into gun-stones, rallies his men at Harfleur with 'Once more unto the breach', and on Saint Crispin's day promises that 'we few, we happy few, we band of brothers' will be remembered for ever. His speeches change with his audience: royal 'we' to ambassadors, warm 'thee' to Scroop in his betrayal, plain prose to soldiers and to Katharine.","He can also be ruthless. He condemns the traitors without mercy, threatens Harfleur with rape and the murder of babies, lets his old friend Bardolph hang without a word, and orders the French prisoners killed. Each time he presents the harsh act as justice or necessity, and often shifts the blame to others: the Dauphin for the widows, the townspeople for their daughters, the French for the boys.","The night before Agincourt shows his private side. Disguised, he argues with Williams and cannot quite answer him; alone, he feels the weight of being responsible for everyone's lives and sins, and dismisses ceremony as an empty idol; in prayer he remembers his father's crime. After the battle he gives all the glory to God, and in the wooing he plays the plain soldier with great charm. Critics still argue whether he is an ideal king or a skilled politician; the play gives evidence for both."],"CHORUS":["The Chorus speaks before each act and at the end. He apologises for the 'wooden O' and asks the audience to use their 'imaginary forces' to turn a few actors into armies and a bare stage into France. He is the voice of wonder and patriotism, praising England's youth 'on fire' and 'a little touch of Harry in the night'.","He also controls time and place, carrying the audience across the sea and leaping over years, and he links the play to its own moment by comparing Henry to Essex returning from Ireland.","But the Chorus is not always reliable. He describes a nation united, and the scene that follows shows Nym and Pistol quarrelling; he promises glorious battle, and we see Pistol ransoming a Frenchman. His last speech reminds us that everything Henry won was lost under his son. Whether these gaps are deliberate irony or just the limits of the stage is one of the play's open questions."],"FLUELLEN":["Fluellen is the Welsh captain whose accent, long words and love of the 'disciplines of the wars' make him the play's chief comic character after Falstaff's death. He compares everyone to classical heroes and loses track of his own comparisons, as when Monmouth and Macedon both have rivers 'and there is salmons in both'.","But he is also brave, loyal and honest. He drives the cowards to the breach, refuses to plead for Bardolph because 'discipline ought to be used', and tells Gower to keep quiet in the camp, which Henry admires: 'There is much care and valour in this Welshman.'","He loves Henry as a fellow Welshman, though he adds that he need not be ashamed of him 'so long as your Majesty is an honest man'. Henry uses him in the glove trick, and he beats Pistol into eating the leek, a victory for the Welsh and for honesty. He helps show the different nations in Henry's army working, and quarrelling, together."],"PISTOL":["Pistol is the last survivor of the Eastcheap gang from the Henry IV plays. He speaks in a ranting style borrowed from old plays, full of 'hounds of Crete' and 'fatal webs', which makes his cowardice comic. He has married the Hostess and goes to France to steal: 'like horse-leeches, my boys, To suck, to suck, the very blood to suck!'","He shows the war from below. He pleads for Bardolph, though with no thought of the law; he praises the disguised King to his face; and his only fight is taking a terrified Frenchman's ransom, with the Boy as interpreter.","His last scene ends the comic world of the plays. Beaten by Fluellen and forced to eat the leek, he learns that his Doll is dead and resolves to go home as a pimp and pickpocket, passing off his bruises as war wounds. He is the kind of fake soldier Gower describes, and a reminder that not everyone comes home a hero."],"WILLIAMS":["Michael Williams is a common soldier who meets the disguised King on the night before Agincourt. He is honest, blunt and afraid: 'We see yonder the beginning of the day, but I think we shall never see the end of it.'","He asks the play's hardest question: if the King's cause is not good, he must answer for all the men who die badly in battle. Henry argues that each soldier's soul is his own, but never says whether the cause is just. Williams also doubts that the King will refuse to be ransomed, which leads to their quarrel and the exchange of gloves.","After the battle he keeps his word and strikes the man wearing his glove. Faced with the King, he does not grovel but explains that the fault was Henry's disguise. Henry fills his glove with crowns, but Williams refuses Fluellen's shilling. He gives the common soldier a voice and a dignity that the play respects."],"DAUPHIN":["The Dauphin, heir to the French throne, is Henry's opposite. He mocks Henry with tennis balls, calls him a 'vain, giddy, shallow, humorous youth', and refuses to believe the Constable's warning that Henry's wildness was a disguise.","On the night before Agincourt he talks of nothing but his horse, which he calls Pegasus and his mistress, and even his own lords laugh at him behind his back. He is all show and no substance, the kind of prince people once thought Hal was.","In defeat he cries 'all is confounded' and talks of stabbing himself. Through him the play turns its earlier insults about Henry's youth back on the French."],"KATHARINE":["Katharine, the French King's daughter, appears in only two scenes. In the first she has an English lesson with Alice, learning the words for parts of the body and blushing at 'foot' and 'gown'. The scene is comic, but she is learning the language of the man who is invading her country.","In the last scene Henry woos her in a mixture of plain English and comic French. She is witty and careful: 'Is it possible dat I should love de enemy of France?' and 'Your Majestee ave fausse French enough to deceive de most sage damoiselle dat is en France.'","She has little choice: Henry calls her 'our capital demand', and she answers that it will be as her father pleases. Her marriage seals the peace and promises a son who, the Epilogue reminds us, will lose France."]},"motifs":[["Tennis balls and games","The Dauphin's tennis balls mock Henry's youth, and Henry answers in tennis terms: rackets, sets, hazards, chases. War becomes a deadly game, and gambling runs through the play, from Rambures's bet for prisoners to Henry's 'gentler gamester'.",[26,27,28,30,99]],["The stage and imagination","The Chorus apologises for the 'wooden O' and asks the audience to piece out the play with their thoughts. Henry is always performing, and he sees ceremony itself as a kind of theatre.",[0,1,2,3,112,113,130,200]],["Ceremony and the king's two bodies","Henry is both a man, who smells the violet as others do, and a king who must bear everyone's sins. His soliloquy asks what ceremony is worth, and Williams's defence ('Your Majesty came not like yourself') shows how much kingship depends on appearance.",[119,120,128,129,130,131,173]],["Blood, bodies and the cost of war","Widows' tears, babies on pikes, the limbs that will cry out at the Last Judgement, York kissing Suffolk's wounds, the lists of the dead: the play keeps showing what the glory costs.",[31,82,84,124,125,160,174]],["Brothers and band","Henry calls his men 'brothers, friends, and countrymen', and 'we band of brothers' promises that shared blood makes every man noble. The idea is tested by the hanging of Bardolph and by Williams.",[111,146,147,172]],["The garden and the weed","Ely's strawberry under the nettle, Henry's honey from the weed, and Burgundy's ruined garden of France: the play uses gardens for growth, corruption and peace.",[8,114,185,186]]],"bigq":[["Is Henry an ideal king or a ruthless politician?",["He seeks a just cause and gives the glory to God","He is loved by his soldiers and fights beside them","He threatens Harfleur with horrors and kills prisoners","He lets old friends die and shifts blame onto others"],[10,25,81,98,162,168]],["Is the war just?",["Canterbury gives a legal case and Henry asks for honesty","The Church has its own reasons for wanting the war","Williams asks whether the cause is good and gets no direct answer","The French are arrogant and the victory seems God's"],[15,16,121,122,124,175]],["Does the Chorus tell the truth?",["He praises Henry and the united nation","The scenes often contradict him","He admits the stage cannot show the real events","The Epilogue undercuts the triumph"],[34,36,39,112,113,202]],["Does Henry really change from Hal?",["The bishops say he was transformed overnight","He lets Bardolph hang and condemns Scroop","He still plays tricks, as with the glove","Fluellen reminds everyone of Falstaff"],[5,6,9,29,165,171]],["How does the play show the common soldier?",["Pistol, Nym and Bardolph go to war to steal","The Boy sees through them and is killed","Williams speaks with honesty and courage","Henry promises the band of brothers will be gentled"],[58,76,118,124,147,172]],["How united is Henry's Britain?",["The four captains serve together","Macmorris and Fluellen nearly fight","The Scots are feared as invaders","Henry claims to be Welsh and Gower defends Fluellen"],[19,79,80,117,169,182]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Henry as a leader?",[67,71,111,126,140,146]],["How does Shakespeare present war in Henry V?",[30,67,82,124,162,174]],["How does Shakespeare use the Chorus?",[0,3,36,112,113,202]],["How does Shakespeare present the relationship between king and subjects?",[111,119,124,131,147,173]],["How does Shakespeare present the French?",[59,89,103,108,157,186]],["How does Shakespeare present justice and mercy?",[46,48,50,85,98,162]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (Chorus, soliloquy, imagery, prose and verse, contrast, irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Henry","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[67,"Shakespeare presents Henry at Harfleur as a leader who turns fear into action through language.","'Once more' admits that the attack has already failed, but the repetition makes failure sound like a chance to try again.","The alternative, 'close the wall up with our English dead', is grim, yet it makes death sound like a patriotic act.","The speech moves from 'noblest English' to 'good yeomen', so that every rank feels included."],[124,"Williams challenges the heroic view of war that the Crispin's day speech will later offer.","His picture of severed 'legs and arms and heads' joining together at the Last Judgement makes the cost of battle physical and frightening.","The phrase 'heavy reckoning' turns war into a debt the King must pay to God.","Henry's reply never addresses whether the cause is good, which leaves the question hanging over the battle."],[146,"The Crispin's day speech turns the English weakness into a source of pride.","'We few, we happy few' repeats and expands, so that 'few' changes from a fear to a privilege.","'Band of brothers' suggests equality, and Henry promises that fighting will 'gentle' the condition of the lowest man.","Yet the lists of the dead in 4.8 name only the nobles, which makes some readers doubt the promise."],[130,"Henry's soliloquy on ceremony shows the private cost of kingship.","By addressing Ceremony as an 'idol', he suggests that people worship an empty show.","The contrast with the 'wretched slave' who sleeps soundly is ironic, since Henry is about to ask such men to die for him.","The speech makes him sympathetic, but also self-pitying, as Williams's harder questions remain unanswered."],[162,"The order to kill the prisoners is one of the most debated moments in the play.","It comes straight after Exeter's tender account of York and Suffolk, so the change of tone is shocking.","The short, plain line 'Then every soldier kill his prisoners' has none of the rhetoric of Henry's great speeches.","In the next scene Gower praises it as revenge for the boys, so the play offers two reasons and lets the audience judge."],[202,"The Epilogue ends the play by undercutting its triumph.","'Small time' reminds us that Henry died young, and 'this star of England' makes him a bright but brief light.","The line 'they lost France and made his England bleed' sets everything Henry won against what followed.","Shakespeare reminds his audience of his own Henry VI plays, so this history ends by pointing to tragedy."]],"lang":{"un":[["2","Piece out our imperfections with your thoughts:","Fill in our shortcomings with your imaginations."],["209","Now he weighs time Even to the utmost grain.","Now he values every moment exactly."],["522","Every subject's duty is the King's; but every subject's soul is his own.","Each subject owes service to the King but answers to God for his own soul."],["589","This day shall gentle his condition;","This day will make him a gentleman."]],"pun":[["62","Hath turn'd his balls to gun-stones,","balls","double meaning","Tennis balls become cannonballs."],["69","for the gilt of France,--O guilt indeed!--","gilt","sound pun","Gold (gilt) and guilt."],["543","it is no English treason to cut French crowns,","crowns","double meaning","Crowns as heads and as coins; clipping as cutting both."],["384","the basest horn of his hoof is more musical than the pipe of Hermes.","horn","double meaning","The horny hoof and a musical horn."],["848","for the other I have no strength in measure, yet a reasonable measure in strength.","measure","word play","Measure as dancing and as amount."],["298","De sin. Le col, de nick; le menton, de sin.","sin","sound pun","Katharine's 'sin' for 'chin'."]],"st":[["222","Once more unto the breach, dear friends, once more,",[["Once more","Repetition: the attack has failed before."],["dear friends","Warm, equal address."]]],["589","We few, we happy few, we band of brothers.",[["few","Repeated, turning weakness into pride."],["brothers","Unity across ranks."]]],["543","O Ceremony, show me but thy worth!",[["Ceremony","Addressed like a person or idol."],["worth","Value, as in money."]]]]},"dir":[["62","Hath turn'd his balls to gun-stones,","How should Henry answer the tennis balls?",[["With cold, quiet fury","He is dangerous because he is controlled."],["Laughing, then turning hard","He matches the joke and then destroys it."],["Shouting with rage","The insult really hurts."]]],["151","Thou that didst bear the key of all my counsels,","How should Henry speak to Scroop?",[["With personal grief","He loved and trusted him."],["With public anger","He makes an example of him."],["Coldly, as a judge","The king has replaced the friend."]]],["270","Your naked infants spitted upon pikes,","How should Henry deliver the threats to Harfleur?",[["As genuine threats","He means every word."],["As a bluff he hates to make","He wants surrender, not slaughter."],["Calmly, as policy","It is a tactic, not a feeling."]]],["361","We would have all such offenders so cut off;","How should Henry react to Bardolph's death?",[["Without a flicker","The king has buried Hal."],["With a pause and a hard swallow","He remembers, and hides it."],["Turning away from Fluellen","He cannot let anyone see."]]],["669","Then every soldier kill his prisoners;","How should the order to kill the prisoners be staged?",[["Quickly, as a military necessity","There is no time to think."],["After a long silence","He knows what he is doing."],["Cut, as in some films","To keep Henry heroic."]]],["848","I speak to thee plain soldier.","How should Henry woo Katharine?",[["Awkwardly and sincerely","He really is a plain soldier."],["Confidently, as a performance","He is as skilled at wooing as at speeches."],["Ironically, aware she has no choice","The power is all on his side."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["784","907"],["61","669","41"],["272","589","694"],["681","907","166"],["166","681","907"],["222","467","589"],["249","259","700"],["467","503","520"],["669","673","674"],["2","217","457"],["222","589","669"]],"cr":{"kingship":[["39","272","669"],["222","589","467"]],"war":[["222","272","589"],["2","272","669"]],"nation":[["259","589","700"]],"theatre":[["467","503","520"]],"youth":[["15","61","681"]],"god":[["39","374","694"]]}}};
